window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"toby-fox-undertale":["More than a hundred short tracks for a game about a child who falls into an underground world of monsters and may choose never to fight. The sound mixes chip-like tones with cheap sampled pianos, brass and strings, deliberately homemade. There are cosy town tunes, a frantic spider waltz, a heroic spear battle, and Megalovania, a pounding, furious battle track that became a worldwide internet anthem. The music is everywhere, and almost every tune returns somewhere else.","Toby Fox, a young American, wrote, designed and programmed Undertale almost alone, funded by a Kickstarter campaign, and released it in September 2015. He had grown up on the Japanese role-playing game EarthBound and on Homestuck, a sprawling web comic for which he had written music. Megalovania itself predates the game, first appearing in an EarthBound fan hack. He composed with inexpensive software and stock sounds.","The score is a web of leitmotifs: the gentle tune of the opening, called Once Upon a Time, turns up in dozens of disguises, slowed, sped up, flipped, and in the final battle the themes of every character are woven into a single piece, Hopes and Dreams. Listen for the melody of a friendly character hidden inside a menacing boss theme. The music changes depending on whether the player has spared or killed monsters.","The game became a phenomenon and its soundtrack an unusual commercial and cultural success for a one-person project, played by school bands, orchestras and pianists online. Critics compared its motivic web to Wagner's, half jokingly and half not. Some find the sampled sound crude; Fox's admirers argue that its lo-fi charm is part of its honesty, and that the melodic invention outweighs any polish.","Start with Fallen Down and Once Upon a Time, then jump to Hopes and Dreams and try to count the earlier tunes inside it. Hear Megalovania last. Compare Kondo's chip scores for the roots and Wagner's Rheingold prelude for the idea of a whole drama grown from a few musical cells."],"toby-fox-deltarune":["Music for Toby Fox's second role-playing game, released in chapters, set in a world parallel to Undertale's. It is fuller and more confident than its predecessor: Rude Buster is a punchy battle theme driven by funk bass and brass stabs; Field of Hopes and Dreams is a sweeping, nostalgic overworld march; Chapter 2 adds a frantic, jazz-rock boss track, BIG SHOT, and a gentle song, Don't Forget, sung by Laura Shigihara.","Fox released the first chapter free and without warning on Halloween 2018, with a second in September 2021, now working with a small team after the success of Undertale. Further chapters followed later. The score again carries much of the story, and fans dissect it obsessively online for hints about plot, a kind of musical detective work that the game encourages.","Many new themes are rebuilt from Undertale melodies, and some Deltarune tunes are themselves recycled from music Fox wrote years earlier, so listening across both games reveals a long web of borrowings. Listen to Don't Forget, which first appears as a lullaby-like song and then echoes through other cues. Battle themes often hide a character's calmer theme beneath the noise.","The chapters were received ecstatically by Undertale's fans, and critics praised a richer, more varied sound. Some reviewers felt the episodic release made it hard to judge the whole, and debate continues over whether the later chapters will match Undertale's emotional weight. The music has already gathered a vast life of covers and orchestral arrangements.","Start with Field of Hopes and Dreams, then Rude Buster, and listen for the funk bass. Hear Don't Forget for the gentle core. Then go back to Undertale's soundtrack and try to spot shared melodies. Compare Uematsu's Final Fantasy VI and Shimomura's Street Fighter II for the battle-music tradition Fox draws on."],"djawadi-game-of-thrones":["Eight seasons of music for HBO's fantasy saga, gathered on many albums. The main title is the anchor: a driving cello melody in a rocking triple rhythm over pounding strings, heard every week over a clockwork map of the continent. Each great family has its own theme, from the grand Lannister melody, also a song called The Rains of Castamere, to the Targaryen music for Daenerys and her dragons. The sixth season ends with Light of the Seven, built from piano, boys' voices and organ.","HBO's series, adapted from George R. R. Martin's novels, premiered in April 2011 and ended in May 2019, becoming one of the most watched shows of its era. Djawadi, a German-born composer of Iranian descent who had trained under Hans Zimmer in Los Angeles, was hired shortly before the first season aired. The producers asked him to avoid flutes and violins, which they felt sounded too much like familiar fantasy films.","Light of the Seven is a remarkable departure: the show's first long use of piano, it plays for almost ten minutes under a wordless sequence, and its slow build is the scene's whole structure. Listen too for the Rains of Castamere tune, which first appears as a song and later returns as an instrumental warning shortly before one of the most shocking scenes in the story. The main title's cello melody was deliberately designed to be hummed.","The theme became a global earworm, played by street musicians and orchestras alike, and Djawadi toured the music in arenas. Critics were cooler at first, finding the early seasons functional, but praise grew as the scoring deepened; Light of the Seven is often singled out. Some argue the leitmotif system is more Wagnerian in ambition than in execution; others point out how well it carried a vast story over a decade.","Start with the main title and count the rocking rhythm in threes, then hear Light of the Seven from beginning to end. Listen for a family's theme returning in a new context. Compare Wagner's Die Walküre for the original saga built from leitmotifs, and Howard Shore's Lord of the Rings for a different fantasy answer."],"djawadi-westworld":["Music for HBO's science-fiction series about an American theme park staffed by lifelike robots. The main title is a cool, brooding piano and string theme that builds as machines assemble a human body on screen. Its signature, though, is a series of arrangements of rock songs for a player piano in the park's saloon: Radiohead's No Surprises and Fake Plastic Trees, Soundgarden's Black Hole Sun and the Rolling Stones' Paint It Black, turned into tinkling Wild West music.","Jonathan Nolan and Lisa Joy's series premiered on HBO in October 2016, adapted from Michael Crichton's 1973 film. Djawadi was working on Game of Thrones at the same time. The producers wanted the familiar songs to feel anachronistic and slightly wrong, as if the park's robots were repeating something without understanding it, and the mechanical player piano, which plays without a human, became the perfect emblem.","The player piano is the show's central metaphor: an instrument that performs by itself from a punched roll, like the robots who follow their programmed loops. Watch the opening titles, where a piano roll appears beside the mechanical hands. Listen for the main theme's slight changes from season to season. The song choices often comment on the plot, though the words are never sung.","The cover versions became the most talked-about element of the music, prompting viewers to seek out the originals and spawning endless online lists. Some critics found the device a gimmick that wore thin; others thought it a precise image of the show's ideas about repetition and consciousness. The main title was nominated for an Emmy, and its sound shaped a fashion for slow, eerie piano covers in trailers.","Hear the player-piano No Surprises, then Radiohead's original on OK Computer, and notice what is lost and gained. Then watch the main title sequence. Compare Conlon Nancarrow's Studies for Player Piano, music written for a machine to play what human hands cannot, and Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach, familiar music re-voiced through a machine."],"mike-post-hill-street-blues-theme":["A gentle, melancholy piano melody for a gritty police drama. Over soft strings and a light rhythm section, the piano plays a slow, singing tune that feels more like a ballad than a cop-show theme; in the single, the guitarist Larry Carlton takes a warm, lyrical solo. There is no siren and no brass, just a wistful tenderness that suggested this show would care about its exhausted characters as much as its crimes.","Steven Bochco and Michael Kozoll's series premiered on NBC in January 1981 and changed American television with its sprawling cast, overlapping storylines and handheld camera. Post, already the most prolific theme writer in the business, wrote against the expected action music. Released as a single, the theme became a top-ten hit in the United States and won Grammy awards, a rare crossover for television music.","The theme quietly mirrors the show's approach: instead of announcing danger, it sets a mood of weary compassion, so the opening credits prepare viewers for character drama. Listen for how short and simple the melody is, a few notes repeated and varied, which made it easy to recognise in a three-second sting. The single version stretches the tune and adds Carlton's guitar solo, which is not in the broadcast titles.","Critics and the public loved it at once, and it was often cited as proof that television music could stand on its own. It has since become a byword for the gentle piano theme in a tough drama, imitated and parodied. Some later critics find its soft-rock gloss dated; admirers say its restraint was radical in a genre dominated by brass fanfares and funky chase music.","Hear the short television version and then the full single, and listen for Carlton's guitar. Notice how the melody avoids drama. Compare Post's own Law & Order theme, a decade later, for a different crime sound, and Elfman's Simpsons theme for how much character a television theme can pack into seconds."],"mike-post-law-and-order":["A cool, bluesy groove for a show split between police and prosecutors. A funky electric piano and bass set up a steady, slightly jazzy pulse; a reedy, wistful lead melody floats above it, followed by a guitar figure. But the most famous sound in the show is not in the theme at all: it is the heavy, metallic double clang, often written as dun dun, that marks every scene change, as recognisable as any melody in American television.","Dick Wolf's series premiered on NBC in September 1990 and ran for twenty seasons, returning in 2022, while its spin-offs multiplied. Post, who had written themes for The Rockford Files, Magnum, P.I. and Hill Street Blues, was by then the most prolific theme composer in Hollywood. The scene-change sound was designed to move viewers briskly from one location to the next in a show built on a strict two-part structure.","Post has described the scene-change clang as a layered combination of many sounds, including, he has said, a large group of men stamping on a wooden floor, processed into a single blow. Listen for the theme's two halves, which loosely match the show's two halves, police and court. The lead melody's bluesy bends give a slightly melancholy tone, hinting at the moral greyness of the cases.","The clang became a cultural shorthand, used in parodies, comedy sketches and everyday speech, and is often cited among the most recognisable sounds in television history. The theme itself is admired for its economy. Critics seldom take it seriously as music, yet sound designers point to the clang as a model of how a few hundred milliseconds of sound can carry a whole brand.","Hear the opening titles and then one scene change, and notice how the clang works as punctuation. Listen to the bluesy lead melody. Compare Post's Hill Street Blues theme, softer and more lyrical, and Mark Snow's X-Files theme, from the same era, for television music that relies on a single unforgettable sound."],"mccreary-battlestar-galactica":["Music for a war in space that sounds nothing like Star Wars. Instead of brass fanfares, battles are driven by thundering Japanese taiko drums and metal percussion in irregular rhythms; grief is carried by the Armenian duduk, a double-reed instrument with a dark, sobbing tone, and by the Chinese erhu. There are passacaglias for strings, gamelan-like chimes, and, in the third season's finale, a fierce, sitar-tinged arrangement of Bob Dylan's All Along the Watchtower.","Ronald D. Moore's reimagining of a 1978 series ran on the Sci Fi Channel from 2004 to 2009. McCreary, in his twenties and a protégé of Elmer Bernstein, had assisted Richard Gibbs on the 2003 miniseries and took over the score for the series. The producers wanted to avoid the clichés of space opera and asked for music that sounded raw and documentary-like, matching the show's handheld camera.","All Along the Watchtower is not just a cover; in the story, characters hear the song in their heads, and it turns out to be a crucial plot clue, so McCreary built motifs from its melody into the score long before the finale. Listen for the rhythms in odd groupings, sevens and fives, in the battle drumming. The first season's titles set a Sanskrit mantra, sung over drones.","The score built a devoted following, with sold-out concerts by McCreary's band, and was widely credited with changing the sound of science-fiction television. Critics praised its use of non-Western instruments as structural rather than decorative. A few found the drums overused by the later seasons; many composers since have adopted the percussion-led battle style it popularised.","Start with a battle cue, listening for the taiko, then a quiet duduk passage, and then the Watchtower arrangement. Compare Hendrix's version on Electric Ladyland to hear what McCreary borrowed. Then hear Steve Reich's Drumming for another work built from interlocking percussion."],"mccreary-god-of-war":["Music for a game in which a Greek god of war, now living in the Norse lands with his young son, sets out to scatter his wife's ashes. The main theme is a slow, grim melody for low male choir chanting in Old Norse over pounding drums and brass, building to a massive climax. Against it sits a gentle, mournful voice for the dead mother, sung by the Faroese singer Eivør, and Nordic instruments that give the landscape its chill.","Sony's Santa Monica Studio released the game for the PlayStation 4 in April 2018, rebooting a series known for brutal Greek mythology. McCreary was asked to reflect the move north and the more intimate story of father and son. He researched Scandinavian folk music and instruments, recorded a choir singing in Old Norse, and wrote a score that is more restrained and sorrowful than the series' earlier music.","The main theme's first three notes form a short motif that runs throughout the score, in brass, voice and strings, and sometimes slowed into a lament. Listen for Eivør's wordless vocal lines, which represent the absent mother; they appear at moments of grief and tenderness, so the dead woman is present through sound. The choir's words come from Old Norse, giving the music the feel of ancient chant.","The score won awards and was praised for its emotional weight in a series once known for spectacle; the main theme became a concert favourite. Some reviewers felt the choral bombast echoed Hollywood fantasy clichés; others singled out the intimate passages as among McCreary's finest. Its success helped make the Norse sound a fixture of game and television music.","Start with the main theme and listen for the three-note motif, then hear a track featuring Eivør. Notice how the score shifts between huge and intimate. Compare Wagner's Götterdämmerung for the Norse gods in music, and Orff's O Fortuna for choral grandeur. Then hear McCreary's own Battlestar Galactica for his percussion side."],"tapia-de-veer-utopia":["Music for a lurid, violent British conspiracy thriller, built from sounds no orchestra makes. Tapia de Veer used his own voice, breaths, gasps, clicks and hiccup-like noises, layering and processing them into jittering electronic rhythms and strange, catchy melodies. The main theme bounces with a twitching, almost comic menace; other cues sound like animal calls, stuttering machines or children's songs heard through a fever, matching the show's saturated yellow colours.","Dennis Kelly's series ran on Channel 4 in Britain in 2013 and 2014, about a group of comic-book fans hunted by a shadowy organisation. Tapia de Veer, born in Chile and based in Canada, was little known outside the industry. He wanted music as unsettling and stylised as the show's visuals, and he chose to make it from the human body.","Much of the percussion is vocal: listen closely and the snares and shakers turn out to be breaths, clicks and mouth sounds, edited into grooves. The main theme hides a nursery-rhyme simplicity under its distortion, which makes it sinister, since the show's villains are often polite and childlike. Tapia de Veer has said he wanted listeners to feel something was alive inside the music.","The score won a British television craft award and was hailed by critics as one of the most original sounds on British television; it gathered a cult following after the series was cancelled. Some viewers found it irritating, which was partly the intention. It made Tapia de Veer's name and pointed to a wider turn in screen music towards voice-based, textural scoring.","Start with the main theme and try to identify which sounds come from a human mouth. Listen with headphones to catch the detail. Compare Mica Levi's Under the Skin for another alien, body-based screen score, and Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album for the same mix of childlike melody and twitching beats."],"tapia-de-veer-the-white-lotus":["Music for Mike White's satire about wealthy guests and the staff who serve them at a luxury resort. The main title, Aloha!, begins with ululating, chant-like voices and hollow log drums suggesting Hawaii, then twists into a wild, almost deranged tribal-electronic frenzy, with yelps and sudden swerves. Each season remakes the theme for a new place: the second, set in Sicily, turns it into a pulsing, operatic dance track; the third moves to Thailand.","The series premiered on HBO in July 2021, during pandemic lockdowns, and became a hit across three seasons. White asked Tapia de Veer, admired for Utopia, for music that would undercut the paradise on screen. The title theme won an Emmy, and the second season's version, Renaissance, became a club hit when DJs released remixes, rare for a television theme.","The theme sounds like a celebration but behaves like a warning: listen for how the cheerful chants keep collapsing into screams and lurches, mirroring the dark comedy beneath the luxury. The score uses the show's location to play with exoticism, then makes the exoticism uncomfortable, as the wealthy guests consume local culture as decoration. Each season's version keeps the same frantic structure under a new costume.","The main titles became the most discussed television opening of the early 2020s, with fans and critics debating whether the theme's wildness sat as satire or as appropriation of Hawaiian sounds. Most critics praised its nerve. Its remixes crossed into dance culture, and Tapia de Veer's name, previously known to specialists, became widely recognised.","Watch the opening titles of the first season and listen for the moment the chant breaks into frenzy. Then hear the Sicilian season's version, Renaissance, and notice how the same idea dresses as opera and house music. Compare Tapia de Veer's Utopia for his earlier, body-based style, and Mike Post's Law & Order for how differently a theme can set a tone."],"mark-snow-the-x-files-theme":["An eerie, whistled melody over a ticking, echoing keyboard pattern and a sudden drum beat: the theme for a television series about FBI agents investigating the paranormal. It begins with a cold, repeated figure bouncing on an echo effect, then the whistle enters with a melancholy tune that seems to come from a long way off. A slow, heavy drum groove and washes of synthesizer give it the feel of a dark, slow-motion pop single.","Chris Carter's series premiered on Fox in September 1993 and became a pop-culture phenomenon in the 1990s. Snow, a New York-trained composer working in television, wrote and performed the theme and the weekly score on synthesizers in his home studio. A single release in 1996 became a substantial hit in Britain and Europe, unusual for an instrumental television theme.","Snow has said the echoing figure that opens the theme came about by accident, when he rested his arm on the keyboard with an echo effect switched on and liked the pattern it produced. The whistle is a keyboard sound, not a person, which gives it an unnatural steadiness. Listen for the long silences in the weekly score, built from synthesizer drones, which owe a lot to Badalamenti's Twin Peaks.","It became one of the most recognisable television themes ever, endlessly whistled as shorthand for anything uncanny. Critics have praised its economy and its sense of unease. Some dismiss it as a novelty; others note that Snow's weekly underscore, mostly drones and textures, influenced the ambient, electronic sound of later prestige television and its quiet, unsettling atmospheres.","Hear the theme and listen first for the echoing figure, then the whistle. Then try a stretch of the weekly underscore, which is much quieter. Compare Badalamenti's Twin Peaks for the dreamlike television sound it inherited, and Vangelis's Blade Runner for synthesizer melancholy in science fiction."],"badalamenti-twin-peaks-fire-walk-with-me":["Music for David Lynch's film prequel to the Twin Peaks television series, darker and jazzier than the show's dreamy score. The main theme is a slow, smoky nocturne for a lonely horn over brushed drums, upright bass and soft synthesizer, like a late-night club at the end of the world. Around it come the ballad Sycamore Trees, sung in a fragile high voice by the jazz singer Jimmy Scott, a floating Julee Cruise song, and The Pink Room, a grinding, sleazy blues-rock vamp.","Lynch made the film in 1992, after the television series had been cancelled, to tell the story of Laura Palmer's last days. Badalamenti, his partner since Blue Velvet, worked with him as before, improvising to Lynch's spoken descriptions of mood. The two had also formed an experimental jazz project, Thought Gang, and its loose, menacing sound fed directly into the score, which is closer to noir jazz than to the series' synthesizer reverie.","The television themes are almost absent: Badalamenti withholds the familiar Laura Palmer melody for most of the film, so its few appearances land like memories. Listen to The Pink Room, a long, repetitive groove for a deafening roadhouse scene in which dialogue is subtitled because the music drowns it out. Jimmy Scott had sung in the series' final episode, in the dream room, and his return links film and show.","The film was booed at Cannes in 1992 and dismissed by many critics, though the soundtrack was admired even then. Over the following decades the film was reassessed as one of Lynch's most powerful works, especially after the 2017 revival of the series drew on it heavily, and the score is now often ranked above the original television album by those who prefer its darker jazz.","Start with the main theme and listen for the lonely horn over the brushes, then hear Sycamore Trees. Play The Pink Room loud. Compare Badalamenti's television Twin Peaks album for the dreamier source, Mark Snow's X-Files theme for its television descendant, and Miles Davis's Kind of Blue for the late-night modal jazz it echoes."],"rodgers-south-pacific":["Two love stories play out on a Pacific island during the Second World War: a nurse from Arkansas falls for a French planter with a past, and a young Marine lieutenant falls for a Tonkinese girl, and both affairs run aground on prejudice. Rodgers's score splits along those lines, with lush operatic ballads such as \"Some Enchanted Evening\" and \"This Nearly Was Mine\" for the planter, brassy comic numbers for the sailors, and the hazy, floating \"Bali Ha'i\" for the island that lures them all.","Rodgers and Hammerstein adapted James Michener's Pulitzer-winning book of stories, with Joshua Logan co-writing the book and directing. It opened on Broadway in April 1949, four years after the war, starring Mary Martin and the Metropolitan Opera bass Ezio Pinza, whose casting explains why the planter's songs sit so low and so grandly. It ran for nearly five years and won the 1950 Pulitzer Prize for Drama, only the second musical to do so.","The three-note figure that opens the overture is the call of Bali Ha'i, and Rodgers threads them through the score whenever the island, and the forbidden love it stands for, comes back into the characters' minds. Listen too for how short and plain \"You've Got to Be Carefully Taught\" is: a song about learned racism given almost no musical decoration, so that the argument lands bare.","It was an immediate commercial and critical triumph, and its cast album sold in enormous numbers. The song about learned prejudice drew protest from some Southern politicians on tour, and the writers refused to cut it. Later decades found the show's view of Asian characters dated, but Bartlett Sher's 2008 Lincoln Center revival, with a full-size orchestra, persuaded many critics that it is Rodgers and Hammerstein's most serious work.","Start with \"Some Enchanted Evening\", sung by an operatic bass rather than a crooner, then hear \"Bali Ha'i\" for the orchestration by Robert Russell Bennett, all harp and muted strings. The 2008 Lincoln Center cast recording restores the full orchestral sound; the original 1949 album with Martin and Pinza is the historical reference. Compare the comic \"There Is Nothing Like a Dame\" with the ballads to hear the range."],"rodgers-the-king-and-i":["A widowed English schoolteacher arrives at the court of the King of Siam in the 1860s to teach his many children, and the two strong-willed adults clash, learn from each other and almost fall in love. The score moves between Anna's warm Western ballads, such as \"Hello, Young Lovers\" and \"Getting to Know You\", and the King's halting, self-questioning monologue \"A Puzzlement\". Its most famous moment is the whirling polka \"Shall We Dance?\", where the two finally touch.","Gertrude Lawrence, who owned the rights to Margaret Landon's novel Anna and the King of Siam, brought the project to Rodgers and Hammerstein as a vehicle for herself. It opened in March 1951; Lawrence died the following year during the run. The unknown Yul Brynner played the King and made the role his own for over thirty years. Jerome Robbins choreographed, and Hammerstein wrote at a moment when Americans were newly curious about Asia after the war.","The long ballet \"The Small House of Uncle Thomas\", in which the court retells Uncle Tom's Cabin in Siamese dance, carries the show's argument about slavery and freedom. Its music, arranged by the dance composer Trude Rittmann from Rodgers's material, uses gongs, wood blocks and spoken narration, and it is the most daring passage in any Rodgers and Hammerstein score. Rodgers deliberately avoided real Thai music, writing a Western idea of the East.","It was a huge success and won the Tony for Best Musical. The 1956 film with Brynner and Deborah Kerr fixed its image worldwide, though it was banned in Thailand for its portrait of the King. Modern critics debate its orientalism and its tale of a Westerner civilising a monarch. Bartlett Sher's 2015 Lincoln Center revival, with Kelli O'Hara and Ken Watanabe, gave the King new dignity and won the Tony for revival.","Listen first to \"Shall We Dance?\" for the sheer lift of the polka as the tempo gathers, then to \"A Puzzlement\" for how Rodgers sets a man thinking aloud. Seek out a recording that includes the full \"Small House of Uncle Thomas\" ballet. The 2015 Lincoln Center cast album is excellent; the 1951 original preserves Lawrence and Brynner. Compare its view of East and West with Puccini's Madama Butterfly."],"sondheim-a-funny-thing-happened-on-the-way-to-the-forum":["A crafty Roman slave named Pseudolus schemes to win his freedom by getting his young master the girl next door, who unfortunately has been sold to a vain soldier. The farce runs on disguises, doors, mistaken identity and a fake corpse. Sondheim's songs are deliberately light and witty, giving the actors breathing space between the jokes: the opening \"Comedy Tonight\", the leering \"Everybody Ought to Have a Maid\", the dim-witted love song \"Lovely\" and the pleading \"Free\".","This was Sondheim's first Broadway show as both composer and lyricist, after writing only the lyrics for West Side Story and Gypsy. Burt Shevelove and Larry Gelbart built the book from several comedies by the Roman playwright Plautus. It opened in May 1962 with Zero Mostel as Pseudolus, after a disastrous out-of-town tryout, and became a long-running hit and Tony winner for Best Musical, giving Sondheim his first big commercial success under his own name.","The famous opener \"Comedy Tonight\" was written late in the tryout, replacing a gentler number, after the director Jerome Robbins, called in to fix the show, said the audience needed to be told from the start that this was a farce. Once the opening promised slapstick, the same jokes began to land. Notice how many of the songs stop the plot rather than drive it: a deliberate choice, in the spirit of Roman comedy.","Audiences loved it, and it won six Tony Awards, though Sondheim's score was little praised at the time and was often dismissed as the weakest element of a great farce. It has since been revived successfully with Phil Silvers, Nathan Lane and Whoopi Goldberg in the lead. Critics now hear in it Sondheim's craftsmanship in miniature: tight, funny lyrics and tunes built to serve the actor.","Begin with \"Comedy Tonight\" to hear how a single song can set up an entire evening's tone, then the trio \"Everybody Ought to Have a Maid\", which keeps adding voices. The original 1962 cast recording with Zero Mostel is the classic; the 1996 revival with Nathan Lane is lively. Afterwards compare it with Sondheim's Company to hear how far he travelled in eight years."],"sondheim-pacific-overtures":["The story of Commodore Perry's arrival in Japan in 1853, and the forced opening of the country to the West, told from the Japanese side. A Reciter guides us through a minor samurai and a fisherman whose friendship is undone as Japan Westernises. Sondheim writes in spare, pentatonic lines over plucked, percussive textures, then lets Western styles creep in: a Gilbert and Sullivan-style patter song for a British admiral, a French march, and a final number in which modern Japan explodes into the present.","Written with the book writer John Weidman and directed by Hal Prince, it opened in January 1976, during America's bicentennial year, as a pointed counter-history of American power. Prince staged it in a style borrowed from Kabuki, with an all-Asian male cast playing women's roles, stage-hands in black and painted sets. Jonathan Tunick's orchestrations blend Western instruments with Japanese ones. It was the most experimental of the Sondheim and Prince collaborations and lost money.","Sondheim considered \"Someone in a Tree\" one of his best songs: the treaty negotiations are recalled by an old man, his younger self hidden in a tree, and a warrior under the floor, each a fragment of a witness, building a meditation on how history is made from partial views. Notice also \"A Bowler Hat\", where one character's Westernisation is told through small changes of habit, verse by verse, over the same patiently ticking accompaniment.","Critics were respectful but puzzled, and it closed after under two hundred performances, though it won Tonys for design. It became a connoisseur's favourite, revived Off-Broadway in the 1980s and on Broadway in 2004 in Amon Miyamoto's staging, which grew from his production at Tokyo's New National Theatre. Debate continues over whether a show by white Americans about Japan escapes the orientalism it criticises, but many musicians rank the score among Sondheim's most original.","Start with \"Someone in a Tree\", then \"Please Hello\" for the comic parade of Western admirals in their national styles. The original 1976 cast recording captures Tunick's mixture of shamisen colour and pit orchestra. Compare it with the Japanese court music of gagaku, which it echoes only loosely, and with Rodgers's The King and I, which tells a similar encounter from the opposite side."],"sondheim-merrily-we-roll-along":["Three friends, a composer, a lyricist and a writer, are traced backwards over twenty years, from a bitter Hollywood party in middle age to the rooftop night when they were young and idealistic. Each scene steps further back in time. The score is Sondheim at his most tuneful and openly theatrical, built from bright, driving songs such as \"Old Friends\", \"Good Thing Going\", \"Not a Day Goes By\" and the patter number \"Franklin Shepard, Inc.\", where a friendship collapses on live television.","Adapted by George Furth from a 1934 play by George S. Kaufman and Moss Hart, it was directed by Hal Prince with a deliberately young cast. It opened in November 1981 after troubled previews and closed after sixteen performances. The failure ended the long partnership of Sondheim and Prince and left Sondheim briefly considering leaving the theatre. A documentary by the original cast member Lonny Price later told the story of that production.","Because the story runs backwards, we hear the reprises of songs before we hear them in their original, hopeful form: a melody first appears bruised and ironic, and only in later scenes do we hear the innocent version that was its source. \"Good Thing Going\", the hit song the characters write together, is heard first as a jaded cabaret turn and then in its first, tender performance. The overture already contains every tune.","The original production was savaged by critics, who found it confusing and cold. Revisions over the following decades, notably in La Jolla, London and at the Menier Chocolate Factory, made the book work, and the 2023 Broadway revival with Jonathan Groff, Daniel Radcliffe and Lindsay Mendez became a sell-out hit and won the Tony for Best Revival. It is now often called Sondheim's most beloved score.","Listen to \"Old Friends\", then \"Franklin Shepard, Inc.\" for a breakdown delivered as rapid patter. Then play \"Good Thing Going\" knowing you will hear it twice. The original 1981 cast album is youthful and bright; the 2023 Broadway revival recording is the fullest. Compare its backward structure with Jason Robert Brown's The Last Five Years, which owes it a clear debt."],"sondheim-assassins":["In a fairground shooting gallery outside time, the men and women who killed or tried to kill American presidents, from John Wilkes Booth to John Hinckley, meet, argue and egg each other on. A folk-singing Balladeer narrates. Each assassin sings in a period style: a Stephen Foster-style ballad for Booth, a cakewalk for Charles Guiteau, a soft-rock love duet for two would-be killers. The one-act show ends with a dark, chilling anthem in which the assassins demand to be heard.","Written with John Weidman, it opened Off-Broadway at Playwrights Horizons in December 1990, just as the first Gulf War was beginning, a moment when its bleak view of American patriotism felt out of step. It is Sondheim's most deliberately unpleasant subject: the violent underside of the American dream of fame and success. The planned Broadway transfer in 2001 was postponed after the September 11 attacks; it finally opened in 2004.","The score is a gallery of American popular music, so the assassins speak in the nation's own voice: Sousa marches, hymns, barbershop, folk ballad and Carpenters-era pop. \"Hail to the Chief\" turns up in distorted form. For Guiteau's scene, Sondheim drew on the poem the real Guiteau wrote on the morning of his execution, setting it as a jaunty cakewalk he dances up the gallows steps.","Initial reviews were cool, many critics feeling the show sympathised with murderers. Its reputation rose quickly, especially after Sam Mendes's 1992 production at the Donmar Warehouse in London. The 2004 Roundabout revival on Broadway won the Tony for Best Revival, and productions since have found new urgency in an era of mass shootings. It is now regarded as one of Sondheim's most daring and relevant works.","Start with \"The Ballad of Booth\" for the Balladeer's folk guitar, then \"The Ballad of Guiteau\" for its unsettling cheerfulness, then \"Unworthy of Your Love\", which sounds like a 1970s radio ballad. The 2004 Broadway recording with Neil Patrick Harris is the most complete. Compare it with John Adams's Nixon in China, another stage work about American politics."],"sondheim-passion":["In 1863 Italy, a handsome army captain is posted to a remote garrison, away from his beautiful married lover, and becomes the object of an obsessive love from his colonel's cousin Fosca, a sickly and plain woman. Played in one long act without an interval, the score is almost continuous, a flow of letters, arguments and confessions sung over restless, rippling accompaniment. The music grows darker and more intense until the captain finally recognises a love he had first found repulsive.","Sondheim and the writer-director James Lapine adapted Ettore Scola's 1981 film Passione d'amore, itself based on a nineteenth-century Italian novel, Fosca, by Iginio Ugo Tarchetti. It opened on Broadway in May 1994 with Donna Murphy as Fosca. Sondheim was in his sixties, and this was his most intimate and operatic score, closer to a chamber opera than a Broadway musical. It won the Tony for Best Musical despite a short run.","Much of the score is sung letters: the characters read each other's words aloud, and lines from one letter migrate into another's mouth, so the music itself mimics how love seeps from one person into another. Listen for small, recurring phrases tied to Fosca, which return transformed when the captain finally sings them himself. Jonathan Tunick's orchestration keeps the strings tense and the textures thin, almost like chamber music.","Many in the original audience laughed nervously at Fosca, and the production closed after 280 performances, one of the shortest runs for a Best Musical winner. Critics were divided between those who found it cold and those who called it Sondheim's most profound score. A 1996 television broadcast of the Broadway production, and later revivals in London and New York, have steadily raised its stature, though it remains an acquired taste.","Hear it in one sitting if you can, as a continuous ninety-minute chamber opera. Notice how Fosca's music sits low and dark against the brighter tone of the lover, Clara. The original Broadway cast recording with Donna Murphy is essential. Afterwards try Tchaikovsky's Eugene Onegin or Janáček's Jenůfa, other operas about love that arrives at the wrong time and in the wrong form."],"bock-and-harnick-fiddler-on-the-roof":["Tevye, a poor Jewish milkman in the village of Anatevka in Tsarist Russia around 1905, tries to hold on to tradition as his daughters choose their own husbands and the authorities drive the Jews from their homes. The score is soaked in the minor modes and dance rhythms of klezmer and synagogue music: the swirling opening \"Tradition\", Tevye's daydream \"If I Were a Rich Man\", the girls' \"Matchmaker\", the wedding song \"Sunrise, Sunset\" and the quiet, displaced farewell \"Anatevka\".","Jerry Bock wrote the music, Sheldon Harnick the lyrics and Joseph Stein the book, based on Sholem Aleichem's Yiddish stories of Tevye. Jerome Robbins directed and choreographed, drawing on his own family memories and visits to Hasidic weddings. It opened in September 1964 starring Zero Mostel and ran for 3,242 performances, then the longest Broadway run ever. Its image of the rooftop fiddler comes from the paintings of Marc Chagall.","Bock uses a scale with a wide, wailing gap between its second and third notes, the sound of a mode used in Jewish prayer and klezmer, so that even the comic songs carry an ache of loss. The solo violin of the title opens and closes the show: in the final moments the fiddler follows Tevye out of the village, carrying the tune into exile. \"If I Were a Rich Man\" is built on wordless chanted syllables, a nod to cantorial singing.","Some critics at the premiere thought it too sentimental, and a few Jewish writers called it a sanitised picture of the shtetl. Audiences adored it everywhere, from Tokyo to Buenos Aires, as a story about tradition and change in any culture. Norman Jewison's 1971 film with Topol made it global. A 2018 Yiddish-language production directed by Joel Grey was praised for bringing the show back to its source.","Start with \"Tradition\" to hear how the whole village is introduced in rhythm, then the bottle dance at the wedding, then the quiet ending of \"Anatevka\". The 1964 original cast album with Zero Mostel is the classic, the 1971 film soundtrack has the violinist Isaac Stern as the fiddler. Compare its folk modes with Bernstein's West Side Story, also shaped by Robbins."],"bock-and-harnick-she-loves-me":["In a perfume shop in 1930s Budapest, two clerks who bicker constantly are, without knowing it, falling in love through anonymous letters as lonely-hearts correspondents. The score is intimate and continuous, with songs flowing in and out of dialogue in the manner of operetta, and full of Central European waltz and café colour. Its showpiece is \"Ice Cream\", in which the heroine, Amalia, struggles to write a letter and realises her feelings mid-song; other gems include \"Dear Friend\" and \"Tonight at Eight\".","Bock and Harnick wrote it with the book writer Joe Masteroff, adapting Miklós László's play Parfumerie, the same source as the Ernst Lubitsch film The Shop Around the Corner and later You've Got Mail. Hal Prince directed it in 1963, with Barbara Cook as Amalia. It opened in a season of brassy hits, and its gentle charm was rewarded more by critics than by the box office. It ran for about three hundred performances.","\"Ice Cream\" is a little operatic scene: Amalia sings, stops, starts again, and a melody that begins as polite letter-writing breaks open into a soaring high note as she realises she likes the man she has despised. The orchestra keeps the shop's bustle alive in the background throughout, and there are more than twenty numbers, short and dialogue-like, giving it the feel of a chamber operetta rather than a musical comedy.","Critics loved it from the start; audiences took longer to find it. Its cast album became a cult favourite, and revivals in London and on Broadway in 1993 and 2016 confirmed its reputation as perhaps the most perfectly made of all musical comedies. The 2016 Roundabout revival was the first Broadway production to be live-streamed over the internet, introducing it to a new audience.","Start with Barbara Cook's \"Ice Cream\" on the 1963 cast album, one of the great recorded musical theatre performances, then \"Tonight at Eight\" for the nervous hero waiting for his date. The 2016 revival recording with Laura Benanti is excellent. Compare it with Sondheim's A Little Night Music, another waltz-shadowed romantic comedy in Central European dress."],"styne-gypsy":["Rose, a ferociously ambitious stage mother, drives her two daughters across America's dying vaudeville circuit in the 1920s, until the shy one, Louise, becomes the burlesque star Gypsy Rose Lee and Rose is left with nothing. Styne's score is brassy, big-hearted show business: the overture, the anthem \"Everything's Coming Up Roses\", the yearning \"Some People\", the vaudeville routine \"Let Me Entertain You\" that grows up with the girls, and the shattering final breakdown \"Rose's Turn\".","Jule Styne wrote the music, the twenty-nine-year-old Stephen Sondheim the lyrics and Arthur Laurents the book, based on the memoirs of Gypsy Rose Lee. Jerome Robbins directed. Sondheim wanted to write the music too, but the star, Ethel Merman, insisted on an established composer. It opened in May 1959, a showcase for Merman's huge voice, and has since been the role every great musical theatre actress wants to play.","\"Rose's Turn\" is a nervous breakdown assembled from the show's earlier songs: fragments of \"Everything's Coming Up Roses\", \"Some People\" and the children's vaudeville number tumble out in pieces, as if her mind were shuffling through her life. Listen to how the orchestra stutters, stops and restarts behind her. The vaudeville song \"Let Me Entertain You\" returns as a striptease, the same tune transformed by tempo and orchestration as Louise grows up.","Reviews were excellent, but it lost the Tony for Best Musical to The Sound of Music and Fiorello! Its reputation has grown with every revival, from Angela Lansbury in 1974 to Tyne Daly, Bernadette Peters, Patti LuPone, Imelda Staunton in London and Audra McDonald in 2024. Many critics now call it the greatest of all American musicals, the perfect fit of book, music and lyrics.","Start with the overture, often called the best in Broadway history, with its stripper's trumpet blaring the tune of \"Let Me Entertain You\". Then hear \"Rose's Turn\" as a dramatic aria. The 1959 cast album with Merman is essential; compare Patti LuPone's 2008 version or Imelda Staunton's for very different Roses. Then hear Sondheim's Follies for another view of old show business."],"styne-funny-girl":["The rise of the comedian Fanny Brice from Lower East Side kid to Ziegfeld Follies star, and her doomed marriage to the charming gambler Nick Arnstein. Styne's score is built around one voice, big and belting, full of brass and swagger: \"I'm the Greatest Star\" for her ambition, the ballad \"People\" for her loneliness, the Act One closer \"Don't Rain on My Parade\" for her refusal to be stopped, and the poignant \"The Music That Makes Me Dance\" near the end.","The producer Ray Stark, Brice's son-in-law, developed the show; Styne wrote the music, Bob Merrill the lyrics and Isobel Lennart the book. After a long search the role went to a twenty-one-year-old Barbra Streisand, who had made her name in nightclubs. It opened in March 1964 after a famously troubled preview period with many rewrites, and made her a star; she repeated the role in the 1968 film and won an Academy Award.","\"People\" was nearly cut in tryouts because the creators thought it did not fit the show, but Streisand's recording became a hit before the opening and it stayed. Listen to how \"Don't Rain on My Parade\" is built: short, punching phrases over rushing brass, then a final note held over a modulation, written to give a single voice maximum stamina.","The show was a hit but has been hard to revive, because it was written around one performer. The 2022 Broadway revival with Beanie Feldstein was poorly reviewed, but Lea Michele's replacement run became a sell-out. Critics still debate whether Funny Girl is a great musical or a great star vehicle; its songs are unquestionably among the most famous ever written for Broadway.","Hear Streisand in \"Don't Rain on My Parade\" and \"People\" on the 1964 cast album, then the 1968 film soundtrack for a more polished version. Notice how much of the drama is carried by a single voice. Compare with Styne's Gypsy, which takes a far darker view of show business, and with Ella Fitzgerald's songbook records for a cooler kind of popular singing."],"cy-coleman-sweet-charity":["Charity Hope Valentine, a dance-hall hostess in 1960s New York, keeps giving her heart to the wrong men and keeps getting up again. The music is brash, jazzy and urban: the slow, sleazy vamp of \"Big Spender\", the strutting \"If My Friends Could See Me Now\", the hippie revival meeting \"The Rhythm of Life\", the girls' furious \"There's Gotta Be Something Better Than This\" and the joyous march \"I'm a Brass Band\". The score lives in big brass and dance rhythms.","Bob Fosse conceived, directed and choreographed the show for his wife Gwen Verdon, adapting Federico Fellini's film Nights of Cabiria and moving it from Rome to Times Square. Neil Simon wrote the book, Cy Coleman the music, and the veteran Dorothy Fields, then in her sixties, the lyrics. It opened in January 1966. Fosse made it into a film in 1969 with Shirley MacLaine, his debut as a film director.","\"Big Spender\" is built on a dragging, almost stationary vamp with a blaring brass hook, while the hostesses lean over a barre, motionless: the music and the choreography both deliberately refuse to move, showing the boredom behind the come-on. Dorothy Fields, who had written lyrics in the 1930s for Jerome Kern, wrote in the street slang of the 1960s, a remarkable act of reinvention. Coleman's jazz-pianist background shows in the chord colours.","It was a hit, though overshadowed at the Tony Awards by Man of La Mancha, and Fosse's film was a box-office failure that later became a cult classic. Revivals in 1986 with Debbie Allen and 2005 with Christina Applegate kept it in view. Critics usually praise the songs and the dances above Simon's book, and its portrait of a woman exploited by men is now read with more sympathy.","Start with \"Big Spender\", then hear \"The Rhythm of Life\", sung by Sammy Davis Jr. in the film, then the girls' \"There's Gotta Be Something Better Than This\". The original Broadway album with Gwen Verdon and the 1969 film soundtrack are both essential. Watch the film's dance numbers to see how Fosse's style grows from the music. Compare with Kander and Ebb's Chicago."],"cy-coleman-city-of-angels":["A novelist in 1940s Hollywood struggles to turn his detective book into a screenplay for a bullying producer, while his fictional private eye acts out the film in front of us. The real world is staged in colour, the film world in black and white. Coleman's score is a jazz-noir tribute, full of big-band brass, saxophone and a scat-singing vocal quartet; the novelist and his detective eventually confront each other in the comic duet \"You're Nothing Without Me\".","Larry Gelbart wrote the book, David Zippel the lyrics and Coleman the music, in a deliberate homage to the novels of Raymond Chandler and the films of the 1940s. It opened on Broadway in December 1989 and won the Tony for Best Musical. Coleman, who had started out as a jazz pianist, wrote for big band rather than a conventional pit orchestra, returning to the music of his youth.","Many characters are played by two actors, one in each world, with the film version a cleaner, more glamorous version of the real person, and the music often moves between the two styles: harder big-band jazz for the film world, more conventional Broadway for the writer's real life. The vocal quartet, singing like a 1940s radio group, works as a narrator, setting scenes in wordless harmony. Billy Byers's orchestrations are prized by musicians.","Critics were delighted by Gelbart's book and its wit, and the show won six Tonys, beating Grand Hotel. It is revived less often than its reputation suggests, being expensive to stage. The 2014 Donmar Warehouse revival in London was well received and returned in a new West End staging in 2020. Musicians often hold it up as the best jazz score written for Broadway in its era.","Begin with the opening number for the vocal quartet and the brass, then the duet \"You're Nothing Without Me\". Notice how the music shifts between the two worlds. The original Broadway cast album is the reference recording. Compare it with Bernstein's On the Town and Coleman's Sweet Charity, then with Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours for the noir atmosphere in song."],"jerry-herman-hello-dolly":["Dolly Levi, a widowed matchmaker in 1890s New York, sets out to marry the grumpy half-millionaire Horace Vandergelder herself, while engineering romances for everyone around her. Herman's score is pure joyous old-fashioned Broadway: the parading \"Put On Your Sunday Clothes\", the ballad \"It Only Takes a Moment\", Dolly's resolve to rejoin life in \"Before the Parade Passes By\", and the title number, in which Dolly descends a staircase at the Harmonia Gardens restaurant to a waiters' galop.","Adapted by Michael Stewart from Thornton Wilder's play The Matchmaker, with music and lyrics by Herman and direction by Gower Champion, it opened in January 1964 starring Carol Channing. Louis Armstrong's recording of the title song, made before the opening, became a number-one hit in May 1964, briefly displacing the Beatles. The show ran for nearly seven years, with stars including Ginger Rogers, Ethel Merman and, in an all-Black cast, Pearl Bailey.","The title number is a stage event built in layers: it begins with Dolly's simple greeting, adds the waiters marching in to a quick galop, then a cakewalk, then a full chorus, building a ten-minute celebration from one catchy phrase. The songwriter Mack David sued, claiming the tune resembled his earlier song \"Sunflower\", and Herman settled out of court. \"Before the Parade Passes By\" was a late addition written during the tryout.","It won ten Tonys, a record that stood for decades, and was for a while the longest-running musical on Broadway. Critics sometimes dismiss it as lightweight, but its craft and good humour are generally admired. The 1969 film with Barbra Streisand was a costly disappointment. The 2017 Broadway revival with Bette Midler was a huge success, reminding audiences how expertly the score builds.","Start with Louis Armstrong's recording of the title song, then Carol Channing's on the original album, to hear how differently one tune can be sung. Then \"Put On Your Sunday Clothes\" for its train rhythm. The 2017 revival recording with Bette Midler is excellent. Compare with Herman's La Cage aux Folles and with Funny Girl, which opened the same spring."],"jerry-herman-la-cage-aux-folles":["In Saint-Tropez, Georges runs a drag nightclub where his partner of twenty years, Albin, is the star attraction. When Georges's son announces his engagement to the daughter of a conservative politician, the family is asked to hide who they are. Herman's score is warm, tuneful and old-fashioned: the romantic \"Song on the Sand\", the tender \"Look Over There\", the nightclub's glittering production numbers, and the Act One finale \"I Am What I Am\", Albin's declaration of self.","Harvey Fierstein wrote the book, based on Jean Poiret's 1973 French farce, with direction by Arthur Laurents. It opened on Broadway in August 1983 with George Hearn and Gene Barry, at the moment when the AIDS crisis was beginning to devastate the gay community. It was the first hit Broadway musical about a gay couple. It won the Tony for Best Musical over Sondheim's Sunday in the Park with George.","\"I Am What I Am\" grows out of the nightclub chorus's earlier number, a showy production song; Albin takes that same material, alone, and turns it from entertainment into defiance, finishing by ripping off his wig. The transformation of a glittering showtune into a protest song is the show's whole argument. The song was soon recorded by Gloria Gaynor and became an anthem for the gay liberation movement worldwide.","The original production was a hit, but some critics found it too cautious, keeping the couple's love offstage. Herman's Tony acceptance speech, defending simple hummable melody, was widely read as a jab at Sondheim. Later revivals in 2004 and 2010, the latter with Douglas Hodge from London's Menier Chocolate Factory, won Tonys for Best Revival. Its tenderness is now seen as quietly radical for its time.","Begin with \"I Am What I Am\" in George Hearn's original version, then Gloria Gaynor's disco recording to hear the song cross over. Then hear \"Song on the Sand\" for Herman's lyrical side. The 1983 original cast album and the 2010 revival recording are both good. Compare with Kander and Ebb's Cabaret for another nightclub as world."],"boublil-and-schonberg-les-miserables":["Victor Hugo's novel squeezed into three hours of nearly continuous song: the ex-convict Jean Valjean, hunted for decades by the policeman Javert, raises the orphan Cosette amid poverty and the Paris student uprising of 1832. The score is through-sung, with recitative between big set-piece ballads: \"I Dreamed a Dream\", \"Bring Him Home\", \"On My Own\", \"Stars\", the rallying \"Do You Hear the People Sing?\" and the Act One finale \"One Day More\", in which every storyline sings at once.","Boublil and Schönberg first wrote it as a French concept album in 1980, staged at the Palais des Sports in Paris. Cameron Mackintosh commissioned an English version with lyrics by Herbert Kretzmer, staged by Trevor Nunn and John Caird for the Royal Shakespeare Company at the Barbican in October 1985. It transferred to the West End, where it has run ever since, and to Broadway in 1987, becoming one of the most performed musicals in history.","Musical themes are reused for dramatic irony. Javert's suicide soliloquy is sung to exactly the music of Valjean's earlier soliloquy of conversion, showing that the two men face the same crisis and choose differently. \"One Day More\" is a quodlibet: five or six melodies from earlier in the show are heard simultaneously, a technique borrowed from opera ensemble. Fantine's melody returns at Valjean's death.","London critics largely panned the RSC opening as bombastic and simplistic, but audiences flocked and the reviews quickly stopped mattering. It became the archetype of the British megamusical. Critics still argue over its sentimentality, but the score's melodic force is undeniable. Tom Hooper's 2012 film, with the actors singing live, was a box-office hit and divided critics over its close-up singing.","Start with \"One Day More\" to hear the layering, then \"Bring Him Home\" for the high, prayer-like tenor line. The 1985 original London cast recording and the 10th anniversary concert at the Royal Albert Hall are the standard recordings. Notice the synthesisers and drum machines of the original orchestration, very much of the 1980s. Compare with Lloyd Webber's Phantom of the Opera."],"boublil-and-schonberg-miss-saigon":["Puccini's Madama Butterfly moved to Saigon in 1975: Kim, a seventeen-year-old Vietnamese girl, falls in love with Chris, an American Marine, in the last days before the city falls, and is left behind with his child. Three years later he returns with an American wife. The through-sung score mixes sweeping ballads, such as \"Sun and Moon\" and \"I'd Give My Life for You\", with a pounding, sleazy club scene and the cynical Engineer's showpiece \"The American Dream\".","Boublil and Schönberg wrote it with the American lyricist Richard Maltby Jr. after Schönberg saw a photograph of a Vietnamese mother handing her child to a soldier. Produced by Cameron Mackintosh, it opened at Drury Lane in London in September 1989 with Lea Salonga as Kim and Jonathan Pryce as the Engineer. Its Broadway transfer in 1991 became mired in controversy over Pryce, a white actor, playing a Eurasian character.","The fall of Saigon is staged as a flashback in Act Two, with the famous helicopter descending in a wash of rotor noise and orchestra, which first-time listeners often do not realise belongs to the past. Schönberg uses Asian percussion and pentatonic scales for Vietnam, and an American rock-ballad idiom for the soldiers, so the musical languages themselves embody the gulf between the lovers.","It was a commercial hit in London and New York, and Salonga's performance was widely praised. Critics have argued for decades over its depiction of Asian women and its yellowface casting; Asian-American activists protested the Broadway production and later revivals. Many now see it as a powerful but problematic show. The 2014 London revival was well reviewed for its sharper, grimmer staging.","Start with \"The Last Night of the World\", a duet against distant saxophone, then \"The American Dream\" for the Engineer's satirical showstopper. The original London cast recording with Salonga is essential. Listen alongside Puccini's Madama Butterfly, especially the duet that ends Act One, and the Humming Chorus, to hear how closely the story follows its model."],"stephen-schwartz-godspell":["A small band of young people, dressed like clowns, act out parables from the Gospel of Matthew under the leadership of Jesus, in a playful mix of improvisation, vaudeville and song, until the mood darkens toward the Last Supper and crucifixion. Schwartz's songs are folk-rock and pop, with gospel and soft rock: the opening \"Prepare Ye the Way of the Lord\", the hit \"Day by Day\", the gentle \"All Good Gifts\" and \"By My Side\", the vaudeville \"All for the Best\".","John-Michael Tebelak created the show as a student project at Carnegie Mellon University. The producers brought in Schwartz, then twenty-three, who wrote a new score in a few weeks for the Off-Broadway opening at the Cherry Lane Theatre in May 1971. It arrived in the same season as Jesus Christ Superstar, during the so-called Jesus movement among young Americans. Its Toronto production launched the careers of Victor Garber, Martin Short, Gilda Radner and Eugene Levy.","Many of the lyrics are not Schwartz's but come from the Episcopal hymnal: \"Day by Day\" sets a prayer attributed to the medieval bishop Richard of Chichester, and \"All Good Gifts\" an old harvest hymn text. Schwartz reset these familiar words in soft-rock and folk styles, which is why parts of the show sound like a Sunday service performed by a coffee-house band. \"By My Side\" was written by Peggy Gordon, a member of the original company, with Jay Hamburger.","It was a huge success Off-Broadway and around the world, and \"Day by Day\" reached the American top twenty in 1972. Some critics found its childlike approach to faith cloying, and some religious groups disliked seeing Jesus portrayed as a clown. It remains one of the most performed musicals by schools and churches. A 1973 film was shot on the streets of New York, and a 2011 Broadway revival was staged in the round.","Start with \"Prepare Ye the Way of the Lord\", which builds from a single voice into a full gospel chorus, then \"Day by Day\". The 1971 original Off-Broadway album is simple and fresh; the 2011 revival is slicker. Compare with Lloyd Webber's Jesus Christ Superstar, a much harder rock telling of the same events, and with Handel's Messiah, an earlier popular setting of the same scriptures."],"stephen-schwartz-pippin":["A troupe of players led by a sinister, smiling Leading Player tell the story of Pippin, the son of Charlemagne, who searches for an extraordinary life through war, sex, politics and revolution, only to find the troupe has a darker climax in mind. Schwartz's score blends pop-rock with vaudeville and folk: the seductive opener \"Magic to Do\", Pippin's yearning \"Corner of the Sky\", the bouncy singalong \"No Time at All\" and the quiet \"Love Song\".","Schwartz began it as a college show at Carnegie Mellon; Roger O. Hirson wrote the book and Bob Fosse directed and choreographed. Fosse reshaped it into a dark, sexy fable, much to Schwartz's distress, and the two fought throughout rehearsals. It opened in October 1972 with Ben Vereen as the Leading Player, and became the first Broadway show to be advertised with a television commercial, featuring a Fosse dance routine. It ran for nearly five years.","Fosse's staging and Schwartz's songs pull in opposite directions, and you can hear the tension: songs that are sweetly sincere in the soft-rock manner sit inside numbers that Fosse turned into ironic, menacing vaudeville. \"Glory\", the war song, is a jaunty soft-shoe that accompanies a bloody battle. The ending, which Fosse rewrote, asks the audience whether ordinary life is enough.","Critics praised Fosse and Vereen more than the score, which some called bland soft rock, but it was a big commercial hit. Diane Paulus's 2013 Broadway revival, which restaged it as a circus with acrobats, was a critical triumph and won the Tony for Best Revival. Debate continues about whether the show is a sincere coming-of-age story or Fosse's cynical satire of one.","Start with \"Magic to Do\" for the ensemble's whispered invitation, then \"Corner of the Sky\", a classic \"I want\" song. The 1972 original cast album with Ben Vereen and the 2013 revival recording are both good. Compare with Schwartz's Godspell, which shares the theatre-troupe framing, and with Kander and Ebb's Cabaret, where a master of ceremonies also manipulates the audience."],"stephen-schwartz-wicked":["The untold story of the witches of Oz: Elphaba, green-skinned and outcast, and the popular, privileged Glinda meet as unlikely roommates at school and form a complex friendship before Elphaba is branded the Wicked Witch of the West. Schwartz's score is pop-driven and anthemic: the comic \"Popular\", the yearning \"The Wizard and I\", the Act One finale \"Defying Gravity\", in which Elphaba rises into the air, and the farewell duet \"For Good\".","Based on Gregory Maguire's 1995 novel, which retold The Wizard of Oz from the witch's view, with a book by Winnie Holzman and direction by Joe Mantello. It opened at the Gershwin Theatre in October 2003 with Idina Menzel and Kristin Chenoweth. It became one of the highest-grossing shows in Broadway history, especially popular with teenage girls. A two-part film directed by Jon M. Chu, with Cynthia Erivo and Ariana Grande, followed in 2024 and 2025.","Elphaba's recurring motif, heard first in \"The Wizard and I\" and again in \"Defying Gravity\", is built from the opening notes of Harold Arlen's \"Over the Rainbow\" from the 1939 film, rearranged; Schwartz has said it was a deliberate in-joke, hiding Dorothy's song inside the witch's. The show's opening chord blast returns at the very end, closing the loop on a story that begins with the witch's death.","Initial reviews were mixed, with many critics dismissing it as overblown, and it lost the Tony for Best Musical to the tiny puppet show Avenue Q. Audiences ignored the critics, and it has played for more than twenty years. Its power ballads became staples of auditions and talent shows. Critics now acknowledge its craft and its unusual focus on a friendship between two women.","Start with \"Defying Gravity\" in Idina Menzel's original performance, noticing how the music lifts as she rises. Then hear \"Popular\" for comic timing, then \"For Good\" for the duet. The 2003 original Broadway cast album is the reference; the 2024 film soundtrack has larger orchestration. Compare with Lloyd Webber's Phantom of the Opera for the megamusical tradition it continues."],"jason-robert-brown-the-last-five-years":["A five-year marriage between Jamie, a rising novelist, and Cathy, a struggling actress, told by two people who are almost never onstage together. Cathy tells the story backwards, from the end of the marriage, Jamie forwards from the first date; they meet only once, in the middle, at their wedding. The intimate score, driven by piano and strings, ranges from Cathy's bruised opening \"Still Hurting\" and Jamie's giddy \"Shiksa Goddess\" to the wedding duet \"The Next Ten Minutes\".","Brown based the show on the breakdown of his own first marriage, which led to legal threats from his ex-wife and some revisions. It premiered at Northlight Theatre near Chicago in 2001 and Off-Broadway in 2002. Though not a commercial hit at first, it became one of the most performed small musicals in the world, beloved of drama students. A 2015 film starred Anna Kendrick, and a 2025 Broadway production starred Nick Jonas.","The final song braids two farewells: Jamie leaving the marriage at its end, Cathy saying goodbye after their first date in its beginning, sung at once so that the most hopeful and the most hopeless moments of the story share a single musical space. Brown's six-piece band, with two cellos, gives the show its warm, chamber-music colour. The two characters' styles differ: Jamie is more pop, Cathy more theatrical.","Critics were respectful of the Off-Broadway production but it ran only a few months; the cast album built its following. Later productions in London and New York were much better received. Some argue that the story favours Jamie, the author's stand-in. Its structure, borrowed from Sondheim's Merrily We Roll Along, has been widely admired and imitated. Brown's piano writing is regarded as among the most demanding in modern musicals.","Listen to the songs in order: watch how Cathy's songs grow happier as Jamie's grow darker. Start with \"Still Hurting\" and \"Shiksa Goddess\", then jump to \"The Next Ten Minutes\". The 2002 original cast recording with Sherie Rene Scott and Norbert Leo Butz is definitive. Compare with Sondheim's Merrily We Roll Along, which also tells a story backwards."],"jason-robert-brown-parade":["The true story of Leo Frank, a Jewish factory manager from Brooklyn living in Atlanta, wrongly convicted in 1913 of the murder of a thirteen-year-old girl who worked for him, and lynched by a mob two years later. As public hysteria and antisemitism mount, his wife Lucille fights for him and their marriage deepens. Brown's score draws on Southern music, marches, gospel, ragtime and blues, from the opening Confederate anthem \"The Old Red Hills of Home\" to the tender duet \"All the Wasted Time\".","Alfred Uhry, author of Driving Miss Daisy, wrote the book, and Hal Prince directed the premiere at Lincoln Center Theater in December 1998. Brown was twenty-eight; it was his Broadway debut. The show was commercially unsuccessful, closing after about three months, but won the Tony Awards for Best Score and Best Book. The Frank case had led both to the founding of the Anti-Defamation League and to the revival of the Ku Klux Klan.","The title is literal: Atlanta's Confederate Memorial Day parade frames the story, appearing at the start, in the middle and at the end, so the cheerful marching band becomes more menacing each time we hear it. Brown gives different communities different music: Southern gospel for the Black characters, march and hymn for the white townspeople, and something more urban and anxious for Leo, the outsider.","Critics praised the score but found the show dour, and it closed quickly. Its reputation grew through regional productions and a Donmar Warehouse revival in London in 2007. The 2023 Broadway revival with Ben Platt and Micaela Diamond won the Tony for Best Revival; neo-Nazi protesters outside a preview showed how close its subject still was. It is now regarded as one of the finest scores of its decade.","Start with \"The Old Red Hills of Home\" to hear a song both beautiful and troubling, then \"This Is Not Over Yet\" for Leo and Lucille's hope, then \"All the Wasted Time\". The 1999 original cast recording and the 2023 revival recording are both good. Compare it with Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, another Broadway score set in the American South."],"pasek-and-paul-dear-evan-hansen":["Evan Hansen, a teenager with crippling social anxiety, is mistaken for the best friend of a classmate who has taken his own life, and lets the lie grow until it brings him the family and online fame he craves. The score is contemporary pop-rock, piano-driven and close to the singer-songwriter sound of radio: the confessional \"Waving Through a Window\", the deceptive comic duet \"Sincerely, Me\", the grieving trio \"Requiem\" and the viral anthem \"You Will Be Found\".","Benj Pasek and Justin Paul wrote the songs, with a book by Steven Levenson; Michael Greif directed. It premiered at Arena Stage in Washington in 2015 and opened on Broadway in December 2016 with Ben Platt. It won six Tonys including Best Musical. Its themes of teenage isolation in the age of social media made it hugely popular with young audiences. A 2021 film, with Platt repeating the role, was poorly received.","The band, arranged by Alex Lacamoire, includes strings and guitars but sounds like a pop record rather than a pit orchestra, and many songs build from a single quiet guitar or piano figure to a full-band climax, mirroring how Evan's lie grows from a private moment to a public phenomenon. Projections of social media feeds on the set are part of the story, as the music and the internet amplify each other.","Critics initially praised it highly, especially Platt's performance. Later commentators questioned its sympathy for a protagonist who exploits a grieving family, and the film's failure intensified that debate. Its depiction of mental health has been both praised and criticised. Musically, it is seen as a key example of the pop singer-songwriter sound that dominated Broadway in the 2010s.","Start with \"Waving Through a Window\", notice how it builds, then \"Requiem\", where three family members grieve differently over the same music. The original Broadway cast recording with Ben Platt is the reference. Compare with Larson's Rent, an earlier musical in a pop-rock idiom, and with Taylor Swift's confessional pop, whose sound it often shares."],"tesori-fun-home":["The cartoonist Alison Bechdel looks back at her childhood in a small Pennsylvania town, her own coming out as a lesbian at college, and her closeted father, who ran the family funeral home and died, perhaps by suicide, soon afterward. Three actresses play Alison as a child, a student and an adult. Tesori's score ranges from the children's mock-Jackson 5 funeral-home commercial \"Come to the Fun Home\" to the student's giddy \"Changing My Major\" and the child's moment of recognition in \"Ring of Keys\".","Lisa Kron wrote the book and lyrics, adapting Bechdel's 2006 graphic memoir. It premiered at the Public Theater in 2013 and opened on Broadway in 2015 at Circle in the Square, staged in the round. It won five Tonys including Best Musical, and Tesori and Kron became the first all-female team to win the Tony for Best Original Score. It arrived as same-sex marriage was being legalised across the United States.","The adult Alison, a cartoonist, sings short spoken-sung \"captions\", like the text boxes in her comic, over the action, mirroring the book's format. \"Ring of Keys\" is a child's first glimpse of a butch woman, and the song stumbles as if the girl can't quite find words for what she sees. \"Telephone Wire\" stops time in a car journey, the last time Alison sees her father, built on a few repeated notes.","Critics praised it almost unanimously, and Sydney Lucas's performance of \"Ring of Keys\" at the Tony Awards became a widely shared moment. Its subject, a lesbian coming-of-age and a father's tragedy, was unprecedented for a Broadway hit. Some suggested it was too small for Broadway, but its success proved otherwise. It is now regarded as one of the most important musicals of its decade.","Start with \"Ring of Keys\", then \"Changing My Major\" for its comic exuberance, then \"Telephone Wire\". The original Broadway cast recording is the reference. Notice how Tesori writes different styles for each age, from bubblegum pop to art song. Compare with Herman's La Cage aux Folles, an earlier Broadway story about gay identity in a family."],"tesori-caroline-or-change":["In Lake Charles, Louisiana, in 1963, Caroline Thibodeaux, a Black maid, works in the hot basement of a Jewish family for thirty dollars a week, while the eight-year-old son, grieving his dead mother, clings to her. When a twenty-dollar bill left in a pocket becomes a test of loyalty, the relationship breaks. The through-sung score is a mosaic of Motown, spirituals, blues, klezmer and classical, with singing washing machine, dryer, radio and moon.","Tony Kushner, author of Angels in America, wrote the book and lyrics, drawing on his own Louisiana childhood. It premiered at the Public Theater in late 2003 and moved to Broadway in 2004, with Tonya Pinkins as Caroline; it struggled commercially. Set in the months around President Kennedy's assassination and the civil rights movement, the story explores what change means, both in coins and in society.","The radio is played by three women singing like a Motown girl group, the washing machine by a woman singing gospel, and the dryer by a man in a menacing bass; the appliances are Caroline's companions in the basement, voicing her inner life. Her eleven-o'clock number \"Lot's Wife\" is a ten-minute aria of rage and despair, closer to opera than musical theatre, in which she asks to be released from her feelings.","Reviews were deeply divided: some critics found it overlong and preachy, others called it a modern classic, and it closed on Broadway within months. A 2006 London production at the National Theatre was better received. The 2021 Broadway revival with Sharon D Clarke, from Chichester and the West End, brought it renewed acclaim, and many now see it as a modern American opera.","Start with \"Lot's Wife\", Caroline's aria, ideally in Tonya Pinkins's or Sharon D Clarke's performance, then the opening scene with the washing machine and radio. The original Broadway cast recording is the reference. Compare with Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, another American work with Black characters written by white authors, and with Bock and Harnick's Fiddler for its Jewish musical colour."],"anais-mitchell-hadestown":["The Greek myth of Orpheus and Eurydice retold in a Depression-haunted American setting: Hermes narrates as the poor young singer Orpheus woos Eurydice, who is lured underground by the industrial tycoon Hades to work in his mine. Mitchell's score is folk, blues, New Orleans jazz and gospel, played by an onstage band with trombone, violin, cello, guitar, accordion and piano: \"Wait for Me\", \"Way Down Hadestown\", the menacing \"Why We Build the Wall\" and Orpheus's song \"Epic III\".","Mitchell first staged a version with friends in Vermont in 2006, then made a concept album in 2010 with Justin Vernon of Bon Iver as Orpheus and Ani DiFranco as Persephone. The director Rachel Chavkin developed it into a full show at New York Theatre Workshop in 2016 and the National Theatre in London in 2018. It opened on Broadway in April 2019 and won eight Tonys including Best Musical.","\"Why We Build the Wall\", a call-and-response song in which Hades explains how fear and walls keep his workers in line, was written around 2006, years before border walls dominated American politics, and audiences often assume it was written later. Orpheus's great song is heard in fragments across the evening, and he only completes it, in a high, fragile falsetto, when he finally sings it to Hades in \"Epic III\".","The Broadway production was widely acclaimed, especially for its warm, hand-made sound and Chavkin's staging, and Mitchell's lyrics were praised as poetry. Some critics found the story slight for its length. It has been one of the most successful new musicals of its era, playing in London and on tour. Many see it as a bridge between the folk world and Broadway.","Start with \"Wait for Me\", which builds from a simple guitar line to a full ensemble with lighting effects, then \"Why We Build the Wall\". Compare the 2010 concept album, with Justin Vernon, against the Broadway cast recording. Then hear Monteverdi's \"Possente spirto\" from L'Orfeo, the first operatic Orpheus, and Offenbach's comic Orphée aux enfers for very different takes on the myth."],"marlow-and-moss-six":["The six wives of Henry VIII reunite as a pop girl group, competing in a concert to decide who suffered most at his hands, before realising they would rather tell their own stories. Each queen sings in the style of a modern pop diva: Catherine of Aragon as a Beyoncé-style powerhouse, Anne Boleyn as a sassy pop-punk star, Jane Seymour as a soaring balladeer, Anna of Cleves as a hip-hop queen. The show lasts about eighty minutes, without an interval.","Toby Marlow and Lucy Moss, students at Cambridge University, wrote it for the Edinburgh Fringe in 2017. It moved to London's Arts Theatre in 2018, then to the West End and Broadway, where its opening night in March 2020 was cancelled when the pandemic shut theatres; it finally opened in October 2021. It won the Tony for Best Original Score. Its cast album and social media following made it a hit with teenagers.","Each queen's song has specific pop-star models, which Marlow and Moss acknowledged: listen for the Adele and Sia in \"Heart of Stone\", the Lily Allen and Avril Lavigne in \"Don't Lose Ur Head\", the Rihanna and Nicki Minaj in \"Get Down\". The comic \"Haus of Holbein\", in which Henry's portrait painters prepare women for his choice, is a German techno parody. The show opens with a brief a cappella phrase in mock-Renaissance harmony before the beat drops.","Critics largely loved it as fresh, funny and feminist, though some thought the history thin and the pop pastiche shallow. It has been compared to Hamilton for its use of contemporary music to reclaim history, and for making a hit out of a story about forgotten voices. Its brevity and small cast made it hugely profitable, with productions around the world.","Start with \"Ex-Wives\", the opening, then each queen's solo in turn. The studio cast recording is the reference; a live Broadway recording, made on its intended opening night, exists too. Compare with Beyoncé or Adele, the styles it borrows, and then hear Dowland's lute songs or Vaughan Williams's Fantasia on Greensleeves for a more traditional picture of Tudor England."],"galt-macdermot-hair":["A loose plot follows a \"tribe\" of long-haired hippies in New York's East Village as one of them, Claude, wrestles with whether to burn his draft card or go to Vietnam. The show is a nearly continuous stream of rock, soul, folk and gospel songs: the mystical opening \"Aquarius\", the rollicking title song, the bittersweet \"Easy to Be Hard\", the sweet \"Good Morning Starshine\", the funky \"I Got Life\" and the closing chant \"Let the Sunshine In\".","The actors Gerome Ragni and James Rado wrote the book and lyrics from their experience of the counterculture; the Canadian composer Galt MacDermot, a family man with no hippie connections, wrote the music. It opened Off-Broadway at Joseph Papp's new Public Theater in October 1967, then on Broadway in April 1968 in a freer staging by Tom O'Horgan. It was the first rock musical on Broadway, notorious for its anti-war politics, drug references and a brief nude scene.","MacDermot, though not a rocker, was steeped in jazz, funk and the African music he had studied in South Africa, which is why the score grooves more than most rock musicals. The closing \"Let the Sunshine In\" grows out of an earlier song of despair, \"The Flesh Failures\", so the show's famous upbeat ending is shadowed by the death that precedes it. The Fifth Dimension's medley of two of its songs topped the American charts in 1969.","It shocked many critics and audiences, but it ran for over 1,700 performances and spawned productions around the world. Some called it an incoherent mess; others hailed it as the first Broadway musical to sound like its own time. Miloš Forman's 1979 film rewrote the story. Diane Paulus's 2009 Broadway revival won the Tony for Best Revival and showed it as both period piece and protest.","Start with \"Aquarius\", then the title song, then \"Let the Sunshine In\". The original Broadway cast album of 1968 is the reference, with the 2009 revival as a good modern alternative. Listen to the Fifth Dimension's medley to hear the songs as pop. Compare with The Who's Tommy and the Beatles' Sgt. Pepper, rock records of the same moment."],"hamlisch-a-chorus-line":["Seventeen dancers audition for a place in the chorus of a Broadway show, and the unseen director asks each to talk about their life. Their stories, about childhood, sex, ageing, family and failure, unfold in song, monologue and dance over a single audition. Hamlisch's score blends showtunes with 1970s pop and disco: the frantic opening \"I Hope I Get It\", \"At the Ballet\", the comic \"Dance: Ten; Looks: Three\", the ballad \"What I Did for Love\" and the gold-sequined finale \"One\".","The director-choreographer Michael Bennett taped long late-night sessions in 1974 in which Broadway dancers talked about their lives, and the show grew from them, with a book by James Kirkwood Jr. and Nicholas Dante and lyrics by Edward Kleban. It opened at the Public Theater in May 1975 and transferred to Broadway in July. It ran for 6,137 performances, the longest in Broadway history until Cats, and won the Pulitzer Prize for Drama and nine Tonys.","\"One\" is a song from the show the dancers are auditioning for, a tribute to an anonymous star, and the finale shows them dressed identically in gold, singing about someone else: the individuals we have come to know disappear into a perfect line. There are no bows; the dancers kick on as the lights fade. \"At the Ballet\" braids three women's memories, building from separate voices into a soaring trio.","It was an overwhelming success with critics and audiences, and the template for the concept musical drawn from real lives. Some later critics found its emotions manipulative and its 1985 film adaptation was poorly received. The 2006 Broadway revival was respectful rather than revelatory. It remains a touchstone for dancers, and its workshop process changed how Broadway musicals are developed.","Start with \"At the Ballet\", then \"What I Did for Love\" and \"One\". The 1975 original Broadway cast recording is the reference. Notice the sound of 1970s pop in the drums and electric bass. Watch the documentary Every Little Step about the 2006 revival's audition. Compare with Kander and Ebb's Chicago, which opened the same summer, and Sondheim's Company."],"dave-malloy-natasha-pierre-and-the-great-comet-of-1812":["A seventy-page slice of Tolstoy's War and Peace: in 1812 Moscow, the young Natasha, engaged to a prince away at war, is seduced by the rake Anatole, while the melancholy Pierre drinks and searches for meaning until a comet streaks across the sky. Malloy's through-sung score mixes Russian folk song, electropop, indie rock, classical, and club music. Pierre sits at piano and accordion; the audience sits among the actors in a supper-club staging.","It premiered in 2012 at Ars Nova, a small New York theatre, in an immersive staging by Rachel Chavkin, then moved to a tent in the Meatpacking District and finally to Broadway's Imperial Theatre in November 2016, with the pop singer Josh Groban as Pierre. It received twelve Tony nominations, the most of its season, but won only two, for design. Malloy himself had played Pierre in the original production, accompanying on accordion.","The lyrics are mostly Tolstoy's own prose, taken from a standard English translation and set almost word for word, including narrative and stage directions sung by the characters themselves: Malloy called it an \"electropop opera\". The prologue introduces every character in a cumulative list song, in the manner of a children's nursery rhyme. Pierre's \"Dust and Ashes\", added when the show was enlarged for Josh Groban, is a big rock-ballad soliloquy.","Critics praised it as among the most original shows on Broadway in years, but it closed in September 2017 after a casting controversy over replacing the Black actor playing Pierre with the white star Mandy Patinkin. It has become a cult classic of its era, a forerunner of more experimental shows such as Hadestown. Its fusion of classical source and pop music is widely admired.","Start with the \"Prologue\", then \"Dust and Ashes\" and \"The Great Comet of 1812\", where everything stops for Pierre's vision. The original Off-Broadway recording and the Broadway cast album are both good. Then hear Prokofiev's War and Peace, which covers the same episode as grand opera, and Lin-Manuel Miranda's Hamilton for another pop retelling of history."],"ariana-grande-yours-truly":["Grande's debut album is a deliberate throwback: a dozen songs that sound like late-1990s rhythm and blues crossed with the doo-wop and girl-group ballads of the early 1960s. Snapping finger-clicks, soft electric pianos, swelling strings and close-harmony backing vocals frame a lead voice of startling range, which glides from a breathy low register up to a whistle-high top. The single The Way, a duet with the rapper Mac Miller, sets the tone with its rolling piano loop and relaxed groove.","Grande was twenty and best known as an actress on the Nickelodeon sitcoms Victorious and Sam & Cat when Republic Records released the album. Instead of the electronic dance-pop that dominated radio in 2013, she and her team chose an older, warmer palette. The veteran producer and writer Kenneth 'Babyface' Edmonds, architect of much 1990s rhythm and blues, worked on several tracks, alongside Harmony Samuels and other producers who leaned into live-sounding keyboards and strings.","Listen to the backing vocals rather than the lead. Grande arranged and sang most of her own harmony stacks, and they move in the close, sliding voicings of a 1950s vocal group, which is why ballads such as Tattooed Heart sound as if they were recorded decades earlier. Baby I, made with Babyface, borrows the bright horn-and-handclap bounce of early-1990s Mariah Carey almost wholesale.","Reviewers were struck by the voice before anything else, and the Mariah Carey comparisons began immediately, sometimes as praise and sometimes as a charge of imitation. Some critics found the retro concept charming but slight, a showcase rather than a statement. In hindsight the record reads as a foundation: it established the vocal identity, the love of rhythm and blues harmony and the Babyface lineage that her later, more personal albums built on.","Start with Baby I and listen for the moment the voice leaps an octave in the final chorus. Then hear Tattooed Heart and imagine it on a jukebox in 1961. For context, play Mariah Carey's Vision of Love beside it, then jump ahead to Grande's Thank U, Next to hear how far the same instrument travelled in six years."],"ariana-grande-sweetener":["Fifteen tracks split between two very different producers. Pharrell Williams supplies the strange half: sparse, lopsided beats, sudden key shifts and songs that change shape halfway through, as in the title track and the dreamy R.E.M. Max Martin and his protégé ILYA supply the polished half, above all No Tears Left to Cry, which opens as a slow, hymn-like intro before snapping into a light, skipping house groove. Through both, Grande's voice is used as a choir of overdubbed layers.","Grande made the album after the attack that followed her Manchester concert in May 2017, and she has described it as a record about choosing light and healing rather than dwelling on darkness. Her collaboration with Pharrell Williams was the bold move: he was known for idiosyncratic rhythm and blues production with the Neptunes, not for radio pop. Republic released Sweetener in August 2018, during an intensely public year in her personal life.","The album opens with a forty-second a cappella treatment of An Angel Cried, a 1960s song associated with the Four Seasons, sung by Grande alone in stacked harmony: a quiet nod to the vocal groups she grew up imitating. The closing track, Get Well Soon, ends with a long stretch of silence, padding its running time to five minutes and twenty-two seconds as a tribute to the date of the Manchester tragedy.","Critics praised the album's adventurousness and its emotional frankness, especially the songs about anxiety such as Breathin, and it won the Grammy for Best Pop Vocal Album. Opinion divided over the Pharrell tracks: some heard inspired eccentricity, others half-finished sketches beside the sleek Max Martin singles. Many now regard it as the album where Grande became an artist with her own point of view rather than a voice in search of material.","Play No Tears Left to Cry and notice how the slow opening fools you before the beat arrives. Then try the Pharrell-produced title track and listen for how the chords never land where you expect. Breathin is the emotional centre. Compare Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for another pop record built from stacked, self-harmonised voices."],"ariana-grande-thank-u-next":["Twelve songs, most of them short, built on the hard, skittering hi-hats and deep bass of contemporary trap production softened by airy keyboards and Grande's multi-tracked voice. The mood swings from the floating, string-lit opener Imagine, with its whistle-register flourishes, through mid-tempo confessions about need and loneliness, to the title track, a breezy, conversational song about thanking former partners for what each taught her. The voice is often close-miked and quiet, closer to speech than to a showpiece.","The album was written and recorded in a few weeks in late 2018, only months after Sweetener, following the death of her former partner Mac Miller and the end of a much-publicised engagement. Grande made it with a close circle of friends, including the writers Victoria Monét and Tayla Parx and the producer Tommy Brown, alongside Max Martin and ILYA. Republic released it in February 2019, and the speed of its making shows in its loose, diary-like intimacy.","7 Rings is built on a reworking of My Favorite Things from Rodgers and Hammerstein's The Sound of Music, turned from a lilting waltz into a slow trap march; the Rodgers and Hammerstein estate received the large majority of its songwriting royalties. Listen too for how often Grande sings in her low, breathy register here, saving the famous high notes for brief flourishes at the edges of songs.","Reviewers praised its candour and the way it turned tabloid events into something calmer and more generous, and the title track was widely celebrated for its gracious, unbitter tone. Some found the second half thinner and the trap production generic. The 7 Rings interpolation started arguments about borrowing, from the Rodgers and Hammerstein credit to accusations from other artists of lifted flows, which have never quite settled.","Begin with the title track and listen for how plainly she sings it, almost talking. Then play Ghostin on headphones in the dark. Hear 7 Rings back to back with John Coltrane's My Favorite Things and Julie Andrews's original to follow one melody through three completely different worlds."],"ariana-grande-eternal-sunshine":["A compact, thirteen-track album that moves between soft rhythm and blues, glowing synth-pop and late-1980s house. Yes, And? rides a crisp house piano and a dry drum machine; We Can't Be Friends has a gentle, insistent synthesizer pulse under a quietly heartbroken melody. Short interludes join songs together so the record plays almost as a single sequence. The vocals are restrained by Grande's standards, with the big whistle notes held back for moments of release.","The title comes from Michel Gondry's 2004 film Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind, about a couple who erase their memories of each other, and the album circles divorce, new love and public scrutiny. Grande made it with Max Martin and ILYA, her longest-standing collaborators, while she was also filming the screen adaptation of the musical Wicked. It was released in March 2024, her first album in over three years.","The album closes with Ordinary Things, which ends with a recorded voice note from Grande's grandmother, Marjorie Grande, offering her own plain-spoken advice about love: the record's final word belongs to someone else. Yes, And? borrows its spirit and its piano-house groove from Madonna's Vogue, and the title itself is a phrase from improv theatre, where performers must accept and build on whatever is offered.","Reviewers broadly welcomed it as her most cohesive and grown-up album, admiring its concept and its restraint, though a few wished it took more musical risks. The album's subject, unfolding alongside heavy press coverage of her private life, made discussion of the music hard to separate from gossip. Critics singled out We Can't Be Friends as one of her finest songs.","Listen straight through without shuffle, since the interludes are part of the design. Notice how the house piano of Yes, And? recalls Madonna's Vogue, then put on Robyn's Dancing On My Own after We Can't Be Friends to hear the same sad-dance tradition. Finish with Ordinary Things and wait for the voice at the end."],"dua-lipa-new-rules":["A break-up song framed as a list of resolutions a friend repeats to keep herself from going back. The track is built on a light, plucked synthesizer riff with a faint tropical-house lilt, a loping beat and a chorus that is half-sung, half-chanted, with Lipa's low, smoky alto placed front and centre. The drop after each chorus replaces words with a chopped, pitched vocal hook, a device then everywhere in dance-pop, here used sparingly.","New Rules was a single from Lipa's self-titled debut album of 2017. It was written by Caroline Ailin, Emily Warren and Ian Kirkpatrick and produced by Kirkpatrick. Lipa, born in London to Kosovo Albanian parents, had spent part of her childhood in Pristina before returning to London alone as a teenager to pursue music. The song's video, a choreographed sleepover in a Miami hotel, did much to make it her breakthrough.","Notice how low Lipa sings for a pop lead: the verses sit in a dark contralto range that most producers of the time would have pushed higher, and the song's cool, unsentimental attitude depends on it. The chorus is structured as a numbered list, so the melody resets after each item, giving the hook a stop-start, almost rhythmic-spoken quality instead of a soaring line.","It was received as one of the defining pop singles of 2017 and established Lipa as a major star. Critics noted the novelty of a break-up song about female friendship and self-discipline rather than romance, and the video was praised for its choreography. Some found the production generic for its year, a fair charge that the far more distinctive Future Nostalgia later answered.","Listen for the contrast between the low, flat verses and the punchier chorus, then pay attention to the wordless synth-vocal drop. Compare Robyn's Dancing On My Own for another stoic, dance-floor break-up song. Then go straight to Future Nostalgia to hear Lipa trade this sound for disco."],"dua-lipa-future-nostalgia":["Eleven tightly built dance tracks that rebuild disco, 1980s synth-pop and early-1990s house with modern punch. The bass guitar is the lead instrument: Don't Start Now opens on a springy, octave-jumping bassline, and Levitating bounces on a funk-slap groove with handclaps and a laser-like synth. Strings sweep in on Cool and Hallucinate, which lifts from a filtered house pulse. Every song is short, every chorus arrives early, and the album runs barely over half an hour.","Lipa set out to make a nostalgic but forward-looking dance album, working with a range of producers including Stephen 'Koz' Kozmeniuk, Ian Kirkpatrick, Jeff Bhasker and Stuart Price, who had produced Madonna's disco-revival records. After a leak, the release was brought forward to 27 March 2020, just as much of the world went into lockdown: a party album arrived when clubs had shut, and it became the soundtrack to kitchen dancing at home.","Break My Heart builds its guitar-and-bass groove on a borrowed riff from INXS's Need You Tonight, and the Australian band's members are credited as writers. Listen to how often the bass doubles the vocal hook: in Don't Start Now, the bass guitar answers each line of the chorus, a disco arranging trick from Chic's Bernard Edwards that holds the whole record together.","Critics greeted it as one of the strongest pop albums of its year and it won the Grammy for Best Pop Vocal Album; it is now often credited with leading the early-2020s disco revival alongside The Weeknd's After Hours. A minority found it pastiche, a skilful collage of borrowed decades. The debate about retro pop's originality has followed it ever since.","Start with Don't Start Now and follow only the bass guitar the whole way through. Then play Chic's Good Times to hear where that bass comes from, and Kylie Minogue's Can't Get You Out of My Head for the cool, clipped vocal style. Play the album loud and in order: it is paced like a DJ set."],"dua-lipa-radical-optimism":["Lipa's third album keeps the dance tempos but softens the edges, with hazy, phased synthesizers, live-sounding drums and a sunny, Mediterranean warmth. Houdini rides a rubbery, psychedelic bass and a drum fill-heavy groove; Training Season is sleeker and more insistent; Illusion runs on a breathless, galloping beat. The eleven songs are mostly about breaking patterns in relationships and approaching chaos with calm, sung in Lipa's familiar low, steady voice.","For this record Lipa worked with Kevin Parker of the Australian psychedelic band Tame Impala, the British producer Danny L Harle, the writer Caroline Ailin and the songwriters Andrew Wyatt and Tobias Jesso Jr. She cited British psychedelic dance music of the early 1990s, particularly Primal Scream, and Massive Attack's trip-hop, as touchstones. It followed the enormous success of Future Nostalgia and her hit for the Barbie film soundtrack, and was released in May 2024.","Listen for Kevin Parker's fingerprints: the woozy, phased drums and the warm, slightly wobbling synthesizers on Houdini are the same textures he uses on Tame Impala's Currents. The album's title is spelled out as a philosophy rather than a story, so its songs, unusually for a break-up record, end on curiosity rather than resentment.","Reviews were mixed to positive. Critics admired the production polish and the lighter mood but many felt that the psychedelic influences she had trailed in interviews were barely audible, and that the songs lacked the unforgettable hooks of Future Nostalgia. It is generally ranked below its predecessor, though Houdini and Illusion found admirers as some of her best grooves.","Start with Houdini on good speakers and listen to the drums swim. Then play Tame Impala's Currents to hear Parker's sound in its native setting, and Primal Scream's Screamadelica for the 1990s spirit Lipa was chasing. Judge the album as a summer record rather than as a sequel."],"harry-styles-sign-of-the-times":["A slow piano ballad nearly six minutes long that builds like a 1970s rock anthem. It begins with a lone, rocking piano figure and Styles's low, weary voice, then adds guitars, drums and swelling backing vocals. The climax has Styles leaping into a ringing falsetto over a massed, gospel-tinged choir of overdubbed voices, before the song drops back to almost nothing. Its stately tempo and grandeur are the opposite of what a boy-band singer was expected to release.","This was Styles's first solo single after the hiatus of One Direction, the British boy band that made him famous as a teenager. It was produced by Jeff Bhasker with Alex Salibian and Tyler Johnson, and much of the accompanying album was recorded at a studio in Port Antonio, Jamaica, away from the music industry. Styles has described the song as a message from someone near the end of life to the person they leave behind.","The piano and the slow, deliberate pace come from the British glam-rock balladry of David Bowie and Elton John, and the falsetto climax recalls Queen's layered vocals; Styles was announcing his lineage. Listen for the guitar lines near the end, which are mixed underneath the choir and sound almost like distant church bells. The long fade is part of the design.","Critics were surprised by its ambition and many praised it as a credible rock ballad, comparing it to Bowie and to Prince's Purple Rain. Others found the comparisons flattering and the song derivative. It immediately changed how the press saw Styles, from teen idol to aspiring classic rocker, an image he has played with since.","Listen with good headphones and follow the build: piano alone, then each layer arriving, then the falsetto. Pair it with Bowie's Ziggy Stardust album and Prince's Purple Rain to hear the models. Then compare the lighter, sunnier Fine Line to hear where he went next."],"harry-styles-fine-line":["Twelve songs in the warm, sunlit sound of 1970s California soft rock and pop. Golden opens with chiming guitars and stacked harmonies; Adore You lopes along on a rubbery bass groove; Falling is a hushed piano confession; Canyon Moon is a light, acoustic road song; Treat People with Kindness is a gospel-tinged sing-along. The closing title track builds slowly over six minutes from a soft guitar figure to a full brass climax. The production is live-sounding and rich.","Styles made the album after a painful break-up, working again with Jeff Bhasker and Tyler Johnson and with the producer Kid Harpoon. He has spoken of immersing himself in Joni Mitchell, Fleetwood Mac and the Laurel Canyon singer-songwriters during the sessions, partly in Malibu. Released in December 2019 by Columbia, it arrived as Styles was becoming a fashion figure as much as a musician.","Listen for the close-harmony backing vocals, sung in the stacked, slightly loose style of Crosby, Stills and Nash and Fleetwood Mac's Rumours; Styles often doubles his own lines. Canyon Moon's title nods to Laurel Canyon, and the record's whole arc runs from bright opening to the slow, brass-lit fade of Fine Line, deliberately sequenced as a journey through heartbreak toward acceptance.","Critics welcomed it as a big step forward from his solo debut, more personal and more confident, though some found it too dependent on its 1970s models. It became his commercial breakthrough as a solo artist and is often cited as one of the defining pop-rock records of its time. The debate about whether Styles is a stylist or an original continues around it.","Start with Golden, then Falling, and notice how intimate the voice is. Then hear Joni Mitchell's Blue and Fleetwood Mac's Rumours to recognise the inheritance. Listen to the title track at the end in one sitting and wait for the horns: it is the album's real conclusion."],"harry-styles-harrys-house":["Thirteen songs that trade guitars for synthesizers, horns and funky, light-footed grooves. As It Was, the lead single, races along on a bright synth riff and a galloping drum machine, a melancholy song about change disguised as a dance tune. Elsewhere there are horn-driven funk tracks, Satellite's slow-building synthesizer swell and Matilda's spare acoustic guitar and voice. The mood is domestic and intimate: a day spent at home, with all its small comforts and disturbances.","The title comes from Hosono House, the 1973 album by the Japanese musician Haruomi Hosono, later of Yellow Magic Orchestra, which was recorded in Hosono's own home. Styles made the album during the pandemic with Kid Harpoon and Tyler Johnson, drawing on Japanese city pop and on 1980s synth-pop. It was released by Columbia in May 2022 and became the most commercially dominant record of his career.","As It Was begins with the voice of a child, Styles's goddaughter, calling out to him before the synth riff begins: a domestic snapshot that frames the whole album. Listen also to how many tracks lean on gentle, syncopated basslines and glassy electric pianos, the signatures of Tatsuro Yamashita and other city-pop producers, rather than the 1970s American rock of Fine Line.","It won the Grammy for Album of the Year, but critical opinion was more divided than that suggests: many praised its warmth and craft, while others found it light and even bland compared with his rock ambitions. As It Was was celebrated as one of the decade's biggest pop songs. The Hosono connection prompted wider interest in Japanese city pop among Western listeners.","Start with As It Was and notice how fast and bright it is for a sad song. Then play Matilda, almost voice alone. Put on Yellow Magic Orchestra and Tatsuro Yamashita's Ride on Time to hear the Japanese pop that inspired it, and a-ha's Take On Me for the synth riff's ancestry."],"olivia-rodrigo-drivers-license":["A break-up ballad sung from behind the wheel, newly licensed and newly alone. It opens with the soft electronic chime of a car warning signal, then settles into slow, sustained piano chords and Rodrigo's close, almost whispered voice. The verses stay small and conversational; the song builds gradually, and in the bridge the drums crash in and her voice is multiplied into a wall of stacked harmonies before falling back to a quiet, repeated closing line.","Rodrigo was seventeen and known for the Disney series High School Musical: The Musical: The Series when she released this, her debut single, in January 2021. She had written it at the piano and refined it with the producer and co-writer Dan Nigro, who had previously played in the indie band As Tall as Lions. Released during pandemic lockdowns, it spread through social media and streaming at extraordinary speed.","Listen to the bridge on headphones: the stacked harmony vocals spread across the stereo field and are slightly detuned against one another, making them sound like an echo of her own thoughts rather than a choir. The car chime at the beginning is a small but telling sound-design choice by Nigro that grounds the whole song in a single image.","The song was an immediate global phenomenon and was hailed as a debut that sounded like a seasoned songwriter. Critics heard a strong debt to Taylor Swift's confessional ballads and to Lorde's minimal production, both of which Rodrigo has acknowledged. Some dismissed it as teen melodrama; it is now widely regarded as one of the most important pop debuts of its decade.","Listen once in the car at night, as intended. Notice how quiet the first two minutes are before the bridge explodes. Then hear Taylor Swift's Red and Lorde's Melodrama for the songwriters she studied, and follow with the whole of Sour to hear the ballad in context."],"olivia-rodrigo-sour":["Eleven songs that swing between pop-punk and hushed piano and guitar ballads. The opener, Brutal, begins with a soft string prelude before crashing into distorted guitars and a snarling, half-spoken vocal. Good 4 U is driving pop-punk with a sing-shout chorus; Deja Vu floats on dreamy synthesizers and harmonies; Traitor and Enough for You are quiet, fingerpicked confessions. The emotional territory is adolescence: heartbreak, jealousy and insecurity, delivered with sharp humour and a forceful voice.","Rodrigo made Sour with Dan Nigro in Los Angeles after the enormous success of Drivers License, and it was released in May 2021. She cited Taylor Swift, Lorde, Alanis Morissette and the pop-punk and riot grrrl bands of the 1990s and 2000s as influences. It arrived at a moment when guitar-based pop was returning to the charts after a decade dominated by electronic production.","After release, songwriting credits were added: Paramore's Hayley Williams and Josh Farro for Good 4 U, which echoes the shape of Misery Business, and Taylor Swift, Jack Antonoff and Annie Clark for Deja Vu, which echoes Swift's Cruel Summer. Listen to Brutal's opening strings, a short faux-classical prelude that is promptly blown away by the guitars: a little joke about expectations.","Critics praised its songwriting, honesty and range, and it won the Grammy for Best Pop Vocal Album, with Rodrigo also winning Best New Artist. The retroactive credits sparked a lively debate about influence, interpolation and the line between homage and borrowing in pop. It is widely regarded as the defining teen pop album of the early 2020s.","Listen in order and feel the whiplash between loud and soft. Start with Brutal, then Traitor. Play Paramore's Riot and Alanis Morissette's Jagged Little Pill afterwards for the lineage, and Green Day's Dookie for the guitar sound. The album is short enough to hear twice in one sitting."],"olivia-rodrigo-guts":["Twelve songs that push further into guitar rock, with louder drums, crunchier riffs and more humour. Vampire begins as a piano ballad and builds into a pounding, theatrical rocker; Get Him Back! alternates deadpan spoken verses with a gang-shout chorus; Ballad of a Homeschooled Girl is a frantic, awkward comedy of social anxiety. Between these are delicate piano and guitar songs such as Lacy and Logical, and the closing Teenage Dream, a song about growing up and fearing one's best years are behind.","Rodrigo made Guts with Dan Nigro again, writing in the years after her sudden fame at seventeen. She has spoken of wanting the album to capture the confusion of being nineteen and twenty: self-doubt, social embarrassment and frustration with expectations. Released in September 2023, it confirmed that Sour's guitar sound was not a phase, and was followed by a major world tour.","Listen to the drums: the album was recorded with a heavier, roomier drum sound than Sour, closer to 1990s alternative rock, and Nigro often lets them run slightly raw. The song titles and spoken interjections often carry the joke, but the musical jokes are just as pointed, such as sudden tempo changes that mimic panic attacks or second thoughts.","Reviewers praised it as a confident, funnier follow-up, often ranking it above Sour for its consistency and humour, though some missed the debut's emotional rawness. It was widely regarded as among the best albums of 2023. Its success helped consolidate the renewed presence of guitar-driven pop and alternative rock among young listeners.","Start with Vampire and listen for the moment the piano gives way to drums. Then Ballad of a Homeschooled Girl for the comedy. Compare Nirvana's Nevermind and Alanis Morissette's Jagged Little Pill for the 1990s sound, then return to Sour to hear the growth."],"carly-rae-jepsen-call-me-maybe":["A song about a sudden crush on a stranger and the nerve it takes to make the first move. Its signature is a bright, staccato string riff played on synthesizer, stabbing out the chords like a disco orchestra. Under it run a light four-to-the-floor beat and a chugging guitar. Jepsen's voice is light and eager, the verses rising in anticipation before a chorus so simple and catchy that it seems to have always existed.","Jepsen had come third on Canadian Idol in 2007 and had released a folk-pop album before this song. It was co-written with Tavish Crowe and Josh Ramsay of the Canadian band Marianas Trench, who produced it, and released by the Vancouver label 604 Records in 2011. After Justin Bieber praised it online, it was picked up by Schoolboy and Interscope and spread worldwide through amateur lip-sync videos.","Listen to the string riff carefully: it is a synthesized imitation of a disco string section, playing short, punchy chords rather than a melody, which gives the song its springy energy. The guitar is mixed low and almost unnoticed. The verses deliberately hold back on rhythm, so when the chorus arrives the beat seems to leap forward.","It was one of the most inescapable songs of 2012 and was immediately received as a perfect pop single, admired even by critics who disdained mainstream pop. For a time Jepsen was considered a one-hit wonder; her later albums changed that view. It is now regarded as a classic of 2010s bubblegum pop.","Play it loud and focus on the string riff. Then listen to Spice Girls' Wannabe and Britney Spears's Baby One More Time for earlier hooks of the same kind. Next go to Emotion to hear how Jepsen developed into an album artist."],"carly-rae-jepsen-emotion":["Twelve songs of pure, bright synth-pop modelled on the mid-1980s. Gated drums, shimmering keyboards, chunky synth bass and occasional saxophone frame Jepsen's light, yearning voice. Run Away with Me opens the album with a blast of saxophone; Emotion and All That are slower, glowing ballads; Gimmie Love and Boy Problems are bouncy and playful. Nearly every song is about wanting someone, and almost none of them resolves the longing.","Jepsen wrote a huge number of songs for the record, reportedly around two hundred, after the pressure of Call Me Maybe. She worked with indie-leaning producers and songwriters, including Rostam Batmanglij of Vampire Weekend, Dev Hynes, Ariel Rechtshaid and the Swedish duo Mattman & Robin, alongside mainstream figures such as Greg Kurstin and Shellback. It was released first in Japan, then elsewhere in 2015.","Listen for Rostam Batmanglij's touch on Warm Blood, where the voice is digitally chopped and the beat stutters, and Dev Hynes's on All That, a slow, sensual ballad very close to Prince's mid-1980s sound. The album's consistency comes from a shared palette of 1980s textures, but each producer quietly bends it toward his own style.","The album sold modestly but was adored by critics and by a devoted fan base, and it became a cult object: proof that a mainstream pop star could make a beloved, artful record. It is now often ranked among the best pop albums of the 2010s. Its follow-up, Emotion: Side B, gathered outtakes that fans regard as almost equally strong.","Start with Run Away with Me and listen to the saxophone. Then All That, slowly. Compare Cyndi Lauper's Time After Time and Madonna's early records for the 1980s template, and Robyn's Body Talk for the cult-pop kinship. Listen to the whole record straight through."],"carly-rae-jepsen-run-away-with-me":["A song about the urge to escape somewhere with someone, opening the album Emotion. It begins with a booming, reverb-drenched saxophone riff, then a pounding, gated drum beat and a glittering synthesizer. Jepsen's voice rises from soft verses into a chorus that bursts open, almost shouted, with the saxophone returning between lines. The structure is simple and the effect is huge: a burst of pure exhilaration in four minutes.","The song was produced by the Swedish duo Mattman & Robin, who were part of the Scandinavian pop production world shaped by Max Martin. It appeared as the first track of Emotion and was released as a single in 2015. Its saxophone riff was inspired by 1980s pop and rock, when the instrument was a staple of arena anthems, from Bruce Springsteen to George Michael.","Listen to how the saxophone riff is processed: it is placed in a vast reverb, so it sounds as if played in a stadium, and it is doubled by synthesizer for extra brightness. The drums use the gated-reverb technique of the 1980s, where a big echo is cut off abruptly, giving a punchy, explosive sound.","The song was not a big commercial hit, but it became one of the most beloved pop songs of its decade among critics and fans, and its saxophone intro became a widely shared online joke and celebration. It is often ranked high on lists of the best songs of the 2010s, and it is the anthem of the Emotion cult.","Turn it up and wait for the saxophone. Feel the drums hit like explosions. Then listen to Bruce Springsteen's Born to Run for the saxophone tradition, and Kylie Minogue's Can't Get You Out of My Head for the Euro-pop sheen. Then play all of Emotion."],"carly-rae-jepsen-dedicated":["Thirteen songs in a softer, disco-tinged mode than Emotion: Jepsen herself called it chill disco. Julien opens with a slinky bass and soft strings; Now That I Found You is a bright, galloping dance tune; Party for One celebrates being single with a chunky, self-confident beat; Too Much is an affectionate song about overdoing everything. The production is lighter and more relaxed, with mid-tempo grooves and a warmer, more spacious mix.","Jepsen worked with a wide circle of producers, including Jack Antonoff, John Hill, Captain Cuts and Kyle Shearer. As with Emotion, she wrote far more songs than were used. Released in May 2019, the album continued her status as a cult favourite rather than a chart star, with a devoted following and enthusiastic critical support.","Listen to Julien's bassline, which recalls Chic's fluid disco basslines, and the soft string pads that evoke late-1970s disco orchestration rather than 1980s synthesizers. The songs often avoid big, explosive choruses, preferring a steady, rolling groove; that relaxed restraint is what Jepsen meant by chill disco.","Critics were generally positive, praising the craft and warmth while noting that it lacked Emotion's sense of discovery. Some found it too relaxed. Its outtakes, collected as Dedicated Side B in 2020, were again widely admired, reinforcing Jepsen's reputation as a songwriter with an unusually deep reserve of strong material.","Start with Julien and follow the bass. Then Now That I Found You. Play Chic's Le Freak and Donna Summer's I Feel Love afterwards to hear the disco lineage, and compare Emotion to hear the difference between 1980s gloss and 1970s groove."],"troye-sivan-blue-neighbourhood":["Sivan's debut album is moody, atmospheric synth-pop: soft, echoing synthesizers, crisp programmed beats and a light, breathy tenor voice. Wild opens with a children's choir and a rolling, insistent beat; Youth is a bright, euphoric anthem; Fools and Talk Me Down are slow and wistful. Throughout, the arrangements are spacious, with lots of air around the voice, and the mood shifts between longing and late-night melancholy.","Sivan was born in Johannesburg and raised in Perth, Australia; he became known first as a YouTube personality and child actor before releasing music. He made the album with producers including Alex Hope and Bram Inscore, among others. Its three music videos, Wild, Fools and Talk Me Down, form a trilogy telling the story of two boys growing up, an openly queer narrative rare in mainstream pop then.","Listen to the children's choir that opens Wild and returns in the background: it adds an eerie innocence that suits the album's theme of youth and memory. The production often places Sivan's voice in a slight reverb, so it sounds distant and dreamlike, as if recalled rather than sung live.","Critics praised it as an accomplished debut and an important moment of queer visibility in pop, with the video trilogy singled out for its emotional honesty. Some found it uneven or too reliant on contemporary synth-pop production. It established Sivan as a significant pop artist and influenced younger queer pop singers.","Start with Wild and listen for the choir. Then Youth for the euphoric pop. Compare Lorde's Pure Heroine, made in New Zealand only two years earlier, and Pet Shop Boys' Actually for an earlier queer pop sensibility. Watch the video trilogy alongside the songs."],"troye-sivan-youth":["A song about the reckless freedom of young love, and the most direct, upbeat track on Blue Neighbourhood. It opens with a soft, rippling synthesizer and Sivan's light voice, then gathers momentum with a punchy beat and a soaring chorus. The production is bright and euphoric, with wide synthesizer chords and a pulsing bass. The whole song feels like a release of tension, a burst of energy against the moodier album around it.","Youth was released from Blue Neighbourhood, Sivan's debut album, and became one of his breakthrough songs internationally. It was written with a small team of collaborators and produced in the clean, atmospheric synth-pop style of the album. Sivan was then twenty, making the song's subject close to his own experience.","Listen to how the beat drops out briefly before the final chorus, then returns with extra layers of synthesizer: a classic pop build that makes the last chorus feel larger. Sivan's voice is double-tracked in the chorus, a subtle effect that adds weight without obvious harmony.","Critics welcomed it as a strong, euphoric pop song and it became one of Sivan's most popular tracks. Some found it formulaic compared with the album's moodier songs. It is often regarded as a defining youth anthem of the mid-2010s.","Listen first for the build into the final chorus and the way the synthesizers widen around the voice. Then play Robyn's Dancing On My Own for a similar emotional lift, and Lorde's Royals for the minimal pop of the same years. Finally hear Blue Neighbourhood complete, where Youth works as the bright window in a darker house."],"sabrina-carpenter-emails-i-cant-send":["An album that mixes confessional piano and guitar ballads with playful, bouncy pop. The title track opens quietly, voice and piano; Vicious and Because I Liked a Boy are pointed, rueful songs about relationships and public gossip; Nonsense is a light, bubbly song built on a skipping beat and a sly, conversational melody. The production is clean and varied, with Carpenter's voice at the centre, clear and expressive, moving easily from soft balladry to bright, comic delivery.","Carpenter had been a Disney Channel actor, best known for Girl Meets World, and had released several albums with Hollywood Records. This was her first album with Island Records, released in July 2022 after a period of intense online scrutiny of her personal life. Its frankness and humour marked her shift from teen star to adult songwriter.","Nonsense became famous for its outros on tour: at each concert Carpenter improvised or pre-wrote a new rhyming final verse tailored to the city she was playing, so no two performances ended the same way. The song's light, nonsensical structure was designed to accommodate this, making it a rare pop song built for live variation.","Critics welcomed it as a step forward, praising its wit and songwriting, though some found it uneven. It built a dedicated fan base and, with the deluxe edition's Feather, laid the foundations for her explosive success two years later. It is now seen as the turning point in her career.","Start with the title track for the confessional mood, then Nonsense for the humour. Look up a few of the live outros. Compare Olivia Rodrigo's Sour for a parallel confessional pop album of the same years, and Taylor Swift's Red for the songwriting tradition."],"sabrina-carpenter-espresso":["A breezy, self-assured song about her own effect on other people, delivered with a wink. It rides a funky, rubbery bassline and a light disco-funk groove, with crisp guitar and airy synthesizers. Carpenter's voice is cool and deadpan in the verses, then lifts into a chorus whose melody bounces in short, clipped phrases. The track is lean and catchy, only three minutes, with a relaxed swagger that made it one of the songs of its summer.","Espresso was produced by Julian Bunetta and written by Carpenter with Amy Allen, Steph Jones and Bunetta. It was released in April 2024, just before Carpenter performed at Coachella, and preceded her album Short n' Sweet. Its viral success transformed her from a cult favourite into a major pop star.","Listen to the bassline, which carries the melody's rhythm more than the drums do; it is a disco-funk device borrowed from Chic and Nile Rodgers's productions. The vocal is recorded close and dry, with little reverb, so Carpenter's deadpan delivery sounds intimate and conspiratorial.","It was received as one of the biggest and most enjoyable pop songs of 2024 and won the Grammy for Best Pop Solo Performance. Critics praised its humour, groove and Carpenter's comic timing, though some found it slight. It confirmed her arrival as a major pop figure.","Follow the bassline from start to finish and notice how little else is going on. Pay attention to how cool and dry her voice sounds, almost spoken. Then play Chic's Good Times for the disco-funk lineage and Dua Lipa's Future Nostalgia for the contemporary revival, before moving on to Please Please Please for contrast."],"sabrina-carpenter-please-please-please":["A song asking a partner not to let her down in public. It has a gentle, swaying groove, twangy guitars and a hint of country and 1960s pop, with soft synthesizer pads and a rolling bass. Carpenter's voice is sweet and pleading, with a touch of humour, and the chorus melody falls in a long, sighing line. The arrangement is spare and warm, building only slightly toward the end.","Please Please Please was produced by Jack Antonoff, known for his long collaborations with Taylor Swift and Lorde, and written by Carpenter with Amy Allen and Antonoff. Released in June 2024, only weeks after Espresso, it preceded the album Short n' Sweet. Arriving while Espresso was still everywhere, it confirmed that her breakthrough was not a fluke and showed a more vulnerable side.","Listen to the twangy guitar and the retro, slightly country feel, which recall 1960s girl-group ballads and Nancy Sinatra's records. Antonoff's production keeps the drums soft and the bass round, giving the song a warm, analogue character unusual in contemporary pop.","Critics praised it as a charming, carefully crafted follow-up to Espresso, admiring its retro warmth and the humour folded into its pleading, though some found it less distinctive than its predecessor. It was a major commercial success and, with Espresso, confirmed Carpenter as one of the defining pop stars of 2024, with a range wider than one novelty hit.","Listen for the guitar twang in the intro and the long, sighing fall of the chorus melody. Then play Dolly Parton's Jolene for the great country plea and ABBA's The Winner Takes It All for the pop ballad tradition behind it. Finally compare Espresso, released weeks earlier, to hear how different two Carpenter singles can be."],"chappell-roan-midwest-princess":["Fourteen songs of maximalist, theatrical pop: glittering synthesizers, big drums, campy humour and sudden ballads. Femininomenon opens with a cheerleader chant and a pounding beat; Hot to Go! is built around a spelled-out chant designed for audience participation; Pink Pony Club, a song about leaving a small town to perform in Los Angeles, is a soaring, piano-driven anthem. Casual and Kaleidoscope are quieter, more intimate songs. Throughout, Roan's voice is big, flexible and dramatic.","Roan, born Kayleigh Rose Amstutz in Missouri, had been dropped by Atlantic Records in 2020. She rebuilt her career with the producer Dan Nigro, who released this, her debut album, on his Amusement Records imprint with Island in September 2023. Her drag-inspired stage persona and openly queer songs found a growing audience through touring, before her breakthrough in 2024.","Listen to the theatrical shifts within songs: sudden changes of tempo and texture that suggest musical theatre and camp performance. Hot to Go! is built for crowd participation, like a cheerleading routine or a disco line dance, and its chant structure recalls Village People's YMCA. The album's title sets the story arc: a young woman leaving home and finding herself.","The album initially sold modestly but grew into a major success through word of mouth and live performance, and Roan won the Grammy for Best New Artist. Critics praised its humour, theatricality and emotional range, and it is now regarded as one of the defining pop albums of its decade.","Start with Pink Pony Club and feel the anthem build. Then Hot to Go! for the participatory fun. Compare Kate Bush's Wuthering Heights for the theatrical voice, Lady Gaga's The Fame Monster for camp pop, and Cyndi Lauper for exuberance."],"chappell-roan-good-luck-babe":["A song addressed to someone who will not admit her own feelings. It opens with a pulsing synthesizer and a steady beat, building through verses to a soaring chorus. The production recalls mid-1980s synth-pop, with bright keyboards, a driving bass and big drums. In the bridge Roan's voice rises to a long, held, ringing high note, the song's dramatic climax, before the final chorus.","Good Luck, Babe! was released as a standalone single in April 2024, produced by Dan Nigro and written by Roan with Nigro and Justin Tranter. It followed her debut album and arrived as her popularity grew rapidly through touring and festival performances. It became her biggest hit and helped propel her to mainstream success.","Listen to the held note in the bridge, which lasts several seconds and is sung with full, ringing power, a theatrical gesture recalling Kate Bush. The synthesizers are layered to recall 1980s pop, especially the bright, glassy sounds of that decade.","Critics praised it as one of the best songs of 2024, admiring its emotional power, its widescreen 1980s production and above all Roan's vocal performance, especially the bridge. Some heard it as too close to its models. It became the defining song of her breakthrough year and introduced many listeners to the album that preceded it.","Wait for the held note in the bridge and notice how the band drops back to let it ring. Then play Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for the theatrical synth-pop lineage and Cyndi Lauper's Time After Time for the 1980s sound. Then return to The Rise and Fall of a Midwest Princess for the wider story."],"billie-eilish-ocean-eyes":["A slow, floating ballad of about three and a half minutes, sung by a thirteen-year-old with almost no vibrato and a great deal of air in the tone. Soft synthesizer chords move in long washes, a gentle programmed beat ticks underneath, and the chorus rises into a high, unforced head voice that hangs over the track like mist. It is a song about being quietly overwhelmed by someone's gaze, and the arrangement never raises its voice to say so.","Finneas O'Connell wrote the song for his own band, then gave it to his younger sister Billie because her dance teacher wanted something new to choreograph to. They recorded it at home in Highland Park, Los Angeles, and uploaded it to SoundCloud in late 2015 simply so the teacher could download it. Strangers found it, blogs passed it around, and within months the two homeschooled siblings had a management deal and a record contract.","Listen to how many Billies there are. The backing harmonies are her own voice stacked many times and panned wide, so the chorus sounds like a small choir made of one person breathing. The bedroom origin is audible too: there is no big studio room around the voice, just close microphone intimacy, which became the defining texture of everything the siblings made afterwards.","It was received at first as a charming internet discovery rather than a statement, and some listeners filed it with the moody, whispery pop then everywhere. In hindsight it reads as a manifesto for a new way of making hits: two siblings, one laptop, no outside writers. Debate now is less about the song than about how much the bedroom story was later polished by the industry around it.","Use headphones and listen for the moment the chorus lifts: the voice jumps up rather than pushes harder, a lesson in singing quietly at height. Then hear Bon Iver's self-titled second album for another record built from a stacked, treated single voice, and Cocteau Twins' Heaven or Las Vegas for the older dream-pop haze it inherits."],"billie-eilish-when-we-all-fall-asleep":["Fourteen tracks in about forty-three minutes that move between whispered horror-movie trap, toy-box electropop and bare piano ballads. The sound is built on huge, clean sub-bass and tiny close-up details: clicks, breaths, snaps, a ukulele, a voice sung so near the microphone that it seems to sit inside your ear. The album is loosely framed around night terrors and sleep paralysis, and its signature is the contrast between that enormous low end and a voice that hardly rises above a murmur.","Billie Eilish was seventeen when it came out on Darkroom and Interscope in March 2019. The whole record was made with her brother Finneas O'Connell in his small bedroom in their parents' house in Los Angeles, using a laptop, consumer monitors and one good microphone. Streaming had changed how young listeners found music, and a fanbase built online was waiting. In 2020 she became the youngest artist to win all four major Grammy categories in one night.","The record opens with a short track of studio chatter in which she takes out her dental aligner and the siblings laugh, an in-joke that announces the homemade method. On Bury a Friend, the scrape of a dentist's drill is used as a sound effect, and many of the album's percussive sounds are household noises recorded and treated rather than drum machines. Finneas mixed the vocal with almost no reverb, which is why it feels so close.","Critics praised its strangeness and its confidence in quiet; few blockbuster pop albums had dared to be this hushed. Some older writers heard mere gloom or a gimmick of whispering, and parents worried about its darker subjects. The argument that has outlasted those concerns is about production: whether a bedroom record could sound this large, and the answer reshaped how a generation of pop was mixed.","Start with Bad Guy and then When the Party's Over, the album's two poles: dry, bass-heavy swagger and a choral ballad built from stacked voices. Listen on good headphones or a system with real bass, because half of the music lives below where laptop speakers reach. Then hear Lorde's Pure Heroine and Massive Attack's Mezzanine for its ancestors in minimal, shadowy pop."],"billie-eilish-bad-guy":["Just over three minutes of almost nothing: a bouncing, rubbery synth-bass line, finger snaps, a kick drum and a voice delivered deadpan, half-spoken and close. The song plays with the persona of a tough character and undercuts it with a smirk. Then, after the last chorus, the track stops, the tempo collapses and a slow, distorted, lurching coda arrives, as if a different song had broken through the floor.","Written by Billie Eilish and Finneas O'Connell and recorded in his bedroom studio, it was released in March 2019 alongside When We All Fall Asleep, Where Do We Go? Most pop singles of the time were dense, loud and built by teams of writers; this one was made by two siblings and left deliberately bare. It became the song through which much of the world first heard her.","The empty space is the hook. Count the elements in the verse and there are barely four, so every snap and click lands with force. The bass is pitched so that it rumbles on large speakers but still reads on a phone. Listen at the end for the coda's sudden drop into a slower tempo with a thick, buzzing synth: it was written as a separate idea and simply bolted on.","It was greeted as a novelty by some and as a reinvention of the pop single by others; the minimalist arrangement drew comparisons with early hip-hop and with Missy Elliott and Timbaland's rhythmic wit. It won Record of the Year and Song of the Year at the 2020 Grammys. Critics still argue whether the mischievous coda is the best idea in the song or a party trick.","Listen once for the bassline alone, then once for the snaps and the tiny vocal ad-libs panned left and right. Wait for the coda and notice how the mood shifts from playful to menacing in one bar. Compare it with Missy Elliott's Get Ur Freak On, another hit built from very few sounds, and with Kraftwerk's minimal electronic pop."],"billie-eilish-happier-than-ever":["Sixteen songs, nearly an hour, and a deliberate turn from the horror-trap of the debut toward torch song, bossa nova, soft jazz chords and quiet electronic pop. Most of it is sung low and steady, with Finneas's arrangements leaving wide space around the voice. The title track begins as a gentle, almost ukulele-sized guitar lament and then explodes into distorted rock guitar and full-throated singing, the loudest she had ever been on record.","Made largely during the pandemic in Finneas O'Connell's new home studio in Los Angeles and released in July 2021, when Billie Eilish was nineteen. After a debut that had made her a global figure, much of the album reflects on the pressures of early fame and on being watched, and she reworked her public image alongside it. She cited old Hollywood singers and the Great American Songbook as touchstones for the mood.","Listen for how the vocal is recorded differently from the debut: still close, but warmer, with more room and less processing, closer to a 1950s ballad singer than a bedroom whisper. Not My Responsibility is a spoken interlude over ambient synths, a pause before the album's second half. Billie Bossa Nova borrows the rhythm of Brazilian bossa nova without any of its percussion, carried by guitar and voice alone.","Reviewers broadly admired its maturity and restraint, though some missed the jolt and humour of the debut and found the middle stretch too even in tempo. The title track was singled out as a set piece and became a showstopper in concert. Its reputation has settled as the record where the siblings proved they were songwriters as well as sound designers.","Begin with the title track and wait the full five minutes for the switch from whisper to roar. Then try Halley's Comet and My Future to hear the jazz-ballad side. Compare with Billie Holiday's Lady in Satin for an older singer weighing fame and weariness, and with Taylor Swift's Folklore, another pandemic record that turned inward."],"lana-del-rey-video-games":["A slow ballad nearly five minutes long, built on piano, harp glissandi, soft strings and church-like bells, with no real drum beat at all. The voice is low and languid, sliding into notes rather than hitting them, and the melody moves in long sighs. It describes a quiet domestic devotion to someone who barely notices, and the arrangement feels like an old film soundtrack heard from another room.","Lana Del Rey, born Elizabeth Grant, had already released music under her own name with little notice. She wrote this with Justin Parker, and it was produced by Robopop. In 2011 she posted it on YouTube with a homemade video cut from found film clips, old Hollywood footage and her own webcam. It spread online before any label campaign, and it led to her signing with Interscope and Polydor.","The arrangement imitates a lush orchestral studio session from decades earlier, but it was made on a small scale, and the space around the voice is intimate rather than grand. Listen for the bells and the way the harp enters on the chorus like a curtain opening. The absence of drums is the bold choice; nothing marks time except the slow pulse of the chords.","Online audiences treated it as a revelation and critics named it among the songs of 2011. Almost at once came a backlash: arguments about authenticity, her earlier name, her family's money and whether the persona was manufactured. Those debates now look like a stale question about women in pop, and the song is widely heard as the starting point of a whole melancholy, cinematic strand of 2010s pop.","Listen in a quiet room and follow the harp through the song. Notice how little changes and how the tension comes from the voice holding back. Afterwards hear Dusty Springfield's You Don't Have to Say You Love Me for the 1960s orchestral ballad behind it, and Billie Eilish's Ocean Eyes to hear its hush passed to a younger singer."],"lana-del-rey-born-to-die":["Twelve songs that set sweeping, sampled orchestral strings over slow, heavy hip-hop drum programming, with a voice that switches between a low, smoky alto and a girlish upper register. The album paints a dreamlike Americana of highways, motels, old movies and doomed glamour. The title track opens with massed strings and a thudding beat, the template for the whole record: Hollywood score on top, boom-bap underneath.","Released in January 2012 by Interscope and Polydor after Video Games had spread online. Most of it was produced by Emile Haynie, a hip-hop producer, with contributions from Rick Nowels, Justin Parker and others. Lana Del Rey jokingly called herself a gangsta Nancy Sinatra, which captures the mix. Days before release she gave a nervous performance on Saturday Night Live that was savagely criticised, and the album arrived amid an argument about her.","Listen for the drums: they are mixed loud and dry, as on a rap record, while the strings bloom with reverb, so two different acoustic spaces are layered on purpose. The voice is often double-tracked an octave apart, low and high, a trick that gives her the sense of two characters singing at once.","Reviews in 2012 were sharply split; some critics heard a pose rather than a person, and authenticity was the word of the season. Listeners disagreed, and the record stayed in circulation for years. Retrospective judgment has moved strongly in its favour, recognising how its blend of hip-hop production, orchestral nostalgia and a sad, cinematic persona became one of the most copied sounds of the decade.","Start with the title track and Video Games, then Blue Jeans for the twanging, slow guitar. Listen to how the beats sit heavy against the soft strings. Then compare with Portishead's Dummy, an earlier marriage of hip-hop drums and noir orchestration, and with Lorde's Pure Heroine, which took the moody minimalism in another direction."],"lana-del-rey-chemtrails-over-the-country-club":["Eleven songs in about forty-four minutes, mostly gentle piano and acoustic guitar with soft brushed drums, far from the hip-hop beats of her early work. The voice often floats in a breathy upper register and leaves long silences. The songs drift between small-town American scenes, friendship and the wish to step away from fame. The opener, White Dress, is sung almost entirely in a fragile, cracking high voice over sparse keys.","Released in March 2021 and produced with Jack Antonoff, it followed the widely praised Norman Rockwell album and was made partly during the pandemic. Lana Del Rey was by then treated as a senior songwriter whom younger artists cited openly. The album's quieter folk and country colours fit a broader turn of the time, as many pop artists made inward, acoustic records while touring was impossible.","The album ends with a cover of Joni Mitchell's For Free, from Ladies of the Canyon, sung as a trio with Zella Day and Weyes Blood: a statement of lineage, placing herself in the Laurel Canyon singer-songwriter tradition. Listen also to how White Dress pushes her voice to the edge of breaking, a deliberate rawness unusual for a singer whose early persona depended on control.","Critics generally liked its intimacy and craft, though some found it too hushed and uneventful after the scope of its predecessor. Its publicity cycle drew controversy over her public comments, which coloured some reviews. The music itself has worn well, and it is often grouped with Taylor Swift's Folklore as a sign of pop stars turning toward the singer-songwriter album.","Start with the title track for its slow, hazy groove, then White Dress for the fragile high voice. End with the Joni Mitchell cover and then play Mitchell's own Ladies of the Canyon. Listen through good speakers at low volume; this is a record that rewards close attention to small sounds rather than to big gestures."],"lana-del-rey-did-you-know-that-theres-a-tunnel-under-ocean-blvd":["A long, sprawling album of about seventy-eight minutes that moves from gospel-tinged piano ballads to hazy trap beats and back. Songs often abandon verse-and-chorus shape and wander like diary entries, with sudden key changes and abrupt switches of tempo. The opener, The Grants, begins with a small choir of backing singers, and the voice throughout is unusually close and conversational, as if she were thinking aloud at the piano.","Released in March 2023 and produced mainly with Jack Antonoff, Drew Erickson, Zach Dawes and Mike Hermosa. The title refers to a sealed-off tunnel beneath Ocean Boulevard in Long Beach, California, used as an image of something beautiful and forgotten. The album turns repeatedly to family, ageing, faith and legacy. By now Lana Del Rey was regarded as one of the defining American songwriters of her generation.","Two interludes are not songs at all: one is a sermon by the pastor Judah Smith, the other a short piano improvisation credited to Jon Batiste. They frame the record as something between a church service and a family album. A&W, the long central track, starts as a quiet guitar confession and halfway through switches into a heavy trap beat, two songs welded together.","Critics were mostly enthusiastic, praising its ambition and its loose, digressive songwriting; some found it overlong and the interludes self-indulgent. A&W in particular was discussed as one of her boldest pieces of structure. It is often now cited as her most personal record and evidence that a pop career could deepen rather than repeat itself.","Do not try to take it in at one sitting. Start with The Grants, then the title track for its slow piano and strings, then A&W and wait for the change of beat halfway through. Compare with Nina Simone's In Concert for another singer at the piano mixing gospel and confession, and with Frank Ocean's Blonde for its loose song forms."],"lizzo-cuz-i-love-you":["A short, punchy album of about half an hour that fuses gospel belting, Minneapolis funk, rap verses and brassy pop. The title track opens like a 1960s soul ballad with a huge held note, then lurches into a stomping horn chart. Elsewhere Juice rides a bright, disco-funk guitar groove, and Lizzo switches within a single song from a full-chested soul cry to rapid, playful rapping. The theme throughout is self-respect and joy.","Released in April 2019 on Nice Life and Atlantic, with producers including Ricky Reed. Lizzo had spent years in Minneapolis playing in rap and indie groups before going solo, absorbing that city's Prince-shaped funk. She trained as a classical flautist at the University of Houston, and the flute appears on stage and record. Her earlier single Truth Hurts, from 2017, found a large audience at the same time and was added to later editions.","Listen for the flute: it pops up in solos and fills, a classical instrument in a pop record, played by the singer herself with jazz phrasing. The horn arrangements nod deliberately to Stax and to Prince's Minneapolis sound rather than to modern synthetic brass. The title track's opening is tracked as if live, with a deliberately raw, slightly overdriven vocal.","Reviewers loved its energy and charisma and praised her as a singer who could belt like a gospel soloist and rap with comic timing. Some critics found the production too eager and the lyrics too close to slogans of self-help. It won the Grammy for Best Urban Contemporary Album. Debate continues about whether it is a lasting record or a brilliant moment of personality.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the ballad becomes a stomp. Then Juice, for its guitar and handclaps, and Jerome for her ballad singing. Compare with Aretha Franklin's I Never Loved a Man the Way I Love You for the gospel-soul power behind it, and Prince's 1999 for the Minneapolis groove."],"lizzo-special":["Twelve songs in about thirty-five minutes, brighter and more disco-centred than the album before it. Live bass, clipped funk guitar and string sweeps carry most tracks, with a few slower soul ballads. The lead single About Damn Time is built on a strutting bass line and a mid-tempo groove that recalls late 1970s dance records, and it gives Lizzo room for both rap phrasing and a gospel flourish. The mood is celebratory and warm.","Released in July 2022 on Nice Life and Atlantic, with About Damn Time produced by Ricky Reed and Blake Slatkin. It arrived as audiences returned to concerts after the pandemic, and many of the songs were pitched for communal singing. Lizzo had become a mainstream television and festival figure, and the album aimed squarely at broad pop radio while keeping the flute and the funk.","Listen to the bass on About Damn Time: it is played and phrased like a disco session bassist, with short, bouncing notes and slides, rather than programmed. A brief flute solo is slipped into the song's middle, almost as a signature. Several songs reference older records openly; the album is built from the sound world of Chic and Nile Rodgers far more than from contemporary trap.","Reviewers found it polished and joyful, though many judged it safer than Cuz I Love You and lighter in personality. About Damn Time won the Grammy for Record of the Year. The album is often discussed as part of a 2020s revival of disco and funk sounds in pop, alongside Beyoncé's Renaissance.","Start with About Damn Time and follow the bass line from start to finish. Then hear Chic's Good Times to hear where the groove comes from, and Beyoncé's Renaissance for another 2022 album rooted in disco and dance history. Play it loud and in company; it was designed for singing along in a crowd."],"bad-bunny-x-100pre":["A debut album of fifteen tracks that refuses to stay inside Latin trap, the style that made Bad Bunny's name. There are sombre, slow trap songs with deep 808 bass, a Dominican dembow track with El Alfa, a duet with Drake, and Tenemos Que Hablar, which turns suddenly into a guitar-driven pop-punk song. The low, nasal baritone and the habit of drawling the ends of lines are already unmistakable.","Released on Christmas Eve 2018 on Rimas Entertainment, with Tainy and other Puerto Rican producers. Bad Bunny had risen through singles uploaded to SoundCloud while working bagging groceries in Vega Baja, and he had already appeared on many hits as a guest. Latin trap had grown from Puerto Rico into a continental movement, and the album was his statement that he would not be confined by it.","Listen to Estamos Bien for the restraint: a slow trap beat, almost no melody instruments, and a voice sitting in front of space. The pop-punk turn on Tenemos Que Hablar was a deliberate shock, with distorted guitars and live-sounding drums that come from American emo rather than any Latin tradition. The album title is a slang spelling meaning forever.","It was widely praised in the Latin and English-language press as a more inventive debut than expected from a singles artist. Critics pointed to its genre-crossing and its emotional openness. It is now heard as the first statement of an artist who would make Spanish-language pop a global centre without crossing into English.","Start with Estamos Bien, then Tenemos Que Hablar, and listen to how the same voice works over trap and over punk guitars. Then hear his later Un Verano Sin Ti for how the genre-mixing expanded. For older roots, Héctor Lavoe's El Cantante shows another Puerto Rican voice whose personality outran any genre."],"bad-bunny-yhlqmdlg":["Twenty tracks, over an hour, almost all built on the dembow rhythm that drives reggaeton: a thumping kick and a skipping snare pattern descended from Jamaican dancehall. The album is a celebration of the reggaeton of Bad Bunny's childhood, with sirens, air horns and the busy, sample-heavy textures of early-2000s Puerto Rican club records, alongside slower, moodier songs. The title abbreviates a Spanish phrase meaning I do whatever I want.","Released at the end of February 2020, just before pandemic lockdowns closed the clubs it was made for. Produced with Tainy and other Puerto Rican collaborators on Rimas Entertainment, it features Daddy Yankee, the genre's founding star, on La Santa. Bad Bunny had become a political figure the previous summer by joining the street protests in San Juan that forced the island's governor to resign.","The album is full of homage: beats and textures modelled on the DJ mixtapes and underground cassettes of 1990s and early-2000s San Juan, the era when reggaeton was being shaped. Listen for the abrupt beat switches within songs, a mixtape habit in which a track changes rhythm halfway as if a DJ had cut to the next record.","Critics praised it as a joyful, history-minded reggaeton record and noted its scale; some felt twenty tracks was too many. It won the Latin Grammy for Best Urban Music Album and has since been ranked among the defining reggaeton albums. It is often discussed for restoring respect to the genre's Puerto Rican roots at the moment of its global spread.","Pick out three or four tracks rather than playing it straight through; start with Vete and La Santa. Listen for the dembow pattern under almost everything, and the way small changes in the snare make each song feel different. Then compare with Juan Luis Guerra's Bachata Rosa for another Caribbean dance form made into an album."],"bad-bunny-un-verano-sin-ti":["Twenty-three tracks, eighty minutes, conceived as a Caribbean summer: reggaeton and dembow sit beside bachata, mambo, indie pop, synth-pop and bossa nova touches. Moscow Mule opens with a beach-wave sound and a slow reggaeton groove; Tití Me Preguntó switches between a dembow and a bachata section; Después de la Playa erupts into a full horn-driven mambo; El Apagón, about Puerto Rico's electricity failures and displacement, becomes a dance-music protest.","Released in May 2022 on Rimas Entertainment, produced with MAG, Tainy, La Paciencia and others. Bad Bunny conceived it as a record for beaches and summer nights across the Caribbean, and he brought in guests from the Dominican Republic, Puerto Rico, Colombia and the American indie scene, including The Marías and Buscabulla. Its scale and its insistence on Spanish made it a landmark for Latin music on the global stage.","Listen for the horn section on Después de la Playa: real brass playing a 1950s-style mambo, the dance of Pérez Prado and Tito Puente, dropped into the middle of a reggaeton album. The Marías duet Otro Atardecer is closer to dreamy American synth-pop than to anything Caribbean, a deliberate widening of the palette.","It received broad critical acclaim and was nominated for the Grammy for Album of the Year, the first entirely Spanish-language album to be so honoured. Critics praised its range and coherence; some found its length unwieldy. It is now treated as the album that made a Spanish-language Caribbean record a shared global pop event.","Start with Moscow Mule and Después de la Playa, and listen for the moment the mambo horns arrive. Then El Apagón for the protest, and Otro Atardecer for the indie-pop turn. Compare with Rubén Blades's Siembra for an earlier landmark of Latin pop with a social conscience, and with Celia Cruz for the older dance traditions it echoes."],"bad-bunny-debi-tirar-mas-fotos":["Seventeen tracks in about an hour that mix reggaeton and dembow with the traditional music of Puerto Rico: plena, bomba, jíbaro music with its cuatro guitar, and big-band salsa. Baile Inolvidable is a full salsa with piano montuno and a horn section; the title track joins a reggaeton beat to plena drums and a group chorus. The album's subject is memory, home and the fear of losing a place and its people.","Released in January 2025 on Rimas Entertainment, it was made as a love letter to Puerto Rico at a time of gentrification, tourism pressure and emigration. Bad Bunny worked with young Puerto Rican musicians, including students from a local music school who play the salsa sections. He followed it with a long residency of concerts in San Juan before taking it abroad, bringing audiences to the island.","Listen to the panderos, the hand drums of plena, under the title track: their interlocking rhythms are older than reggaeton by a century. The cuatro, a small ten-string Puerto Rican guitar, appears in the jíbaro-flavoured passages. The salsa tracks are arranged in the classic 1970s manner of the Fania era, with brass mambo sections and a piano vamp, played by a young live band rather than sampled.","Critics greeted it as his most personal and musically rooted album, praising its use of traditional Puerto Rican forms inside modern production. Some English-language reviewers admitted they were hearing plena and bomba for the first time through it. It is already discussed as a model for how a global star can bring a local tradition into the mainstream without diluting it.","Start with Baile Inolvidable and listen for the piano vamp and the horn breaks. Then the title track for the plena drums. Follow it with Héctor Lavoe and Willie Colón's records or the Fania All Stars' Live at Yankee Stadium to hear the salsa it honours, and with Celia Cruz for its Caribbean ancestors."],"rosalia-los-angeles":["A stark duo record: Rosalía's voice and Raül Refree's guitar, nothing else. The songs are mostly traditional flamenco cantes and older popular songs, reworked with long silences, slow tempos and unexpected harmonies. Her singing uses the bent notes, ornaments and sudden cries of flamenco but keeps a clear, almost fragile tone. The album circles the theme of death, and its atmosphere is closer to a candlelit vigil than a flamenco tablao.","Released in February 2017 by Universal Music Spain, it came out while Rosalía was still completing her flamenco studies in Barcelona. Refree, a Barcelona producer and guitarist from the indie and experimental scene, was not a flamenco guitarist by training, which was the point. Purists and outsiders were both drawn in. It won her a reputation as a serious new voice in flamenco.","Listen to the guitar: Refree often plays chords and textures no flamenco guitarist would choose, with open, ringing strings, quiet distortion and odd harmonic turns instead of the usual rasgueado strumming. That outsider harmony is what makes old songs sound newly strange. The album's title evokes angels as messengers of death rather than the Californian city.","It was admired by critics and by many in the flamenco world for its seriousness, though some traditionalists questioned the liberties taken with the cante. It is now heard as the foundation for El Mal Querer: proof that Rosalía knew the tradition from inside before she transformed it. Debate about her right to reshape flamenco, as a Catalan singer outside Gitano lineages, began here.","Listen in a dark room, and notice how much silence surrounds each phrase. Follow the voice's ornaments in the slow songs and the moments when the guitar holds a single ringing note. Then compare with Camarón's Al verte las flores lloran for traditional cante, and Enrique Morente's Omega for an earlier, radical rethinking of flamenco."],"rosalia-el-mal-querer":["Eleven tracks in about thirty minutes, each titled as a chapter, telling the story of a jealous, possessive love from courtship to release. Flamenco forms supply the bones: the clapped palmas, the compás rhythms, the melismatic cries. Around them sit trap beats, sub-bass, Auto-Tune, choirs and samples. Malamente, the first single, is built almost entirely from palmas, a deep bass and her voice, and it became the album's calling card.","Rosalía conceived it as her graduation project at the Escola Superior de Música de Catalunya, inspired by Flamenca, an anonymous thirteenth-century Occitan romance about a jealous husband. She co-produced it with El Guincho, a Canary Islands producer, and released it on Sony in November 2018. It won the Latin Grammy for Album of the Year and made her one of the most watched artists in the world.","Bagdad interpolates the melody of Justin Timberlake's Cry Me a River and sets it inside a church-like choral setting with organ. On De Aquí No Sales, the roar of motorcycle engines is used as percussion, chopped into the rhythm. Listen also to how the palmas are often recorded close and dry, then layered, so a handclap pattern becomes as hard as a drum machine.","Critics hailed it as a reinvention of flamenco for the streaming era; many called it the best album of 2018. Some flamenco purists objected strongly, and there was a sustained debate over whether a non-Gitano Catalan singer was appropriating Andalusian Romani culture. The argument has not ended, but the record's musical achievement is broadly accepted, and it opened doors for experimental flamenco.","Start with Malamente and listen for how the palmas lock together with the bass. Then Pienso en Tu Mirá and Bagdad. Play it straight through to hear the chapter structure. Compare with Camarón's La leyenda del tiempo, the 1979 album that first brought rock and electric instruments into flamenco, and with Paco de Lucía's Almoraima."],"rosalia-motomami":["Sixteen short tracks in about forty-two minutes that swerve between reggaeton, bachata, bare piano ballads, jazzy harmonies, industrial noise and flamenco. Saoko opens with distorted bass and stomping drums; La Fama, with The Weeknd, is a languid bachata; Como un G is just voice and piano; Candy floats over a ghostly, crackling UK garage beat. The theme is transformation and the double life of fame, of strength and tenderness.","Released in March 2022 by Columbia, three years after El Mal Querer had made her a star. Rosalía had moved partly to the United States and spent time in Miami and Los Angeles, among reggaeton and American hip-hop producers. The album reflects that move: it trades the single-story structure of El Mal Querer for a collage. She toured it with a stage show filmed vertically for phones, as if live television.","Candy is built on a sample of Burial's Archangel, the London producer's ghostly garage track from Untrue, so a south London night-bus sound sits under a Spanish pop song. Saoko takes its title from a reggaeton classic by Daddy Yankee and Wisin, nodding to the genre's history. The closing track, Sakura, is framed with the sound of a live audience, a brief, intimate coda after so much processing.","It drew broad acclaim, and many critics placed it among the best albums of 2022, praising its daring and its fragmented form. Some fans of the flamenco Rosalía felt she had abandoned the tradition, and some reviewers found it scattered. It won the Latin Grammy for Album of the Year. The conversation now is about how her collage approach reflects the habits of listening on phones.","Start with Candy and then Burial's Archangel to hear the sample. Then try La Fama for bachata and Como un G for voice and piano. Expect sharp cuts; part of the pleasure is the jolt. Compare with Björk's Homogenic for another singer combining folk roots with electronics, and with Frank Ocean's Blonde for its fragmentary form."],"rosalia-lux":["An album in four movements for voice, full orchestra and choir, sung in many languages, including Spanish, Catalan, Latin, German, Italian, Japanese and Arabic. Strings and choral writing carry much of the record, with electronics and beats appearing like interruptions. The voice moves between operatic projection, flamenco ornament and plain speech. The subject is spiritual searching, drawing on the lives and writings of female mystics and saints from many traditions.","Released in November 2025 by Columbia, after a long silence following Motomami. Rosalía recorded with the London Symphony Orchestra and the boys' choir of the Escolanía de Montserrat, the Catalan monastery choir. She studied the languages she sings and spent years on the project. Berghain, the first single, features Björk and Yves Tumor. It was received as a deliberate retreat from the fast cycle of streaming pop.","Listen for the Montserrat boys' choir, whose sound connects the record to one of the oldest continuous choral traditions in Europe. In Berghain, the orchestral strings play short, driving repeated figures that recall minimalist concert music, while Björk's voice enters in a register far from Rosalía's own. The multilingual texts mean that many listeners hear the voice purely as sound and gesture.","Early reviews were overwhelmingly enthusiastic, praising its ambition and its seriousness, and many critics named it among the albums of the year. Some found its scale and language-switching overwhelming. Its place in her career, and in pop more widely, is still being argued, particularly whether it opens a path for pop artists to work at symphonic scale.","Start with Berghain, then play one movement straight through. Listen for the choir and the moments when beats break through the orchestra. Compare with Björk's Vespertine for a pop singer writing for choir and harp, and with Arvo Pärt-style sacred minimalism or Monteverdi's Vespers for the older choral sound it reaches toward."],"lorde-solar-power":["Twelve songs in about forty-three minutes, almost all built on strummed acoustic guitars, soft bass and light drums, with gentle harmony vocals. After the stormy synths of Melodrama, it is calm and sunny, closer to early 1970s California soft rock than to 2010s pop. The title track slowly gathers into a sing-along guitar groove. The songs turn away from celebrity toward nature, the sea and wellness culture, often with a wry smile.","Released in August 2021 and produced with Jack Antonoff, after Lorde had stepped away from public life in New Zealand for several years. Phoebe Bridgers and Clairo sing backing vocals on several songs. Concerned about waste, she chose not to release it on CD, offering instead a music box: a small physical object with a download card and artwork. The pandemic shaped its mood of retreat and reflection.","Listen to the layered backing harmonies: Bridgers and Clairo are used like a 1970s vocal group, singing soft, close parts rather than featured verses. Many of the songs are in fact gently satirical about wellness and self-help, a tone that sits under the sunny surface. The production is deliberately dry and uncluttered, avoiding the huge drums of her earlier work.","Reviews were mixed: some critics found it slight and its satire unclear, others admired its quiet and its refusal to repeat Melodrama. Many fans were initially disappointed. Its reputation has risen as listeners hear it as a deliberate rest and a portrait of withdrawal, though it remains her most debated album.","Listen outdoors or with windows open. Start with the title track, then the gentler The Path and Fallen Fruit. Notice the backing harmonies and the warm, unfussy guitars. Compare with Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark and Fleetwood Mac's Rumours for its California soft-rock ancestors."],"lorde-te-ao-marama":["A five-song EP of about eighteen minutes in which Lorde re-sings songs from Solar Power in te reo Māori, the Māori language of Aotearoa New Zealand. The backing tracks are largely those of the album, so the guitars and harmonies are familiar, but the new words change the rhythm and colour of every line. Long vowels and different stresses reshape the melodies, and some phrases are sung with new ornament.","Released in September 2021 alongside Solar Power. Lorde, who is Pākehā, of European descent, worked with Māori translators and language experts, including the scholar Sir Tīmoti Kāretu, to render the songs. The project came during a revival of te reo Māori in New Zealand public life, and she described it as a way of learning and showing respect rather than claiming the language.","Compare a song here with its English version and listen for how the melody bends to fit the language: vowel sounds stretched, syllables redistributed, small rhythmic changes. That is translation as recomposition. The title means the world of light, linking the EP to the sun imagery of the album it reworks.","It was welcomed warmly in New Zealand by many Māori listeners and language advocates, while some debated whether a Pākehā star should be the voice of such a project. Most critics outside New Zealand saw it as a gentle, sincere companion to the album. Its long-term importance lies in visibility for te reo Māori in global pop.","Play a song in its English version on Solar Power, then the Māori version here, and listen for the changes in rhythm and phrasing. Notice how unfamiliar words make you hear the voice as pure sound. Compare with any recording of Māori choral singing or waiata to hear the tradition of the language in song."],"caroline-polachek-pang":["Fifteen songs of shimmering, glassy electronic pop built around one of the most agile voices in pop. Caroline Polachek leaps between registers with quick vocal flips, almost like a yodel, and decorates lines with ornaments closer to early music than to R&B runs. Synths shimmer and pulse, beats are crisp but light, and melodies take unexpected turns. Door, Pang and Ocean of Tears give the range from slow and dreamy to brightly danceable.","Released in October 2019 on Perpetual Novice and Columbia, after the end of Chairlift, the Brooklyn duo with which she had made several albums. She co-produced it largely with Danny L Harle, a London producer associated with the PC Music circle, which linked her with the experimental pop scene around A. G. Cook and Charli XCX. The album grew from a sudden flood of feeling after a personal upheaval.","Listen to the vocal leaps: she often snaps from chest voice to a pure head voice in the middle of a word, a deliberate break with roots in folk singing and yodelling rather than pop. Danny L Harle often layers several synth sounds on one line, giving the shimmer. The record ends with Parachute, a near-ambient coda where the voice floats with almost no beat.","Critics greeted it warmly, and many placed it in year-end lists; it built a passionate following that grew over the next years. Some found it too delicate at first hearing. It is now heard as the founding document of her solo career and as a bridge between experimental electronic pop and the mainstream.","Start with Door, then the title track, and listen for the vocal flips. Wear headphones to catch the layered synths. Compare with Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for another singer whose voice is an instrument of surprise, and Cocteau Twins' Treasure for the shimmering, ornamented vocal tradition behind it."],"caroline-polachek-desire-i-want-to-turn-into-you":["Twelve songs in about thirty-nine minutes, wider in colour and more euphoric than Pang. Welcome to My Island opens with a long wordless cry; Sunset brings in flamenco-flavoured Spanish guitar and handclaps; Blood and Butter is a pastoral song that ends with a solo on Scottish smallpipes; Fly to You joins her with Grimes and Dido. Throughout, the voice soars and ornaments over rich, sometimes trip-hop, sometimes folk-tinged production.","Released in February 2023 on Perpetual Novice, co-produced chiefly with Danny L Harle and made between Los Angeles and Europe. Several songs appeared as singles over the preceding years, and the record was shaped partly during the pandemic. Polachek's profile had risen sharply, and she toured widely with the album. The title points to longing as a force that transforms rather than possesses.","On Blood and Butter, the solo at the end is played on Scottish smallpipes by the piper Brighde Chaimbeul, a folk instrument dropped into a pop production with no apology. Sunset uses Spanish guitar and palmas, a nod toward the flamenco revival around Rosalía. The final track, Billions, ends with a children's choir.","It received wide acclaim and appeared near the top of many 2023 year-end lists; critics praised its ambition and range. Some felt it lacked the emotional unity of Pang. It is often now cited as one of the strongest art-pop records of the decade, and as evidence that experimental pop could find a large audience.","Start with Welcome to My Island for the opening cry, then Sunset and Blood and Butter, and wait for the smallpipes. Compare with Kate Bush's The Dreaming for another singer collaging folk instruments and electronics, and Björk's Homogenic for strings and beats in dialogue. Play it loud."],"charli-xcx-how-im-feeling-now":["Eleven tracks of jagged, distorted, euphoric electronic pop, made fast and sounding it. Beats clatter, synths are overdriven until they buzz, and Charli XCX's voice is pitched, stretched and chopped by Auto-Tune into another instrument. Claws is a rush of bubblegum melody over grinding synths; Forever floats on a warped, aching chord; Anthems is a frantic dance track. The theme is life in lockdown, with all its boredom, longing and intimacy.","Made in about six weeks during the first COVID-19 lockdown in Los Angeles and released in May 2020 by Atlantic. Charli XCX announced the project and shared stages of the work in livestreams, asking fans to send ideas, artwork and video. Her producers included A. G. Cook, BJ Burton, Dylan Brady of 100 gecs and Danny L Harle. It was one of the first albums made openly in response to the pandemic.","Listen for distortion used as feeling: the synths on Forever are deliberately clipped and crackling, so a tender song sounds like it is breaking. Many tracks keep the rough edges of quick demos rather than polishing them away. The vocal processing is often stacked so heavily that her voice becomes a choir of glitches, a hallmark of the hyperpop circle around A. G. Cook.","Critics praised its immediacy and emotional honesty; many listed it among the best albums of 2020. Some found it too abrasive or slight. It is now heard as an important bridge between Pop 2 and Brat, and as a key text of hyperpop's moment of crossover.","Start with Claws and Forever, and listen for how harsh sounds carry sweet melodies. Then play Pop 2 to hear the earlier, mixtape version of this style, and Brat to hear where it went. Use headphones but keep the volume reasonable; the distortion is part of the music."],"charli-xcx-brat-and-its-completely-different-but-also-still-brat":["A remix album that rebuilds nearly every song of Brat with guest artists, new verses, new beats and often entirely new production. Some remixes are brutal club reworkings; others slow songs down or change their meaning through a guest's perspective. Girl, So Confusing becomes a conversation with Lorde; Everything Is Romantic adds Caroline Polachek; I Think About It All the Time brings in Bon Iver's processed falsetto. The sound swings between grinding electronic club tracks and soft ballads.","Released in October 2024 by Atlantic, a few months after Brat had become a summer-long cultural event. Charli XCX and A. G. Cook used the remix album as a sequel, inviting artists including Lorde, Billie Eilish, Ariana Grande, Troye Sivan and Caroline Polachek. The idea drew on club culture, where tracks exist in many versions, and on the habit of fans remixing songs online.","Listen to how the remixes change the original songs' emotional angle: the Lorde version of Girl, So Confusing turns a one-sided song into a real dialogue, as she adds her own verse responding to Charli. Several remixes keep only the vocal and replace every instrument. The record functions as a collective portrait of the pop scene in late 2024.","Critics praised its ambition and called it an unusually creative remix album; several remixes were singled out as improvements on the originals. Others found it uneven, as remix albums tend to be. It is now part of the story of Brat as an event, and it is discussed as a model for how albums can live on in versions.","Play a song from Brat, then its remix here, and listen for what the new production adds or strips away. Start with Girl, So Confusing with Lorde. Then compare with Daft Punk's Discovery for an album rooted in club remix culture, and with Robyn's Body Talk for its pop-club ancestry."],"randy-travis-storms-of-life":["Ten songs, barely more than half an hour, played by a Nashville session band with fiddle, pedal steel, piano and a light, brushed rhythm section. Nothing is crowded: each arrangement leaves a wide space in the middle for Randy Travis's baritone. The hits were On the Other Hand, a song about a married man tempted by another woman, Diggin' Up Bones, 1982 and No Place Like Home. The sound you remember is that voice settling onto the lowest note of a line and holding it, dry and calm.","By the mid-1980s Nashville had chased the Urban Cowboy craze into soft pop, and country sales were falling. Warner Bros. signed Travis in 1985 after years of club work, and producer Kyle Lehning gave him a deliberately old-fashioned setting. On the Other Hand flopped when first released, then was reissued after 1982 became a hit and went to number one. The album sold in huge numbers, and record companies began scouting for other young traditionalists.","Listen to how little the band does behind the verses: the steel guitar often plays only a few sustained notes, and the drums barely touch the snare, so tiny details in Travis's phrasing, a breath or a slide into a note, become the drama. The title song, written by others, gave the album its weather-beaten frame. The comic Diggin' Up Bones uses the same honky-tonk grammar as the sad songs, which is the whole lesson of the record.","Critics welcomed Storms of Life as a return to the sound of George Jones and Lefty Frizzell, and it has kept that reputation as the album that opened the neotraditional era. Some reviewers at the time found it too reverent, a museum piece. In hindsight its restraint sounds more radical than nostalgic, and later generations of traditionalists, from Alan Jackson to Chris Stapleton, are routinely measured against it.","Start with On the Other Hand and listen only to the voice: notice where Travis stays soft when a pop singer would push. Then play 1982 and Diggin' Up Bones to hear how the same band shifts from regret to comedy. Follow it with George Jones's He Stopped Loving Her Today to hear the model, and with Alan Jackson's Chattahoochee to hear what came next."],"randy-travis-forever-and-ever-amen":["A mid-tempo love song, in which a man promises lifelong devotion, set to a loping shuffle beat with fiddle and steel guitar trading short phrases between the lines. Travis sings the verses almost conversationally and saves his deepest, lowest note for the end of the chorus, where the title lands on a held word that drops into his chest. The arrangement is clean and bright, with a brief fiddle and steel break in the middle, and the whole track runs about three and a half minutes.","The song was written by Paul Overstreet and Don Schlitz, two of Nashville's most successful songwriters of the 1980s, and released as the lead single from Travis's second album, Always and Forever, in 1987. It went to number one on the country chart and won awards from the Country Music Association and the Academy of Country Music, and the album became one of the biggest country sellers of the decade, confirming that the neotraditional sound was a commercial force.","Listen to the very last chorus: Travis lets the bottom note of the title phrase sink lower and stay longer than before, the one place he allows himself a showpiece. The song's homely images, a man promising to love his wife even as they grow old, are drawn from everyday courtship language, and the gentle humour keeps it from turning sentimental. Its title borrows the closing words of a prayer, which is why it became a wedding favourite.","The song was hugely popular on release and has become one of the standard country wedding songs. Critics have sometimes called it corny, but its craft, the light humour, the rhyme scheme and the economy of the arrangement, is widely admired by songwriters. It is usually named as Travis's signature recording, and it featured in tributes after his 2013 stroke.","Listen for the drop at the end of each chorus and count how low Travis goes. Notice the fiddle answering him between lines, like a second voice in the conversation. Compare with Patsy Cline's Crazy for a classic Nashville love song sung with the same restraint, then with George Strait's Amarillo by Morning for another traditionalist of the same era."],"dwight-yoakam-guitars-cadillacs-etc-etc":["Ten songs in half an hour, played fast and hard by a small band built around twangy Telecaster guitar, pedal steel and a crisp, driving backbeat. Yoakam's high tenor has a catch and hiccup that recalls rockabilly. Alongside his own songs, including the title track about a young singer stranded in the city and the sorrowful It Won't Hurt, he covers Johnny Horton's Honky Tonk Man and Johnny Cash's Ring of Fire. The recognisable sound is that bright, trebly guitar answering every line.","Rejected by Nashville labels, Yoakam built a following in Los Angeles, playing honky-tonk in punk and roots-rock clubs. With guitarist Pete Anderson producing, he self-released an EP in 1984 that drew attention; Reprise signed him and expanded it into this debut album in 1986. Honky Tonk Man became a country hit, and Yoakam, alongside Randy Travis and Steve Earle, became a face of the so-called new traditionalists, though he came from the opposite coast and a rock audience.","The sound is a deliberate homage to the 1960s Bakersfield records of Buck Owens and his guitarist Don Rich: listen for the clipped, ringing guitar fills and the hard snare, a California answer to Nashville's smoother strings. Yoakam's Ring of Fire is played faster and harder than Johnny Cash's, without the famous mariachi trumpets. The album's punk-club origins show in its length and pace: no song lingers past what it needs.","Critics loved the record's energy and its open debt to Bakersfield, and it sold strongly, reaching the top of the country album chart. Some Nashville insiders were uneasy about Yoakam's rock audience and his outspoken criticism of the industry. It is now regarded as one of the essential country debuts of the decade and a bridge between roots rock and country.","Play Honky Tonk Man first for the sheer drive, then the title track for Yoakam's own writing. Listen for the guitar fills between his lines; they are as much a part of the song as the voice. Then compare Johnny Cash's Ring of Fire with this version, and follow with Steve Earle's Guitar Town, released the same year with a similar spirit."],"dwight-yoakam-streets-of-bakersfield":["A duet between Dwight Yoakam and his hero Buck Owens, in which a drifter tells the locals who scorn him that he has come a long way and means to stay. The beat is a brisk, two-step shuffle with a Tex-Mex flavour: accordion rides alongside the twangy guitars, and the two voices alternate verses before joining in harmony. Owens's older, rounder voice and Yoakam's sharp, high tenor make a clear contrast, like a father and son trading lines.","The song was written by Homer Joy and first recorded by Owens in 1973. Owens had largely retired from recording when Yoakam, who had spent years championing him in interviews, persuaded him back into the studio for this duet on Yoakam's 1988 album Buenas Noches from a Lonely Room. The single went to number one on the country chart, a moment of public recognition for Owens and for the Bakersfield sound he had built in the 1960s away from Nashville.","The accordion is the clue to place: Bakersfield sits in California's Central Valley, where Mexican-American conjunto music was part of the local soundscape, and the arrangement nods to it. Listen for how the two men divide the song; the harmony on the chorus is pitched so that Owens takes the familiar Bakersfield high harmony role that his guitarist Don Rich once sang beside him. The song is a small act of homage as much as a single.","The record was celebrated as a passing of the torch and helped revive interest in Owens's catalogue, which had been out of print and neglected. Some critics thought Yoakam's tribute bordered on hero worship, but most heard a genuine partnership. It remains one of the best-loved duets of the era and a key document of the Bakersfield revival.","Listen first for the accordion, then for the handover of verses between the two singers, and notice how Owens sounds relaxed and Yoakam slightly urgent. Then go back to Yoakam's Guitars, Cadillacs, Etc., Etc. for the younger man alone, and to Merle Haggard's Mama Tried for the other great Bakersfield voice."],"kd-lang-shadowland":["A collection of torch songs and country ballads in the lush early-1960s Nashville style: strings, a soft vocal chorus, brushed drums, piano and steel guitar laid down like velvet beneath k.d. lang's enormous alto. She sings old songs such as Lock, Stock and Teardrops and Western Stars with long, perfectly controlled lines that swell and fade. The album closes with the Honky Tonk Angels Medley, sung with three country legends. The texture you remember is the voice floating above a cushion of strings.","Lang had made her name in Canada as a punkish, theatrical country performer who idolised Patsy Cline. For this album she persuaded Owen Bradley, the producer who had made Cline's classic records and who had stepped back from the studio, to produce her in Nashville. Bradley assembled veteran players and arrangements in his old style. The medley brought in Loretta Lynn, Kitty Wells and Brenda Lee, an endorsement from the generation lang had grown up imitating.","The record is a kind of time machine: Bradley recreated the Nashville Sound he had built around Cline, so listen for the soft vocal chorus behind lang, a hallmark of his early-1960s productions. Lang's phrasing is more daring than her models, though; she holds notes far longer and lets the vibrato arrive only at the very end. The medley also quietly maps the history of women in country across three generations.","The album was admired by critics and by Nashville traditionalists, and Lynn, Wells and Lee's presence lent it legitimacy. Some at the time wondered whether lang, with her androgynous image and punk background, was being ironic; the singing settled that question. It is now seen as a bridge between the classic Nashville Sound and the torch-pop voice she developed on Ingénue.","Start with the title song or Western Stars and listen to how long lang holds the final notes without the pitch wavering. Then play Patsy Cline's I Fall to Pieces, an earlier Bradley production, to hear the model. Finish with the Honky Tonk Angels Medley and try to tell the four voices apart."],"kd-lang-ingenue":["Ten slow, intimate songs about longing and unrequited love, set in hushed arrangements of acoustic guitar, accordion, vibraphone-like keyboards, soft strings and gentle percussion. The country instruments have mostly gone; what remains is cabaret, torch song and a hint of Eastern European folk in the accordion. Constant Craving, a song about a desire that never settles, is the best-known track. The sound is lang's alto at close range, often sung near a whisper, then opening up to full power.","After Shadowland and Absolute Torch and Twang, lang felt constrained by country radio, which had grown wary of her after an animal-rights advertisement offended the beef industry. She wrote Ingénue with her collaborator Ben Mink, and the songs were reportedly inspired by an unrequited attachment. Released in 1992 on Sire, it became her best-selling album. Constant Craving won a Grammy for best female pop vocal, and lang came out publicly in the same year.","Listen to the accordion and the way it sometimes plays slow, rocking figures that sound more like a Parisian café or a Ukrainian village than a Nashville studio; Mink's arrangements quietly borrow from European folk. The songs are written to avoid gendered pronouns, which lets them speak to anyone. The final track, Constant Craving, works as the album's resolution: after nine songs of yearning, the craving is named and accepted.","Ingénue was a critical and commercial success and turned lang from a country curiosity into an international pop star. Country fans who had loved Shadowland were sometimes disappointed by the move away. Its reputation has held: it is regarded as one of the defining adult pop records of the early 1990s, and lang's vocal control on it is still cited by singers of every genre.","Listen at low volume and late at night, as the songs intend. Start with Miss Chatelaine for its playful waltz, then Constant Craving, noticing how lang builds from a hush to full voice. Compare her later cover of Leonard Cohen's Hallelujah, and Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark for another singer who left a genre's boundaries behind."],"george-strait-amarillo-by-morning":["A rodeo cowboy drives through the night toward his next competition, having lost his wife and his money to the life, and says he would choose it again. The song opens with a lonely, slow fiddle melody that becomes the track's signature, then settles into a relaxed, swaying tempo with steel guitar and acoustic guitar. Strait sings it plainly, with no strain, as if reporting facts. The fiddle returns at the end, and the effect is of a long empty highway.","The song was written by Terry Stafford and Paul Fraser and first recorded by Stafford in 1973. Strait, who had grown up on a Texas ranch and knew the rodeo world, recorded it for his 1982 album Strait from the Heart and released it as a single in 1983. It peaked just short of the top of the country chart, but it became one of the most requested songs of his career and a fixture of Texas dance halls.","Listen to the opening fiddle: its long, slow melody is closer to a western cowboy song or an old Texas waltz than to a honky-tonk number, and it sets the time and place before a word is sung. Strait's restraint is the trick: the narrator describes broken bones and losses without self-pity, and the absence of any vocal drama makes the song sadder. Amarillo itself is a real stop on the Texas rodeo circuit.","Although it did not reach number one, the song has outlasted many of Strait's chart-toppers and regularly appears near the top of lists of the greatest country songs. Critics treat it as the clearest statement of his style: Texas plainness, steady singing and a great song left to speak for itself. Rodeo cowboys have long adopted it as an anthem.","Wait for the fiddle introduction and let it set the scene before the voice arrives. Notice how evenly Strait sings even the painful lines. Then try Willie Nelson's Red Headed Stranger for another Texan song about the solitary western life, and Garth Brooks's No Fences to hear how Strait's style was scaled up a decade later."],"george-strait-pure-country":["The soundtrack to the film in which Strait plays a country star who walks away from his overblown stage show to rediscover simple music. Its ten songs move between bright Texas two-steps like Heartland, gentle love ballads like I Cross My Heart, and slow, aching honky-tonk numbers. Fiddle and steel guitar carry the arrangements, and Strait's smooth baritone sits easily in front. The record you remember is I Cross My Heart, a wedding ballad sung with quiet conviction.","Pure Country was released in 1992 alongside the Warner Bros. film of the same name, Strait's only leading role. The story, a star tired of smoke machines and spectacle, was a pointed comment on country's growing arena excess in the early 1990s, the era of Garth Brooks. The film was a modest success, but the album became the biggest-selling record of Strait's long career, and I Cross My Heart reached number one on the country chart.","The film's plot and the album quietly argue with the country business of the moment: the songs heard in the movie's flashy concert scenes are performed with louder, rock-style production than the plain recordings that close the story. Listen for that contrast between Heartland and the quieter tracks. Strait, who barely acts on stage, was essentially playing a version of his own stated values.","Critics were lukewarm about the film but generous about the music, and the album has kept selling for decades. I Cross My Heart became one of the most played wedding songs in country music. Some reviewers noted the irony of a multimillion-selling star preaching simplicity, but the album is widely regarded as a high point of Strait's 1990s work.","Start with Heartland for the energy, then I Cross My Heart for the ballad style, and listen for the fiddle and steel answering each other. Then compare Garth Brooks's No Fences to hear the arena approach the film gently criticises, and Alan Jackson's Don't Rock the Jukebox for a kindred defence of honky-tonk."],"garth-brooks-no-fences":["Ten songs that range from barroom singalong to stormy drama and quiet ballad, all played by a Nashville session band with fiddle and steel guitar but recorded with a punch and width that recall rock radio. Friends in Low Places is the rowdy centrepiece; Unanswered Prayers is a gentle story about being grateful a youthful wish never came true; The Thunder Rolls is a dark narrative about a betrayed wife, with real thunder in the mix. Brooks's warm baritone carries everything.","Brooks's 1989 debut had made him a rising star; No Fences, produced by Allen Reynolds and released by Capitol in 1990, made him a phenomenon. It arrived just before Billboard began using point-of-sale data in 1991, which revealed how many records country artists actually sold. No Fences went on to sell well over ten million copies in the United States, and Brooks became the face of a country boom that brought the genre into suburban malls and arenas.","Listen to the sound effects in The Thunder Rolls: the storm is part of the arrangement, rumbling under the verses and breaking at the climaxes, a cinematic trick more common in rock than in country. Reynolds kept the instruments traditional but the drums are miked large and loud, which is the album's quiet revolution. Brooks's vocal often bends slightly flat and then up into a note for emotional effect, a habit borrowed from George Strait.","Critics were more measured than fans: many admired the song selection and Brooks's sincerity but found the production glossy and the showmanship calculated. Traditionalists worried that his rock influences and arena tours were diluting the genre. Over time No Fences has come to be seen as the defining country record of the early 1990s boom, for better and worse, and one of the best-selling albums of the decade.","Play Friends in Low Places for the energy, then Unanswered Prayers for the storytelling, then The Thunder Rolls for the drama, and listen for how big the drums sound beside the fiddle. Then compare George Strait's Amarillo by Morning, the plainer model, and Bruce Springsteen's Born in the U.S.A. for the arena-rock scale Brooks admired."],"garth-brooks-friends-in-low-places":["A man turns up uninvited at his former girlfriend's black-tie party, dressed wrong, and cheerfully tells her he is happier with his rough friends at the local bar. The song moves at a loose, rolling mid-tempo, with piano and guitars and a chorus that a crowd of voices joins, like a bar singalong. Brooks's voice drops to its lowest at the start of the chorus, a famous plunge into the bass, before the whole room seems to join him for the title line.","The song was written by Dewayne Blackwell and Earl Bud Lee, and Brooks sang on their demo recording before he had a record deal. He kept it for No Fences, and it was released as the album's lead single in 1990. It went to number one on the country chart and won single-of-the-year awards from both the Country Music Association and the Academy of Country Music, becoming his signature song and a fixture at sporting events, weddings and bars.","Listen to the low note at the start of each chorus: Brooks dips far below his usual range, almost speaking, which makes the chorus feel like a shrug before it becomes a shout. The crowd noise on the studio track is deliberate, building the barroom setting into the record. In concert Brooks adds a third verse not on the album, and fans sing it word for word, so the song exists in two versions.","It was an immediate hit and has become one of the most recognised country songs of all time, regularly topping polls of fans' favourites. Critics have noted its clever inversion of snobbery: the narrator is cheerful rather than resentful. Some found it a novelty, but its craft and its ubiquity have made it a standard.","Listen for that dive into the bass at the start of the chorus, then the background crowd joining. Notice how cheerful the arrangement is compared to the story's embarrassment. Then try Alan Jackson's Chattahoochee for another 1990s party song with a traditional backbone, and Merle Haggard's Okie from Muskogee for an earlier anthem of pride in plain folk."],"alan-jackson-dont-rock-the-jukebox":["Ten songs of honky-tonk, two-step and heartbreak ballad played by a tight Nashville band with fiddle and steel guitar at the front. The title song, in which a man asks a bar not to play rock songs because he wants to hear George Jones, is a fast shuffle with a grinning guitar hook. Midnight in Montgomery, a ghostly song about meeting Hank Williams at his grave, is slow and eerie, with a lonesome steel. Jackson's mild Georgia tenor ties it all together.","Jackson's 1990 debut had established him as a rising traditionalist; this second album, produced by Keith Stegall and Scott Hendricks and released by Arista in 1991, made him a star. It appeared at the peak of the country boom, as Garth Brooks was filling arenas, and Jackson positioned himself as the genre's conscience. The title song went to number one, and several more singles followed. Jackson wrote or co-wrote most of the record.","Midnight in Montgomery is a homage written as a ghost story: Williams died on the way to a show in 1953, and the song imagines his ghost thanking a young singer at the graveside in Montgomery, Alabama. Listen to how the steel guitar plays a slow wail at the start that imitates the sound of the wind. The title song, meanwhile, slyly uses an upbeat, almost rockabilly groove to argue against rock.","Critics praised the record as one of the strongest traditionalist albums of the early 1990s, and it sold several million copies. Some heard its anti-rock stance as nostalgic or reactionary in a genre that was already absorbing rock; others took it as good-humoured. It cemented Jackson's reputation as the decade's standard-bearer for honky-tonk.","Start with the title song for the humour, then Midnight in Montgomery for the atmosphere, listening for the steel guitar's wailing introduction. Then play Hank Williams's Your Cheatin' Heart to meet the ghost, and George Jones's He Stopped Loving Her Today to hear the singer the title song asks for."],"alan-jackson-chattahoochee":["A fast, cheerful song about growing up beside the Chattahoochee River in Georgia, with summer nights, cars and first lessons in life and romance. It moves at a quick two-step tempo with twangy electric guitar, a pumping piano and fiddle bursts. The chorus is short and catchy, with the river's name stretched across a bouncing melody. At under two and a half minutes, it is built for the dance floor, and the guitar hook at the start is the part everyone recognises.","Jackson wrote the song with Jim McBride for his 1992 album A Lot About Livin' (And a Little 'bout Love), and it was released as a single in 1993. It went to number one on the country chart and won both Song of the Year and Single of the Year at the Country Music Association awards. Its video, with Jackson water-skiing in cowboy boots, became a fixture of country television in the line-dancing craze.","Listen to the song's length and shape: it barely pauses between choruses, packing a whole adolescence into a little over two minutes, which is part of its exhilaration. The Chattahoochee runs through Jackson's own part of Georgia, so the song is more autobiographical than it sounds. The guitar part borrows the springy feel of rockabilly, which keeps the traditional sound youthful rather than nostalgic.","It was one of the biggest country hits of 1993 and became a staple of line-dancing nights and sports arenas. Critics have generally treated it as a perfect piece of pop craftsmanship and an example of how Jackson could be commercial without abandoning tradition. It remains one of his most beloved songs.","Listen for the opening guitar hook and how quickly the song reaches its chorus. Notice the piano's bounce under the verses. Then try Garth Brooks's Friends in Low Places for another 1990s singalong, and Bruce Springsteen's Born to Run for a rock song about youth, cars and escape that this one answers in a lighter key."],"alan-jackson-where-were-you":["A slow ballad responding to the September 11, 2001 attacks, asking listeners where they were and what they did that day, and naming ordinary responses such as calling family and going to church. Over a gentle acoustic guitar and piano, with steel guitar and soft strings, Jackson sings almost in a murmur, building only slightly in the chorus. It avoids anger and slogans entirely, closing on an affirmation of faith, hope and love drawn from a familiar Bible passage.","Jackson said the song came to him in the middle of the night some weeks after the attacks, after he had resisted writing anything about them. He first performed it at the Country Music Association Awards in November 2001, and its radio release followed immediately. It went to number one on the country chart, won the Grammy for best country song, and appeared on his 2002 album Drive. Other country songs of the moment were more militant.","Listen to what the song does not do: there is no drum fill, no key change and no shouted chorus, so the restraint itself carries the meaning. The lyrics turn on a contrast between not understanding world politics and knowing the difference between love and hate, a careful humility. The closing reference to faith, hope and love comes from Paul's first letter to the Corinthians.","It was widely embraced as the most humane of the many songs written after September 11, and critics noted that it gave listeners room for grief rather than anger. Some felt it sentimental, others praised its simplicity. It remains one of the most significant country songs of the 2000s and a standard at memorial events.","Listen on a quiet evening, focusing on how low and soft Jackson keeps his voice. Notice the steel guitar sighing between lines. Then compare Bruce Springsteen's Nebraska for another plain-voiced American record about loss, and the Chicks' Taking the Long Way for a very different country response to the war years that followed."],"shania-twain-the-woman-in-me":["Twelve songs that combine country instruments, fiddle and steel guitar, with the huge drums, layered backing vocals and crisp electric guitars of 1980s arena rock. The hits include Any Man of Mine, a stomping song in which a woman lists what she expects from a partner, Whose Bed Have Your Boots Been Under?, a witty kiss-off, and the title ballad. The sound you recognise is a fiddle hook riding over a booming, rock-sized drum beat.","Twain's 1993 debut had sold poorly. Robert John Lange, the producer behind AC/DC's Back in Black and Def Leppard's 1980s albums, heard her and they married in 1993; he co-wrote and produced this second album, released by Mercury Nashville in 1995. Twain promoted it through glamorous music videos rather than heavy touring. It sold many millions of copies and won the Grammy for best country album, but Nashville insiders were uneasy about its rock gloss.","Listen to the backing vocals: Lange stacked many layers of voices in the choruses, the same technique he used on hard-rock records, so the chorus seems to come from a crowd. Any Man of Mine has a short spoken section and a deliberate stop, like a line-dance cue, built for country dance floors. The writing partnership mixes Nashville storytelling with rock-anthem structure.","The album was a commercial triumph and won major awards, but critics and traditionalists debated whether it was country at all. Some praised Twain's humour and confidence; others called the production synthetic. Its sales proved that country fans would follow a pop approach, and it is now seen as the bridge between the 1990s boom and Twain's Come On Over.","Start with Any Man of Mine and listen for the fiddle cutting through the rock drums, then Whose Bed Have Your Boots Been Under? for the humour. Compare AC/DC's Back in Black to hear Lange's rock sound, and Dolly Parton's Jolene for an earlier country woman speaking her mind."],"shania-twain-come-on-over":["Sixteen songs, about an hour of bright, cheerful country pop: fiddle and steel guitar coexist with big pop hooks, rock guitars and dance beats. The best known include Man! I Feel Like a Woman!, a playful anthem of a night out, That Don't Impress Me Much, a teasing rejection of vain men, and the love ballads You're Still the One and From This Moment On. The album's sound is a bright guitar riff or fiddle hook bolted to a big, simple chorus.","After the success of The Woman in Me, Twain and her husband-producer Robert John Lange set out to make an album that could work equally on country and pop radio. Released in 1997 by Mercury, it was later remixed for international markets with the country instruments replaced by pop and dance arrangements. It became the best-selling country album and the best-selling album by a woman of its era, with sales estimated at around forty million worldwide.","There are two Come On Overs: the original country version and the international version, remixed to remove fiddle and steel and add pop production. Listen to both versions of That Don't Impress Me Much to hear how little the songs change and how much the colour does. Lange's hooks are often in the instruments rather than the voice, short riffs you can hum before the singing begins.","The album was a global phenomenon, but critics were divided, and some country traditionalists treated it as the moment Nashville lost its identity. More recent writing has been kinder, recognising Twain's songwriting, her humour and her influence on later crossover stars such as Taylor Swift. It is now regarded as one of the most important commercial records of the 1990s.","Start with Man! I Feel Like a Woman! for the hook and energy, then You're Still the One for the ballad style. Listen for how the fiddle is used like a pop synthesiser. Then try Taylor Swift's Red to hear the crossover path Twain opened, and Madonna's Ray of Light for a pop album of the same moment."],"the-chicks-wide-open-spaces":["Twelve songs of bright, energetic country with real string-band playing at its core: Martie Maguire's fiddle and Emily Strayer's banjo and dobro are prominent throughout, under Natalie Maines's strong, bluesy lead and the sisters' close harmony. The title song, about a young woman leaving home to find herself, is the anthem; There's Your Trouble and You Were Mine are the other key singles. The sound is acoustic instruments played with bluegrass fire inside a polished pop-country production.","The sisters had played bluegrass and western music around Dallas for years before Natalie Maines replaced their original singer in 1995. Signed to Sony's revived Monument label, they made Wide Open Spaces with producers Blake Chancey and Paul Worley, released in 1998. The title song was written by Susan Gibson. The album sold many millions of copies, won the Grammy for best country album, and made them the most successful female group in country history.","Listen past the voice to the banjo and dobro: Strayer often plays fast, intricate rolls that would sit easily on a bluegrass record, and Maguire's fiddle solos are improvised-sounding rather than tidy session parts. The sisters' harmony parts, sung around Maines, have the tight blend that only years of playing together produce. The album thus smuggles serious bluegrass musicianship into the late-1990s mainstream.","Critics were impressed by the musicianship and by the record's mix of youthful energy and tradition. It was a huge commercial success and won major industry awards. In hindsight it is seen as a turning point in which women and acoustic instruments returned to the centre of country radio, setting up the group's later, more serious work.","Play the title song first, then listen to There's Your Trouble for the fiddle and banjo trading lines. Try to follow the harmony voices separately. Then go to Dolly Parton's Trio for an earlier album of women's harmony and Alison Krauss's So Long So Wrong for the bluegrass side of the same era."],"the-chicks-taking-the-long-way":["Fourteen songs, over an hour long, in a warmer, rock-leaning style than the group's earlier records, with electric guitars, organ and string arrangements beside fiddle and banjo. Not Ready to Make Nice, a defiant song about refusing to apologise after public attacks, builds from quiet to a roaring, string-laden climax. Other songs address motherhood, infertility, ageing and the choices of a life on the road. Natalie Maines's voice is the centre, angrier and more personal than before.","In 2003, at a London concert on the eve of the Iraq invasion, Maines said the group was ashamed that the president was from Texas. Country radio stations boycotted them, and they received death threats. For this response they worked with producer Rick Rubin in Los Angeles, writing all the songs with outside collaborators, and released it on Columbia in 2006. Radio largely ignored it, but it won five Grammys, including Album of the Year.","Listen to how the group deliberately moved away from the country radio sound that had rejected them: Rubin's production draws on 1970s Laurel Canyon rock and singer-songwriter records, and the banjo and fiddle are often mixed into the background. The writing reveals the sisters' private lives, including fertility treatment, a subject almost absent from mainstream country. Not Ready to Make Nice's key change at the climax is the one moment of full release.","The Grammy sweep was widely read as an endorsement of the group's political stand, and critics praised the album's songwriting and ambition. Country audiences and radio largely stayed away, and the group effectively stepped back for years afterwards. Its reputation has grown, and it is now discussed as a landmark of artistic independence and of the cost of dissent in country music.","Start with Not Ready to Make Nice and listen for how it builds from a quiet verse to its full climax. Then hear Easy Silence for a gentler side. Compare their own Wide Open Spaces to hear how much the sound changed, and Alan Jackson's Where Were You for a contrasting country response to the same era."],"brad-paisley-whiskey-lullaby":["A duet with Alison Krauss telling the story of a couple whose separation leads both, one after the other, to drink themselves into grief and early graves. Set to a slow, swaying waltz with acoustic guitar, dobro and fiddle, it sounds like an old Appalachian ballad. Paisley sings the man's story and Krauss the woman's, and their voices join in a high, mournful harmony on the chorus. The dobro's sliding notes give the track its tearful colour.","The song was written by Bill Anderson and Jon Randall, and Paisley recorded it for his 2003 album Mud on the Tires, released by Arista Nashville; it was released as a single in 2004. Krauss, the most acclaimed bluegrass singer of her generation, was a natural partner for its old-time sound. It won Song of the Year at the Country Music Association awards and Musical Event of the Year, and its sombre music video drew wide attention.","Listen to how the song borrows the structure of a traditional murder or death ballad: a story told in two halves, the man first, then the woman, the second half echoing the first in reverse. Krauss's entrance is the turn. Paisley, known as a flashy guitarist, plays almost nothing showy here, letting the dobro and fiddle carry the instrumental colour. The waltz time links it to old mountain laments.","It was celebrated as one of the finest country ballads of the 2000s and is regarded as one of the darkest songs ever to reach the top of country radio. Critics praised its restraint and its revival of the old ballad tradition. Some found the subject matter heavy for radio, but it is widely admired by songwriters and performers.","Listen for the moment Krauss's voice enters, halfway through, and how the harmony changes when they sing together. Notice the dobro's sighs between lines. Then go back to the Stanley Brothers' Man of Constant Sorrow for the mountain tradition this comes from, and to Alison Krauss's Raising Sand for her later duet work."],"miranda-lambert-the-house-that-built-me":["A quiet ballad in which a woman returns to her childhood home and asks the new owners to let her walk through it, hoping to remember who she was. The arrangement is spare and acoustic: fingerpicked guitar, mandolin, soft steel guitar and very light percussion. Lambert sings in a near whisper for most of the song, her voice catching on the memories. There is no big climax; the track simply grows a little warmer in the chorus and fades.","The song was written by Tom Douglas and Allen Shamblin and recorded for Lambert's 2009 album Revolution; it was released as a single in 2010. It was a sharp contrast to her reputation for fiery, defiant songs. It went to number one on the country chart, won the Grammy for best female country vocal performance, and was named Song of the Year by both the Country Music Association and the Academy of Country Music.","Listen to the way the song uses physical details of a house, the handprints, the dog buried in the yard, the room upstairs, as a map of memory; the lyrics never state the emotion directly. Lambert reportedly said she could hardly sing it at first without crying. The arrangement places the mandolin high and clear, giving a sense of childhood brightness against the adult sadness of the voice.","It was universally praised as one of the best country songs of its decade and helped transform Lambert's reputation from tough-talking rebel to serious interpreter. Critics singled out its restraint and specificity. It remains one of the most performed contemporary country ballads and a standard at singing competitions.","Listen on headphones, focusing on how softly Lambert sings and how few instruments play. Notice the details in each verse. Then try Dolly Parton's Coat of Many Colors for an earlier country song about childhood, and Jason Isbell's Southeastern for another quiet record of looking back."],"miranda-lambert-the-weight-of-these-wings":["A double album of twenty-four songs, split into two discs titled The Nerve and The Heart, moving between ragged rock, honky-tonk, folk and quiet ballads. The sound is rough and live, with slightly distorted guitars, loose drums and Lambert's voice placed close and unvarnished. Vice, a slow, dark song about self-destructive habits, and Tin Man, an aching ballad addressed to the Wizard of Oz character, are the best known. The album unfolds like a road trip through the aftermath of a breakup.","Lambert made the album after her highly publicised divorce from fellow country star Blake Shelton in 2015. Released by RCA Nashville in late 2016, it was a deliberate move away from radio-friendly production toward something messier and more personal. A double album was a rare commercial risk in country. It won the Academy of Country Music's album of the year.","The two-disc structure is a deliberate arc: The Nerve is restless, louder and more defiant, while The Heart is quieter and more reflective, so listening straight through tells a story of recovery. Listen to the deliberately rough production: guitars are allowed to distort and vocals are left close and unretouched, an aesthetic choice against Nashville polish. Tin Man reverses the Oz story, the singer offering her heart away.","Critics greeted it as Lambert's most ambitious and personal work and placed it on many year-end lists. Some felt it was too long and uneven; others argued that the sprawl was the point. It confirmed her status as one of the most significant country artists of her generation and is often compared to the great breakup records of rock.","Start with Vice to hear the album's mood, then Tin Man for its quietest moment. If you have time, listen to one disc at a sitting and notice the shift in tone between them. Then try Fleetwood Mac's Rumours for another record shaped by a breakup, and Kacey Musgraves's Golden Hour for a gentler country album of the same years."],"kacey-musgraves-same-trailer-different-park":["Twelve songs of observant, gently satirical small-town country, played with acoustic guitar, banjo and steel guitar in clean, relaxed arrangements. Merry Go 'Round, about the repetitive lives of a small town, uses a circling banjo figure; Follow Your Arrow, an encouragement to live as you choose, is breezy and catchy. Musgraves sings in a light, conversational voice, never pushing, letting the clever turns of phrase do the work. The sound is warm and unhurried, closer to 1970s country than to 2013 radio.","Musgraves had written songs in Nashville for years before her major-label debut on Mercury Nashville in 2013. Country radio at the time was dominated by male party anthems, sometimes called bro-country, and her wry, liberal-leaning songs, touching on same-sex relationships and small-town hypocrisy, stood out. Radio gave her limited support, but critics championed the record, and it won the Grammy for best country album, beating more popular rivals.","Listen to how Musgraves uses old country clichés, the trailer, the church, the small town, and turns them slightly so they mean something new; Merry Go 'Round takes the image of a fairground ride and makes it a metaphor for a stuck life. The banjo in that song is played like a clock ticking. The album's co-writers, including Shane McAnally and Brandy Clark, were part of a new wave of Nashville songwriters.","Critics welcomed it as one of the smartest country debuts in years, and its Grammy win was seen as a statement in favour of songwriting over radio formula. Some conservative listeners objected to the open-minded themes of Follow Your Arrow. In retrospect it marks the beginning of a broader shift in country toward more varied voices.","Start with Merry Go 'Round, listening for the banjo pattern, then Follow Your Arrow for the humour. Notice how calm Musgraves's voice stays. Then try John Prine's debut album for an earlier songwriter who treated ordinary lives with the same wry warmth, and Musgraves's own Golden Hour to hear where she went next."],"kacey-musgraves-golden-hour":["Thirteen songs that blend country instruments, banjo and steel guitar, with soft rock, disco and dreamy psychedelic pop. Slow Burn opens with gentle acoustic guitar; Space Cowboy is a quiet breakup ballad built on a cowboy pun; High Horse is a glittering disco track; Oh, What a World uses a vocoder to make Musgraves's voice sound robotic. The whole record is warm, hazy and unhurried, like a summer evening, and her light, clear voice floats above it.","Musgraves made the album in Nashville with producers Daniel Tashian and Ian Fitchuk after marrying the songwriter Ruston Kelly, and many songs reflect new love. Released by MCA Nashville in 2018, it received little country radio support but huge critical acclaim, and it won the Grammy for Album of the Year in 2019, along with best country album. It appeared at a moment when country was again debating its boundaries, alongside Lil Nas X and Beyoncé.","Listen to Oh, What a World: the vocoder, an electronic device that makes a voice sound synthesised, is a surprising tool on a country record, and Musgraves uses it to convey wonder rather than coldness. The banjo still appears quietly in many tracks, keeping the record anchored. Space Cowboy plays on the old western image of the lonesome rider to describe letting someone go.","Golden Hour was one of the most acclaimed albums of 2018, topping many critics' lists, and its Album of the Year win was a rare triumph for a country artist. Some traditionalists argued it was not country at all; others saw it as proof that the genre could absorb anything. It has become a reference point for artists blending country and pop.","Start with Slow Burn and let the mood settle, then Space Cowboy, then High Horse for the disco turn. Listen for the banjo and steel hidden in the mix. Then try Fleetwood Mac's Rumours for soft-rock warmth, Daft Punk's Random Access Memories for the disco side, and Beyoncé's Cowboy Carter for another genre-crossing country album."],"chris-stapleton-traveller":["Fourteen songs that move between country, southern rock, soul and blues, played by a small band with an unpolished, live sound. Stapleton's gravelly, enormous voice dominates, soaring into raw high notes and dropping to near silence. The title song is a rolling road song about restlessness and searching; Fire Away is a slow ballad about loving someone through their pain; Tennessee Whiskey is a soul ballad. Morgane Stapleton's harmony voice is woven through, softening the edges.","Stapleton had spent years as a Nashville songwriter and as lead singer of the bluegrass band the SteelDrivers. Traveller, produced by Dave Cobb with Stapleton and released by Mercury Nashville in 2015, sold modestly at first. At the Country Music Association awards that November he performed with Justin Timberlake, and the album leapt to the top of the Billboard 200. It won the Grammy for best country album and the CMA album of the year.","The title song was reportedly written on a drive across the American Southwest after Stapleton's father died, which explains its mood of searching. Listen for how few overdubs there are: many tracks sound like a band playing together in a room, a choice Cobb favoured against Nashville's layered production. Morgane's harmonies are often barely audible but change the colour of every chorus.","Traveller was acclaimed as one of the best country albums of the decade, and its sudden success was read as evidence of an audience hungry for something rawer than radio offered. Critics praised Stapleton's voice above all. Some noted that the success came via a television performance rather than radio, a sign of changing times. It remains a key record of the 2010s country revival.","Start with the title song, then Fire Away, then Tennessee Whiskey, listening to how Stapleton's voice changes in each. Notice how live the band sounds. Then compare Sturgill Simpson's Metamodern Sounds in Country Music and Jason Isbell's Southeastern, both produced by Dave Cobb, and Otis Redding's Otis Blue for the soul roots."],"chris-stapleton-tennessee-whiskey":["A slow soul ballad in which a man compares the woman he loves to the smoothest of drinks, saying she has taken the place of his old habits. Stapleton sets it in a rocking, three-beat feel with gentle electric guitar, organ and drums, closer to a 1960s Memphis soul record than to Nashville. His voice starts soft and builds to raw, gritty climaxes, stretching single words across many notes in the manner of a gospel singer. Morgane Stapleton's harmony enters in the chorus.","The song was written by Dean Dillon and Linda Hargrove and first recorded by David Allan Coe in 1981, then by George Jones in 1983, both as straight country ballads. Stapleton reworked it for Traveller in 2015. His performance with Justin Timberlake at the Country Music Association awards that year, combining it with a Timberlake song, became a sensation and drove the album to the top of the charts. The track became one of the most streamed country songs ever.","Listen to the guitar figure at the start: its slow, rocking pattern echoes Etta James's I'd Rather Go Blind, and Stapleton has acknowledged the soul influence. That shift, from a country two-step to a soul ballad, is the whole reinvention. Compare George Jones's version to hear how the same melody can sound entirely different when the rhythm changes. Stapleton's improvised vocal runs at the end are unusual in modern country.","The recording was widely celebrated and became Stapleton's signature, eclipsing earlier versions in popular memory. Critics praised its soulful reimagining. It became one of the best-selling country songs of the streaming era, certified many times over, despite never being a conventional radio hit, an example of how audiences found music differently by 2015.","Listen first to the opening guitar, then for the moment the voice breaks into a growl. Then play Etta James's I'd Rather Go Blind to hear the source of the feel, and George Jones's He Stopped Loving Her Today for the country tradition Jones brought to his own version of this song."],"sturgill-simpson-a-sailors-guide-to-earth":["Nine songs written as a letter from Simpson to his young son, framed by a sailor's advice for life. The sound is a wide mix: country songs with pedal steel sit beside brassy soul with a full horn section, lush string arrangements and psychedelic touches. Welcome to Earth opens with a gentle lullaby that turns into a horn-driven stomp; Keep It Between the Lines offers fatherly advice over funk horns; a slow, mournful cover of Nirvana's In Bloom is the surprise.","After the success of Metamodern Sounds in Country Music, Simpson signed with Atlantic and produced this album himself, released in 2016. He had served in the Navy, and the nautical framing draws on that experience. The horns are played by the Dap-Kings, the band behind Sharon Jones and Amy Winehouse. It received little country radio play but was nominated for the Grammy for Album of the Year and won best country album.","Listen to how the album travels: each song is a stage of the sailor's journey, and the instruments change with it, from country steel to soul horns to orchestral strings, a structure modelled on the concept albums of the late 1960s and early 1970s. The In Bloom cover slows Nirvana's grunge song into a gentle ballad and changes its meaning into a father's tenderness. The record is short, under forty minutes, so the whole voyage can be heard at a sitting.","The album was acclaimed for its ambition and its emotional directness, and its Album of the Year nomination surprised many, since Simpson was little known to the general public. Some country critics thought it had strayed too far from the genre; others celebrated it as a soul record by a country singer. It established him as an artist unwilling to be confined.","Start with Welcome to Earth and listen for the moment the lullaby gives way to horns. Then hear the In Bloom cover and compare it with Nirvana's original on Nevermind. Then try Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, another concept album of advice and concern, and Simpson's earlier Metamodern Sounds."],"sturgill-simpson-cuttin-grass-vol-1":["Twenty songs from across Simpson's career re-recorded as straight bluegrass, played by a band of acoustic virtuosos on banjo, mandolin, fiddle, dobro and upright bass, with no drums. The tempos are often fast and driving, with instrumental breaks passed from player to player in classic bluegrass fashion. Songs that had been psychedelic, rock or soul on earlier albums become tight, high-lonesome string-band numbers. The sound you remember is the banjo rolling under Simpson's voice, the whole band playing live in one room.","Simpson recorded the album quickly in 2020 at the Butcher Shoppe, a Nashville studio, during the pandemic, and released it on his own label. He had grown up in Kentucky surrounded by bluegrass and had long said he would make such an album. A second volume followed later in 2020. The project came after his rock album Sound and Fury, a sharp turn back toward the roots of his home state and Bill Monroe.","Listen to how the songs change character in bluegrass form: a song that was slow and spacey becomes urgent and bright, showing how much of a song's meaning depends on its arrangement. The band plays the traditional bluegrass roles, each instrument taking a solo in turn and then backing off. Simpson's own guitar provides the steady rhythm, the job Bill Monroe's guitarists did in the 1940s.","The album was warmly received by critics and bluegrass audiences, who praised the musicianship and the energy of the live recording. Some saw it as a palate cleanser after the rock of Sound and Fury; others as a statement of roots. It became one of the most popular bluegrass records of the year and introduced the genre to many of Simpson's rock fans.","Play a few songs you know from earlier albums and compare them with the originals. Listen for each instrument taking its turn. Then go back to Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky for the source of the style, and to Tyler Childers's Long Violent History for another Kentuckian's return to old-time music."],"tyler-childers-purgatory":["Ten songs of Appalachian country, mixing honky-tonk, bluegrass and folk with fiddle, banjo and steel guitar. Childers sings in a high, unpolished voice with a strong eastern Kentucky accent, and the songs are full of local places, slang and characters. Whitehouse Road is a driving honky-tonk number about a rough life on the edge; Feathered Indians is a tender love song; Lady May, a quiet closing ballad, is sung with just a guitar. The sound is close, warm and rootsy.","Childers had spent years playing bars across Kentucky and West Virginia and had released a small self-made album before Purgatory, produced by Sturgill Simpson and David Ferguson in Nashville and released in 2017 on his own label through Thirty Tigers. Simpson's endorsement brought attention, and the album spread by word of mouth and streaming rather than radio. It made Childers a leading figure in the independent country scene alongside Simpson, Jason Isbell and Chris Stapleton.","Listen to the language: Childers uses words and place names from eastern Kentucky that outsiders may not know, and the songs read as a portrait of a specific community. The title track turns on Catholic imagery of purgatory, unusual in a region of Protestant churches, as a metaphor for a restless life. Simpson's production keeps everything dry and close, with little reverb, so it sounds like a front-porch session.","Critics praised the album as one of the most authentic Appalachian records in years, and it has steadily built a large following. Some noted the influence of Simpson's production; others focused on the strength of the writing. It is now regarded as a key album of the 2010s independent country movement and an influence on younger writers such as Zach Bryan.","Start with Feathered Indians, then Whitehouse Road, then Lady May, listening for the change from full band to solo voice. Notice the accent and local detail in the words. Then try Jean Ritchie's Singing the Traditional Songs of Her Kentucky Mountain Family for the deep roots of the region's music, and Sturgill Simpson's Metamodern Sounds for the producer's own style."],"tyler-childers-long-violent-history":["Eight traditional fiddle tunes played by Childers and his band, followed by one new song. The fiddle tunes, including Bonaparte's Retreat and Midnight on the Water, are played in old-time style, with fiddle leading, banjo and guitar underneath, and a loose, live feel. The final, title song, sung with only a few instruments, asks white rural listeners to imagine how they would feel if their own communities faced the injustice Black Americans describe. The contrast between instrumental tradition and direct song is the record's shape.","Childers released the album in September 2020, months after the protests that followed the killing of George Floyd, alongside a video statement addressed to his largely white, rural audience. He had played fiddle since childhood, and the instrumental tunes come from the Appalachian tradition he grew up with. The album was released through his own label and RCA. Its release brought a younger country audience face to face with old-time fiddle music.","The structure is the argument: eight tunes from the shared, centuries-old fiddle tradition of the mountains, music shaped by both European and African-American influences, then a single song that asks listeners to look at their neighbours. Listen to Bonaparte's Retreat, a tune that has also been played as a song, and notice its age in the drone of the fiddle strings. The title song's spare setting keeps attention on the words.","The album was praised for its musicianship and for Childers's willingness to speak to his audience directly, though some listeners objected to the message. Critics noted the courage of a country artist taking such a stand in 2020. It is regarded as one of the most significant statements by a country musician on race in recent years.","Listen to the fiddle tunes first as a set, letting the drone and rhythm settle in, then listen to the title song separately and attentively. Then go back to the Carter Family's Wildwood Flower for another old instrumental and song tradition, and Sam Cooke's A Change Is Gonna Come for a song addressing the same history from another side."],"zach-bryan-american-heartbreak":["Thirty-four songs over about two hours, ranging from bare acoustic confessions to full-band anthems with fiddle, steel guitar and crashing drums. Bryan's voice is raw, often pushed to a crack, and the recordings keep breaths, mistakes and room noise. The songs deal with heartbreak, home, friendship, small-town life and the pull between leaving and staying. Something in the Orange, a slow song about a relationship ending at dusk, is the best known. The sound is handmade, intimate and sprawling.","Bryan had built a following by posting rough performances online while serving in the Navy and releasing self-made albums from 2019. After leaving the service in 2021, he signed with Warner, and American Heartbreak, released in 2022, became one of the biggest debuts of the year, propelled by streaming and social media rather than country radio. It made him a central figure in a new country boom driven by young listeners online.","Listen for the imperfections: Bryan insisted on keeping takes where his voice cracks or the guitar buzzes, a deliberate rejection of Nashville polish. Some songs were recorded in houses and on the road rather than in studios. The album's huge length is itself a statement about the streaming era, where there is no vinyl side to fill and listeners choose their own sequence.","The album was a commercial phenomenon and received broadly positive reviews, with critics praising Bryan's emotional directness and the Springsteen-like sweep of his writing. Many argued that it was too long and uneven, and that a tighter album lay inside it. It is now seen as the defining country record of the early streaming-era boom.","Do not try to play it all at once. Start with Something in the Orange, then a few of the full-band songs, then the acoustic ones, and notice how the rawness is part of the meaning. Then try Bruce Springsteen's Nebraska for the model of plain, home-recorded feeling, and Tyler Childers's Purgatory for a kindred writer."],"zach-bryan-something-in-the-orange":["A slow, aching song in which a man waits at sunset for a partner who may not return, reading the orange light of dusk as a sign the relationship is over. It begins with only acoustic guitar and Bryan's voice, then gradually adds pedal steel, bass and light drums, building to a fuller sound without ever becoming loud. His voice strains at the top of the melody, almost breaking. The repeated guitar pattern and the sense of a fading evening are the song's signatures.","Bryan teased the song online before release, and its popularity on social media led to its release as a single in 2022, before American Heartbreak. It became one of the biggest crossover hits of the year, reaching the top ten of the Billboard Hot 100 and finding a huge audience beyond country. It made Bryan a household name and helped set the template for a wave of raw, emotional country hits in the streaming era.","Listen to how slowly the arrangement builds: each instrument enters only after the previous one has settled, mirroring the slow fading of light in the story. The steel guitar enters like a sigh at the moment the narrator admits what he knows. The song's central image, the colour of the sky, is an old device of country and folk songwriting, where landscape mirrors feeling.","The song was widely praised for its emotional directness and became one of the most streamed country songs of the decade. Critics compared Bryan's writing to that of Springsteen and the Oklahoma Red Dirt songwriters. Some argued its success said more about online trends than the song itself, but its staying power has quieted that view.","Listen on headphones and follow each instrument as it enters, noticing how the song grows without getting loud. Then go back to Townes Van Zandt's Pancho and Lefty for an older, plain-voiced country ballad, and Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago for another intimate, raw record of loss."],"kitty-wells-it-wasnt-god-who-made-honky-tonk-angels":["A plain, almost conversational country waltz-time ballad in which a woman answers back. Kitty Wells sings without vibrato or ornament over a small Nashville band, steel guitar sighing between the lines and a fiddle trading phrases with her. The melody is borrowed whole from Hank Thompson's hit The Wild Side of Life, so listeners in 1952 heard the familiar tune at once and then heard the argument turned around: the song says that unfaithful men, not wayward women, are to blame for broken homes.","Songwriter J. D. Miller wrote the reply within weeks of Thompson's record dominating jukeboxes, and Decca's Paul Cohen offered it to Wells, then known mainly as a radio singer married to Johnnie Wright of the duo Johnnie and Jack. She reportedly agreed to record it chiefly for the session fee. Postwar honky-tonk was a male world of barrooms and jukeboxes, and radio stations and the Grand Ole Opry were uneasy about a woman voicing such a pointed complaint on air.","The tune goes back further than Thompson. The same melody carried the Carter Family's I'm Thinking Tonight of My Blue Eyes and Roy Acuff's hymn The Great Speckled Bird, so this single sits at the end of a chain of country songs passed hand to hand. Listen to how Wells's restraint does the arguing: she never raises her voice, and the calm itself is what made the lyric sound so unanswerable.","Despite some broadcast bans it became the first record by a woman singing alone to top Billboard's country chart, and it sold strongly enough that Decca built a long career around Wells. Historians treat it as the moment country's audience showed that it wanted women's perspectives, and later writers have read it as an early, quiet piece of feminist pop. Its influence runs through Loretta Lynn, Dolly Parton and every country answer song since.","Play Hank Thompson's The Wild Side of Life first, then this, back to back: the same tune, opposite verdicts. Notice the steel guitar fills that echo her final words in each line. Then jump to Loretta Lynn's Don't Come Home A-Drinkin' to hear how the next generation of women turned Wells's dignified rebuke into open defiance."],"kitty-wells-making-believe":["A slow, aching ballad about a woman pretending that a love she has lost is still hers. Kitty Wells takes it at an unhurried walking pace, her voice level and clear, with the steel guitar carrying most of the emotion in long, crying slides between her phrases. The song is built from short, simple lines that rise slightly at the end of each verse, and the arrangement stays deliberately sparse so that every word lands. It runs well under three minutes and never once raises its volume.","Written by Jimmy Work, a Kentucky-born honky-tonk songwriter who also recorded his own version, the song arrived at the height of Wells's run on Decca, when she was the undisputed queen of country music and the label was issuing her singles in quick succession. In 1955 rock and roll was just beginning to pull young listeners away from country radio, and Nashville's response was not yet the smooth Nashville Sound but straight honky-tonk like this.","Listen to the way Wells holds back the expected sob. Many singers of the period added a catch or a crack at the saddest word; Wells keeps the line steady and lets the steel guitar answer with the cry she refuses to give. That division of labour, singer composed and instrument weeping, became a model for countless later country ballads, including Patsy Cline's.","It became one of Wells's biggest hits and one of her most covered songs, recorded later by Emmylou Harris and many others. Critics who once found Wells's style too plain have come to hear the plainness as the point: an unvarnished realism that stands closer to the folk ballad than to pop. Many historians rank it alongside her 1952 breakthrough as her finest single.","Listen first to the steel guitar alone: notice how it enters on the last word of each line, like a second voice finishing her thought. Then compare Emmylou Harris's 1977 recording, which is warmer and more openly emotional. Patsy Cline's I Fall to Pieces makes a good third, showing the same idea dressed in Nashville strings."],"emmylou-harris-elite-hotel":["Emmylou Harris's second major-label album moves between honky-tonk covers, country-rock and slow ballads, with her silvery soprano set against the Hot Band, a crack group of Los Angeles and Nashville players. Buck Owens's Together Again and Patsy Cline's Sweet Dreams are taken slowly and reverently; Gram Parsons's songs are driven harder, with electric guitar and steel trading licks. Several tracks are live recordings, giving parts of the record a looser, roadhouse feel. The record's signature is that voice: clear, fragile-sounding, and far tougher than it first seems.","Harris had been Gram Parsons's duet partner until his death in 1973, and her first solo records on Reprise, produced by Brian Ahern, set out to carry on his idea of a country music that rock audiences could love without irony. Elite Hotel appeared in late 1975, at a moment when Nashville was polishing country into pop and the outlaws were rebelling from Texas. Harris took a third path: tradition, played by a hot young band.","Listen to the three Parsons songs as a memorial hidden inside a country record. Harris sings Sin City and Wheels, both from the Flying Burrito Brothers years, and Ooh Las Vegas, and she sings them straight and unsentimental, as working songs rather than relics, which is a tribute of its own. The pairing of Buck Owens and Patsy Cline with Parsons is itself an argument: that his songs belong in the same canon.","The album reached the top of the country album chart and won Harris her first Grammy, for Best Female Country Vocal Performance, and both Together Again and Sweet Dreams became country number ones. Purists who had distrusted the long-haired country-rock crowd accepted her because her taste in old songs was so sure. It is now seen as a cornerstone of the bridge between Nashville and the Los Angeles country-rock scene.","Start with Together Again, then put on Buck Owens's original: you will hear a hard Bakersfield shuffle turned into a slow, open-hearted ballad. Then play Sin City and go back to the Flying Burrito Brothers' version on The Gilded Palace of Sin. The original vinyl mastering is warm and roomy; later remasters add bonus tracks."],"emmylou-harris-luxury-liner":["The most driving of Emmylou Harris's 1970s albums. It opens with Gram Parsons's title song played as full-tilt country-rock, the Hot Band's lead guitar and piano racing each other, and moves through old honky-tonk numbers, a Chuck Berry song, Kitty Wells's Making Believe and Townes Van Zandt's Pancho and Lefty. Harris's high voice rides on top of a band that plays much louder than most Nashville sessions of the day, so the album feels like a hot live set compressed onto two sides.","By 1977 Harris was a star with a regular touring band, and producer Brian Ahern recorded much of the album with the musicians who played behind her every night. The country charts were dominated by smooth crossover singers, and Harris's insistence on fiddle, steel and old songs set her apart. Her band was also a school: Rodney Crowell, Ricky Skaggs and others passed through it on the way to their own careers in the following years.","Listen to how Pancho and Lefty sits in the middle of the record. Townes Van Zandt was then a cult figure almost unknown outside Texas, and Harris's recording was one of the first to bring the song to a wide audience, years before Willie Nelson and Merle Haggard made it a number one. Her version is slower and more mournful than theirs, closer to a border ballad.","Luxury Liner topped the country album chart and was widely praised for its energy, though a few critics felt the rockier numbers overpowered her voice. Its reputation has grown as the record that best captures the Hot Band at full strength. It is often cited by alt-country and Americana musicians of the 1990s as proof that traditional country and rock volume could live together.","Begin with the title track at loud volume and listen for the guitar answering every vocal line. Then hear her Making Believe straight after Kitty Wells's 1955 original, and her Pancho and Lefty after Townes Van Zandt's own. Those pairings show what Harris did: she gave older and overlooked songs a new, wider life."],"emmylou-harris-red-dirt-girl":["An album of Emmylou Harris's own songs, unusual for a singer known as an interpreter. Its sound is dark and atmospheric: droning guitars, echoing drums, keyboards and quiet loops create a misty bed under her voice, which is now lower, breathier and more weathered than in the 1970s. The songs are portraits and elegies: the title track follows a girl from Alabama whose hopes narrow, other songs deal with faith, a dead father and lost friends. Little here sounds like conventional country radio.","Harris had reinvented herself in 1995 with Wrecking Ball, made with Daniel Lanois, and Red Dirt Girl continued that mood with Lanois's associate Malcolm Burn producing. It came out on Nonesuch, a label better known for classical and world music, marking how far she had moved from Nashville's commercial centre. Guests include Bruce Springsteen, Patti Scialfa and Dave Matthews, and Guy Clark and other writers shared credits on a few songs.","Listen to Bang the Drum Slowly, written with Guy Clark, which addresses Harris's father, a Marine aviator who had been a prisoner of war in Korea. The song is cast as a series of questions she never got to ask him. It is among the most personal things she has recorded, and its slow military drum is a quiet tribute to his service.","The album won the Grammy for Best Contemporary Folk Album and was widely reviewed as a late-career peak, with critics praising her emergence as a songwriter in her fifties. Some longtime fans missed the fiddles and steel guitar, and the album sits more naturally in Americana than country. It is now often paired with Wrecking Ball as her great atmospheric diptych.","Start with the title song and listen for the way the band builds slowly around a single guitar figure. Then try The Pearl and Bang the Drum Slowly. Play Wrecking Ball first if you want the sonic context, and compare Lucinda Williams's Car Wheels on a Gravel Road for another Southern songwriter's portrait of home."],"reba-mcentire-fancy":["A big, cinematic country-pop production of a story song in which a woman tells of escaping childhood poverty through the hard bargain her mother pushed her into. Reba McEntire opens quietly over keyboard and guitar, then the band and strings swell as the narrative moves forward, until she is belting over a full arrangement. Her voice does the storytelling: twangy and girlish in the early verses, steely and triumphant by the end. It runs nearly five minutes, long for country radio, and plays like a short film.","The song was written and first recorded by Bobbie Gentry in 1969, a minor hit for her. McEntire, who had loved it as a teenager, recorded it for her 1990 album Rumor Has It, co-produced with Tony Brown, and it was released as a single the following year. By then McEntire was the leading woman in country music and was building a reputation for songs about women's hard choices, often staged as elaborate music videos.","Listen for how the arrangement mirrors the story's social climb. The early verses sit on a small, rootsy band; as the narrator rises, strings and a heavier rhythm section enter, so the sound itself becomes richer. Gentry's original was swampier and darker, with horns and a funk groove, and the contrast shows how McEntire reframed the song from Southern gothic into a triumph anthem.","Fancy became one of McEntire's signature songs and a concert showstopper for decades, though it was never one of her biggest chart hits. Some listeners have found its subject uncomfortable; many others, particularly women and LGBTQ audiences, have embraced it as a story of survival and self-making. It is now better known than Gentry's original, which has itself been rediscovered.","Listen once just for McEntire's voice: notice how she changes colour from verse to verse as the character ages. Then put on Bobbie Gentry's 1969 recording, slower and murkier, and hear the same story told as quiet fact rather than victory. Dolly Parton's Coat of Many Colors offers a gentler poverty story for comparison."],"reba-mcentire-my-kind-of-country":["A short, tight album of fiddle-and-steel country on which Reba McEntire put aside the soft-pop strings of her earlier records. The arrangements are spare, with a shuffle beat, crying steel guitar, honky-tonk piano and fiddle fills, and her voice sits right at the front, bright and supple, bending notes in the old Western style she heard growing up in Oklahoma. The two hit singles, How Blue and Somebody Should Leave, show the two moods: a brisk lonesome shuffle and a slow, adult ballad about a marriage running out.","McEntire had recently moved to MCA after years on Mercury and insisted on choosing songs closer to traditional country, a decision she later described as finding her own sound. It came out at the start of the movement critics would call neotraditionalism, when George Strait, Ricky Skaggs and soon Randy Travis turned Nashville back toward fiddles after the urban-cowboy pop of the early 1980s. Both hit singles reached number one on the country chart.","Listen for the songs she chose from older country catalogues. Alongside new material, McEntire reached back for older honky-tonk songs, a declaration of lineage. Her vocal swoops and yodel-like breaks, which later become larger and more theatrical, are here still close to the Oklahoma dance-hall singing she grew up with.","The album marked the turning point of McEntire's career: within two years she had been named the Country Music Association's Female Vocalist of the Year more than once and was on her way to superstardom. Critics welcomed it as a return to roots, though some later noted that her greatest success came when she moved back toward big pop production. It remains a favourite among traditionalist fans.","Play How Blue first and listen for the fiddle and steel splitting the fills between them, then Somebody Should Leave for her ballad control. Compare George Strait's records of the same years to hear the neotraditional wave, and then McEntire's Fancy to hear how far she later travelled from this lean sound."],"rosanne-cash-seven-year-ache":["A crisp, punchy album that sounds as much like late-1970s Los Angeles rock as Nashville country. Rosanne Cash's cool, low voice sits over tight drums, chiming electric guitars and keyboards, with steel guitar used sparingly as colour. The title song, her own, is a mid-tempo portrait of a strained relationship, cast in an urban nighttime setting rather than a honky-tonk; elsewhere she covers songs by Leroy Preston, Rodney Crowell and others with a swagger that keeps the record moving. Ten songs, barely half an hour.","Cash was married to the songwriter Rodney Crowell, who produced the album and much of her 1980s work. Columbia, her father's label for decades, was eager to reach younger listeners after the Urban Cowboy boom, and Crowell's production borrowed from the punchier sounds of Linda Ronstadt and the new wave. Cash was then in her mid-twenties and wary of trading on her surname; she wanted to be judged as a writer.","Listen to the drum sound. Country records of the time usually kept drums small and in the background; here they are bright and up-front, closer to a rock record, which is why the album still feels modern. Also listen for the steel guitar on the title track: it plays phrases more like a synthesiser pad than a classic honky-tonk cry.","The title track became a number one country single and crossed into the pop top forty, and the album was among the first by a woman in country to be hailed by rock critics. Traditionalists were uneasy, but the record helped define a smart, adult country pop that would run through the 1980s. It is now seen as a key early document of that decade's new country songwriting.","Begin with the title song and listen to how little it relies on country signifiers yet still feels country in its storytelling. Then hear My Baby Thinks He's a Train for the album's rockabilly side. Compare her father's I Walk the Line to hear how far, and how close, the family sound had travelled."],"rosanne-cash-kings-record-shop":["A polished, warm-sounding country-rock album that balances radio hits with introspective songs about marriage and self-doubt. Rosanne Cash's voice is calm and close-miked; the band plays clean guitars and light keyboards with a gentle backbeat. It contains her up-tempo version of her father's Tennessee Flat Top Box, John Hiatt's The Way We Make a Broken Heart, and John Stewart's Runaway Train, alongside her own songs. The cover photograph shows a small-town record store, setting the album's mood of looking back at roots from an adult distance.","Recorded with Rodney Crowell producing, the album came at a high point of commercial country songwriting, when artists such as Crowell, Steve Earle and Lyle Lovett were blurring country and rock. Cash was a mother of young children and increasingly ambivalent about Nashville's machinery. The album's success on the charts gave her the freedom to make the far more personal, less commercial records that followed.","Tennessee Flat Top Box was written and first recorded by Johnny Cash in 1961. Listen for how the guitar solo nods to the original's twangy Luther Perkins-style picking while the rest of the arrangement is sleek and modern. It became a family conversation carried out on country radio.","Four singles from the album reached number one on the country chart and critics praised the songwriting and production. Later, Cash herself became critical of the slickness of her 1980s records, and some writers now prefer her rawer 1990 album Interiors. Still, King's Record Shop is widely regarded as the commercial and artistic peak of 1980s country pop.","Play Tennessee Flat Top Box first, then her father's 1961 original. Then try The Way We Make a Broken Heart and listen to how the harmony vocals frame her. For context, Steve Earle's Guitar Town from the year before shows the rockier, more rebellious end of the same Nashville wave."],"patty-loveless-mountain-soul":["An acoustic album of bluegrass, mountain ballads and gospel, recorded with a string band of mandolin, banjo, fiddle, dobro and acoustic guitar. Patty Loveless's voice, high and raw at the edges, is the centre: she sings hard and plaintively, with the unpolished ache of Appalachian singers. The material ranges from fast bluegrass to slow hymns, and the most powerful track, Darrell Scott's You'll Never Leave Harlan Alive, is a bleak song about generations trapped in eastern Kentucky's coal country.","Loveless had been a reliable country hitmaker through the 1990s, but radio was moving toward glossy pop by 2001. She and her husband-producer Emory Gordy Jr. chose to go back to the music of her eastern Kentucky childhood, arriving alongside the O Brother, Where Art Thou? soundtrack and a broader revival of acoustic roots music. The project was a risk for a major-label artist.","Listen to the harmony voices: she is joined by bluegrass and country singers who share her mountain background, and the blend has the slightly rough, unblended edge of family singing rather than studio polish. Also notice that she rerecorded one of her own early songs in a bluegrass arrangement, showing how close her radio country always was to this music underneath.","Critics hailed Mountain Soul as the best album of her career, and it is regularly listed among the finest roots records of its decade. It sold respectably without radio support and helped show that mainstream country stars could make credible acoustic records. She later followed it with a second volume. Its version of You'll Never Leave Harlan Alive is often regarded as the definitive one.","Start with You'll Never Leave Harlan Alive and listen to how the band stays almost still while her voice carries the story. Then try a fast track to hear the banjo and mandolin drive. Compare the Stanley Brothers' Man of Constant Sorrow for the older mountain sound she is drawing on, and Loretta Lynn's Coal Miner's Daughter for the same world told brighter."],"patty-loveless-when-fallen-angels-fly":["A mainstream 1990s country album made with unusual emotional seriousness. The band is a classic Nashville unit of electric guitar, steel, fiddle and piano, but the arrangements are restrained, leaving space for Patty Loveless's keening voice. The songs move between upbeat honky-tonk and painful ballads; the most celebrated, Gretchen Peters's You Don't Even Know Who I Am, follows a wife who leaves a husband who no longer notices her. Throughout, her voice has a raw catch that makes even polished arrangements feel urgent.","The album came during the commercial boom of 1990s country, when Garth Brooks and others were selling records in pop quantities. Loveless had a couple of years earlier recovered from surgery on her vocal cords, and she and producer Emory Gordy Jr., her husband, chose songs that pressed deeper emotionally than standard radio fare. She had moved to Epic Records in the early 1990s after several years on MCA.","Listen to the way she approaches high notes: instead of belting cleanly in the manner of 1990s country pop, she pushes into the top of her range with a slight crack, a sound inherited from mountain gospel and bluegrass singers. That roughness sounds deliberate: the production leaves the strain audible rather than smoothing it out, and it is what makes the ballads feel lived in.","When Fallen Angels Fly won the Country Music Association's Album of the Year award, making Loveless one of the very few women to win it at that time. Critics praised it as proof that mainstream country could still be serious. It is often cited alongside her later Mountain Soul as the work that defines her career.","Start with You Don't Even Know Who I Am and listen to how little the band does: the song is carried by her voice and a few steel guitar phrases. Then try a fast track to hear her honky-tonk side. Compare Reba McEntire's albums of the same years for a bigger, more theatrical approach to similar stories."],"lefty-frizzell-if-youve-got-the-money":["A brisk honky-tonk shuffle about a man inviting a woman out for a night of dancing, if she can pay for it. The band is small and loose: electric guitar, steel, fiddle and piano trading licks over a walking bass. What makes it distinctive is Lefty Frizzell's voice. He stretches syllables, rolling them across several notes in a lazy, curling slide, and leans behind the beat as if he has all the time in the world. It is a joke song, but the singing turns it into a lesson in phrasing.","Frizzell recorded the song in Dallas with producer Jim Beck, who brought him to Columbia, and it was released in 1950. He had been playing Texas honky-tonks and radio shows since his teens. Postwar Texas was full of dance halls and jukeboxes hungry for honky-tonk, and Frizzell's arrival was sudden: within a year he was one of the biggest stars in country music.","Listen to how he sings a single vowel. On words that most singers would hit and release, Frizzell slides down and back up, turning one syllable into a little melody. This vocal trick, later called the Frizzell style, can be heard in Merle Haggard, George Jones, Willie Nelson and Randy Travis. It is easy to miss because he does it so casually.","The song became a country number one and launched Frizzell's extraordinary run of early 1950s hits. Critics now see his phrasing as one of the most influential developments in country singing, and the song has been covered widely, including by Willie Nelson. Frizzell's fame faded quickly, but historians have restored him as one of the genre's foundational voices.","Listen once for the band, then again only for the vowels: count how many notes he puts into one word. Then play Merle Haggard's Mama Tried and George Jones's She Thinks I Still Care to hear his phrasing reborn. Hank Williams's Lovesick Blues shows the other great vocal style of the moment."],"lefty-frizzell-long-black-veil":["A slow, haunting story song, sung by a dead man who was hanged for a crime he did not commit because his alibi would have exposed a secret love affair. The arrangement is spare: acoustic guitar, bass, light percussion and soft backing voices. Lefty Frizzell sings it plainly and slowly, letting the old-ballad melody carry the weight. The recurring image of a woman visiting a grave at night gives the song the atmosphere of an Appalachian murder ballad, though it was newly written.","The song was written in Nashville by Danny Dill and Marijohn Wilkin, who modelled it deliberately on older folk ballads; Dill said he drew on newspaper stories and old legends. Frizzell recorded it in 1959, when his own hit-making days had slowed and Nashville was moving toward the smooth Nashville Sound. The folk revival was just gathering pace, and the song's antique feeling suited the moment perfectly.","Listen to the narrative trick: the song is told by a man already in the grave, and you realise this only gradually. That posthumous voice is rare in country music and gives the song its eerie stillness. The melody uses a simple, repeating shape like a folk ballad, which is why many listeners assume it is centuries old.","Frizzell's version was a moderate hit, but the song's life has been enormous: Joan Baez, Johnny Cash, The Band, Nick Cave and many others recorded it. Folklorists sometimes initially mistook it for a traditional song. Critics now regard it as one of the great modern Nashville compositions and Frizzell's recording as the source that started its journey.","Listen first to how slowly the story unfolds, and notice when you realise who the narrator is. Then play The Band's version on Music from Big Pink, which turns it into a dark, spiritual soul ballad. Compare the Stanley Brothers' Man of Constant Sorrow for a truly old mountain song."],"ernest-tubb-walking-the-floor-over-you":["A medium-paced honky-tonk song about a man pacing the floor, unable to sleep over a woman who has left. Ernest Tubb's deep, flat baritone carries a plain, catchy melody, and the defining sound is a clean electric guitar playing single-note phrases between his lines. The rhythm is steady and danceable, built for jukeboxes and crowded rooms rather than front-porch listening. It lasts under three minutes and is as simple as a country song can be, which is the secret of its power.","Tubb wrote the song himself and recorded it for Decca in 1941 after years of struggle, including a long period imitating Jimmie Rodgers. Texas honky-tonks were noisy places, and acoustic guitars could barely be heard; amplified guitar solved the problem. The record was a big seller, and in 1943 it helped bring Tubb to the Grand Ole Opry, where electric instruments had been treated with suspicion.","Listen to the electric guitar: it is played with a clean, almost jazzy tone, closer to swing guitar than to later twangy country leads. This was one of the earliest hit country records to feature electric lead guitar so prominently, a turning point that eventually led to the Telecaster sound of Bakersfield and Nashville. Tubb himself played only rhythm.","The record sold very well and became Tubb's signature for the rest of his life; he performed it at nearly every show. Historians now treat it as a founding record of honky-tonk and a key step in the electrification of country music. Tubb's slightly off-key singing was mocked by some, but he reportedly joked that every ordinary listener thought they could sing as well as he could, which was the point.","Listen first only to the electric guitar between the vocal lines, then to the steady rhythm guitar underneath. Compare Jimmie Rodgers's Blue Yodel No. 1 for Tubb's model, then Hank Williams's Your Cheatin' Heart and Buck Owens's Act Naturally to hear how the electric guitar grew."],"ernest-tubb-waltz-across-texas":["A gentle, swaying waltz about the happiness of dancing with the person you love. Ernest Tubb's warm baritone sings the simple melody over his Texas Troubadours band, with a steel guitar gliding and a lead guitar picking short phrases. It is in three-quarter time, the old dance-hall waltz rhythm of Texas, and the whole record has the relaxed feeling of a Saturday night in a small-town hall. It is one of the few purely joyful songs in his catalogue.","The song was written by Talmadge Tubb, Ernest's nephew, and released in 1965. By then Tubb was an elder statesman: he had his famous record shop in Nashville, a weekly radio show from it, and a reputation for helping young performers, including Loretta Lynn and Johnny Cash. The Nashville Sound with its strings dominated radio, but Tubb kept his band lean and traditional.","Listen to the steel guitar's long glides at the end of each line: they imitate dancers turning across the floor. Also notice how the song uses the waltz, a dance associated with German and Czech immigrants in Texas, a reminder of how much central European music fed Texas country. Tubb's slightly rough voice makes the sentiment homely rather than sugary.","It was a modest hit at first but became one of the most loved songs in Texas dance halls, played at weddings and dances for decades. It is often described as the unofficial anthem of Texas dance halls. Critics see it as a good example of Tubb's lasting appeal: plain, honest, and rooted in a real social world.","Listen to it as dance music, ideally with someone to dance with: try to feel the one-two-three of the waltz and the steel guitar's long glide. Compare Bob Wills's Faded Love for another Texas waltz, and Patsy Cline's slower ballads for a more polished Nashville approach to similar feelings."],"bob-wills-steel-guitar-rag":["A bright instrumental showcase for Leon McAuliffe's electric steel guitar with Bob Wills and His Texas Playboys. The steel takes the lead, sliding between notes and bending chords, while fiddles and piano provide brisk rhythmic backing. The tune is a ragtime-style melody with a jaunty bounce,, and somewhere in the mix you can usually catch Wills's famous holler of encouragement. It runs about three minutes and is pure dance music, built to keep a ballroom moving.","Wills and his band were based in Tulsa, broadcasting daily on radio station KVOO and playing dances at Cain's Ballroom. In 1936 they made their first recordings for the American Record Corporation in Dallas. The electric steel guitar was new, and McAuliffe was among the first to make it a lead instrument. The 1930s Southwest was full of dancers who wanted swing, blues and fiddle tunes from the same band.","The melody is based on Sylvester Weaver's Guitar Rag, a 1923 recording by an African American bottleneck guitarist from Louisville. Listen to the slide technique: McAuliffe translates blues slide guitar into electric steel. That connection between Black blues and western swing is often overlooked but central to the music's sound.","The record was a big hit and turned the steel guitar into a lead instrument in country music. It became a standard for steel players, and every later steel guitarist learned it. Historians cite it as one of the defining records of western swing and of the electrification of country music.","Listen first for Wills's holler, follow the steel guitar's slides. Compare Count Basie's One O'Clock Jump to hear the swing beat Wills shared with jazz, and Django Reinhardt's Minor Swing for a European cousin of string-band swing. Then seek out Sylvester Weaver's Guitar Rag to hear the bottleneck blues it came from."],"bob-wills-new-san-antonio-rose":["The vocal version of Bob Wills's most famous tune, arranged for his full Texas Playboys orchestra with horns. Tommy Duncan's smooth baritone sings a nostalgic song of longing for a lost love tied to a place, while saxophones and trumpets play the melody in big-band style, fiddles and steel guitar adding the country flavour. The beat is a relaxed swing, and the tune is memorable from the first bars. It sounds like a dance band playing in a Texas ballroom.","Wills had recorded the tune as an instrumental, San Antonio Rose, in 1938. Its popularity led him to add lyrics and rearrange it for a bigger band with horns. By 1940 the Texas Playboys were among the largest and most popular dance bands in the Southwest, and the swing era was at its height nationally. Bing Crosby's cover helped make the song a national hit beyond country audiences.","Listen for the horns: they are arranged in the manner of swing bands such as those of Benny Goodman and Count Basie, but the melody itself has the shape of a fiddle tune. That fusion of big-band brass and fiddle-band melody is the essence of western swing. The original 1938 instrumental makes a revealing comparison, with fiddles playing what horns play here.","The record was a huge seller and became Wills's signature. Critics see it as the peak of western swing's popularity. The style declined after World War II, but its influence returned with Merle Haggard, Asleep at the Wheel and George Strait.","Listen first for the horns playing the melody, then for the fiddles answering, and then go back to the 1938 instrumental San Antonio Rose to hear the same tune carried by strings alone. Compare Benny Goodman's Sing, Sing, Sing for the swing-band sound, and Copland's Rodeo Hoe-Down for a classical composer's take on Western dance energy."],"bob-wills-faded-love":["A slow, mournful waltz built on a fiddle tune. The twin fiddles play the melody in close harmony, sweet and sad, and the vocal, a song of memory and lost love, adds a nostalgic layer. The arrangement is smaller than the big-band records of the 1940s, centred on fiddles and steel guitar. The three-quarter rhythm and the plaintive melody give it a quiet, elegiac feeling, unlike the hot swing numbers Wills is known for.","The melody came from a fiddle tune Wills learned from his father, John Wills, and the lyrics were written by his brother Billy Jack Wills. It was released in 1950 on MGM, when the big swing bands were in decline and Wills had moved to California. The song became a standard and was covered by Patsy Cline, Willie Nelson and many others.","Listen to the twin fiddles: they play in harmony, a technique that became a hallmark of Texas fiddle music. The song's origins in Wills's family fiddle tradition connect it to the old-time music he grew up with in Texas, long before western swing. That continuity of old and new is what gives it its depth.","It became one of Wills's most enduring songs, and Patsy Cline's 1963 recording is particularly famous. Critics see it as proof that western swing was not only dance music but also capable of deep emotion. Merle Haggard's later tribute recordings of Wills kept songs like this in circulation, and it is still played by Texas fiddle bands and dance-hall groups.","Listen to the twin fiddles first, following the way the lower fiddle shadows the tune a few notes beneath, then to the steel guitar filling the gaps. Compare Patsy Cline's later version for a more polished Nashville take, and Ernest Tubb's Waltz Across Texas for another Texas waltz."],"roy-acuff-the-great-speckled-bird":["A sacred song about the church as a persecuted but faithful bird, sung by Roy Acuff in a strong, tearful tenor. The band, the Crazy Tennesseans and later Smoky Mountain Boys, plays fiddle, dobro and guitar in a simple, solemn arrangement. The melody is slow and hymn-like, and Acuff's voice dominates, full of emotional intensity. It is a country hymn rather than a church hymn, sung with the urgency of a revival meeting.","Acuff recorded the song in 1936 for the American Record Corporation. The lyric draws on an image from the Book of Jeremiah and had circulated among Southern churches. Acuff's performance of it on the Grand Ole Opry helped make him a star after he joined in 1938. Country music of the 1930s mixed secular songs with gospel, and audiences expected both.","The melody is the same one used for the Carter Family's I'm Thinking Tonight of My Blue Eyes, Hank Thompson's The Wild Side of Life, and Kitty Wells's It Wasn't God Who Made Honky Tonk Angels. Listen for how the same tune carries sacred and secular meanings, a common practice in country music where melodies were shared freely.","The song became Acuff's signature and helped establish him as the leading voice of the Opry in the late 1930s and 1940s. Critics see it as an example of how gospel and country were intertwined. Its melody's long life through other songs shows the communal nature of early country music.","Listen first for Acuff's voice, which seems close to tears on the long notes, then for the dobro sliding underneath like a second, wordless singer. Compare the Carter Family's Keep on the Sunny Side for another early country gospel sound, and Kitty Wells's answer song for the same melody in a secular setting."],"marty-robbins-gunfighter-ballads-and-trail-songs":["Twelve cowboy songs, old and new, sung by Marty Robbins with a small acoustic group and close harmony singers. The sound is clean and intimate: a Spanish-style nylon-string guitar, rhythm guitar, bass and brushed drums, with the voices of a vocal trio echoing his lines like a campfire chorus. The centrepiece is El Paso, a nearly five-minute border ballad, about a cowboy whose love for a cantina dancer leads to a fatal gunfight, that unfolds like a short western film. Big Iron, Cool Water and The Strawberry Roan fill out a frontier songbook.","Robbins had grown up in the Arizona desert listening to cowboy films and to stories from his grandfather, and in 1959 he persuaded Columbia to let him record a whole album of western material in Nashville. Television westerns such as Gunsmoke were then the most popular programmes in America, so the subject was commercially shrewd. El Paso, at a length radio programmers distrusted, was released as a single anyway and went to number one on both the pop and country charts.","Listen to the guitar on El Paso. The ornamental Spanish-style runs that answer each verse were played by session guitarist Grady Martin, and they do much of the storytelling, conjuring the border town before the words do. Also notice how the backing trio enters only at certain moments, like a Greek chorus commenting on the action rather than simply harmonising.","The album became one of the best-selling country records of its time and El Paso won the first Grammy given for a country and western recording. Critics have long treated it as the great revival of the cowboy ballad in the rock and roll era, and later writers praise Robbins's narrative control. Some find the album's romantic view of the frontier dated, but its craftsmanship is rarely questioned.","Start with El Paso and follow the story as you would a film: notice when the tempo seems to quicken during the chase. Then play Big Iron, a tighter, more dramatic tale. Compare Copland's Billy the Kid for a concert-hall vision of the same mythic West, and Willie Nelson's Red Headed Stranger for a later, darker western concept album."],"marty-robbins-dont-worry":["A gentle, slow country-pop ballad in which a man tells a departing lover not to worry about him. Marty Robbins sings smoothly over soft piano, guitar and a hushed vocal chorus in the Nashville Sound manner. Then, in the middle, comes something nobody had heard on a hit record: a solo on six-string bass that sounds buzzing, torn and distorted, like a broken loudspeaker. The contrast between the velvet ballad and that raw, fuzzy sound is the record's lasting surprise.","The song was recorded in Nashville and released by Columbia in 1961. The Nashville Sound, with smooth vocal choruses and soft rhythm, was then the industry's way of competing with rock and roll for adult listeners. Session guitarist Grady Martin played the bass solo, and during the take a fault in the studio's mixing equipment distorted the signal. The producers decided to keep it rather than redo the solo.","The distorted solo is widely cited as the first fuzz tone on a hit record. The engineers could not easily repeat it, and Martin later recorded an instrumental built around the same sound. A few years later, manufacturers developed fuzz-box pedals to produce the effect on purpose, and it became central to 1960s rock, from the Rolling Stones onward. Listen to how unsettling the sound is in this polite context.","The record was a country number one and a top-ten pop hit, among Robbins's biggest successes. Its accidental fuzz has given it a second life in histories of the electric guitar, where it is treated as a founding moment of distortion. Fans of Robbins value it as a fine example of his smooth ballad style, a side of him often overlooked next to the western songs.","Listen first to the ballad as it was meant: smooth vocal, gentle backing. Then wait for the solo and hear how strange it sounds. Compare a 1960s rock record built on fuzz, or Glenn Branca's guitar works for a later art-music embrace of distortion, to hear what that accident set in motion."],"buck-owens-act-naturally":["A cheerful, fast country shuffle in which a jilted man jokes that he will be cast in a film as a sad and lonely fool, a role he can play without acting. Buck Owens sings in a high, bright tenor with Don Rich's harmony close behind, and the Buckaroos play a hard, clipped rhythm with trebly Telecaster guitars stinging between lines. There are no strings or choirs; the record is lean, loud and over in barely two and a half minutes, sounding like a bar band at full volume.","The song was written by Johnny Russell and Voni Morrison, and Owens recorded it in 1963 for Capitol, whose producer Ken Nelson let him use his own touring band in the studio. In Bakersfield, California, a town of Dust Bowl migrants and oil workers, honky-tonks demanded loud, danceable country. Act Naturally became Owens's first number one country single and began a run of hits that dominated the mid-1960s.","Listen to the drums: they are louder and more prominent than on almost any Nashville record of the time, giving the song a backbeat closer to rock and roll. In 1965 the Beatles recorded it, with Ringo Starr singing, on their album Help!, and Owens and Starr later recorded it together in 1989. That transatlantic journey started with this bright, punchy sound.","Critics see Act Naturally as a defining Bakersfield record and the beginning of Owens's peak years. The Beatles' cover gave it international fame and showed how much British rock had absorbed from country. Owens's lean style has been credited as an influence on country rock, from Gram Parsons to Dwight Yoakam.","Listen first for the guitar stabs and the loud drums, then for the two voices singing close together. Compare the Beatles' version, gentler and more playful, and then Merle Haggard's Mama Tried, the other great Bakersfield sound. The Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo shows where this lean style led."],"buck-owens-together-again":["A slow, tender ballad about the joy of reunion with a lost lover. Buck Owens sings softly and sweetly, far from his usual bright, hard style, and the band plays gently. The record is remembered above all for Tom Brumley's steel guitar solo, which glides and sings with remarkable smoothness and emotion. The arrangement is simple, with guitar, bass, drums and steel, and the song lasts just over two minutes.","Owens wrote the song and recorded it in 1964 for Capitol. It was released as the B-side of My Heart Skips a Beat, and both sides reached number one on the country chart. At the time Owens was the most successful country artist in America, and the Bakersfield sound was challenging Nashville's dominance. The ballad showed a softer side to his music.","Listen to the steel guitar solo: Brumley plays with a smooth, singing tone and long, sustained notes, and the solo is often cited as one of the finest steel guitar passages in country music. Steel players still study it. Also notice how Owens's voice is gentle and hushed, unlike his usual hard tenor.","The song became a standard, covered by Ray Charles in 1966 and by Emmylou Harris, whose 1975 version was a number one country hit. Critics regard it as one of Owens's finest songs, and Brumley's solo is legendary among steel guitarists. It shows that the Bakersfield sound could be tender as well as tough.","Listen to the steel guitar solo first, then to Owens's voice. Compare Emmylou Harris's version on Elite Hotel, slower and more open-hearted, and Patsy Cline's Sweet Dreams for a Nashville ballad of the same years. A good compilation of Owens's Capitol singles lets you hear this ballad beside the hard shuffles."],"charley-pride-kiss-an-angel-good-mornin":["A cheerful, mid-tempo country song about treating the woman you love with tenderness every day. Charley Pride's rich, smooth baritone carries the melody over a bouncy arrangement of guitars, steel, piano and a light backing chorus. The tune is simple and singable, and the chorus is the kind that sticks after one hearing. It is a love song with no trouble in it at all, unusual in country music, and its warmth is the whole point.","The song was written by Ben Peters and released by RCA in 1971, when Pride was at the height of his popularity; that year he was named the Country Music Association's Entertainer of the Year. Pride, a Black singer in a predominantly white genre, had been signed in the mid-1960s and built a huge audience through radio and touring. The song crossed over to the pop charts, unusual for a straight country record of the time.","Listen to Pride's phrasing: he sings directly, with little ornament and perfect pitch, a style that owes much to Hank Williams and Ernest Tubb. The arrangement, with its bouncy rhythm and light chorus, shows the polished Nashville production of the early 1970s, but his voice keeps it grounded in traditional country.","The song became Pride's biggest hit, a country number one and a pop crossover, and it remains his signature. Critics see it as a high point of early-1970s country pop and of Pride's career. His success challenged assumptions about who could sing country music, though he himself avoided talking about race in his performances.","Listen first for Pride's voice, then for the steel guitar. Compare Glen Campbell's Wichita Lineman for another early-1970s crossover hit, and Hank Williams's Your Cheatin' Heart for the tradition Pride came from. A greatest-hits set of his RCA singles is the easiest way to hear his range."],"charley-pride-just-between-you-and-me":["A mid-tempo honky-tonk ballad about a man trying to hold together a love that is slipping away. Charley Pride's deep, resonant voice sits over a classic Nashville band, with steel guitar and piano adding colour. The song is short and direct, with a strong, simple melody, and Pride sings it with restraint, letting the steel guitar carry much of the emotion. It sounds like the work of a seasoned country singer, though it was his breakthrough.","The song was written by Jack Clement, who also produced Pride's early records, and released by RCA in 1966. Pride had been signed by Chet Atkins, and RCA famously sent his early singles to radio stations without a photograph, so many listeners did not know he was Black. The song was his first top-ten country hit and launched his career.","Listen to how traditional the record sounds: Clement kept the arrangement free of the smooth Nashville Sound strings, giving Pride a honky-tonk setting closer to Ernest Tubb and Hank Williams. That choice emphasised Pride's deep roots in country music, a powerful statement given the racial politics of the time.","The song was a hit and earned Pride a Grammy nomination, beginning a run of success that made him one of the best-selling country artists of the next decade. Historians see it as a landmark moment in country music's history, when a Black singer broke into the mainstream on the strength of his voice.","Listen first for Pride's deep voice, then for the steel guitar. Compare Ernest Tubb's Walking the Floor Over You for the honky-tonk tradition, and Pride's later Kiss an Angel Good Mornin' for his polished crossover sound. Listen for how calmly he holds the last line of each verse."],"glen-campbell-wichita-lineman":["A three-minute portrait of a telephone lineman working alone on the plains, thinking of someone he misses. Glen Campbell's clear tenor sings Jimmy Webb's melody over a lush orchestral arrangement: strings swell and shimmer, a low guitar picks out the bass, and an eerie, pulsing figure at the end imitates the signal running down the wires. The song has no conventional chorus, ends without resolving and fades into strings. It feels both vast and intimate.","Jimmy Webb wrote the song after Campbell asked him for something like his earlier By the Time I Get to Phoenix; Webb said the image came from seeing a lineman on a pole in the flat landscape of western Oklahoma. Campbell recorded it in Los Angeles in 1968, with producer Al De Lory, using session musicians he had worked with for years. It became a top-ten pop hit and a country number one.","Webb considered the song unfinished and sent it to Campbell with a gap where a verse would be. In the studio the gap was filled by an instrumental break played on a six-string bass guitar, reportedly by Campbell himself. Listen to that low, twanging solo: it fills the empty space in the song with the sound of loneliness, and the incompleteness became its power.","The song was a huge hit and is now regarded as one of the greatest songs of the 1960s, often cited by critics and songwriters as a perfect example of pop craft. It has been covered widely, from soul to rock to jazz. Some country purists found the orchestral production too polished, but its emotional depth has won it a place in every tradition.","Listen first to the strings at the opening, then to the bass solo in the middle, then to the pulsing figure at the end. Compare the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds for another orchestral 1960s pop record, and Elvis Presley's Suspicious Minds for a different crossover approach."],"glen-campbell-gentle-on-my-mind":["A breezy, rambling song in which a drifter tells of a love that stays with him wherever he travels. A quick banjo figure drives the rhythm, guitars and bass keep a light pulse, and Glen Campbell's clear voice carries the long, winding lines of the melody, which pour out words like a stream of thought. The song has no chorus in the usual sense; it rolls along from image to image, open roads and railway yards, giving it a restless, free feeling.","The song was written by John Hartford, a banjo player and songwriter, who said he wrote it quickly after seeing the film Doctor Zhivago. Campbell recorded it in 1967 for Capitol in Los Angeles, and it won Grammy awards for both writer and singer. It launched Campbell's solo stardom and led to his television show, The Glen Campbell Goodtime Hour.","Listen to the banjo: it gives the song a bluegrass feel, but the rest of the arrangement is smooth Los Angeles pop. That mixture of country instrumentation and pop production was the key to Campbell's crossover success. Hartford's lyric is also unusually free in structure, more like a folk song than a Nashville hit.","The song became one of the most recorded songs of its era, covered by Elvis Presley, Dean Martin, Aretha Franklin and many others. Critics see it as a landmark of country-pop crossover, and it established Campbell as a major star. Hartford's royalties from it allowed him to pursue his own adventurous bluegrass music.","Listen first to the banjo, then follow the long lines of the lyric. Compare Bob Dylan's Blonde on Blonde, recorded in Nashville a year earlier, for a different approach to free-flowing songwriting, and Campbell's Wichita Lineman for his more orchestral style."],"bobbie-gentry-ode-to-billie-joe":["A quiet, spare song in which a Mississippi family's dinner-table conversation turns to news that a local young man has taken his own life by jumping from a bridge. Bobbie Gentry sings in a low, husky voice over her own fingerpicked acoustic guitar, with a string arrangement that enters slowly, dark and dissonant. The song tells its story through ordinary chatter, with the narrator's silent reaction hinted at rather than stated. It runs over four minutes and never raises its voice.","Gentry wrote and recorded the song in 1967 for Capitol, and Jimmie Haskell arranged the strings. She was unknown when it was released, and it went to number one on the pop charts and won several Grammys, including Best New Artist. The late 1960s were a time of social upheaval, and the song's quiet, rural Southern setting stood out against psychedelic rock and protest music.","The song never reveals what the narrator and Billie Joe threw off the bridge, and Gentry always refused to say, insisting the point was the family's indifference. Listen to the strings: they play dark, unsettled chords that suggest something is wrong long before the story reveals it. The guitar figure, repetitive and hypnotic, gives the song a sense of fate.","The song was a huge hit and remains one of the most discussed story songs in American music, its mystery inspiring a 1976 film. Critics praise its literary quality, comparing it to Southern Gothic fiction. Gentry's later withdrawal from public life has added to its mystique, and the song is often cited as an influence on country storytellers.","Listen once just for the story, then again for the strings. Compare Lefty Frizzell's Long Black Veil for another quiet story song with a hidden secret, and Lucinda Williams's Car Wheels on a Gravel Road for a later portrait of the rural South."],"bobbie-gentry-the-delta-sweete":["A loose concept album about growing up in rural Mississippi, mixing Bobbie Gentry's own songs with covers of Southern standards. The sound is swampy and soulful: horns, strings, rhythm guitar and a funky rhythm section, with Gentry's husky voice moving between sultry, playful and melancholy. Songs portray family reunions, river towns, church and work, and covers such as Big Boss Man and Parchman Farm draw on blues. The album flows like a set of short stories about one place.","After the huge success of Ode to Billie Joe, Gentry made this ambitious second album for Capitol in 1968, taking more control over the arrangements. It sold poorly compared to her debut, and Capitol was unsure how to market it. The late 1960s saw a turn toward Southern roots among rock and soul musicians, but Gentry's personal, literary approach was unusual for a woman artist at the time.","Listen to the album as a whole: songs connect through recurring places and characters, an approach closer to a novel than a collection of singles. Reunion, a song of overlapping voices, captures the chaos of a family gathering. Gentry's arrangements blend country, soul and blues, anticipating the Southern roots music of the 1970s.","The album was a commercial disappointment at first but has been reappraised as a lost classic of Southern music. A 2018 box set reissued it, and the band Mercury Rev recorded a tribute version, The Delta Sweete Revisited, in 2019. Critics now see it as an early example of the concept album in country music.","Listen from start to finish to follow the story of the place. Start with Okolona River Bottom Band for its swampy groove, then Reunion. Compare The Band's Music from Big Pink for another 1968 portrait of rural America, and Reba McEntire's Fancy to hear how a later star reshaped Gentry's songwriting."],"kris-kristofferson-kristofferson":["Kris Kristofferson's debut album gathers songs he had been pitching to other singers. His voice is gravelly and limited, half spoken, over simple band arrangements of guitar, bass, drums and keyboards. Songs include Sunday Mornin' Comin' Down, a portrait of a lonely man facing an empty Sunday morning, Me and Bobby McGee, Help Me Make It Through the Night and For the Good Times. The production is plain, putting the lyrics first, and the record feels like a songwriter's demo raised to art.","Kristofferson, a former Rhodes Scholar and Army helicopter pilot, had moved to Nashville in the mid-1960s and worked as a janitor at Columbia's studios while trying to sell songs. Fred Foster of Monument Records signed him and produced this album, released in 1970. Johnny Cash's recording of Sunday Mornin' Comin' Down that year won Song of the Year from the Country Music Association, and Janis Joplin's posthumous Me and Bobby McGee topped the pop chart in 1971.","Listen to the lyrics' detail: Kristofferson writes with a novelist's specificity, naming small physical things rather than general feelings. That approach, closer to Bob Dylan than to traditional Nashville, opened the way for the outlaw movement and later singer-songwriters. The album was reissued in 1971 as Me and Bobby McGee, after Joplin's hit made the song famous.","The album sold modestly at first but became influential once other singers' versions of its songs were hits. Critics now see it as one of the most important songwriting records in country history. Kristofferson's voice was often criticised, but his songs changed Nashville's sense of what a country lyric could say.","Start with Sunday Mornin' Comin' Down, then compare Johnny Cash's version. Play Me and Bobby McGee and then Janis Joplin's. Compare Willie Nelson's Shotgun Willie for the outlaw country it helped create, and Bob Dylan's Blonde on Blonde for its literary model."],"otis-rush-double-trouble":["A slow minor-key blues sung by a young man who sounds far older than his years. Otis Rush lets the band lay down a heavy, dragging pulse, with saxophones breathing long chords underneath, and then answers his own voice with guitar phrases that bend upward and hang there, quivering, before falling away. The song is about a man who feels shut out and short of luck, and the whole record is built on tension that never quite resolves: high, strained singing against a low, smouldering band.","Rush recorded for Cobra, a small West Side Chicago label run by Eli Toscano, where Willie Dixon worked as producer and songwriter after a falling-out with Chess. The West Side clubs favoured a newer, more urgent sound than the Delta-rooted South Side style of Muddy Waters: minor keys, gospel-tinged vocals and guitar lines influenced by B.B. King. Cobra was chronically short of money, and the label collapsed within a couple of years, but its handful of Rush singles defined the West Side style.","Rush was left-handed and played a right-handed guitar flipped over without restringing it, so the high string sat on top. That meant he bent strings by pulling down rather than pushing up, which gives his long bends their peculiar, almost vocal swell. Listen to how each guitar answer starts quietly and grows louder as the note rises: it is the opposite of the usual attack-and-decay of a plucked string.","The single sold modestly, but among musicians its reputation grew steadily, and young British players learned it from imported copies. Stevie Ray Vaughan named his trio after it, which tells you how deep the influence ran a generation later. Critics now treat Rush's Cobra sides as the purest statement of the West Side sound, and many rank this as the finest record he ever made.","Start by listening only to the guitar answers between the sung lines: each is a small speech of its own. Then listen again for the saxophones, which act like a church choir behind a preacher. Follow it with Magic Sam's West Side Soul to hear the same neighbourhood a decade on, and with B.B. King's Live at the Regal for the model Rush was stretching."],"otis-rush-all-your-love":["A record in two halves. It opens as a brooding minor-key blues with a stately, almost Latin rocking rhythm, Rush singing about the love he misses with a cry that cracks at the top. Then, partway through, the band suddenly kicks into a fast shuffle and Rush tears off a guitar break full of sharp, stinging notes before everything drops back to the slow groove. That switch of gears, from smoulder to sprint and back again, is the gesture everyone remembers.","Cut in Chicago for the small Cobra label with Willie Dixon supervising, this was one of Rush's last sessions for the company before it folded. The late fifties were a hard time for blues singles: rock and roll and doo-wop were taking the jukeboxes, and Chicago blues labels were looking for anything that sounded fresh. The West Side scene answered with a sound shaped as much by gospel and B.B. King's polished guitar as by the Delta.","The tempo change is not a gimmick tacked on: listen to how the bass line of the slow section already implies the shuffle, waiting to be doubled. The tune's rocking minor-key pattern is close kin to Magic Sam's own song called All Your Love from a year earlier on the same label, and the two West Side guitarists were clearly listening to each other.","The song became a cornerstone of the British blues boom: John Mayall's Bluesbreakers opened their 1966 album with Eric Clapton on a version of it, and young British players treated Rush's original as a set text. In America it remained a connoisseur's record. Today it sits on most lists of essential postwar Chicago blues sides, and the tempo-switching structure has been copied countless times.","Wait for the moment the band shifts into double time and notice how the guitar suddenly sounds brighter and harder. Then put on the Bluesbreakers version with Clapton straight after: the British take is louder and more distorted, but the shape is borrowed note for note. Magic Sam's All Your Love makes a revealing third."],"otis-rush-right-place-wrong-time":["Otis Rush's finest full-length album, a set of slow blues, funky shuffles and soul-tinged ballads played by a tight studio band with horns. Rush's voice has deepened since the fifties, and his guitar has grown more spacious: long, patient solos that build from a whisper to a cry. The title track sets the tone, a weary blues about bad timing, and the album moves between that ache and brighter, swaggering numbers that show how much soul music had rubbed off on Chicago blues by 1971.","Rush recorded the album in San Francisco in 1971 for Capitol, with producer Nick Gravenites, a Chicago-born songwriter who had moved west. Capitol then declined to release it, and the tapes sat on a shelf for five years until the small Bullfrog label issued them in 1976. By then the rock audience's appetite for blues had cooled, and Rush, already known for a stop-start career dogged by poor record deals, saw his best studio work arrive almost unnoticed.","Listen for how often Rush leaves silence before he plays. Where many blues-rock guitarists of 1971 filled every bar, he lets the horns and rhythm section carry the groove and drops in a single bent note, then waits. The album's tracking also mixes blues standards with newer soul material, a deliberate attempt to show that Chicago blues could live alongside Memphis soul rather than in a museum.","When it finally appeared it was well reviewed in the specialist press, and its reputation has grown ever since; many critics now call it the best blues album of the 1970s, and the delay has become a standard example of a major label misjudging a blues artist. Some listeners still prefer the rawer Cobra singles, but few dispute that Rush never had a better band or a better recording.","Begin with the title track and simply follow the guitar solo from its first quiet phrase to its peak. Then compare it with his 1958 Double Trouble to hear how the same man's style matured. Albert King's I'll Play the Blues for You, from the same years, is the natural companion, blues dressed in soul horns."],"magic-sam-west-side-soul":["A lean, urgent album by a small band: Magic Sam's guitar, a second guitar, bass, drums and occasional piano. Sam sings in a high, pleading tenor closer to soul singers than to the Delta shouters, and his guitar has a shimmering, wobbling tone from a tremolo effect, with chords that flicker like a neon sign. The tunes range from tearing fast shuffles to slow, aching minor blues and a driving version of Sweet Home Chicago. Everything is played as if the band were in a packed club at two in the morning.","Sam had made a few singles for Cobra in the late 1950s, but his career stalled after army service, and by the mid-sixties he was playing West Side Chicago taverns. Bob Koester's Delmark label, which had started with jazz and traditional blues, recorded him in 1967 for his first full album. The record arrived just as young white audiences in America and Britain were discovering Chicago blues, and it put the West Side style in front of them for the first time.","Listen to the rhythm guitar behind Sam's solos: it locks into a pattern with the bass so tightly that the band sounds bigger than it is, and Sam often plays chords and lead lines at once, thumb on the low strings and fingers on the high ones, rather than using a pick. The tremolo on his amplifier is part of the tune, not decoration; switch it off in your mind and the songs lose half their colour.","It was greeted at once as a modern blues classic and has never lost that standing; it is routinely named among the best Chicago blues albums ever made and was inducted into the Blues Foundation's Hall of Fame. Because Sam died at thirty-two only two years later, it has also become a record of what might have been, and debate continues over how far he would have taken blues toward soul.","Start with Sweet Home Chicago, then go back to Robert Johnson's original to hear how a solo Delta song became a full-band West Side romp. Notice how Sam's voice cracks upward at the end of lines like a gospel singer. Play the album loudly; it was made to be heard over a crowd. Then try Black Magic, its sequel."],"magic-sam-black-magic":["Magic Sam's second and final studio album widens the sound of West Side Soul with saxophone and fuller arrangements. There are hard shuffles that drive like a train, slow minor blues where Sam's voice soars and breaks, and soul-flavoured numbers with a lilting beat borrowed from Memphis. His guitar still shimmers with tremolo, but here he takes longer solos, building them in waves. The overall feeling is of a band at full stretch and a singer finding his range.","Recorded in Chicago for Delmark after the success of West Side Soul, the album came out in 1969, the year Sam's fortunes finally turned: his set at the Ann Arbor Blues Festival that summer was hailed as a triumph and offers of tours followed. In December 1969 he died suddenly of a heart attack at thirty-two. The album therefore became, without anyone intending it, his farewell, released as the blues revival he had helped to fuel was at its height.","Listen to the saxophone lines: they rarely solo, instead riffing in short repeated figures that push the rhythm, a trick taken from soul records of the time. The album shows Sam choosing songs from soul and rhythm-and-blues as readily as blues, which hints at the direction he was moving. His habit of jumping from a husky low voice into a sudden falsetto cry is at its most daring here.","Reviewers have long been divided over which of his two albums is better. West Side Soul has the rawness and the reputation; Black Magic has the stronger band and the more ambitious arrangements, and some critics consider it the more complete statement. Both are now regarded as essential, and together they are the main evidence for claims that Sam was the most gifted Chicago bluesman of his generation.","Play it straight after West Side Soul and notice what the saxophone adds: a punch on the off-beat, a sense of a bigger room. Then listen to one of Otis Rush's Cobra singles to hear the West Side style at its source. For a live comparison, seek out recordings of Sam's 1969 festival appearances."],"albert-collins-frosty":["A short guitar instrumental built on a loping, danceable groove, with Albert Collins playing a simple riff and then cutting across it with bright, piercing notes that sound almost like ice cracking. There is no singing at all; the guitar does the talking. The tone is thin, sharp and brilliant, very different from the warm, round sound of most blues guitar of the time, and the melody is catchy enough to hum after one hearing.","Collins was working the club circuit in Houston, a city with a lively Texas blues scene shaped by T-Bone Walker and the gospel and rhythm-and-blues labels based there. Instrumental guitar singles were popular in the early sixties, with Freddie King and others scoring with them, and Collins cut a run of tunes with chilly titles. Frosty became a regional hit and gave him the nickname and the image he kept for the rest of his career.","Collins tuned his guitar to an open minor chord and clamped a capo high up the neck, often around the seventh fret or above, which is why his notes sound so high and glassy. He also plucked with his bare thumb and fingers, snapping the strings against the frets. That combination, not any electronic effect, is the secret of the frozen sound in the title.","The record made Collins a name in Texas and across the South, though it did not make him nationally famous; that came only in the late seventies. Guitarists remembered it, however, and younger Texans such as Stevie Ray Vaughan and Robert Cray cited his early instrumentals as formative. Today it is treated as the calling card of one of the most individual guitar voices in the blues.","Listen first just to the guitar tone and compare it with Freddie King's Hide Away from a year earlier: King sounds warm and bluesy, Collins sounds sharp and cold. Then jump ahead to his 1978 album Ice Pickin' to hear the same sound in a bigger band."],"albert-collins-ice-pickin":["Albert Collins with his band the Icebreakers: horns, organ, bass and drums behind his searing guitar and gruff, good-humoured voice. The album mixes slow blues, tough shuffles and funky grooves, and several songs are half-spoken comic stories about domestic life delivered with deadpan timing. Then the guitar comes in, high, cutting and sudden, sometimes a single note held and shaken until it seems to scream. The sound is big, bright and full of personality.","After years of small labels and modest success, including a spell in California, Collins signed with Bruce Iglauer's Chicago-based Alligator Records, then a young label devoted to contemporary electric blues. Ice Pickin' was his first album for them and a turning point: it brought him to the international blues audience, set off a run of Alligator records and touring, and showed that a living Texas bluesman could make records that sounded modern rather than nostalgic.","Collins's guitar solos often start with a long held note that he pushes higher and higher, then release with a burst of fast picking. Listen too for the horn section's riffs, which come out of Houston rhythm-and-blues rather than Chicago. The comic spoken passages are carefully timed: the band drops away just enough for the punchline, then crashes back in.","The album was nominated for a Grammy and is widely regarded as Collins's best studio record, the moment his style found its ideal setting. Critics praised its energy and humour, and it helped establish Alligator's reputation. Some later listeners find the production dated in places, but the guitar playing is rarely questioned, and it remains a standard recommendation for anyone new to modern Texas blues.","Start with one of the spoken-story songs for Collins's wit, then a slow blues for the guitar at its most piercing. Notice how much space the band leaves him. Compare with Albert King's Born Under a Bad Sign for another big-band blues sound, and follow with Showdown!, where Collins trades solos with two younger players."],"albert-collins-showdown":["Three Texas-rooted guitarists in one studio: Albert Collins, Johnny Copeland and the young Robert Cray. They take turns singing and trade solos, sometimes all three in one song, so the record becomes a conversation in three distinct accents: Collins sharp and icy, Copeland rough and soulful, Cray smooth and clean. The repertoire is mainly blues standards and shuffles, played with the looseness of a jam session but the discipline of seasoned bandleaders.","Alligator's Bruce Iglauer brought the three together in the mid-eighties, when the blues was enjoying a modest revival. Collins and Copeland had both come up on the Houston club scene in the fifties, and Cray, then in his early thirties, openly counted Collins as one of his main influences. The album was conceived as a summit across generations, and the timing was good: Cray's own career was about to take off nationally.","Listen to how each player answers the others: they often pick up the last phrase of the previous solo and twist it, which is how cutting contests in Texas clubs worked. The differences in tone are not studio tricks but come from their hands and tunings; Collins's open minor tuning and capo give his solos a pitch range the others never reach.","The album won the Grammy for Best Traditional Blues Recording and became one of Alligator's best-selling releases. Reviewers praised the generosity of the meeting, with no one trying to crush the others. It is often cited as the record that introduced Robert Cray to a wider audience, and it remains a favourite example of the blues as a living tradition passed hand to hand.","Try to identify each guitarist by tone alone before the vocals tell you who is who. Then listen to Cray's Strong Persuader to hear where the youngest went next, and Collins's Ice Pickin' for the oldest in his own setting. B.B. King's Live at the Regal makes a fine companion."],"johnny-winter-johnny-winter":["Johnny Winter's major-label debut is a blues album, not a rock album dressed up as one. Over a spare trio of guitar, bass and drums, Winter plays blazing, rapid-fire lead lines and slashing slide guitar, singing in a hoarse, strained voice that pushes hard against the beat. There are slow blues stretched out to let him solo, fast shuffles, an acoustic Delta-style number on National steel guitar, and a cover of a B.B. King song. The playing is astonishingly fast but always rooted in the blues forms.","A young albino guitarist from Beaumont, Texas, Winter had played the Texas club circuit for years when a 1968 Rolling Stone article on the Texas scene singled him out. Record companies competed to sign him, and Columbia won with what was reported as one of the largest advances of its day. The album was released in 1969, as the American rock audience was turning to blues-based guitar heroes in the wake of Cream and Jimi Hendrix.","On Mean Mistreater, Willie Dixon plays upright bass and Walter Horton blows harmonica, a deliberate nod to the Chicago tradition Winter revered. Listen to the acoustic slide number: Winter's bottleneck technique there is drawn from the old Delta players, and it shows that his speed on electric guitar rests on a deep study of the country blues.","Reviews praised his technique but some critics found the speed overwhelming and the singing forced. Blues fans were more welcoming than many expected, and Winter's insistence on playing straight blues for a rock audience earned lasting respect. The album is now considered his strongest pure blues record before the Muddy Waters collaborations of the late seventies.","Start with the slow blues, where you can hear how Winter builds tension before releasing a flurry of notes. Then play the acoustic track and compare it with Son House's Death Letter Blues. Follow with Second Winter for the rockier side and with Muddy Waters's Hard Again for Winter as producer."],"johnny-winter-second-winter":["Johnny Winter's second album of 1969 turns up the volume. With his brother Edgar on keyboards and saxophone alongside the trio, Winter tears through rock and roll classics and blues originals at a furious pace. The record includes a version of Chuck Berry's Johnny B. Goode played as a breakneck guitar showcase and a slide-guitar version of Bob Dylan's Highway 61 Revisited. The sound is raw and loud, closer to hard rock than the debut, but the guitar language is still pure Texas blues.","Columbia released it in the same year as Winter's debut, riding the wave of interest after his appearance at Woodstock and his growing live reputation. Edgar Winter, a multi-instrumentalist with jazz leanings, would soon lead his own successful group. The late sixties rock audience was hungry for guitar heroes, and this record was aimed squarely at that crowd while keeping faith with the blues and early rock and roll.","The original vinyl release was a curiosity: three sides of music on two discs, with the fourth side left blank, because the band had more material than one record could hold but not enough for two. Listen to Highway 61 Revisited for Winter's slide playing, which turns a wordy Dylan song into something closer to a Mississippi stomp.","The album was a commercial success and is often regarded as Winter's best rock record. Some blues purists missed the restraint of the debut, while rock critics loved its energy. The Johnny B. Goode cover became a radio favourite and a staple of his live shows. Today it is valued as a high point of late-sixties blues rock.","Play Johnny B. Goode right after Chuck Berry's original to hear rock and roll reclaimed by a blues guitarist. Then listen to Highway 61 Revisited alongside Dylan's own version. For the opposite approach to blues guitar, try Otis Rush's Right Place, Wrong Time, all patience where this is all speed."],"muddy-waters-hard-again":["Muddy Waters, in his sixties, back at full power with a band that sounds like a Chicago club in 1955 but recorded with the punch of the seventies. James Cotton blows harmonica, Pinetop Perkins plays piano, and Johnny Winter adds guitar and whoops of encouragement. The album opens with a remake of Mannish Boy, with the band shouting and cheering through the stop-time riff, and moves through hard shuffles and slow, heavy blues. It sounds loose, joyful and enormous.","By the mid-seventies Waters's records for Chess had grown uneven, and after the label's decline he signed with Blue Sky, a Columbia-distributed label associated with Johnny Winter. Winter, a lifelong admirer, produced the album, recording it quickly with Waters's touring musicians and old friends. The goal was simple: capture Waters as he actually sounded on stage, not as earlier producers had tried to update him for a younger audience.","Winter left in the studio chatter and the band's whooping, which gives Mannish Boy its party atmosphere; listen for the voices egging Waters on. The arrangements deliberately avoid the psychedelic experiments of Waters's late-sixties Chess records, returning to the stripped Chicago sound. Winter's own guitar is kept modest, a producer serving his hero rather than showing off.","The album won a Grammy and is widely seen as Waters's finest late record, a triumphant comeback that restored his reputation. It led to further collaborations with Winter and a final burst of success. Critics now praise it as a model of how to record a veteran artist: get out of the way and let the band play.","Start with Mannish Boy and compare it with the 1955 original: the same riff, but heavier and crowded with shouting voices. Then listen for Cotton's harmonica, a direct link to Little Walter. Johnny Winter's own albums show what he brought to the room."],"taj-mahal-taj-mahal":["Taj Mahal's debut is a lean, rocking album of country blues reworked for an electric band. His voice is warm and rough, his harmonica cuts in short bursts, and Jesse Ed Davis's slide guitar slices across the arrangements. Songs by Blind Willie McTell, Sleepy John Estes and Robert Johnson sit beside traditional tunes, all played with a loose, swinging groove. Statesboro Blues, with its slide riff and driving beat, is the track that announced him.","Born Henry St. Claire Fredericks in New York and raised in Massachusetts, Taj Mahal studied agriculture before moving to California, where he formed the Rising Sons with Ry Cooder. That group's album went unreleased, and Columbia signed Mahal as a solo artist. The debut appeared in 1968, in the thick of a blues revival among young white audiences, and stood out for treating the old songs as living music rather than relics.","Listen to the slide guitar on Statesboro Blues: Duane Allman heard this version and built the Allman Brothers Band's famous recording on it, so the slide part here is the ancestor of one of the most copied guitar parts in American rock. Ry Cooder plays on parts of the album too, adding rhythm guitar and textures that hint at his later work.","The album sold modestly but was admired by critics and musicians. Its influence grew through the Allman Brothers' version of Statesboro Blues and through Mahal's long career of mixing blues with Caribbean, African and other traditions. Today it is regarded as one of the key albums of the blues revival, notable for its respect for the sources and its refusal to sound antique.","Begin with Statesboro Blues and listen for the interplay of harmonica and slide guitar. Then go back to Robert Johnson's King of the Delta Blues Singers to hear one of the sources. For a later step in Mahal's journey, try Kulanjan, where he meets Malian music."],"taj-mahal-kulanjan":["Taj Mahal meets the Malian kora master Toumani Diabaté and a group of West African musicians. The kora, a harp-lute with twenty-one strings, ripples in fast, interlocking patterns while Mahal's guitar and voice weave through them. Some songs are blues reshaped by Malian rhythms, others are Malian songs given a blues inflection. The sound is gentle, luminous and full of crossing lines, more like a conversation among neighbours than a fusion experiment.","Mahal had long argued that the blues had deep roots in West Africa, and this project put the idea into practice. The album was recorded with Diabaté and other Malian musicians, including singers from griot families, and released by Hannibal, a label known for world music. It came out at the end of a decade when Ali Farka Touré's work with Ry Cooder had already brought Malian guitar to Western listeners.","Listen for the moments when the kora and Mahal's guitar play the same pattern a beat apart: the result sounds like one instrument splitting in two. Malian griot songs often praise patrons or recount history, and Mahal adapts his singing to their phrasing rather than imposing blues structures, which is why the album never feels like a guest appearance.","Reviewers praised the album's warmth and the genuine exchange between musicians. It is often cited alongside Ali Farka Touré and Ry Cooder's Talking Timbuktu as a key cross-Atlantic blues record. Some critics found it too polite, missing the grit of Mahal's electric work, but most see it as a mature statement of a lifelong interest.","Start by listening only to the kora, then notice how Mahal fits his guitar into its cascading patterns rather than over them. Compare with Toumani Diabaté's solo album Kaira for the kora alone, and with Ali Farka Touré's self-titled album for Malian guitar blues. Then return to Mahal's 1968 debut to hear how far he travelled."],"bonnie-raitt-give-it-up":["Bonnie Raitt's second album blends blues, folk, New Orleans rhythm and blues and singer-songwriter ballads into a warm, relaxed whole. Her slide guitar is clean and expressive, her voice smoky and direct. There are horn-driven numbers with a strut, quiet ballads by contemporary writers including Jackson Browne, and Raitt's own songs. The title track has a jaunty, Dixieland-inflected swing, and the album moves between joy and reflection with an easy confidence.","Raitt, daughter of the Broadway singer John Raitt, had left Radcliffe College to play blues in clubs, learning from veterans such as Mississippi Fred McDowell and Sippie Wallace. Warner Bros. signed her, and Give It Up was recorded near Woodstock, New York, with local musicians. The early seventies saw a boom in singer-songwriters, and Raitt stood out for bringing blues slide guitar into that world.","Listen to the slide guitar: Raitt plays with a glass bottleneck on her middle finger, a technique learned partly from McDowell, which gives her slide a smooth, singing tone rather than a rough rasp. She chose songs by unknown writers such as Eric Kaz and Chris Smither, and the album helped introduce them.","The album sold modestly at first but was praised by critics, and many still consider it her best early record. It established her as a rare woman slide guitarist in a field dominated by men, and as a fine interpreter of other people's songs. Its easy mix of blues, folk and New Orleans horns anticipated the later Americana movement by two decades.","Start with the title track for its bounce, then a slow ballad for her voice. Notice the slide guitar's smoothness. Compare with Taj Mahal's debut for another blues revival album, and follow with her 1989 album Nick of Time to hear how her sound changed."],"bonnie-raitt-nick-of-time":["A polished, mature record that mixes blues, rock and adult pop. Raitt's slide guitar is still there, sleek and singing, but now framed by crisp late-eighties production. The title song, which she wrote, is a reflective piano-based ballad about ageing and time running short; other tracks swing with a rootsy groove, including a slide-driven version of John Hiatt's Thing Called Love. Her voice, now deeper and more weathered, carries the whole album.","After being dropped by Warner Bros. in the mid-eighties, Raitt signed with Capitol and worked with producer Don Was, who encouraged a cleaner, more direct sound. She was approaching forty and had rebuilt her life after a difficult decade. The album came out in 1989, during a period when established artists were finding new audiences through adult-oriented radio and MTV.","Listen to how Don Was keeps the slide guitar near the front of the mix even in the poppier songs: it is the thread that ties the album to Raitt's blues roots. The title track's lyric is about the changes that come with middle age, and its quiet piano setting is a deliberate contrast to the more rocking tracks around it.","The album won the Grammy for Album of the Year and became a huge commercial success, transforming Raitt from a respected cult artist into a star. Some longtime fans found it too slick, but most critics welcomed the deserved recognition. It is now seen as one of the great late-career comebacks in American music.","Start with Thing Called Love for the slide guitar, then the title track for the voice. Compare with Give It Up from 1972 to hear seventeen years of change. B.B. King's Live in Cook County Jail makes an interesting contrast in a very different blues sound."],"stevie-ray-vaughan-texas-flood":["A power trio playing Texas blues with the force of a rock band. Stevie Ray Vaughan's guitar is thick, loud and fluid; his voice is gruff and earnest. Backed by Double Trouble, bassist Tommy Shannon and drummer Chris Layton, he races through shuffles, slow blues and instrumentals. Pride and Joy, with its loping shuffle and bright riff, became his signature, while the title track is a long, slow blues with soaring, howling solos. The sound is direct, almost live, with little overdubbing.","Vaughan had spent years on the Austin club scene. In 1982 the band played the Montreux Jazz Festival, where David Bowie heard him and invited him to play on Let's Dance. The trio recorded Texas Flood quickly in Los Angeles at Jackson Browne's studio, and veteran producer John Hammond, who had signed Bob Dylan and Bruce Springsteen, helped bring it to Epic. It was released in 1983, when blues had little commercial presence.","The title track is a cover of a 1958 song by Larry Davis, a little-known Texas bluesman, and Vaughan's version introduced it to millions. Listen to Lenny, a quiet instrumental named for his wife, where soft, ringing chords recall Jimi Hendrix's ballads rather than any blues. His heavy strings and tuning down a half step give the guitar its huge, rounded sound.","The album was a surprise commercial success and earned Grammy nominations. Critics hailed Vaughan as the most exciting blues guitarist in years, though some found his debt to Jimi Hendrix and Albert King too obvious. It is now credited with sparking a blues revival in the eighties and remains one of the best-selling blues albums of its era.","Start with Pride and Joy for the shuffle, then the title track for the solos. Compare with Albert King's Born Under a Bad Sign to hear one of Vaughan's main models, and with Otis Rush's Double Trouble, the source of the band's name."],"stevie-ray-vaughan-couldnt-stand-the-weather":["Stevie Ray Vaughan and Double Trouble's second album pushes further in every direction. It opens with Scuttle Buttin', a breakneck instrumental, and includes the title track, a funky blues with a stuttering riff, and a nine-minute slow blues, Tin Pan Alley, that shows his control. Its centrepiece is a long version of Jimi Hendrix's Voodoo Child (Slight Return), played with fierce wah-wah and heavy distortion. The sound is bigger and more varied than the debut.","After the success of Texas Flood, Epic gave the band more time and a bigger budget, and they recorded in New York with John Hammond again overseeing. Vaughan's older brother Jimmie, guitarist of the Fabulous Thunderbirds and his first hero, plays rhythm guitar on the title track. The album came out in 1984 and reached a much larger audience, as blues guitar began its return to the mainstream through Vaughan and the players who followed him.","Tin Pan Alley was a favourite of Vaughan's in concert, and its slow pacing reveals his debt to the subtle, sparse playing of the Chicago greats as much as to Hendrix. Listen to Voodoo Child for the moments where he imitates Hendrix's feedback and tremolo-arm effects, then departs into his own phrasing.","The album was even more successful than the debut and received Grammy nominations. Critics praised its range, though some still felt the Hendrix cover was too faithful. Today it is regarded by many fans as his best studio album, and Tin Pan Alley is often cited as one of the finest slow blues of the decade.","Start with Tin Pan Alley and follow the solo's slow climb, noticing how often he lets a single note hang. Then play Voodoo Child next to Hendrix's original on Electric Ladyland and listen for where the two part company. Finish with In Step to hear Vaughan's last studio statement, leaner and more controlled."],"stevie-ray-vaughan-in-step":["Stevie Ray Vaughan's final studio album with Double Trouble is tighter and more focused than its predecessors. Crossfire, a punchy, horn-backed song, became a rock radio hit, and Tightrope has a gospel-flavoured lift. The guitar is as powerful as ever but more disciplined, with shorter, sharper solos. The album ends with Riviera Paradise, a long, gentle instrumental in a jazz-tinged mood, where Vaughan plays softly and lyrically.","Recorded in Memphis with producer Jim Gaines, In Step followed a period in which Vaughan put his personal life in order and returned with new focus. Keyboardist Reese Wynans had joined the band. It was released in 1989, and Vaughan toured widely, playing large venues. In August 1990 he died in a helicopter crash after a concert in Wisconsin, making this album his last studio statement with the band.","Accounts of the sessions say Riviera Paradise was cut with the studio lights down, and that the tape was nearly exhausted by the time the band finished, which is why it fades. Listen to how Vaughan plays it almost like a jazz ballad, with soft chord voicings, far from his usual roar. Several of the lyrics speak of renewal and balance, giving the album an autobiographical cast.","The album won a Grammy and was his most successful release in his lifetime. Critics praised its maturity and focus, and after his death it gained added poignancy. Some fans prefer the rawer early albums, but many see In Step as his most complete record and a sign of where he might have gone.","Start with Crossfire for the band's power, then end with Riviera Paradise for the quiet. Compare with Texas Flood to hear six years of growth. Buddy Guy's Damn Right, I've Got the Blues, from two years later, continues the blues revival Vaughan helped spark."],"robert-cray-strong-persuader":["Robert Cray's breakthrough album blends blues guitar with the smooth, emotional singing of sixties soul. His guitar tone is clean and stinging, with short, precise solos rather than long jams. The songs are tightly written stories of temptation, betrayal and regret, sung in a warm, controlled voice. Smoking Gun, about a man discovering a partner's betrayal, has a brooding groove and a sharp, biting guitar break, and became an unexpected pop hit.","Cray, who had grown up in a military family moving around the United States and Germany, formed his band in Oregon and spent years on the club circuit. His earlier albums for the independent Hightone label had built a following, and Strong Persuader was released in partnership with Mercury in 1986. Producers Bruce Bromberg and Dennis Walker shaped the album's polished, soul-blues sound.","Listen to the horn arrangements: they come from the Memphis soul tradition, and the band includes members of the Memphis Horns on several tracks. Cray's guitar solos are short but built carefully, often echoing the vocal melody before twisting it. Many of the songs were co-written by the producers, giving the album a unified, almost novelistic set of characters.","The album won a Grammy and became one of the best-selling blues albums in decades. Critics praised Cray for bringing a fresh voice and songwriting craft to the blues, though some purists found his sound too slick. It is now regarded as a key record in the blues revival of the eighties.","Start with Smoking Gun and wait for the guitar break, short and sharp as a slap. Then listen to a slower song for Cray's singing, which owes more to O.V. Wright and Otis Redding than to Muddy Waters. Compare with Otis Redding's Otis Blue to hear the soul influence, and with Showdown!, where Cray holds his own beside two veterans."],"rl-burnside-too-bad-jim":["Raw, hypnotic hill country blues from north Mississippi. R.L. Burnside plays electric guitar in long, repeating patterns over a single chord, while a drummer pounds out steady, trance-like rhythms. His voice is deep and rough, and the songs stretch out with little change, building intensity through repetition rather than through chord changes or solos. The effect is closer to a dance groove than to the structured blues of Chicago.","Burnside, a farmer and juke-joint musician from near Holly Springs, Mississippi, had been recorded by folklorists in the late sixties but was little known outside the region. Fat Possum Records, a small label founded in Oxford, Mississippi, to document local bluesmen, recorded him with music critic Robert Palmer producing. The album came out in 1994, at a moment when alternative rock audiences were looking for authentic, unpolished music.","Burnside learned guitar partly from his neighbour Mississippi Fred McDowell, and the droning, one-chord patterns come from that local tradition rather than from the Delta. Listen to how the drummer and guitarist lock into a groove and stay there, sometimes for minutes, with tiny variations; the music is about endurance and trance, not development.","The album was praised by critics and helped bring hill country blues to a wider audience. Burnside's raw sound appealed to rock fans, and he soon toured with younger bands. Some blues traditionalists questioned the label's marketing, but the music itself is now regarded as a vital document of a regional style that might otherwise have vanished.","Do not wait for a chorus or a guitar solo; let the groove carry you. Listen for the slight changes in the guitar pattern. Compare with John Lee Hooker's Boogie Chillen' for a similar one-chord approach, and with Junior Kimbrough's All Night Long."],"rl-burnside-come-on-in":["An unexpected collision of hill country blues and electronic production. Producers took Burnside's vocals and guitar parts and set them over drum loops, samples, scratching and studio effects, turning his trance-like grooves into something close to dance music. Some tracks keep his raw voice upfront, others bury it in beats. The result is strange, catchy and divisive, with Burnside's deep growl cutting through the electronics.","By the late nineties Fat Possum was looking for ways to reach new audiences, and the label turned to producers including Tom Rothrock, known for his work with Beck. Remixing older recordings and adding new elements, they created an album that sounded unlike any blues record before it. It appeared in 1998, at a time when electronic music and hip-hop were dominating youth culture.","Listen to how the producers loop short fragments of Burnside's guitar so they repeat exactly, which exaggerates the hypnotic quality already present in his live playing; the machines are doing on purpose what his hands did by instinct. One track was later used in a popular television drama, bringing Burnside's voice to millions who had never heard of hill country blues.","The album split listeners. Blues purists objected to the electronics, while others hailed it as a bold experiment that showed how naturally Burnside's repetitive style fitted with modern beats. It sold well and broadened his audience. Today it is seen as a pioneering example of blues meeting electronic music, and a precursor to later experiments.","Play it alongside Too Bad Jim to hear how the same voice and guitar patterns sound in two worlds. Notice how naturally the loops fit his music. For further listening, try Moby's experiments with old field recordings, or the more traditional Junior Kimbrough."],"junior-kimbrough-all-night-long":["Dark, droning hill country blues from Junior Kimbrough, who plays electric guitar in long, slowly shifting patterns over a single chord or two. His voice is low and moaning, and the songs unfold slowly, sometimes for seven or eight minutes, with a bassist and drummer locking into a heavy, rolling groove. The sound is hypnotic and brooding, like a trance that gradually deepens.","Kimbrough ran his own juke joint in Chulahoma, Mississippi, where he played for local audiences for decades. He was featured in Robert Palmer's 1991 documentary Deep Blues, and Palmer then produced this album for the newly founded Fat Possum label. Released in 1992, it was one of the first records to bring the north Mississippi hill country sound to a wider audience.","Listen to how Kimbrough's guitar lines circle and shift by tiny degrees, sometimes adding one note, sometimes dropping one, so the music seems to breathe. He largely ignores the standard twelve-bar form. The droning, one-chord style is related to West African music, and some scholars see it as a direct survival of older practice rather than a coincidence.","Critics praised it as a revelation, and it became an underground favourite among rock musicians. Bands such as The Black Keys later covered Kimbrough's songs, spreading his influence. Kimbrough died in 1998, and his albums are now regarded as essential documents of one of the last distinctive regional blues styles.","Let the music wash over you rather than listening for changes. Compare with R.L. Burnside's Too Bad Jim for a related but harder-edged sound, and with Ali Farka Touré for the possible African connection. Tinariwen's Amassakoul offers another kind of trance blues."],"junior-kimbrough-sad-days-lonely-nights":["Junior Kimbrough's second album is even darker and more hypnotic than his first. The guitar patterns are slower and more brooding, the grooves heavier, and his voice sounds weary and haunted. Songs stretch out for long minutes with little change, building a mood of loneliness and quiet intensity. The title track sets the tone, a slow, mournful drone that feels like a late night alone.","Following the critical success of All Night Long, Fat Possum recorded Kimbrough again, keeping the same small band and the same unpolished approach, with little interest in studio gloss. Released in 1993, the album reinforced his reputation among critics and among rock musicians who had begun to make pilgrimages to north Mississippi. Kimbrough continued to play at his juke joint in Chulahoma on weekend nights until his death in 1998.","Listen to the way the bass and guitar sometimes play slightly against each other, creating a subtle tension that never resolves. The recording makes no attempt to tidy the small slips and rough edges, which gives a sense of being inside the juke joint itself. Kimbrough's approach avoids the standard twelve-bar blues form almost entirely.","Critics praised its intensity and mood, and some consider it his finest album, darker and more concentrated than the debut. It has influenced a range of rock and indie musicians drawn to its drones. Kimbrough's work is now seen as a unique contribution to the blues, distinct from both the Delta and Chicago traditions, and the album remains a touchstone for anyone exploring the hill country style.","Listen in a quiet room at night and let the drones take hold; resist the urge to wait for a change. Notice how the drums push and pull against the guitar. Compare with All Night Long and with R.L. Burnside's Too Bad Jim. For a pointed contrast, play Burnside's Come On In, where similar patterns meet electronic beats."],"keb-mo-keb-mo":["Keb' Mo's debut album is a warm, intimate set of acoustic blues, mixing original songs with covers of Robert Johnson. His guitar playing is clean and fluid, his voice gentle and soulful. Songs move from gentle reflection to upbeat country blues, often with slide guitar. The album feels both rooted in tradition and personal, a modern singer-songwriter taking up the acoustic blues.","Kevin Moore, born in Los Angeles to parents from the South, had spent years in rhythm-and-blues and pop bands, including the band of violinist Papa John Creach, before turning to acoustic blues in his forties. He signed with Okeh, a historic blues and jazz label revived by Sony, and released this album in 1994, during a resurgence of interest in acoustic and roots music. Its success helped revive interest in the acoustic blues tradition among new audiences.","Listen to his versions of the Robert Johnson songs: he keeps Johnson's guitar figures recognisable but sings them warmly and without strain, giving the songs a reflective, personal tone rather than a haunted one. His own songs borrow Johnson's guitar ideas but wrap them in contemporary subjects and pop-shaped melodies, which is the album's quiet argument about what tradition is for.","Critics praised the album as a fresh, unpretentious take on acoustic blues, though some felt it was too polished and too gentle for the music's roots. It won awards from the blues community and established Keb' Mo' as a leading figure in modern acoustic blues; his later albums went on to win several Grammys. It remains his most often recommended starting point.","Start with one of the Robert Johnson covers, then compare it with Johnson's originals on King of the Delta Blues Singers. Notice how Keb' Mo' softens and opens out the songs. Then try one of his own songs to hear the tradition carried into the present. Follow with Taj Mahal's debut for another modern interpreter."],"gary-clark-jr-blak-and-blu":["Gary Clark Jr.'s major-label debut blends blues, rock, soul and hip-hop textures. His guitar tone is thick and fuzzy, his voice smooth and soulful, sometimes in falsetto. Bright Lights, with its slow, heavy riff, is the centrepiece, while other tracks range from retro soul to garage rock. A medley pairs Jimi Hendrix's Third Stone from the Sun with an old soul-blues song. The album is diverse, sometimes uneven, but always grounded in his guitar.","Clark grew up in Austin, Texas, and was encouraged as a teenager by Clifford Antone, owner of the city's famous blues club, where he played with veterans. He gained national attention after playing Eric Clapton's Crossroads Guitar Festival in 2010. Warner Bros. signed him and released Blak and Blu in 2012, at a time when the blues was finding new audiences through younger artists willing to mix genres freely.","Listen to the production: Clark and his producers layer heavy guitar distortion over soul and hip-hop rhythms, aiming for a sound that could reach beyond the blues audience. The medley pairs Hendrix's Third Stone from the Sun with Little Johnny Taylor's If You Love Me Like You Say, joining psychedelic rock to soul blues in one breath.","The album received mixed reviews; critics praised Clark's guitar playing and Bright Lights but found the stylistic range scattered and the production sometimes over-busy. It earned a Grammy and established him as a major figure in modern blues. Today it is seen as a key album of the blues revival of the 2010s, and an early sign of how far he would roam.","Start with Bright Lights for the slow, heavy riff, then the Hendrix medley. Notice how the fuzz on his guitar sits beside clean soul vocals. Compare with Stevie Ray Vaughan's Texas Flood for another Austin guitarist a generation earlier, and with Jimi Hendrix's Electric Ladyland for the source of much of his sound."],"christone-ingram-662":["Christone 'Kingfish' Ingram's second album is a confident set of modern blues. His guitar playing is fluent and fiery, his voice deep and expressive. The songs range from hard shuffles to slow, soulful ballads, with lyrics about growing up in Mississippi and finding his way. The title refers to the area code of his home region. The production is clean and polished, letting his guitar shine.","Ingram grew up in Clarksdale, Mississippi, the crossroads town of Delta blues legend, and learned the music through local youth programmes, including lessons at the Delta Blues Museum. His 2019 debut album for Alligator drew wide attention and a Grammy nomination. 662 was recorded with producer Tom Hambridge and released in 2021, during the pandemic, when live performance, the lifeblood of a young blues musician, was severely limited.","Listen to how Ingram's guitar solos combine traditional blues phrasing, the bent notes and repeated cries of B.B. King and Albert King, with faster runs drawn from rock players such as Jimi Hendrix. The title, his home region's telephone area code, signals the album's theme: many of the lyrics reflect on Clarksdale and its history.","The album won the Grammy for Best Contemporary Blues Album and was widely praised in the blues and mainstream press. Critics hailed Ingram as the leading young voice in the genre, a player whose roots and technique are equally secure. Some reviewers found the production too clean for his raw talent, but it is now seen as a sign of the genre's continued vitality.","Start with a shuffle for the guitar, then a slow ballad for the voice, which is deeper and older-sounding than his age suggests. Compare with B.B. King's Live at the Regal for one of his models, and with Gary Clark Jr.'s Blak and Blu for another modern bluesman pulling the tradition in a different direction."],"john-mayall-blues-breakers-with-eric-clapton":["The album that launched British blues rock. John Mayall leads the band on vocals, organ and harmonica, but the star is Eric Clapton, whose guitar is louder, thicker and more sustained than any blues guitar before it. The record mixes Chicago blues covers, including Freddie King's Hide Away and Otis Rush's All Your Love, with Mayall originals. Clapton also sings for the first time on record, on Robert Johnson's Ramblin' on My Mind.","Recorded in London in 1966 and produced by Mike Vernon for Decca, the album captured the British blues boom at its height. Clapton had left the Yardbirds because they were drifting toward pop, joining Mayall to play pure blues; within months he left again to form Cream. The cover photograph shows him reading a children's comic, which gave the record its lasting nickname, the Beano album.","Clapton played a Gibson Les Paul through a Marshall combo amplifier turned up very loud, creating a sustained, overdriven tone that alarmed the studio engineers and changed rock guitar. Listen for the sustain on long notes, which lets the guitar sing like a voice. Bassist John McVie, who plays on the record, would later co-found Fleetwood Mac.","The album was a major success in Britain and is now regarded as a landmark of blues rock. Clapton's tone influenced countless guitarists, and the album is often credited with creating the template for heavy rock guitar. Some critics argue the covers are too faithful, but its impact is undeniable.","Start with All Your Love, then compare it with Otis Rush's original: the same shape, but louder and thicker. Notice how long Clapton's notes ring. Follow with Cream's Fresh Cream to hear where he went next, and with Led Zeppelin's debut to hear what the next wave of British bands built on this sound."],"john-mayall-a-hard-road":["John Mayall's Bluesbreakers with Peter Green on guitar. Green's playing is quieter and more lyrical than Clapton's, with a haunting, singing tone and a gift for silence. The album mixes Mayall originals, sung in his high, reedy voice, with covers, including Freddie King's The Stumble, and features Green's instrumental The Supernatural, built on long, eerie sustained notes. The overall sound is moodier and more atmospheric than the Beano album, with horns on a few tracks.","Recorded in London late in 1966 and released early in 1967 on Decca, the album followed Clapton's departure to form Cream. Green, a young Londoner who had briefly deputised for Clapton, faced audiences who chanted for his predecessor. Mayall's band was a training ground for British blues musicians, and within months Green left to form Fleetwood Mac with drummer Mick Fleetwood, soon joined by bassist John McVie.","Listen to The Supernatural: Green uses long sustained notes and carefully controlled feedback, anticipating the mood of Fleetwood Mac's Albatross. His Les Paul had a neck pickup whose magnet had been reversed, so that with both pickups on it produced a thin, hollow, out-of-phase tone, one of the most imitated guitar sounds of the era.","The album sold well, though it has always been overshadowed by the Beano album. Critics now often rate Green's playing as more subtle and expressive than Clapton's, and B.B. King famously praised his tone. Many guitarists cite this record as a major influence. It is seen as a key document of the British blues boom and of Green before Fleetwood Mac.","Start with The Supernatural and listen to how much Green leaves unsaid. Then compare it with Fleetwood Mac's Albatross, recorded about two years later. Play The Stumble against Freddie King's original. Finally, set the whole album beside the Beano album to hear two very different guitarists in the same band."],"fleetwood-mac-then-play-on":["Peter Green's Fleetwood Mac moves beyond the blues into moody, atmospheric rock. The album includes long jams, gentle ballads and hard rockers, with Green and the new guitarist Danny Kirwan weaving guitar lines around each other over Mick Fleetwood's drums and John McVie's bass. Some tracks are quiet and reflective, others heavy and loud. The sound is adventurous, sometimes dreamy, sometimes intense, a step toward psychedelia and the progressive rock to come.","Recorded in 1969 and released on Reprise, the band's new label, the album followed the success of Albatross. Green was increasingly drawn to open-ended improvisation and to spiritual questions, while Kirwan, still a teenager, contributed softer, melodic songs. Jeremy Spencer, the band's Elmore James specialist, barely appears. The original edition did not include Oh Well, which was added to later pressings after its success as a single.","Listen to how Green and Kirwan trade parts, sometimes playing close harmonies that blur into a single voice. Some of the instrumental tracks were edited down from much longer studio jams, with sections spliced together, so what sounds like a continuous performance is often a collage. The album's title is taken from the opening line of Shakespeare's Twelfth Night.","The album was a success and is now regarded as the high point of the Peter Green era. Critics praise its ambition and atmosphere. Some find it uneven, but many see it as a bridge between blues rock and later art rock. Green left the band in 1970, and Fleetwood Mac later reinvented itself as a pop group.","Start with one of Kirwan's gentle songs, then move to a heavy jam to hear the album's range. Notice how often two guitars move as one. Compare with A Hard Road to hear how far Green travelled in two years, and with Fleetwood Mac's Rumours to hear how utterly the band changed after he left."],"fleetwood-mac-albatross":["A slow, gentle instrumental with no vocals. Peter Green and Danny Kirwan play a gliding, harmonised guitar melody over soft cymbal rolls and a quiet bass, creating a sound like waves or wind. The melody moves slowly, with long sustained notes and a dreamy, floating quality. It is calm and atmospheric, almost ambient, and utterly unlike the blues the band was known for.","Released late in 1968 on Mike Vernon's Blue Horizon label, Albatross became a surprise hit, reaching number one in Britain early in 1969, an unlikely feat for an instrumental by a band known for hard blues. It marked a clear break from the band's roots and showed Green's growing interest in mood and atmosphere. The success brought much wider fame and pushed the group toward the more experimental music of Then Play On.","George Harrison later said that the Beatles' Sun King on Abbey Road began as an attempt at something in the style of Albatross; play the two side by side to hear the shared guitar tone and drift. Listen also to the drums: Mick Fleetwood keeps almost entirely to soft rolling cymbals and toms, so there is no backbeat at all.","It was a commercial success and is now regarded as one of the classic British instrumentals. Some blues fans felt the band had abandoned them, but many critics praised its beauty and restraint, and it has been reissued and reused many times. Writers now often cite it as an early example of rock reaching toward the calm, textural music later called ambient.","Listen with headphones to hear how the harmonised guitars glide over the rolling cymbals. Notice that nothing in the piece hurries. Then compare it with the Beatles' Sun King on Abbey Road, and with Debussy's Prelude to the Afternoon of a Faun for a similar sense of drifting, unhurried calm from seventy years earlier."],"edwin-hawkins-singers-let-us-go-into-the-house-of-the-lord":["A church youth choir from Oakland and Berkeley, recorded almost as if for a keepsake. Edwin Hawkins plays piano and directs, a light rhythm section ticks along beneath, and a few dozen teenage voices sing hymns and new songs in bright, clean harmony. The sound is gentle by the standard of sixties choir gospel: no roaring, no long shouted vamps, but relaxed tempos, close voicings and a soloist who floats over the group. The record is best known as the home of 'Oh Happy Day'.","The Northern California State Youth Choir of the Church of God in Christ made the album to raise money for a trip to a youth congress, recording it in a Berkeley church on simple equipment and pressing only a small run. A San Francisco disc jockey picked up a copy and began playing one track, and within months a small independent label had reissued it nationally under the new name the Edwin Hawkins Singers. Gospel had found an unexpected path onto pop radio.","Listen to the arrangements rather than the famous single. Hawkins voices the choir in the close, slightly jazzy chords of contemporary pop and soul rather than the stark open harmonies of older Pentecostal singing, and he lets the rhythm section play softly on the beat instead of driving it. Several numbers are old hymn texts dressed in that new harmony, which is the album's quiet revolution: the words of the nineteenth century, the chords of 1968.","Older church leaders were divided: some heard a youth choir carrying the gospel to millions, others heard worldliness in the rhythm section and the commercial success. For pop audiences the album was a curiosity attached to a hit. Gospel historians now treat it as the founding document of contemporary gospel, the record that showed a choir could sound modern, sell widely and remain unmistakably sacred, opening the road for the Hawkins family, Andraé Crouch and everyone after.","Hear it straight through rather than jumping to the hit, and notice how few times anyone raises their voice. Compare a track with James Cleveland's 'Peace Be Still', made five years earlier, to hear the difference between a preacher's storm and a youth choir's sunshine. Then play Walter Hawkins's Love Alive to hear where the family took the sound next."],"walter-hawkins-love-alive":["A live service-length album recorded with the choir of Walter Hawkins's own Oakland congregation. The band is a full soul rhythm section, with organ and electric bass, and the choir sings with the polish of a studio group and the heat of a Sunday morning. Walter preaches and leads, and his sister-in-law Tramaine Hawkins takes several solos, most famously 'Changed' and 'Goin' Up Yonder', her voice clear, controlled and rising slowly to great power.","Walter Hawkins, Edwin's younger brother, had founded the Love Center Church in Oakland in the early seventies, and this album was recorded there for Light Records, the Los Angeles label that was building contemporary gospel as an industry. The Bay Area was alive with soul, funk and the Black Power movement, and Love Center was a deliberately open, modern congregation. The record became one of the best-selling gospel albums of its decade and started a long series of Love Alive sequels.","Listen to the bass player. Where older gospel bands leaned on piano and organ, the bass here moves like a soul record's, walking and syncopating under the choir, and the drummer uses the hi-hat patterns of Bay Area funk. The choir parts are written in tight, sustained chords that owe as much to the Philadelphia soul arrangements of the same years as to the church, which is why the album sounded so contemporary on radio.","Love Alive was received in the church world as a revelation and in the wider music press barely at all, a pattern gospel would repeat for decades. Its standing has only grown: 'Changed' and 'Goin' Up Yonder' became standards sung at funerals and services across the country, and Tramaine Hawkins's performances are still held up to young singers as models of restraint before release. Critics now place it beside Amazing Grace as a defining live gospel record of the seventies.","Start with 'Changed' and follow Tramaine Hawkins's shape: soft and nearly conversational at first, then a gradual climb until the choir surges in behind her. Then listen to the whole side as a service, with its preaching and its pauses. Pair it with Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace, recorded three years earlier, to hear two different West Coast church nights."],"andrae-crouch-the-blood-will-never-lose-its-power":["A slow hymn of devotion, sung by Andraé Crouch with his group the Disciples over piano and a light rhythm section. The melody is simple and wide-spaced, built to be sung by whole congregations, and the harmony under it moves in warm, gently surprising chords. Crouch's voice is not a powerhouse; it is light, slightly husky and intimate, and the arrangement leaves space around it. It sounds like a hymn that has always existed, which is exactly the effect he wanted.","Crouch wrote the song as a young man in Los Angeles, the son of a Church of God in Christ pastor, and recorded it on the Disciples' debut album, Take the Message Everywhere, for the new Light Records. He was working at the edge of two worlds: the Black Pentecostal church he grew up in, and the white evangelical youth movement of Southern California, which would soon become the Jesus Movement. The song travelled through both, ending up in hymnals of many denominations.","The song's strength is structural. The verse melody stays within a narrow range while the chorus opens up, rising to its highest note on the title phrase, so that a congregation reaches its loudest point exactly where the meaning is. Underneath, Crouch uses chord changes from the jazz and pop songs he loved, small chromatic slides between familiar harmonies that make an ordinary hymn shape sound modern without unsettling anyone singing it.","Within a decade the song had become one of the most widely sung gospel hymns written in the twentieth century, recorded by hundreds of choirs and soloists, Black and white. That reach was itself a kind of argument: Crouch showed that new gospel songs could cross the colour line in church at a time when radio and record shops still divided the market sharply. Hymnal editors now treat it as a standard alongside Dorsey's 'Precious Lord'.","Listen once to the original Disciples recording, with its spare piano, and once to any large choir version, to hear how well the melody carries a crowd. Notice how the chorus lifts. Then play Thomas A. Dorsey's 'Take My Hand, Precious Lord' to hear the earlier songwriting tradition Crouch inherited and quietly modernised."],"andrae-crouch-live-at-carnegie-hall":["Andraé Crouch and the Disciples in concert in New York, a small group rather than a choir, with electric guitar, bass, drums and keyboards behind a handful of singers. The set moves between slow worship songs and fast numbers that sound close to the soul and pop-rock of the early seventies, with crisp backbeats and bright vocal harmonies. Crouch talks to the audience between songs like a youth pastor, and the crowd sings back on the songs it already knows.","By 1973 Crouch had become the most visible figure in contemporary Christian music, playing to Black churches and to the young white audiences of the Jesus Movement alike. A Carnegie Hall date was a statement: gospel had played there before, notably with Mahalia Jackson in the fifties, but a band with electric guitars and a rock drummer playing church songs on that stage was new. Light Records released the concert as a double album.","Listen for the guitar. Most gospel of the time was keyboard music, but Crouch's group used electric guitar in a way borrowed from soft rock and soul, playing short chord stabs and fills rather than solos. The vocal arrangements are tight four- and five-part writing, closer to the Fifth Dimension than to a church choir, and Crouch often lets the group sing a cappella for a few bars before the band drops back in.","Traditionalists objected to the beat and the guitars, as they had to the Hawkins Singers; some white evangelical radio stations, for their part, found the group too Black and too funky. Crouch kept both audiences anyway. Historians of Christian music now see this period as the moment two separate religious music industries briefly met in one band, and the Carnegie Hall album as one of its best documents.","Notice how the evening alternates fast and slow songs, and how Crouch uses the slow ones to bring the hall into near silence. Listen for the moments when the audience joins without being asked. Compare Mahalia Jackson's Live at Newport 1958 to hear how much the sound of a gospel concert changed in fifteen years."],"andrae-crouch-soon-and-very-soon":["A brisk, joyful song about the believer's coming meeting with God, built on a short melody that repeats and rises in steps. Crouch's group sings it with handclaps and a bouncing rhythm section, and the tune is so simple that a room learns it on the first hearing. Underneath the cheer is a song about death, which gives it a double colour: celebration on the surface, farewell beneath. It closes in a call-and-response vamp that can be stretched as long as a congregation wants.","The song appeared on Crouch's 1976 album This Is Another Day for Light Records, at the height of his fame. It quickly passed from his records into the congregational repertoire, becoming a favourite at homegoing services, the Black church's name for funerals. In 2009 it was sung at the public memorial for Michael Jackson in Los Angeles, with whom Crouch had worked, carrying it to a vast television audience that had never heard of its writer.","The melody climbs one step higher each time the main phrase returns, a device as old as the spiritual and the field holler, and Crouch places the rise so that the third statement lands at the top of a comfortable congregational range. Listen also to the bass line, which walks upward in step with the melody, so the whole band seems to be ascending together. It is a song about going up, written so that it physically does.","Few songs of the seventies passed into church use as quickly or as permanently. It has been sung across denominations and continents, and its use at funerals has given it an emotional weight far beyond its cheerful surface. Some critics of contemporary gospel found Crouch's catchiest songs too slight; the counter-argument, which has largely won, is that simplicity is exactly what congregational songs need to survive.","Listen first for the step-by-step climb in the melody and the bass. Then hear a recording from a homegoing service or the 2009 memorial, where tempo and mood shift completely, and notice how the song bears both joy and grief. Pair it with Mahalia Jackson's performance of 'Take My Hand, Precious Lord' at Martin Luther King's funeral."],"andrae-crouch-mercy":["A lush, carefully produced album of worship songs and up-tempo numbers, with large choirs, layered keyboards, real horns and the polished studio sound of nineties R&B. Crouch sings less than on his early records and arranges more: songs open quietly on piano or synthesiser pads, then gather choirs and band in long crescendos. The writing ranges from hymn-like ballads to grooves close to new jack swing, held together by his gift for melodies that a congregation can pick up immediately.","Crouch had stepped back from recording through much of the eighties, while his arranging career boomed: he led the choirs heard on Michael Jackson's 'Man in the Mirror' and Madonna's 'Like a Prayer', and worked on film scores. Mercy, released on Quincy Jones's Qwest label, was his return as a recording artist, and it was received as the comeback of an elder statesman.","Listen to how the choirs are recorded: multiple choirs overdubbed and panned across the stereo field, so that a single entry sounds like a cathedral full of singers. Crouch had learned this layering partly in pop sessions, where he stacked his choir behind superstars, and here he turns it back on his own songs. The horn writing, too, sounds like his film work, swelling under the voices rather than punching like a soul section.","The album won wide praise in the gospel press and was a Grammy winner, confirming Crouch's position as a patriarch to the generation of Kirk Franklin and the Winans he had helped launch. Some critics found its production glossy compared with the raw Disciples records. Its standing is respectable rather than legendary; it matters most as the bridge from Crouch's seventies innovations to the big-budget gospel of the late nineties.","Start with a ballad and listen to how long Crouch waits before the full choir enters. Notice the horns behind the voices. Then play Madonna's 'Like a Prayer' and Michael Jackson's Bad album, where his choir work is heard in pop, and Kirk Franklin's Nu Nation Project to hear who inherited the sound."],"shirley-caesar-no-charge":["A spoken and sung story about a child who hands his mother a bill for his chores and receives in reply a list of everything she has done for him, free. Shirley Caesar begins almost conversationally, half talking over a slow piano and organ groove, acting out both voices, then breaks into song as the story turns toward its moral about a mother's love and, by extension, God's. Her voice is sharp, flexible and full of grit, shifting from comedy to tears within a few bars.","The song was written by the country songwriter Harlan Howard and had been a country hit for Melba Montgomery in 1974. Caesar, by then a solo star after years with the Caravans, recognised in it the shape of the sermonette, the short spoken story she had made her signature, and recorded it for Hob, a New York gospel label. Her version took a Nashville tearjerker into Black church repertoire, where it has stayed ever since.","Listen to how Caesar adds to the written song. She expands the spoken sections, inserts asides and lets the band vamp while she tells the story, so a three-minute country song becomes a six-minute performance. That habit comes straight from the Pentecostal preaching she grew up hearing in Durham, where a speaker builds rhythm in speech until it tips into singing. The music swells exactly when her speech starts to swing.","Her recording became one of the best-known gospel performances of the seventies and a staple of her concerts, though it was often treated as a novelty outside the church. It is now a favourite example of the long traffic between Black gospel and white country music, which share hymns, themes and audiences more than their marketing ever admitted. Caesar's sermonette style has since been studied as an art form in its own right.","Listen first to Melba Montgomery's country version, then to Caesar's, and notice what she adds: the acted voices, the pauses, the moment speech turns into song. Then hear Rev. C. L. Franklin's 'The Eagle Stirreth Her Nest' to hear the preaching tradition her sermonettes come from."],"shirley-caesar-hold-my-mule":["A live sermon-song about an old farmer whose church tells him to stop shouting during services, and who decides to praise God his own way anyway. Shirley Caesar tells the story in a rising preacher's cadence while the band answers her phrases, the choir waits, and the crowd laughs and shouts. As the story reaches the farmer's defiant joy, the tempo jumps, the organ starts to roar and the whole performance turns into a holy dance, voices and drums racing together.","Recorded live in Chicago with the Thompson Community Singers, the performance shows Caesar at the height of her powers as a stage preacher, two decades into her solo career and by then also an ordained minister and Durham city councillor. In 2016 a short passage from it, a spoken list of foods, was remixed into a dance track and became a viral video challenge, bringing a gospel sermon from 1988 to millions of teenagers who had no idea where it came from.","Listen to the organist, who never plays a written part but follows Caesar's speech word by word, punching chords at the ends of her phrases. This is the Pentecostal practice of 'tuning up' a sermon, in which the preacher's spoken rhythm gradually becomes musical pitch and the band locks onto it. The jump into the fast shout section happens on her cue, not the band's, and you can hear them scramble joyously to follow.","Inside the church the performance was admired as a model of the sung sermon, and Caesar's comic timing compared to the great preachers. The 2016 viral remix sparked a small debate: some saw sacred material stripped of its meaning, while Caesar herself embraced it, appearing in videos and welcoming the attention. Scholars now cite the episode as a case study in how internet culture recirculates older Black vernacular performance.","Hear the whole performance, not just the famous excerpt, and listen for the point where speech turns to song and then to dance. Follow the organ. Then compare Rev. C. L. Franklin's recorded sermons and Aretha's Amazing Grace, which grew from the same practice, to hear how preaching and singing are one art in this tradition."],"clark-sisters-is-my-living-in-vain":["A slow, searching song in which a believer asks whether her life of faith has been worth it and answers that it has. The four Clark sisters sing in close, sliding harmony over Hammond organ and a patient rhythm section, trading leads and decorating each line with fast runs and bends. The opening is hushed and almost bluesy; the end piles the voices into a dense, shimmering chord and a long vamp of runs that seem to tumble over each other.","The Clark Sisters were the daughters of Mattie Moss Clark, the formidable choir director of the Church of God in Christ in Detroit, who trained them from childhood. This song was written by Elbernita 'Twinkie' Clark, the group's organist and chief writer, for the album of the same name on Sound of Gospel, a small Detroit label. It became one of their signature songs and a standard in Pentecostal churches.","Listen to the harmonies, which use clusters of close notes that classical choirs would call dissonant and that the sisters bend in pitch as they hold them. That sound, along with their lightning-fast vocal runs, became the template for a generation of R&B singers: much of what is heard in nineties and later melisma, from Mariah Carey to Beyoncé, traces back through the Clarks. Twinkie's Hammond comments on every line like a fifth voice.","The song established the Clarks as the most influential female group in modern gospel, and its vocal style was studied and imitated by secular singers who rarely credited it. For years the sisters' recognition lagged their influence, partly because their records were on small labels. Critics and singers now place them at the root of contemporary vocal technique, and the song is a test piece for gospel and R&B vocalists.","Listen for the moment the four voices lock into their final chord, and notice how they bend it rather than holding it still. Then hear their 'You Brought the Sunshine' for the dance-floor side of the group, and Beyoncé's records to hear where the vocal runs went."],"clark-sisters-you-brought-the-sunshine":["A bright, up-tempo song of thanksgiving built on a funk groove: a slapping bass line, crisp drums, a chirping synthesiser and Twinkie Clark's Hammond organ. The sisters sing in tight unison and harmony, trading call-and-response lines, and the chorus repeats like a dance-floor hook. Halfway through the song breaks down to bass, drums and handclaps before the voices return in a stacked, ecstatic vamp. It sounds like a disco or post-disco record that happens to be about God.","The Clark Sisters recorded the song for a 1981 album on Sound of Gospel in Detroit, the city of Motown, P-Funk and, by then, early electronic dance music. It crossed out of the church in an unusual way: DJs in New York clubs began playing it, and it reached R&B radio, making the Clarks one of the few gospel acts of the era to be heard in secular nightlife. For a group from a strict Pentecostal home, that was a remarkable journey for a single song to make.","Listen to the synthesiser line, which Twinkie Clark plays like a Stevie Wonder keyboard figure, and to the breakdown, which sounds designed for a DJ to mix even though it was made for a church audience. The vocal hook is sung in a deliberately simple unison so that the complex harmonies around it stand out. The song's structure, intro groove, verses, breakdown, rebuild, is essentially that of a twelve-inch dance mix.","Some in the church were uneasy at hearing a gospel song in nightclubs; most were delighted at the reach. For dance-music historians the record is part of the gospel undercurrent in New York club culture, where DJs like Larry Levan treated soaring church voices as the music's spiritual core. It is now among the best-loved gospel recordings of the eighties, constantly covered by choirs.","Hear it loud, and listen for the breakdown where everything drops out but bass and drums. Notice the synthesiser. Then play Larry Levan's Paradise Garage recordings and Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life to hear the club and soul sounds it shared a city's air with."],"mighty-clouds-of-joy-mighty-high":["A gospel quartet singing over a full disco production: four-on-the-floor drums, swirling strings, horns and wah-wah guitar. The song is about the joy of faith lifting a person higher than anything else could, and the Mighty Clouds sing it with the rough, shouting lead and stacked harmonies of the quartet tradition, Joe Ligon's gravelly voice cutting through the polished arrangement. The contrast is the point: a sound from the Pentecostal church dropped into the clean, glossy groove of mid-seventies dance music.","The Mighty Clouds of Joy had formed in Los Angeles in the mid-fifties and were among the leading gospel quartets of the sixties. In the seventies they signed with ABC and worked with soul producers to reach wider audiences, and 'Mighty High' became their biggest crossover, played on R&B radio and in discos. Their records reached listeners who had never set foot in a Pentecostal church, and the group kept performing for those new audiences while still singing in churches.","Listen past the strings to the vocal arrangement, which is pure quartet tradition: one lead voice preaching against a group that answers in tight blocks, with the bass voice anchoring the bottom. That call-and-response structure is exactly what disco borrowed from gospel in the first place, so the record is less a betrayal than a homecoming. The production simply makes explicit how close the two musics already were.","The record split opinion. Many gospel listeners thought it worldly, and some radio stations hesitated to play it; disco fans heard it as just another good dance record. Later historians of disco, tracing the genre's roots in Black church music, have treated it as a revealing document, a moment when a gospel group walked through the door disco had opened. The group continued in traditional style for decades afterward.","Notice the lead voice, still the rough quartet shout, against the smooth strings. Then hear the Swan Silvertones' 'Mary Don't You Weep' to hear the quartet style unadorned, and Donna Summer's 'Love to Love You Baby' to hear the disco production at its purest."],"the-winans-introducing-the-winans":["The debut of four Detroit brothers, Marvin, Carvin, Michael and Ronald Winans, singing in smooth, close harmony over keyboards, guitar and a light soul rhythm section. The sound is closer to the Commodores or the Spinners than to the old gospel quartets: sweet tenor leads, falsetto flourishes, gentle grooves and ballads that could sit on quiet storm radio. The songs keep a clear gospel message, but the textures are those of early eighties R&B.","The brothers came from a large, devout family in Detroit; their parents and younger siblings, including BeBe and CeCe, would all record. Andraé Crouch heard them and brought them to Light Records, producing this first album. It arrived at a moment when gospel and R&B were drifting apart commercially, and the Winans set out to close the gap, making church music that sounded like the soul records young people actually played.","Listen to the stacked harmonies. The four brothers sing in voicings borrowed from jazz and soul vocal groups, with the top part often in falsetto, and they use the slick, precise blend of studio R&B rather than the rough edges of the quartet tradition. Crouch's production keeps the band understated so that the voices carry everything, which is why the album still sounds intimate rather than dated.","Traditional listeners heard too much R&B; younger listeners heard their own music speaking about faith. The album launched a family dynasty that would dominate contemporary gospel for twenty years. Critics now place it at the start of the urban contemporary gospel sound, the bridge between Crouch's seventies innovations and the full R&B production of the late eighties and nineties.","Listen to the harmonies on the ballads and compare them with Boyz II Men's Cooleyhighharmony a decade later; the family resemblance is direct. Then hear the Winans with Anita Baker on 'Ain't No Need to Worry' to hear how far they took the sound."],"the-winans-aint-no-need-to-worry":["A soulful, mid-tempo song of reassurance that God will carry a believer through trouble, sung by the Winans with Anita Baker as guest lead. The groove is slow and warm, with electric piano, fat bass and a quiet backbeat, and Baker's low, smoky voice sits over the brothers' high harmonies. Each side takes verses; together they build to a final section where her improvisations and their harmonies wind around each other in a long fade.","By 1987 the Winans had moved to Quincy Jones's Qwest label and were making records with full R&B production. Anita Baker had just become one of the biggest soul singers in America with Rapture. Both came from Detroit churches, and the collaboration, on the Winans' album Decisions, was a public declaration that the line between gospel and R&B was thinner than the industry pretended.","Notice how the arrangement leaves gaps for Baker to fill. She sings slightly behind the beat, as in her own ballads, while the brothers sing on it, so the song has two time-feels at once. The keyboard harmonies are the extended chords of quiet storm radio, and nothing in the instrumentation signals church; only the subject does. That restraint is what let it be played on secular stations.","The record won wide praise and awards, and it is often cited as one of the most successful gospel and R&B collaborations of the decade. A few church voices objected to the soul production, as they had to everything the Winans did. Today it is remembered as the moment urban contemporary gospel fully arrived, polished, adult and radio-ready.","Listen to how Baker and the brothers handle the beat differently. Then play Anita Baker's Rapture to hear her own world, and the Winans' 'It's Time' to hear the group take on new jack swing three years later. Their own debut, Introducing the Winans, shows the starting point."],"the-winans-its-time":["A hard-hitting call to spiritual readiness set to a full new jack swing production: swung drum-machine beats, punchy synthesiser stabs and a bass line built for dancing. The Winans sing in their sweet four-part blend over machinery that sounds like Guy or Bobby Brown, and the chorus is a chant designed to be shouted back. It is gospel sung in the exact musical language of 1990 urban radio.","The track came from the Winans' album Return and was produced with Teddy Riley, the architect of new jack swing, who had built hits for Guy, Bobby Brown and Keith Sweat. Hiring him was a calculated move to reach teenagers who heard gospel as old people's music. It was a bold bet: new jack swing was the sound of street fashion and nightlife, and some churches resisted.","Listen to the drum programming, which uses the swung sixteenth-note shuffle that defined Riley's sound, and notice how the brothers' harmonies sit right on top of it, crisp rather than loose. The production would have fitted unchanged on a Guy album; only the words and the purity of the harmony give the game away, years before gospel and hip-hop production merged fully.","Reaction split along predictable lines: younger listeners and radio embraced it; many church leaders objected that the music was too close to the clubs. In hindsight it is a direct ancestor of Kirk Franklin's 'Stomp', which made the same move with greater success seven years later. Critics now treat it as a key experiment in gospel's long argument about how far to borrow from secular style.","Play it next to Guy's first album and Bobby Brown's Don't Be Cruel to hear how exactly it fits its moment. Then jump forward to Kirk Franklin's 'Stomp' to hear the experiment perfected, and back to the Winans with Anita Baker to hear how smooth the group sounded before Riley arrived."],"bebe-and-cece-winans-heaven":["A sleek, adult R&B album by the youngest Winans siblings, BeBe and CeCe, singing duets of love and faith over keyboards, programmed drums and smooth bass lines. BeBe's warm tenor and CeCe's light, pure soprano blend almost like a single voice, and the songs move from slow ballads to mid-tempo grooves. The production is pristine and late-eighties, close to Whitney Houston or Luther Vandross, with the gospel message carried mostly in the words rather than the sound.","BeBe and CeCe had sung on the Christian television programme The PTL Club before launching as a duo. Heaven, their second album, was released jointly by Sparrow, a Christian label, and Capitol, a mainstream major, a sign of how far the industry thought gospel could now travel. Whitney Houston, a friend, sang on one track, 'Hold Up the Light', underlining the duo's link to the pop world.","Listen to the duet writing. Instead of the usual call-and-response, BeBe and CeCe often sing in close parallel harmony through whole phrases, so the listener hears a single blended line. Their breath, phrasing and vibrato are matched with a precision that suggests years of singing together in a family. The arrangements also use space carefully, leaving whole bars with only keyboard and voice.","The album was a major success on both gospel and R&B charts and established the duo as stars beyond the church. Some gospel critics found it too smooth and too secular in sound. Others argued it was simply the next step in what their brothers had started. It remains one of the defining crossover gospel records of the late eighties.","Notice how the two voices blend in parallel harmony. Listen for Whitney Houston on 'Hold Up the Light'. Then play Whitney Houston's debut album to hear the pop world it borrowed from, and CeCe's Alone in His Presence for her later, plainer worship style."],"cece-winans-alone-in-his-presence":["A quiet album of old hymns and new worship songs, sung by CeCe Winans with piano, soft strings and a minimal rhythm section. After years of glossy R&B duets, the sound here is stripped down and intimate: her light, clear voice sings close to the microphone, with little ornament, and the arrangements build slowly from solo piano. Familiar hymns sit beside contemporary praise songs, all treated in the same unhurried, prayerful manner.","This was CeCe Winans's first solo album after years in the duo with her brother BeBe. Rather than chase a pop crossover, she chose hymns and worship songs, part of a broader turn in nineties Christian music toward praise and worship repertoire suited to congregations. The album was released on Sparrow, a Christian label, and became a major seller and a Grammy winner.","Listen to how much space the arrangements leave. Often the piano plays a single sustained chord while Winans sings a whole phrase, and the strings enter so softly you notice them only when they stop. Her phrasing on the old hymns keeps the gospel habit of bending notes and slightly delaying the melody, but she holds back the big runs, saving them for a few climactic moments where they land with great effect.","The album was praised for its restraint and devotional seriousness and helped launch the praise and worship boom in Black gospel. A few critics wanted more of the R&B energy of her duo records. Its influence has been long: it is often cited as a model for intimate worship albums, and many of its arrangements are sung in churches today.","Hear it in a quiet room and listen for the moments when Winans finally lets loose. Notice how the strings support rather than lead. Then compare BeBe & CeCe's Heaven to hear how much she pared back, and Richard Smallwood's 'Total Praise' for a choir hymn in the same devotional spirit."],"cece-winans-believe-for-it":["A live worship song about trusting God for what has not yet happened, sung by CeCe Winans with a congregation and band. It opens with soft keyboards and a simple, repeating melody, then builds over seven minutes through a series of rising choruses until the band is at full volume and the crowd is singing with her. The style is modern worship music, the anthemic, slowly rising form of contemporary church song, coloured by Winans's gospel phrasing.","Recorded live at a church in Nashville, where Winans and her husband lead a congregation, the song appeared on an album of the same name. Gospel had by then merged with the global contemporary worship movement, which uses long, building songs designed for congregations to sing together. The release reached gospel and Christian audiences alike and won several Grammys, making Winans one of the most decorated gospel artists in history.","Notice the structure: there is almost no verse-chorus alternation in the old sense, but a long series of repetitions, each slightly louder and higher, with the band adding one element at a time. This is the build of modern worship music, designed to bring a congregation gradually from quiet to full voice. Winans's ornaments on the repeats are what mark it as gospel rather than evangelical worship pop.","The song was a major success in both gospel and contemporary Christian markets, and its awards confirmed Winans as an elder of the tradition four decades into her career. Some gospel critics have worried that the worship style has flattened the distinctive sounds of Black gospel; others hear in Winans's version a successful merger. The debate about gospel's place within broader worship music is very much alive.","Follow the build and notice when each instrument joins. Listen to how Winans decorates the repeats. Then compare her Alone in His Presence, a quieter worship album from 1995, to hear how the form of church song has changed, and Marvin Sapp's 'Never Would Have Made It' for another long live build."],"commissioned-go-tell-somebody":["A Detroit male vocal group singing gospel over the synthesisers and drum machines of mid-eighties R&B. The harmonies are smooth and tight, close to New Edition or the Force MDs, and the grooves are bright and danceable, with synth-bass lines and gated drums. Ballads alternate with up-tempo songs, and the leads are shared among several singers, each with a different colour. The gospel message runs through every song, but the sound belongs to Detroit radio of the time.","Commissioned grew out of the Detroit church scene that also produced the Winans and the Clark Sisters, and it was led by the bassist and singer Fred Hammond, who would become one of the most influential figures in later gospel. The group signed with Light Records and aimed squarely at young listeners, matching the sounds of secular R&B. It became the training ground for several later gospel stars, including Marvin Sapp.","Listen to the bass. Fred Hammond's playing, busy and melodic, is the engine of the group's sound and anticipates the funk-driven style of his later solo work. The harmonies use the close voicing of eighties R&B groups, with a falsetto top line, and the arrangements often drop the instruments out for a few bars of pure voices, a nod to the quartet tradition hidden inside the drum-machine production.","Commissioned were embraced by young church audiences and dismissed by some older ones, much like the Winans. The group's greatest legacy may be its alumni: Fred Hammond and Marvin Sapp both became major solo artists. Critics now see Commissioned as a crucial link in the urban contemporary gospel chain, between the Winans and the praise-and-worship and hip-hop-influenced styles of the nineties.","Listen to the bass lines first, then the harmonies. Compare New Edition's Heart Break to hear the R&B vocal groups they shared a sound with. Then play Fred Hammond's 'No Weapon' and Marvin Sapp's 'Never Would Have Made It' to hear where the group's members went."],"fred-hammond-no-weapon":["A slow, steady song of spiritual protection, sung by Fred Hammond with his choir Radical for Christ. The arrangement is built on a deep, rolling bass line, simple keyboards and drums, with the choir singing a firm, unhurried chorus. Hammond leads in a warm baritone, and the song builds by repetition rather than dramatic leaps, ending in a long chant that a congregation can sing indefinitely. Its quiet confidence is what people remember.","After leaving Commissioned, Fred Hammond formed Radical for Christ, a choir that combined traditional gospel singing with funk, R&B and later hip-hop-influenced grooves. 'No Weapon' appeared on his 1996 album The Spirit of David. The nineties were a period of explosive growth for gospel sales, and Hammond became one of the leading figures of the new praise and worship style, aimed at congregational singing.","Listen to the bass line, which Hammond, a bassist first, makes the centre of the arrangement. It moves in long, smooth phrases that support the slow chant of the choir and gives the song its sense of steady strength. The chorus is set in a narrow range so that everyone can sing it, the same principle Andraé Crouch used decades before. Its plainness is entirely deliberate.","The song became one of the most widely sung praise songs of the nineties, heard in churches across the country. Critics of praise and worship music sometimes found such songs repetitive; Hammond's admirers argued that repetition is the point of congregational song. Hammond himself is now regarded as one of the most influential figures in modern gospel, both as performer and producer.","Follow the bass line all the way through and notice how it holds the song together. Then listen to Commissioned's Go Tell Somebody to hear Hammond's earlier group, and Hezekiah Walker's 'I Need You to Survive' for another congregational song of the same era."],"marvin-sapp-never-would-have-made-it":["A long, slow ballad of thanksgiving for surviving grief and hardship, sung live by Marvin Sapp with choir and band. It begins with soft piano and organ and Sapp's quiet, conversational singing, and builds patiently over eight minutes. His voice moves from tender to raw, and in the final section he improvises over a repeating choir chant, stretching phrases, preaching between lines and pushing into a cracked, powerful upper register as the band rises beneath him.","Sapp, a former member of Commissioned and a pastor in Grand Rapids, Michigan, wrote the song in the period after his father's death, and recorded it live for the album Thirsty. It became one of the biggest gospel hits of the decade, crossing to R&B radio and lifting Sapp to national fame. Its subject of endurance resonated far beyond church audiences during years of economic hardship.","Listen to the final section, where the written song is over and Sapp is improvising. He uses the old gospel technique of 'vamping', repeating a short phrase while the choir holds a chant, and each time he varies the rhythm and pitch, building intensity. The band follows him exactly, dropping out and returning on his cues. It is the same practice heard in James Cleveland's long choir performances, in a twenty-first-century setting.","The song was a huge success and is often named among the defining gospel recordings of the 2000s. It made Sapp one of the best-known gospel singers in the country. Some critics have noted its length and repetitiveness; most hear in its long build exactly the cathartic shape of a Black church service. It is now standard repertoire for gospel soloists.","Hear the full live version, not the radio edit, and wait for the final section where Sapp improvises. Notice how quiet it begins. Then compare James Cleveland's 'I Don't Feel Noways Tired' to hear an earlier generation's version of the same slow build."],"take-6-take-6":["Six male voices and nothing else: no band, no piano, only vocal harmony so dense and agile that listeners often assume instruments are hidden in the mix. One singer imitates a walking bass, the others stack chords drawn from jazz, and the leads trade off in phrases of extraordinary precision. The repertoire mixes spirituals such as 'Get Away, Jordan', new gospel songs like 'Spread Love' and 'A Quiet Place', and a Christmas song, 'Mary'.","The group formed at Oakwood College, a Seventh-day Adventist school in Huntsville, Alabama, where a cappella singing was a strong tradition. Signed to Reprise, a mainstream label, they released this debut to unexpected acclaim from jazz critics and pop audiences as well as churches. It won Grammys in both gospel and jazz-related categories, a rare crossover that announced a new kind of gospel group.","Listen to the chords. Take 6 use the harmonies of big-band jazz arrangers like Gil Evans, with added notes and close clusters, and move them at speed, so that each voice is singing what would normally be a horn line. The bass voice, singing in rhythm with no instrument to lean on, is the foundation of everything. Arranger Mervyn Warren and the group wrote harmonies that most vocal ensembles had thought impossible.","The album was greeted as a revelation, by jazz critics as much as gospel ones, and established Take 6 as the most influential a cappella group of their time. Later a cappella groups, from collegiate ensembles to Pentatonix, owe them a direct debt. Some gospel listeners found them too polished and jazz-inflected; the album nonetheless remains a landmark in vocal ensemble writing.","Listen to one song with your attention only on the bass voice, then again only on the highest. Notice how no instrument is needed. Then compare the Fisk Jubilee Singers' early recordings and the Golden Gate Quartet to hear the older a cappella traditions they extend."],"kirk-franklin-why-we-sing":["A gentle, mid-tempo song about why believers sing, built on a theme taken from the old hymn 'His Eye Is on the Sparrow'. Kirk Franklin's choir, the Family, sings with warmth and polish over piano, organ and an easy rhythm section, and Franklin speaks and directs throughout, adding encouragement between phrases. The song grows from a quiet, almost reflective opening to a full, rich choral climax, but never becomes a shouting song; its tone is tender rather than ecstatic.","Franklin was in his early twenties, a Fort Worth choir director and church musician, when he recorded his debut album with the Family for GospoCentric, a small new label. 'Why We Sing' became its breakout single and carried the album to remarkable sales for a gospel record, crossing to R&B radio. It made Franklin the most visible young figure in gospel almost overnight.","Listen for the old hymn tune. Franklin weaves the melody of 'His Eye Is on the Sparrow', a gospel standard famous through Ethel Waters and Mahalia Jackson, into his own song, so the new piece seems to grow out of the old one. His spoken interjections, a habit that became his trademark, were partly a result of his not being a strong lead singer; he turned that limitation into a style.","The album was a sensation, and the song in particular was praised for its warmth and its respect for tradition. Franklin's speaking over the music was later parodied and criticised, but it became part of his signature. Critics now see the song as the beginning of a career that would redefine gospel's commercial scope in the nineties.","Listen for the hymn melody inside the song. Notice Franklin's voice speaking between the choir's lines. Then compare Mahalia Jackson's recordings of 'His Eye Is on the Sparrow' to hear the original, and Franklin's 'Stomp' to hear how dramatically he changed direction four years later."],"kirk-franklin-stomp":["A gospel choir over a funk sample and a hip-hop beat, a song about the joy of worship that makes you want to dance. The groove is built on a sample from Funkadelic's 'One Nation Under a Groove', with a loud, steady beat and heavy bass. The choir, God's Property, sings bright, shouted hooks, and Franklin calls out and hypes the crowd. The remix adds a rap verse by Cheryl 'Salt' James of Salt-N-Pepa. It sounds like a party record.","God's Property was a young Dallas choir that Franklin worked with, and 'Stomp' came from their 1997 album on B-Rite, distributed by Interscope, a label better known for gangsta rap. The song and its video were played on MTV and urban radio, an extraordinary reach for a gospel record. It came at the height of the hip-hop era, and Franklin argued that gospel had to speak young people's musical language to reach them.","Listen to the sample. Funkadelic's 'One Nation Under a Groove' is itself a song about collective liberation through dancing, so its use under a gospel choir turns George Clinton's secular utopia into a sacred one. The beat is constructed exactly like a West Coast hip-hop production of the time, and the choir is arranged in short, chanted hooks rather than long phrases, so it fits the rhythm like a rapper's chorus.","The record was a major crossover hit and sparked fierce debate. Many church leaders denounced it as worldly; young people and many pastors welcomed it as evangelism. It is now regarded as the record that brought gospel and hip-hop into lasting partnership, opening the way for later artists from Lecrae to Chance the Rapper and Kanye West's gospel work.","Play Funkadelic's 'One Nation Under a Groove' first, then 'Stomp', and hear how the sample changes meaning. Watch the video if you can. Then play the Winans' 'It's Time' for the earlier attempt, and Chance the Rapper's Coloring Book to hear where gospel hip-hop went."],"kirk-franklin-the-nu-nation-project":["A long, ambitious album that mixes choir gospel with R&B, hip-hop, funk and pop, bringing in guest stars and big productions. It includes 'Revolution', a hard-hitting track produced by Rodney Jerkins, and 'Lean on Me', a huge ballad featuring Mary J. Blige, Bono, R. Kelly and Crystal Lewis. Between these are worship songs, testimonies and spoken interludes. The overall sound is glossy, layered and constantly changing, like a mixtape with a church at its centre.","Following the success of 'Stomp', Franklin had become a pop-culture figure, and The Nu Nation Project was his bid to bring gospel fully into the mainstream. It was a major seller and a Grammy winner, released at a time when R&B and hip-hop dominated American charts. The guest list, crossing gospel, R&B, rock and Christian pop, showed how far Franklin's reach extended.","Listen to the interludes between songs, where Franklin speaks about his own struggles and invites listeners into his story. These spoken passages turn the album into something like a sermon broken into chapters. On 'Lean on Me', notice how each guest is given a verse matched to their style, Bono's rock rasp against Mary J. Blige's soul, before the choir unites them all.","The album was enormously successful and praised for its ambition, though some found its mix of styles and guest stars overwhelming or commercial. It confirmed Franklin as the central figure in nineties gospel. Critics now treat it as a high-water mark of the gospel crossover era, before the industry's later fragmentation.","Start with 'Revolution' for the energy and 'Lean on Me' for the guest stars. Then listen to the whole album in order to feel its shape as a kind of extended service. Compare Mary J. Blige's My Life and U2's Achtung Baby to hear the worlds two of the guests came from."],"kirk-franklin-i-smile":["A bright, warm song about choosing joy in hard times, built on a bouncing piano figure, a relaxed groove and a big choir chorus. The verses are sung by a lead vocalist over light keyboards and drums, and the choir enters on the chorus with an uplifting, sunny hook that repeats and rises. Franklin's production is clean and contemporary, balancing old choir harmonies with modern pop-R&B textures. It feels like a deliberately simple, open-hearted song.","'I Smile' came from Franklin's 2011 album Hello Fear, released during a period when gospel was competing with the new worship-music industry and with streaming. The song became one of the biggest gospel hits of its decade and crossed onto R&B radio. Franklin had by then been a gospel star for nearly twenty years, and the song showed his ability to write simple, durable melodies.","Listen to the piano figure that opens the song and returns throughout; it is a short, rhythmic pattern borrowed from pop and R&B piano writing rather than church accompaniment, and it drives the whole arrangement. The choir writing is in close, bright harmony with a lifting melodic shape on the chorus, the same uplift Franklin had used since 'Why We Sing'. The song's plainness masks careful craft.","The song was widely embraced and won awards, becoming one of Franklin's best-known later songs. Some critics found it slight; others praised exactly that simplicity. It is now a common choice for church choirs and for weddings, funerals and celebrations alike, showing the kind of everyday usefulness that Franklin's best songs have.","Listen for the piano figure and how it returns. Notice how the chorus lifts. Then play 'Why We Sing' to hear the young Franklin, and CeCe Winans's 'Believe for It' to hear how gospel's sound continued to change in the decade after this record."],"sweet-honey-in-the-rock-good-news":["Five women singing without instruments except for occasional hand percussion, in deep, earthy harmonies drawn from spirituals, gospel, blues and African song. The voices are strong and individual, with a low bass voice anchoring rich, open chords. The songs range from spirituals and freedom songs to new compositions on social justice, apartheid and the lives of working people. The sound is raw and immediate, more like a church meeting or a protest rally than a polished concert.","Sweet Honey in the Rock was founded in Washington, DC, in 1973 by Bernice Johnson Reagon, who had sung with the SNCC Freedom Singers during the civil rights movement. The group carried that tradition of sung protest into the 1970s and 1980s, linking spirituals, gospel and political song. This album, on the independent folk label Flying Fish, came at a moment of renewed activism around South Africa and nuclear weapons.","Listen to the bass voice, often sung by a woman in an unusually low register, and to the way the singers move between unison and harmony. The arrangements draw on the congregational singing of the Black church in the South, where songs were 'lined out' and harmonised freely, and on African vocal traditions, where voices interlock rhythmically. Hand percussion, when it appears, marks time like feet on a church floor.","The group was widely praised by folk, feminist and political audiences, though gospel radio rarely played it. Critics have long admired the group's blend of beauty and protest. Its influence is felt in later vocal groups and in political choirs around the world. Reagon's work as a scholar of African American song, at the Smithsonian and elsewhere, adds to its cultural weight.","Listen to the bass voice first, then to how the other voices weave around it. Notice the hand percussion. Then compare Pete Seeger's 'We Shall Overcome' and the Fisk Jubilee Singers to hear the protest and spiritual traditions the group draws on."],"sweet-honey-in-the-rock-ellas-song":["A slow, steady song written by Bernice Johnson Reagon in honour of the civil rights organiser Ella Baker, about the long work of seeking justice and freedom. Five unaccompanied voices sing a simple, chant-like melody in unison and harmony, the lead voice rising over a quiet, rocking bed of sustained chords. The pace never hurries. The song repeats its central refrain many times, building quiet intensity, and ends almost as it began, as if the struggle continues.","Ella Baker was a key organiser behind the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, where Reagon had been active in the early 1960s, and was known for her insistence on grassroots, collective leadership rather than charismatic leaders. Reagon wrote the song using ideas from Baker's own speeches, and Sweet Honey recorded it on their 1983 album We All... Everyone of Us. It has since become an anthem for activists.","Listen to how little the melody moves; it is built to be learned in one hearing and sung by a crowd, like the freedom songs of the sixties that Reagon helped lead. The harmonies beneath are sustained and close, giving the impression of a group holding steady together, which mirrors Baker's idea of collective leadership. The repetition is the meaning: change comes through endurance, not a single climax.","The song has become one of the best-known protest songs of its generation, sung at marches and gatherings for decades. Critics and scholars praise it as both beautiful and politically sharp, and it is often taught as an example of the continuity between the civil rights era and later movements. Its reach far outstrips Sweet Honey's commercial profile.","Listen first for the steady pulse, then notice how the harmony thickens as the song goes on. Hear it as a song meant for many voices, not just five. Then compare the Staple Singers' 'Freedom Highway' and Pete Seeger's 'We Shall Overcome' to hear the protest-song lineage it carries forward."],"richard-smallwood-total-praise":["A slow, grand hymn of worship for choir, piano and strings. It begins quietly, with piano and voices in rich, sustained chords, then builds through several sections to a powerful climax and closes with a long, slowly expanding cadence of repeated amens. The harmonies are lush and the melodic lines long and arching, closer to an anthem in the European choral tradition than to a gospel shout. Its dignity and depth make it instantly recognisable.","Richard Smallwood, a classically trained pianist from Washington, DC, studied at Howard University, where he combined Bach, Chopin and Rachmaninoff with the gospel of his childhood church. He recorded 'Total Praise' live with his choir Vision for the album Adoration: Live in Atlanta. It became one of the most widely sung choral works in the Black church and has crossed into concert choir repertoire and even classical programs.","Listen to the final section, where the closing amens unfold in a series of slowly building chords, each a little fuller and higher. This technique of a long, climaxing cadence comes from the European choral tradition, from Handel and the Romantic anthem, and Smallwood places it in a gospel context. The piano writing, with its rolling arpeggios and harmonic richness, betrays his classical training.","The song was quickly adopted by choirs across denominations and is now one of the most performed gospel choral works in the world, sung at funerals, graduations and concerts. Critics and choral directors praise it as a rare bridge between gospel and the classical choral tradition. Some gospel listeners find it more anthem than gospel; most hear in it a fusion of the two.","Listen through to the closing amens and notice how the chords build. Then compare Handel's 'Hallelujah' chorus and Rachmaninoff's All-Night Vigil to hear the choral grandeur Smallwood draws on, and Walter Hawkins's Love Alive for the gospel choir tradition behind him."],"hezekiah-walker-i-need-you-to-survive":["A slow, steady song about how members of a community need one another, sung by Hezekiah Walker's Love Fellowship Crusade Choir. A simple melody is repeated by the choir in warm, full harmony over a gentle piano, organ and rhythm section. The song builds gradually by repetition, the choir swaying and the congregation joining, until the final section where voices swell and the lead singer improvises over the chorus. Its simplicity and unity are its power.","Hezekiah Walker, a pastor and choir director from Brooklyn, recorded the song live for the album Family Affair II: Live at Radio City Music Hall. His mass choir, built from his church community, was one of the most popular in gospel. Released in the year after the attacks of September 11, 2001, its message of mutual dependence perhaps carried special weight in New York.","Listen to how the melody is constructed to be learned instantly by a congregation: a narrow range, repeated phrases, and a steady pulse. The choir harmony is close and warm, with each section holding long notes under the melody, creating a feeling of embrace. The lead improvisation at the end follows the old gospel practice of the vamp, but here it stays gentle, emphasising community rather than individual display.","The song became one of the most popular congregational songs in Black churches in the 2000s and remains a staple. Critics praise its simplicity and its theme of community. Some find it repetitive; most hear in its repetition exactly what makes it work as a shared song. It is often sung as a closing song at services and gatherings.","Listen to how the song builds through repetition. Notice the warmth of the choir's harmony. Then compare Fred Hammond's 'No Weapon' and James Cleveland's 'Peace Be Still' to hear other congregational songs built on patient repetition, and notice how Walker keeps the lead singer modest."],"hezekiah-walker-every-praise":["An energetic praise song about giving all worship to God, built on a driving beat, keyboard stabs and a choir chorus that repeats and builds. The verses are short; the chorus is a chant listing names and attributes of God, sung by the choir in bright, powerful harmony. The song rises in key and intensity through its final section, becoming a full congregational shout. It is designed for dancing and celebration, with a clean, modern production.","The song came from Walker's album Azusa: The Next Generation, named after the Azusa Street Revival of 1906, a founding event of Pentecostalism. Walker, by then a veteran gospel star and pastor, aimed at a younger audience with contemporary production. The song became one of the biggest gospel hits of the 2010s, widely sung in churches and played on radio.","Listen to the key changes in the final section, where the music shifts upward several times, a classic gospel technique for building excitement. The production uses programmed drums and synth sounds from contemporary R&B and pop, but the call-and-response between lead and choir is pure church tradition. The song's naming of God's attributes echoes the practice of litany in many religious traditions.","The song was hugely popular and won awards, and it is now one of the most widely sung praise songs in Black churches. Critics praise its energy and simplicity, though some find it formulaic. It confirmed Walker's ability to adapt to changing styles across three decades and remains a staple of church celebrations.","Listen for the key changes in the final section and how they build excitement. Notice the call-and-response. Then compare Kirk Franklin's 'Stomp' for another danceable praise song, and Walker's 'I Need You to Survive' to hear his gentler, slower side a decade earlier."],"james-taylor-sweet-baby-james":["Eleven short songs, half an hour of music, built around one voice and one acoustic guitar. James Taylor sings low and close, almost conversationally, and fingerpicks patterns that sound simple until you try them. Around him a small band plays with restraint: soft drums, a supple bass, piano, a little pedal steel and fiddle. The title song is a cowboy lullaby in a lilting waltz time; Steamroller is a deadpan blues parody; Fire and Rain sits at the heart of side one.","Taylor's first album had come out in 1968 on the Beatles' Apple label in London and gone almost unnoticed while he struggled with his health. Back in America, producer Peter Asher brought him to Los Angeles late in 1969 and recorded this follow-up quickly and cheaply for Warner Bros. The session players, including drummer Russ Kunkel and Carole King on piano, became the core of the Laurel Canyon sound. Released in early 1970, it caught a public tired of noise and protest.","Listen to how much of the arrangement is left out. Kunkel often plays with brushes or barely at all, and several songs have no drums for long stretches, so the guitar carries the rhythm alone. Taylor's own picking is the real engine: he keeps a steady thumbed bass under moving chords, a trick learned from folk and country players, which lets a solo voice and guitar sound like a full band. Oh! Susanna, the Stephen Foster minstrel tune, is slipped in as a quiet curio.","It made Taylor a star within months and put him on the cover of Time in 1971 as the face of a new, inward-looking songwriting. That success later counted against him: punk-era critics used his name as shorthand for soft, self-absorbed music. Judgment has since settled in his favour, with musicians praising the guitar craft and the plain honesty of the writing, though some still find the mood too even across the whole record.","Start with the title track and notice the way the guitar rocks like a cradle under the melody. Then play Fire and Rain and Country Road back to back to hear the range from grief to restless travel. Compare it with Carole King's Tapestry, made by many of the same hands a year later, and with Nick Drake's quieter English records of the same moment."],"james-taylor-fire-and-rain":["A song about the death of a friend and the singer's own breakdown and recovery, told in three verses with a returning chorus. The sound is spare: Taylor's acoustic guitar rings out open, bright chords, Carole King's piano enters with soft rolled figures, and drums arrive late and gently. There is no big climax. The melody stays in a narrow, speaking range and the chorus simply repeats, which makes the song feel like a person thinking aloud rather than performing.","Taylor wrote it in pieces in 1968 and 1969, partly in London while making his first album and partly during a stay in a psychiatric hospital in Massachusetts. It appeared on Sweet Baby James and was released as a single in 1970, when American radio was still dominated by louder rock. Its success showed record companies that quiet, autobiographical songs could sell, and it opened a door for a whole generation of acoustic writers.","Each verse turns to a different hurt, and the music barely changes to mark it; the sameness is the point, as if the singer is circling a wound. Listen also to the guitar voicings, which keep a high open string ringing above shifting chords, giving the song its pale, unsettled shimmer. The bass, played slow and sliding, moves almost independently of the guitar and supplies most of the sense of motion.","It became one of the most covered songs of its era and a fixture of every list of great singer-songwriter recordings. For years listeners treated it as a love song, and Taylor has spent decades patiently explaining its real subjects in interviews. Some critics have found its calm too composed for such painful material; others hear that composure as exactly what makes it honest.","Listen once just for the guitar and piano and how they answer each other without crowding. Then follow the chorus each time it returns and notice how the same words seem to weigh more as the song goes on. Hear it next to Joni Mitchell's Blue, made a year later, which takes the confessional mode further and rawer."],"jackson-browne-late-for-the-sky":["Eight long songs, most of them slow, with Jackson Browne's piano at the centre and David Lindley's slide guitar and fiddle weaving keening lines around his voice. The songs run five or six minutes and unfold patiently, more like spoken letters than pop singles. The title track opens on a broken relationship, For a Dancer meditates on a death, and the closing Before the Deluge widens out into a hymn about a generation's lost ideals. The mood is twilight from first track to last.","By 1974 Browne was the house poet of David Geffen's Asylum label in Los Angeles, already known for songs others had recorded and for co-writing Take It Easy with Glenn Frey of the Eagles. He made this third album with his touring band rather than session stars, which gives it a lived-in, unified sound. The cover, a car parked under a night street lamp beneath a bright daytime sky, borrows the paradox of René Magritte's painting The Empire of Light.","Lindley's lap steel is the record's second voice: listen to how often it finishes Browne's phrases, sliding up to a note just after he leaves it. The album is also sequenced as a single arc, from personal loss at the start to collective loss at the end, so that Before the Deluge reads as the answer to the title song. Martin Scorsese used the title track in Taxi Driver two years later.","Reviewers took it as Browne's most complete statement, and many still rank it as his finest record. The objection, then and since, is that it is earnest and humourless, the sound of a young man taking himself very seriously. Defenders reply that its seriousness is the point and that few records of the decade examine love and mortality so steadily. Its reputation has grown among younger songwriters.","Begin with For a Dancer and listen to how the fiddle and piano carry the grief while the voice stays plain. Then hear the title track and the closer together as bookends. Compare with Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark, released the same year in the same city, which handles similar subjects with more wit and jazz colour."],"jackson-browne-the-pretender":["A fuller, more polished record than its predecessor, with strings, harp and a bigger rock band behind Browne's piano and guitar. Eight songs move from the anxious opener The Fuse through tender ballads to the title song, a long, building portrait of a man settling for a comfortable, compromised life. Linda Paloma brings in a mariachi ensemble with harp and guitarrón; Here Come Those Tears Again is the nearest thing to a hit; Daddy's Tune is a reconciliation with a father.","Browne began the album in a period of personal crisis: his wife, Phyllis Major, died in March 1976, while it was being made, and he finished it in grief. He brought in Jon Landau, Bruce Springsteen's producer and former critic, to give the sessions a firmer shape. Released late that year on Asylum, it arrived as the Los Angeles soft-rock scene was at its commercial peak and just as punk was beginning to sneer at it.","Here Come Those Tears Again was co-written with Nancy Farnsworth, Phyllis Major's mother, a remarkable fact once you hear the song's restraint. Listen to the title track's slow build: it begins almost conversationally and adds instruments verse by verse until the final choruses swell with the full band, so the music enacts the drift into resignation the song describes. Linda Paloma is the first time Browne turned to the Mexican music of his Los Angeles childhood.","It sold better than anything Browne had made and was widely praised, though some reviewers felt Landau's polish smoothed away the intimacy of Late for the Sky. The title song has become one of the decade's emblematic portraits of middle-class compromise, discussed alongside Springsteen's writing. The argument over which of the two albums is Browne's peak has never been settled and probably never will be.","Go straight to the title track and let it build over its six minutes; then go back to Sleep's Dark and Silent Gate, the starkest song here. Notice how differently strings and lap steel are used than on Late for the Sky. Pair it with Springsteen's Born to Run for two views of American aspiration from opposite coasts."],"linda-ronstadt-heart-like-a-wheel":["Ten songs by other writers, sung by one of the most powerful voices in popular music. The record swings between rock and country: You're No Good builds from a slinky groove to a guitar-heavy climax; the Everly Brothers' When Will I Be Loved is bright and twangy; Anna McGarrigle's title song is a hushed ballad with strings; Hank Williams's I Can't Help It is sung in close harmony with Emmylou Harris. Ronstadt's voice can go from a near whisper to full chest power within a phrase.","Ronstadt had spent years in Los Angeles moving between labels, bands and producers without a hit album. For this one Peter Asher, the producer who had shaped James Taylor's sound, took charge, with multi-instrumentalist Andrew Gold playing much of the backing. It was her last album for Capitol before she moved to Asylum, and its success made her one of the best-selling female singers of the decade and the chief interpreter of the new Los Angeles songwriters.","Ronstadt did not write songs, and that is the hidden art here: she was a brilliant chooser and arranger of other people's material. Listen to how You're No Good, an early-1960s soul song, is slowed and stretched until it becomes something closer to a rock epic, with a long guitar coda layered by Gold. Her harmony with Harris on the Hank Williams song is a preview of the Trio records the two would make with Dolly Parton.","It was a commercial breakthrough and a critical success, and it secured her reputation as an interpreter rather than an author, which some rock critics held against her at the time. That bias has faded: she is now widely credited with bringing country material into the rock mainstream and with launching the careers of writers she covered. Some listeners still find Asher's production too clean for the rawness of her voice.","Start with the title song to hear the voice at its quietest, then jump to You're No Good to hear it at full force. Listen for the moment in the Hank Williams song where the two voices lock into a single sound. Then hear Emmylou Harris's Pieces of the Sky, made the following year, as its close cousin."],"linda-ronstadt-simple-dreams":["Ten songs that range from hard rock to old ballads. It's So Easy, a Buddy Holly rocker, is driven by crunching guitars; Roy Orbison's Blue Bayou is slowed to a swaying dream; Warren Zevon's Poor Poor Pitiful Me and Carmelita bring a sardonic edge; the Rolling Stones' Tumbling Dice is reborn as a rock shuffle. Between them, the traditional I Never Will Marry is sung with Dolly Parton over a sparse acoustic accompaniment. The record is short, punchy and confident.","By 1977 Ronstadt was among the biggest concert draws in America, now on Asylum and still working with producer Peter Asher and a crack Los Angeles band including guitarist Waddy Wachtel. The record arrived in the year of punk in London and disco in New York, and it represents the commercial peak of the polished West Coast sound. Her championing of Zevon, then little known, helped his career greatly.","Listen to the gap between the rock songs and the folk song in the middle: I Never Will Marry is almost unaccompanied, and Ronstadt drops all the power of her voice to blend with Parton's high, bright tone. Blue Bayou is taken a little slower than Orbison's original, and its final notes are held so long they almost become a call. Listen too for the way Tumbling Dice is tightened into a straight, hard rock song, trading the Stones' loose swagger for drive.","It was enormously successful, and most reviewers admired the singing while some complained about the predictability of the Asher formula. Later critics of the West Coast scene used records like this as evidence of its over-produced gloss. Its defenders point out that Ronstadt had chosen boldly, putting an unknown songwriter, a Buddy Holly song and an Appalachian ballad on one of the decade's biggest records.","Play Blue Bayou and Orbison's original back to back to hear her slower, more sorrowful approach. Then hear It's So Easy next to Buddy Holly's own rockers. End with I Never Will Marry, which shows where Ronstadt's later work with Parton and Emmylou Harris would go."],"carly-simon-no-secrets":["Carly Simon's third album pairs her piano and warm, slightly husky alto with a glossy rock band and lush strings. Ten songs move between confident mid-tempo rockers and ballads. You're So Vain, a song mocking a self-absorbed former lover, opens with a rippling bass and builds into a sing-along chorus with a second male voice in the background. The Right Thing to Do is a tender love song; Embrace Me, You Child looks back on childhood.","Simon had been one of the Simon Sisters folk duo and was now an Elektra solo star after two successful albums. For this one the label sent her to London with producer Richard Perry, who favoured big, bright, radio-ready sound. She married James Taylor late in 1972, around the time of the album's release, and the pair became the public face of the singer-songwriter era. You're So Vain dominated radio that winter.","Listen in the choruses of You're So Vain for the second voice shadowing hers: it is Mick Jagger, uncredited, who happened to be in the studio. The opening bass figure was played by Klaus Voormann, the German artist who designed the Beatles' Revolver cover. The identity of the song's subject became a decades-long guessing game; Simon revealed in 2015 that at least one verse concerned the actor Warren Beatty.","It was Simon's biggest commercial success, and it established her as more than a folk-pop balladeer. Critics have long regarded it as a model of early-1970s studio craft, though some felt Perry's gloss blunted the sharper edges of her writing. You're So Vain is now valued as one of the few hits of its time in which a woman turns a cool, mocking eye on a man rather than pining for him.","Begin with You're So Vain and listen for the bass at the start and the second voice in the chorus. Then play The Right Thing to Do to hear the gentler side of the same record. Compare its production with Carole King's Tapestry for two very different approaches to the piano-led singer-songwriter album."],"carly-simon-thats-the-way-ive-always-heard-it-should-be":["A song about a young woman's dread at being expected to marry, as she looks at the quiet unhappiness of her parents' and friends' marriages. The arrangement is muted and minor-tinged, with piano and acoustic guitar, a soft bass and a string section that swells gradually. Simon sings in a low, controlled voice, almost reporting rather than complaining. There is no conventional chorus; the tension simply builds through the verses until the final resigned turn.","This was the first single from Simon's 1971 debut album on Elektra, co-written with her friend Jacob Brackman, a journalist and screenwriter. It appeared at the height of the American women's movement, and its frank ambivalence about marriage was unusual on pop radio. It became an unexpected hit and launched a solo career that would carry her through the decade.","Listen to how the strings creep in: at first they are barely there, then they rise under each verse, so the arrangement grows heavier just as the singer's sense of being trapped deepens. The final verse brings a sudden shift in perspective that reverses everything before it, and Simon sings it in the same calm tone, which makes the surrender more chilling than any outburst could.","Reviewers were struck by its unusual subject and bleak mood, and it won Simon a Grammy for best new artist. It has since been taken up by feminist critics as one of the first mainstream pop songs to question marriage from a woman's point of view. Some find it too subdued to make its case; most hear its restraint as its strength.","Listen first to the strings and how slowly they rise. Then follow the story through each verse until the final turn. Hear it next to Joni Mitchell's songs on Blue and Carole King's Tapestry for three different women's views of love in 1971."],"cat-stevens-tea-for-the-tillerman":["Eleven songs built on two strummed acoustic guitars, Cat Stevens's own and Alun Davies's, with a lean bass, sparse drums and occasional strings. The guitar playing is percussive and urgent, full of sudden stops and accelerations. Where Do the Children Play? opens with a question about progress; Wild World is a warning to a departing lover; Father and Son is a dialogue in which Stevens sings both parts, the father low and calm, the son high and impatient.","Stevens had been a teenage pop star in London in 1967 before a severe illness with tuberculosis in 1968 forced a long convalescence. He returned with a new, quieter, introspective style. Paul Samwell-Smith, formerly of the Yardbirds, produced this album for Island in Britain and A&M in America. It caught the same wave as James Taylor in America, and Stevens painted the cover himself.","Listen to Father and Son for the voice change: Stevens drops into a low, mild register for the father and leaps an octave for the son, so the argument is carried by pitch as much as words. The guitars, too, change character between the two voices, calmer under the father and more agitated under the son. Del Newman's string arrangements are used sparingly, so their arrival in Sad Lisa and Into White feels like weather changing.","It was a major success on both sides of the Atlantic and remains Stevens's best-known album. Critics praised its craft but some found its spiritual searching naive. Those reservations have softened, especially after Stevens's later conversion to Islam gave the album's questions an unexpected outcome. Wild World has been read by some as condescending to the woman it addresses; the debate about it continues.","Start with Father and Son and listen for the two voices. Then hear Where Do the Children Play? for its insistent guitar rhythm. Compare with James Taylor's Sweet Baby James, from the same year, which shares the acoustic intimacy but none of the urgency."],"cat-stevens-teaser-and-the-firecat":["Ten songs that are lighter and more playful than its predecessor, still built on two acoustic guitars but with more colour: a Greek bouzouki in Rubylove, a gospel-style choir in Peace Train, a simple piano in Morning Has Broken. Moonshadow is a jaunty tune about finding comfort in loss. The record moves from childlike charm to quiet spiritual longing, ending with the yearning Bitterblue and the hopeful Peace Train.","Made quickly after the success of Tea for the Tillerman, again with Paul Samwell-Smith producing and Alun Davies on second guitar. Stevens also wrote and illustrated a children's book with the same title, about a boy and a cat who try to return the moon to the sky. Morning Has Broken is a Christian hymn with words by Eleanor Farjeon, set to an old Gaelic tune; its success made Stevens briefly a hymn singer on pop radio.","The piano on Morning Has Broken was played by Rick Wakeman, soon to join the progressive rock band Yes; he improvised the introduction and the interludes, turning a plain hymn into a small piano piece. Listen to Rubylove, sung partly in Greek with bouzouki, a nod to Stevens's Greek Cypriot father. The record's light touch masks careful arranging: notice how instruments are added one at a time through Peace Train.","It was another big success, and Peace Train and Moonshadow became staples of the era. Critics admired its charm while finding it less substantial than Tea for the Tillerman. Over time Morning Has Broken has become the song most associated with Stevens, often sung in schools. Peace Train has acquired new resonance as a statement of the faith he later chose.","Start with Morning Has Broken and listen for Wakeman's piano. Then play Moonshadow and Peace Train to hear the album's lightness. Compare with Nick Drake's Pink Moon for a darker, more inward record from the same British scene. Any standard remaster serves; the original vinyl sounds especially warm."],"gordon-lightfoot-if-you-could-read-my-mind":["A song about the slow end of a marriage, told through images of ghosts, old movies and paperback novels. Gordon Lightfoot's warm baritone sits over a fingerpicked twelve-string and six-string guitar pattern that rolls steadily like a turning wheel. A string section enters softly and lifts the later verses without ever overwhelming the voice. The melody rises and falls in long phrases, and the song has no chorus in the usual sense, only a returning thought.","Lightfoot was already a respected Canadian folk songwriter whose songs had been recorded by Ian and Sylvia, Peter, Paul and Mary and others. This came from his first album for Reprise, recorded in Los Angeles in 1970 and later retitled after the song. It was his breakthrough as a performer in the United States, and it came out of the breakup of his first marriage.","Listen to the guitar pattern underneath: two guitars weave a single shimmering figure that never stops, like the song's circling thoughts. The strings enter almost unnoticed and slowly thicken, so by the last verse the song feels fuller without ever getting louder. The narrator's imagery comes from cinema and pulp fiction, a deliberate distancing from the real pain at the song's heart.","It became Lightfoot's first big American hit and remains one of his best-loved songs. Critics praised its craft and restraint. It has been covered by many singers across folk, country and pop. Some listeners find its mood too gentle for its subject; others hear in that gentleness a rare honesty about a marriage that ended without villains.","Listen first to the guitars and how they never stop moving. Then follow the strings as they rise. Hear it next to Joni Mitchell's Blue, another Canadian's view of love's end from the following year. Seek the original 1970 studio recording rather than later live versions, where the strings and the careful balance of the two guitars are clearest."],"gordon-lightfoot-wreck-of-the-edmund-fitzgerald":["A ballad about the sinking of a Great Lakes freighter in a November storm. Over six and a half minutes, Lightfoot tells the story in many verses set to a single repeating, modal melody, with no chorus. An electric guitar plays a short, mournful riff between verses, and a pedal steel moans in the background like wind. The drums and bass keep a steady, rolling pulse that suggests waves. The effect is of an old sea ballad recorded with a modern band.","The Edmund Fitzgerald, an ore carrier, sank on Lake Superior on 10 November 1975 with all twenty-nine crew. Lightfoot read about it in a magazine and wrote the song within weeks; it appeared on his 1976 album Summertime Dream on Reprise. Lightfoot said the melody came from an old Irish dirge. The single became one of his biggest hits, unusual for a long story-song with no chorus.","Listen to the guitar riff between verses: the same brief phrase recurs like a ship's bell, marking the passage of time. Lightfoot later changed a line in live performance after a 2010 documentary suggested the sinking was not caused by crew error, as an early theory had suggested. The Mariners' Church in Detroit, mentioned in the song, still rings its bell twenty-nine times each year in the crew's memory.","It became a hit and has remained the best-known modern maritime ballad. Critics have praised its fidelity to the old ballad tradition, in which a real disaster is turned into song soon after the event. Some find its length and repetition hard going; others hear in that repetition the dirge-like solemnity that gives it power.","Listen for the guitar riff between verses and the pedal steel in the background. Follow the story as a ballad, not a pop song. Hear it alongside Woody Guthrie's Deportee for another modern ballad about a real disaster. Notice how little the melody changes, and let that sameness work like a dirge."],"harry-nilsson-nilsson-schmilsson":["Ten songs that range from rollicking piano rock to orchestral ballad to novelty. Gotta Get Up opens with a bouncy piano vamp; Without You, a Badfinger song, is a towering ballad sung with full-throated anguish over strings; Coconut is built on a single chord and a deadpan comic story; Jump into the Fire is a long, hard-driving rock workout. Nilsson's voice, with a range of several octaves, is the album's main instrument.","Nilsson was a Los Angeles songwriter and singer admired by the Beatles but with little commercial success of his own. He recorded this in London with producer Richard Perry, who pushed him toward a harder, more commercial sound. Without You, originally recorded by the British band Badfinger, became a huge international hit and won Nilsson a Grammy for best male pop vocal.","Listen to Jump into the Fire near its end: the bassist, Herbie Flowers, detunes his strings while playing, so the bass line slides downward in pitch, a startling, comic effect. Coconut uses just one chord for its whole length, its humour and drama carried entirely by different voices Nilsson invents for each character. Without You itself had been a little-noticed album track for Badfinger until Nilsson and Perry rebuilt it around piano, strings and a final soaring chorus.","It was Nilsson's biggest commercial success and a critical favourite. Its blend of rock, balladry and humour baffled some reviewers, who found it uneven. Later critics have come to admire precisely that variety. Without You is now so well known through later versions that many forget it was Nilsson's recording that made it a standard.","Start with Without You for the voice at full power, then play Coconut for its humour, then Jump into the Fire for its rock energy. Listen for the bass detuning near the end. Then hear Nilsson Sings Newman for his gentler, more intimate side."],"harry-nilsson-nilsson-sings-newman":["Ten songs by Randy Newman, with Newman himself at the piano and Nilsson's voice multitracked into choirs of harmony. The record is short, intimate and odd: Vine St. opens with a fragment of a fake pop record before shifting into a quiet piano song; Yellow Man is a jaunty satire; Caroline is a bittersweet waltz; Dayton, Ohio 1903 is a nostalgic vignette. Nilsson's layered vocals turn the songs into miniature chamber pieces.","Nilsson had been championing Newman's songs since the late 1960s, when Newman was a struggling songwriter with one poorly selling album. They recorded this in Los Angeles in 1969, with Nilsson building his vocal parts from many separate takes. It sold poorly but helped draw attention to Newman, whose own career took off soon after.","Listen to how the vocals are built: Nilsson stacks his own voice in harmonies, echoes and counterpoints, so a single singer becomes a small chorus. He reportedly spliced together many takes for each song, an elaborate technique for its time. Newman's piano is understated, leaving space for the voices. The album's ten songs are arranged to flow almost without pause, like a song cycle.","It was a commercial failure but was named record of the year by Stereo Review. Critics have long admired it as a pioneering vocal production and as an early showcase for Newman's songs. Some find it too slight; others hear it as one of the most inventive records of its time, a precursor to later multitrack vocal experiments.","Listen with headphones to hear the layers of Nilsson's voice. Start with Vine St. and Caroline. Then hear Newman's own Sail Away and compare his gruff voice with Nilsson's angelic one on the same kind of song. A quiet room and full attention reward this record more than most."],"laura-nyro-eli-and-the-thirteenth-confession":["Thirteen songs that switch tempo, key and mood within a single number, with Laura Nyro's piano and soaring, gospel-inflected voice at the centre. The arrangements mix soul horns, jazz chords, Broadway strings and doo-wop backing vocals. Luckie opens in buoyant gospel style; Lonely Women is a slow, bluesy lament; Eli's Comin' builds from a whispered warning to a full-throated rave-up; Stoned Soul Picnic is a joyous, swirling invitation. The whole record moves like a single flow of feeling.","Nyro was just twenty when she made this, her second album and first for Columbia, in New York with arranger Charlie Calello. She had been booed, or believed she had been, at the Monterey Pop Festival in 1967, and this record was a defiant leap forward. Her songs were already hits for other artists: the 5th Dimension recorded Stoned Soul Picnic and Sweet Blindness, and Three Dog Night recorded Eli's Comin'.","The original LP came with a lyric sheet scented with perfume, a detail that suits the album's sensual, synesthetic style. Listen to how often a song stops dead and restarts in a new tempo: Nyro treats a song as a series of emotional scenes rather than verses and choruses. Her piano voicings come from jazz and gospel, and her phrasing owes much to the girl-group records of her New York childhood.","It sold modestly but was hugely admired by musicians; Elton John and Todd Rundgren both cited Nyro as an influence. Critics at the time found it overwrought; later ones hear it as a pioneering fusion of soul, jazz and the singer-songwriter form. Its reputation has risen steadily, and Nyro is now recognised as one of the most original songwriters of her era.","Start with Eli's Comin' and listen to how it builds. Then hear Lonely Women for the slow blues side. Compare with Gonna Take a Miracle, her later album of soul covers, to hear the roots of her style. Then play the whole record straight through, as Nyro intended."],"laura-nyro-gonna-take-a-miracle":["Ten soul, doo-wop and Motown songs from Nyro's youth, sung with the vocal trio Labelle: Patti LaBelle, Nona Hendryx and Sarah Dash. The arrangements are sparse at first, often just Nyro's piano and voices, then filled out with a rhythm section and horns. Monkey Time and Dancing in the Street are fused into a single medley; Spanish Harlem is slow and gentle; Nowhere to Run and Jimmy Mack are lively and celebratory. The title song closes the record.","After four albums of her own songs, Nyro wanted to return to the music of her New York adolescence, the street-corner harmony singing of the early 1960s. She recorded in Philadelphia at Sigma Sound with producers Kenny Gamble and Leon Huff, the architects of the Philadelphia soul sound. Labelle were then a little-known trio; within a few years they would have a huge hit of their own.","Listen to the a cappella passages, where the four voices blend without accompaniment, a direct homage to the doo-wop groups Nyro grew up hearing. Patti LaBelle's soaring high notes often answer Nyro's lead, creating a dialogue between two very different voices. Gamble and Huff's Philadelphia rhythm section, light on its feet and crisp on the beat, gives the faster songs their bounce without ever crowding the voices.","It sold modestly and was Nyro's last album for several years. Critics have come to regard it as a pioneering cross-racial collaboration and a loving tribute to the girl-group and soul traditions. Some find it a minor work compared to her originals; others hear it as a joyous celebration that shows exactly where her style came from.","Start with the medley of Monkey Time and Dancing in the Street, then hear the a cappella opening of I Met Him on a Sunday. Compare it with the Shirelles' and the Supremes' originals to hear what Nyro and Labelle add."],"john-martyn-solid-air":["Nine songs that blend folk, jazz and blues, with John Martyn's slurred, horn-like voice and his acoustic guitar at the centre. Danny Thompson's double bass is almost a second lead voice. The title song is a slow, smoky jazz ballad; May You Never is a brisk, warm blessing; I'd Rather Be the Devil turns a Skip James blues into a swirling, echo-drenched trance. The sound is intimate, late-night and loose.","Martyn was a Scottish-raised guitarist who had come up through the British folk clubs and signed with Island Records. He made this in London in 1972 with producer John Wood and players from Fairport Convention and Pentangle. The title song was written for his friend Nick Drake, Island's labelmate, who was then suffering from depression and would die in 1974.","Listen to I'd Rather Be the Devil: Martyn plays his acoustic guitar through an Echoplex, a tape-delay unit, so each note repeats and overlaps into a pulsing, hypnotic wash. The song is Skip James's 1931 Devil Got My Woman, transformed almost beyond recognition. Martyn's vocal phrasing, blurring words into sounds, owed more to jazz saxophonists than to folk singers, and he spoke of wanting his voice to sound like a horn.","It sold modestly at first but grew into a cult classic, now regarded as one of the finest British albums of the 1970s. Critics praised its blend of folk and jazz. Some find the slurred vocals hard to follow; most hear them as part of its seductive, dreamlike mood. May You Never has been widely covered.","Start with the title song for its slow, smoky mood, then hear I'd Rather Be the Devil for the Echoplex effects. Compare the latter with Skip James's original. Then hear Nick Drake's Pink Moon, the record of the friend the title song was written for."],"john-martyn-one-world":["Eight songs that move from dub-inflected grooves to ambient soundscapes, with Martyn's echo-laden guitar and slurred voice at the centre. Dealer and Big Muff are rhythmic and funky; Couldn't Love You More is a tender ballad; Small Hours is a long, slow instrumental-like piece built from sustained guitar tones and quiet voice. The overall sound is spacious and nocturnal, with reverb and delay as important as the notes.","Martyn made this in 1977 at Island boss Chris Blackwell's farm in Berkshire, recording partly outdoors at night. Big Muff was co-written with the Jamaican producer Lee Scratch Perry, reflecting Martyn's interest in reggae and dub, which Island was then promoting heavily. Steve Winwood played keyboards on some tracks, and Danny Thompson's double bass was again central.","Small Hours was recorded outdoors beside a lake at Blackwell's farm in the middle of the night, with the microphones picking up the natural reverb and, faintly, the sounds of the surrounding countryside. Listen for these in the background. Martyn's guitar tone throughout uses the Echoplex not as a trick but as the very fabric of the sound, layering delays into chords.","It sold modestly but has grown in reputation as a precursor to ambient music and trip-hop. Critics praised its atmosphere. Some found its blend of folk, dub and jazz unfocused; later listeners hear it as ahead of its time. Small Hours in particular is often cited as an influence on later ambient and post-rock artists.","Start with Small Hours, ideally at night, and listen to the space around the sound. Then hear Big Muff for the dub groove. Compare with Brian Eno's Another Green World, also from the mid-1970s, for another approach to studio-built atmosphere."],"judee-sill-judee-sill":["Eleven songs that blend country, folk and Baroque counterpoint, with Judee Sill's clear, plain voice and her own guitar and piano. Many songs use religious imagery, mixing Christian, mystical and cowboy themes. Jesus Was a Cross Maker is a gospel-tinged ballad; Lady-O is a delicate, lilting love song; The Lamb Ran Away With the Crown is upbeat and mysterious. Her own string and vocal arrangements give the record an unusual, almost sacred sound.","Sill had a troubled youth, including time in reform school and prison, before turning to songwriting in Los Angeles. She was the first artist signed to David Geffen's new Asylum label, and this was its first release. Graham Nash produced Jesus Was a Cross Maker. The Turtles had already recorded Lady-O. She called her style country-cult-baroque.","Listen to the harmonies and string parts: Sill studied Bach chorales and wrote her arrangements using Baroque voice-leading rules, so inner lines move independently and resolve in ways that pop songs rarely do. The result is a sound that seems to float. Her lyrics draw on mystical texts, Rosicrucian ideas and the imagery of the American West.","It sold poorly despite the support of Asylum and critics. Sill has since been rediscovered and is now widely regarded as one of the most original songwriters of her era. Critics praise her unusual fusion of sacred and secular music. Her reputation has grown steadily since reissues in the 2000s.","Start with Jesus Was a Cross Maker and Lady-O, then listen to the strings on The Archetypal Man. Compare with Bach's chorales in the St Matthew Passion to hear where her voice-leading comes from. Listen through headphones so the inner voices of the arrangements stand apart, and notice how few of the songs end where you expect them to."],"judee-sill-heart-food":["A set of songs more lavishly arranged than her debut, with Sill's own orchestral and choral writing. There's a Rugged Road is a gentle country song; The Kiss is a slow, devotional love song with sweeping strings; The Donor closes the album with a choir of Sill's overdubbed voices singing a Latin prayer for mercy over a hushed, organ-like accompaniment. The record moves from country to near-sacred music.","Sill wrote and conducted the orchestral arrangements herself, a rare achievement for a pop songwriter, and recorded in Los Angeles for Asylum. Despite Asylum's support, the album sold poorly, and Sill's career declined. She later struggled with ill health and died in 1979. Heart Food was her last album released in her lifetime.","Listen to The Donor: Sill multitracked her voice into a full choir singing the Kyrie eleison, the opening prayer of the Latin Mass, in counterpoint that recalls Renaissance and Baroque church music. The arrangement builds slowly over several minutes. Elsewhere, her string parts follow Bach-like voice-leading, with each line moving independently. She studied orchestration on her own.","It sold poorly but has since been recognised as a remarkable achievement, especially The Donor. Critics praise its ambition and beauty. Some find its religious imagery heavy; most hear it as sincere and unique. Reissues and tributes have brought Sill a new generation of admirers.","Start with The Kiss for its strings, then listen to The Donor at the end, ideally without distraction. Compare The Donor with the Kyrie of Bach's Mass in B minor to hear the sacred tradition Sill was reaching for. Play The Donor last, as Sill sequenced it."],"tim-buckley-happy-sad":["Six long songs that blend folk, jazz and blues, with Tim Buckley's flexible, wide-ranging voice and his twelve-string guitar. David Friedman's vibraphone adds a cool, shimmering sound; John Miller's double bass and Carter Collins's congas provide a loose, swinging rhythm. Strange Feelin' is built on a modal jazz groove; Love from Room 109 at the Islander includes the sound of ocean waves; Gypsy Woman is a long improvisation.","Buckley had made two folk-rock albums for Elektra in Los Angeles. For this one he moved toward jazz, influenced by Miles Davis and Charles Mingus, with producers Jerry Yester and Zal Yanovsky. It was his most successful album commercially but also signalled a move away from conventional songs that would alienate his audience.","Listen to Strange Feelin': its bass figure echoes Miles Davis's All Blues, from Kind of Blue, a nod to Buckley's jazz inspiration. Love from Room 109 at the Islander includes the sound of surf recorded on the Pacific coast, mixed in with the music. Buckley's voice moves from a soft croon to wordless cries, prefiguring his later experimental work.","It was his most successful album and is admired as a pioneering folk-jazz fusion. Critics praise its atmosphere. Some find the long improvisations meandering; others hear them as the beginning of Buckley's most adventurous period. It is often cited as an influence on later singer-songwriters.","Start with Strange Feelin' and compare it with All Blues on Kind of Blue. Then hear Love from Room 109 for the ocean sounds. Compare with Van Morrison's Astral Weeks, another late-1960s folk-jazz fusion. Let the long songs breathe; this record rewards an unhurried evening rather than a quick sampling of tracks."],"tim-buckley-starsailor":["Nine songs that range from free jazz to avant-garde vocal experiments, with Buckley's voice pushed to extremes: shrieks, swoops, wordless cries and multitracked layers. The title track is a choir of overdubbed voices in dense, dissonant clusters. Song to the Siren, the gentlest track, is a haunting ballad with echoing guitar. Monterey and Come Here Woman are wild, rhythmic workouts with horns.","Buckley had left Elektra and signed with Straight, the label run by Frank Zappa and Herb Cohen. He recorded in Los Angeles with guitarist Lee Underwood and with Buzz and Bunk Gardner, formerly of Zappa's Mothers of Invention, on horns. The album was a commercial failure, and Buckley soon turned to more conventional funk-rock. He died in 1975.","Listen to the title track: Buckley overdubbed his voice many times into a dense, shifting cluster, an effect closer to avant-garde classical vocal music than to rock. Song to the Siren, with lyrics by Larry Beckett, was later covered by This Mortal Coil with Elizabeth Fraser of the Cocteau Twins, bringing it to a new audience.","It was a commercial failure and baffled many listeners. Later critics have come to see it as a landmark of avant-garde rock and a showcase for one of the most extraordinary voices in popular music. Song to the Siren has become a standard. Debate continues over whether the wilder tracks are visionary or self-indulgent.","Start with Song to the Siren for the gentlest introduction, then try the title track. Compare the title track with Cathy Berberian's performance of Berio's Sequenza III for another extreme exploration of the voice. Give the stranger tracks a second hearing; their logic emerges slowly, like that of free jazz."],"jeff-buckley-grace":["Ten songs that blend alternative rock, folk, soul and devotional music, with Jeff Buckley's extraordinary voice ranging from a whisper to a falsetto wail. Mojo Pin opens with a hushed intro before exploding into rock; Grace is a dramatic, sweeping song; Last Goodbye is a lush, string-laden rock ballad; Hallelujah is a solo guitar and voice cover of Leonard Cohen's song; Corpus Christi Carol is a medieval carol sung in a high, pure tone.","Buckley had built a reputation singing covers at Sin-é, a small café in New York's East Village, before signing with Columbia. He recorded Grace at Bearsville Studios in Woodstock, New York, with producer Andy Wallace and his band. It was his only completed studio album; he drowned in 1997 in the Wolf River Harbor in Memphis while working on a second album.","Listen to Corpus Christi Carol: Buckley learned it from Benjamin Britten's setting of the medieval English carol in A Boy Was Born, and sings it in a high, choirboy-like voice. Lilac Wine comes from Nina Simone's version. Buckley's father was the singer Tim Buckley, whom he met only once, and Dream Brother has been read as a reflection on that absent father.","It sold modestly at first but has become one of the most acclaimed albums of the 1990s. Critics praised Buckley's voice and the album's range. Its reputation grew after his death, and it is now often cited as a major influence on later rock and pop singers. Some find its emotionalism excessive; most hear it as sincere.","Start with Hallelujah for the voice alone, then play the title track and Last Goodbye. Listen to Corpus Christi Carol for the high, pure singing. Compare with Tim Buckley's Starsailor to hear the family voice. The expanded Legacy Edition adds outtakes and live tracks that show how the songs grew on stage."],"jeff-buckley-hallelujah":["A song about love, faith and desire, sung over a single clean electric guitar. Buckley's interpretation of Leonard Cohen's song is slow and intimate, nearly seven minutes long, with his voice moving from a soft murmur to a soaring falsetto. The guitar plays gentle arpeggiated chords throughout, leaving space for the voice. There is no band, just one performer, and the recording captures every breath.","Leonard Cohen wrote and recorded the song in 1984, but it was obscure until John Cale recorded a version in 1991 with piano. Buckley learned Cale's version and made it a centrepiece of his performances at Sin-é before recording it for Grace. His recording became the definitive version for many listeners, especially after his death.","Listen to the guitar: Buckley plays a clean, slightly reverberant electric guitar, not acoustic, which gives the recording its glassy, floating sound. He follows John Cale's choice of verses rather than Cohen's original. Near the end, his voice rises to a long, held falsetto note that has become the most imitated moment in the song's history.","It was not released as a single at the time but grew in popularity after Buckley's death, eventually becoming one of the most celebrated covers in rock. Critics praise its beauty and intensity. Some argue it overshadowed Cohen's original and spawned countless imitations; others credit it with bringing Cohen's song to a wide audience.","Listen with headphones to hear the breath and the guitar. Compare with Cohen's original, which is quite different in tone, and then with John Cale's piano version to hear where Buckley's approach came from. Notice how long Buckley waits before letting his voice open up, and how quietly he ends."],"karen-dalton-its-so-hard-to-tell-whos-going-to-love-you-the-best":["Ten blues and folk songs, sung in Karen Dalton's cracked, aching voice with her twelve-string guitar and banjo. Her singing has a slow, dragging phrasing, like Billie Holiday, with a timbre that sounds old and worn. Little Bit of Rain is a gentle Fred Neil song; It Hurts Me Too is a slow blues; Ribbon Bow is a sad, simple ballad played on banjo. The backing is sparse, a quiet bass and drums.","Dalton was a central figure in the Greenwich Village folk scene of the early 1960s, admired by Bob Dylan and Fred Neil. She was famously reluctant to record. This, her first album, was made for Capitol with producer Nick Venet; it was reportedly cut very quickly. It sold poorly. Dylan later wrote warmly about her in his memoir Chronicles.","Listen to how she places her words late, behind the beat, stretching syllables like a jazz singer. Her twelve-string guitar is played with a heavy, rolling strum that keeps time like a slow heartbeat. She often sings in a register that sounds deliberately strained, giving even gentle songs a sense of weariness. All the songs are by other writers, but her interpretations transform them.","It was largely ignored on release but has been rediscovered and is now regarded as a classic of the folk revival. Critics praise her voice and interpretations. Some find her singing too idiosyncratic; others hear it as uniquely expressive. Reissues in the 2000s brought her a new audience of musicians and listeners.","Start with Little Bit of Rain, then hear Ribbon Bow for the banjo. Compare her voice with Billie Holiday's late recordings on Lady in Satin. Then hear In My Own Time, her second and last album. Listen for how far behind the beat she lets each line fall."],"karen-dalton-in-my-own-time":["Ten songs, more fully arranged than her debut, with a band including keyboards, fiddle and horns behind Dalton's voice and banjo. When a Man Loves a Woman is slowed to a mournful crawl; Katie Cruel is a traditional ballad played on fiddle and banjo; How Sweet It Is is a soulful cover; Something on Your Mind is a gentle folk song. Her voice is again the focus, cracked and achingly expressive.","Dalton made this in Woodstock, New York, at Bearsville Studios, with producer Harvey Brooks and local musicians from the scene around The Band. It was released on Just Sunshine, a small label founded by Woodstock Festival promoter Michael Lang. It sold poorly, and Dalton never recorded another album. She died in 1993.","Listen to Katie Cruel: Dalton sings it unaccompanied except for fiddle and banjo, in a style close to old Appalachian ballad singing. In a Station is by Richard Manuel of The Band; Dalton's version is slower and more desolate than the original on Music from Big Pink. Her versions of soul songs strip away their optimism.","It was largely ignored on release but has been rediscovered, especially after a 2006 reissue. Critics praise its arrangements and Dalton's voice. It is now regarded as one of the great lost albums of the era. Some find the arrangements too busy for her voice; others hear them as supportive.","Start with Katie Cruel, then hear When a Man Loves a Woman. Compare In a Station with The Band's original on Music from Big Pink. Then return to her debut for her sparser sound. The 2006 reissue is the easiest edition to find and sounds clean and close."],"bill-withers-just-as-i-am":["Twelve songs, mostly short and spare, with Bill Withers's warm, unadorned baritone and his acoustic guitar over a light rhythm section and occasional strings. Ain't No Sunshine is a two-minute lament for an absent lover, famous for a single phrase repeated many times; Grandma's Hands is an affectionate portrait of his grandmother; Harlem is a brisk song about the neighbourhood. He also covers the Beatles' Let It Be and Fred Neil's Everybody's Talkin'.","Withers was thirty-two and working in an aircraft parts factory in California when he made this, his debut, for the small Sussex label. It was produced by Booker T. Jones, of Booker T. and the M.G.'s, who brought in Stephen Stills and members of the M.G.'s. The cover shows Withers holding his lunch box. Ain't No Sunshine became a hit and won a Grammy.","Listen to Ain't No Sunshine: in the middle the music drops away and Withers repeats one phrase over and over, a section he meant to fill with lyrics but left as it was on the advice of the musicians. The strings, arranged by Booker T., are used sparingly and make the song feel even barer. Grandma's Hands later supplied the basis for other records.","It was a success and established Withers as a major songwriter. Critics praised its simplicity and honesty. It is now regarded as a classic of 1970s soul. Some found it too plain at the time; later listeners value exactly that plainness. Ain't No Sunshine has become one of the most covered songs of its era.","Start with Ain't No Sunshine and listen for the repeated phrase. Then hear Grandma's Hands. Compare with Otis Redding's Dock of the Bay for another quiet, introspective soul song. Then play Harlem and Hope She'll Be Happier to hear how much feeling Withers draws from very few notes, and notice how often the guitar alone carries the song."],"bill-withers-still-bill":["Ten songs with a funkier, tighter band sound than the debut. Lean on Me is a gospel-tinged song of friendship over a rolling piano; Use Me is a sly, keyboard-and-drums groove; Who Is He (And What Is He to You)? is a tense, jealous funk song; Kissing My Love is built on a crisp drum pattern. Withers's voice is relaxed and conversational throughout, and the band plays with spare, deep-pocketed precision.","After the success of his debut, Withers produced this himself with members of the Watts 103rd Street Rhythm Band, including drummer James Gadson. He recorded in Los Angeles. Lean on Me, inspired by his childhood in a West Virginia coal-mining town, became a huge hit and later an anthem of solidarity.","Listen to James Gadson's drumming on Kissing My Love and Use Me: crisp, spare and deep in the groove, it was later sampled widely in hip-hop. Lean on Me uses a simple rising and falling piano figure, like a hymn. Who Is He is built on a tense, repeated guitar and bass riff that creates mounting suspense.","It was Withers's most successful album and is now regarded as a soul classic. Critics praised the band and the songs. Lean on Me has become one of the most performed songs in American life, sung at schools, churches and rallies. Some found the album uneven; most hear it as a model of economy.","Start with Lean on Me, then hear Use Me and Kissing My Love for the drumming. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, from the year before, for a more orchestral approach to soul. Notice how little the band plays, and how much that leaves for the voice."],"gil-scott-heron-pieces-of-a-man":["Eleven songs that blend soul, jazz and spoken word, with Gil Scott-Heron's deep, rich voice and a crack band. The Revolution Will Not Be Televised opens with a funky bass and flute groove under Scott-Heron's half-spoken, satirical poem about media and protest; Lady Day and John Coltrane is a joyful tribute to jazz; Home Is Where the Hatred Is, about addiction, is a slow, mournful soul song; Pieces of a Man is a tender ballad about a father.","Scott-Heron was a twenty-two-year-old novelist and poet from Tennessee and New York who had released a spoken-word album the year before. This, his first album with a full band, was produced by Bob Thiele for his Flying Dutchman label in New York, with Brian Jackson on piano, Ron Carter on bass, Bernard Purdie on drums and Hubert Laws on flute and saxophone.","Listen to Ron Carter's bass on The Revolution Will Not Be Televised: a jazz musician from Miles Davis's quintet playing a funk groove, it gives the song its urgency. Brian Jackson's piano and arrangements, often uncredited in later discussions, are crucial to the album's sound. Lady Day and John Coltrane refers to Billie Holiday and Coltrane as healing forces.","It sold modestly at first but has become one of the most influential albums of its era. Critics praise its blend of politics and music. Scott-Heron has been called a godfather of hip-hop, a title he resisted. The Revolution Will Not Be Televised has become a widely quoted phrase. Some find the jazz-soul arrangements dated; most hear them as essential.","Start with The Revolution Will Not Be Televised, then hear Lady Day and John Coltrane and Pieces of a Man. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, from the same year, for another soul album about America. Listen to the bass and drums as closely as the words."],"gil-scott-heron-winter-in-america":["Nine songs and pieces, intimate and sparse, built around Brian Jackson's piano, electric piano and flute, with Scott-Heron's voice and sometimes his own piano. Peace Go with You, Brother is a slow, gospel-tinged farewell; Rivers of My Fathers is a meditation on ancestry; The Bottle, about alcoholism, is an upbeat, flute-led groove; H2Ogate Blues is a long, spoken satire of the Watergate scandal over a blues accompaniment.","Credited to Scott-Heron and Brian Jackson, this was recorded in 1973 near Washington, D.C., and released in 1974 on Strata-East, an independent, musician-run jazz label. It came out as Watergate was unfolding and as American cities were struggling with poverty and unrest. The title captured a mood of disillusion after the 1960s.","Listen to Brian Jackson's Fender Rhodes electric piano: its warm, bell-like tone defines the album's sound and became a signature of 1970s jazz-soul. The Bottle became an underground club hit, spreading through DJs rather than radio. H2Ogate Blues includes a spoken introduction about the blues as a response to crisis.","It was released on a small label but became an underground success. Critics now regard it as one of Scott-Heron's finest albums, and it is often cited as a key influence on neo-soul and conscious hip-hop. Some find H2Ogate Blues too long; others hear it as a vital political document.","Start with The Bottle for its groove, then hear Rivers of My Fathers and Peace Go with You, Brother. Listen for the electric piano. Compare with Stevie Wonder's Innervisions, from the year before, for another politically charged soul album. Then hear the whole album as one long, late-night set."],"joanna-newsom-the-milk-eyed-mender":["Twelve songs built almost entirely from one woman and a concert harp. Joanna Newsom plucks interlocking patterns in both hands, the left hand often walking a bass line while the right spins figures above it, and over this she sings in a high, cracked, wildly elastic voice that swoops, yelps and drawls. Peach Plum Pear swaps the harp for a clattering harpsichord; Sprout and the Bean is a light, skipping tune; Sadie is slow and grave. The sound is bare, close and unmistakable from the first phrase.","Newsom grew up in Nevada City, California, studied the harp from childhood, and briefly studied composition at Mills College before leaving to make her own songs. Early home recordings reached the Chicago label Drag City, which released this debut in March 2004, produced with her friend Noah Georgeson. It arrived amid a scene that the press loosely called freak folk, alongside Devendra Banhart, and her voice made her instantly famous and instantly divisive in indie-rock circles that had never heard a harp front a band.","Listen to the harp lines as counterpoint rather than accompaniment: her training is in the Celtic and classical harp, and the left hand often moves independently of the right in the way a Baroque keyboard player's would. Several songs, including Three Little Babes, borrow shapes from old Anglo-American ballads, and the record ends with En Gallop, the earliest of the songs, which already holds the rhythmic restlessness of everything to come.","Few debuts split listeners so cleanly. Many critics praised the originality of the writing and the harp playing; just as many reviewers wrote mainly about the voice, which some heard as childlike affectation and others as fearless. Over time the argument has settled in Newsom's favour, and the album is now heard as the seed of the large-scale work that followed rather than as a curiosity of the freak-folk moment.","Give the voice two or three songs before deciding; it grows familiar quickly and then becomes the point. Start with Bridges and Balloons, then Sadie for the slow, serious side, then Peach Plum Pear for the harpsichord. Follow the harp's lowest line through a whole song. Afterwards compare Alan Stivell's Celtic harp records and Kate Bush's early albums for other private worlds built on unusual voices."],"joanna-newsom-ys":["Five songs running between seven and seventeen minutes, each a long, through-composed narrative with almost no repeated choruses. Newsom's harp and voice carry everything, while a full orchestra, written by Van Dyke Parks, comments, swells, mutters and occasionally sweeps in like weather. Emily opens at twelve minutes; Monkey & Bear is a fable told in a lurching waltz; Sawdust & Diamonds is harp and voice alone; Only Skin, the longest, adds a second male voice near its end; Cosmia closes in a slow blaze of strings.","Released by Drag City in November 2006 and named after the legendary drowned city of Breton folklore. Newsom wrote the songs after a hard year that included a serious illness, the loss of people close to her and the end of a relationship. She recorded harp and voice with Steve Albini in Chicago, Parks then wrote and recorded orchestral arrangements in Los Angeles around the finished takes, and Jim O'Rourke mixed the result. Bill Callahan, her partner at the time, sings on Only Skin.","Because the orchestra was added after the harp and voice were recorded, the strings and winds are always reacting to Newsom's rubato rather than setting a beat, which is why they seem to breathe with her. Emily is addressed to her younger sister, an astrophysicist, and is full of accurate talk of meteors and meteorites. Only Skin quietly brings back melodic shapes from earlier songs, binding the record together.","Reviews were rapturous and the album appeared near the top of many end-of-year lists; it was widely compared with Joni Mitchell's Hejira and with Van Dyke Parks's own Song Cycle. Some listeners found the length and the density of the language exhausting. Two decades on it is regularly named among the most ambitious records of the century so far and the high point of the whole folk revival of the 2000s.","Set aside an hour, use headphones, and do not shuffle. Begin with Sawdust & Diamonds to hear the songs in their bare state, then go back to Emily and listen for the way the orchestra waits, sometimes for minutes, before it enters. Read the words afterward rather than during. Then compare The Smile Sessions for Parks's orchestral humour and Hejira for long-lined songwriting."],"joanna-newsom-have-one-on-me":["A triple album of eighteen songs lasting just over two hours, spread across three discs that each run like a separate evening. The writing is looser and warmer than on Ys: Newsom alternates harp and piano, her voice is lower and smoother, and the band arrangements by Ryan Francesconi bring in strings, horns, banjo and a drum kit. The title song is an eleven-minute portrait of a nineteenth-century dancer; Good Intentions Paving Company is close to a country-soul song; Baby Birch is long and devastating.","Drag City released the set in February 2010 after nearly four years of silence, during which Newsom had lost her voice for a period to vocal nodules and had to relearn how to sing, which accounts for the changed timbre. She insisted that the triple album be sold at roughly the price of a single record, taking the cost onto her own share, because she wanted listeners to hear the whole thing as one work rather than buying fragments.","The final song, Does Not Suffice, quietly reuses the melody of the opening song, Easy, so the two hours end where they began, but with the meaning turned around. The title track's subject is Lola Montez, the dancer and adventurer whose affair with King Ludwig I of Bavaria helped bring down his reign. Listen for the recurring imagery of birds and of California, which links songs across all three discs.","Most critics welcomed the album warmly, finding it more approachable than Ys and admiring the range of songwriting, and it sold well for a two-hour folk record. A few felt that the length diluted the impact and that a single disc would have been stronger. The album has aged well; many listeners now hold it as her richest and most human record, and Good Intentions Paving Company is probably her most popular song.","Treat it as three albums and hear one disc at a sitting. Start the first disc with Easy and stay through Good Intentions Paving Company, where the drums arrive. Later, come back for Baby Birch, which builds from near silence. Pay attention to the piano songs, a new colour for her. Compare Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark for a piano-driven turn toward warmth, then Laura Marling's Once I Was an Eagle."],"joanna-newsom-divers":["Eleven songs, shorter and more compressed than on her earlier albums, in which harp and piano share the lead with old synthesizers, Mellotron, a marxophone and a clavichord, with arrangements by several hands including Nico Muhly and Dave Longstreth. Sapokanikan bounces on a jaunty piano; Leaving the City surges on drums; the title song is a long, still harp ballad; Waltz of the 101st Lightborne races through a time-travel fantasy. The palette is brighter and stranger than before, but every song turns on loss and time.","Released by Drag City in October 2015, five years after Have One on Me, when Newsom was living in New York and newly married to the actor Andy Samberg. She co-produced the record with longtime collaborator Noah Georgeson and spent years on it. Its preoccupations, mortality, the unequal lengths of human lives, and history buried under city streets, run through Sapokanikan, named for a Lenape settlement that once stood where Greenwich Village is now.","The album is a loop. The last song, Time, as a Symptom, breaks off in the middle of a word, and that unfinished word completes itself when the first song begins again, so on repeat the record never quite ends. Birdsong recurs between songs as a structural thread. Sapokanikan is studded with references to Shelley's Ozymandias and to the forgotten dead of the Manhattan grid.","Critics gave Divers some of the strongest reviews of Newsom's career, praising its concision and the density of ideas packed into each song; it made many year-end lists. Some listeners who loved the orchestral sweep of Ys found the synthesizer colours less to their taste. It has since come to be heard as the most tightly constructed of her albums and, after a long silence that followed, her last for some years.","Begin with Sapokanikan, then the title track, then Leaving the City; afterwards play the whole thing twice in a row to hear the loop close. Listen for the birds in the gaps and for the clavichord, a delicate early keyboard rarely heard in popular music. Compare Kate Bush's Aerial for another domestic, time-haunted album, and Sufjan Stevens's Carrie & Lowell, released the same year, for grief handled very differently."],"iron-and-wine-the-creek-drank-the-cradle":["Eleven songs recorded at home on a four-track machine: a whispered double-tracked voice, a fingerpicked acoustic guitar, a little banjo and slide, and the hiss of the tape under everything. Sam Beam sings so softly he seems to be trying not to wake someone. The songs are short and circular, with Southern imagery of creeks, rooster crows, mothers, Bibles and dirt roads. Lion's Mane opens it, Upward Over the Mountain is the emotional centre, and Bird Stealing Bread is a perfect miniature.","Beam was teaching film and cinematography at a college in Miami and writing songs in his spare time, recording them alone at home. A demo reached Sub Pop, the Seattle label best known for grunge, which released this debut in September 2002 more or less as he had recorded it. The lo-fi sound connected him to the bedroom recordings then popular in indie rock, but the songs reached back to Appalachian and gospel forms and to Nick Drake.","The double-tracked vocal is the secret of the sound: Beam sang most lines twice, and the two slightly mismatched whispers make one ghostly voice. Upward Over the Mountain is written as a son speaking to his mother about leaving and being changed, and it frames the album's quiet family drama. Listen for small domestic noises and tape artefacts left in; the record was never cleaned up for release.","The album was a sleeper success, spread by word of mouth and warm reviews that compared Beam to Nick Drake and Elliott Smith. Some critics found the uniform hush monotonous over forty minutes. It became a template for a decade of bearded, home-recorded folk, and is now regarded as one of the founding records of 2000s indie folk alongside the early Sufjan Stevens and Bon Iver albums.","Play it quietly, late at night; it is designed for low volume. Start with Upward Over the Mountain and Bird Stealing Bread, then listen to the whole record. Try to separate the two voices in the double-tracking. Then hear Nick Drake's Pink Moon for the closest ancestor, Elliott Smith's self-titled album for another whispering voice, and Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago for what followed."],"iron-and-wine-our-endless-numbered-days":["Twelve songs that keep the hush of the debut but trade the four-track hiss for a clean studio sound. Sam Beam's voice is still close and soft, now joined by his sister Sarah's harmonies, and the guitar picking is clearer and more intricate, ringed with banjo, slide, piano and brushed drums. Naked as We Came is a two-minute song about love and death that became his best known; Sodom, South Georgia and Passing Afternoon show him writing slow, shapely melodies with a gospel undertow.","Released by Sub Pop in March 2004, this was Beam's first album made in a proper studio, produced by Brian Deck in Chicago with a small band. The same year Beam's whispered cover of the Postal Service's electronic song Such Great Heights, issued on a single, was used in the film Garden State and in advertising, bringing him a far wider audience than most folk singers of the period could expect.","Many songs are about the passage of years in a single household, and the album's title captures that tension: days that feel endless but are counted. Passing Afternoon, which closes the record, is built from a handful of repeating images of seasons and children. Listen also for the slide guitar, used sparingly as a second voice, and for how often the songs fade before their final cadence rather than resolving it.","Reviews were very warm, with most critics hearing a clear step forward in craft while keeping the intimacy. A minority missed the mystery of the tape hiss. Naked as We Came became a fixture at weddings and funerals alike, and the record is now often named as the most perfectly realised of his early, quiet period, before the larger arrangements that came next.","Start with Naked as We Came, then Passing Afternoon, then play the album through. Notice how the second voice in the harmonies is Sarah Beam's rather than his own double-tracking. Compare with The Creek Drank the Cradle to hear what a studio adds, and with Simon and Garfunkel-style close harmony. Then try Fleet Foxes' first album, which absorbed a lot from this record."],"iron-and-wine-the-shepherds-dog":["Twelve songs in which the whispering folk singer becomes a bandleader. Layered percussion, shakers, hand drums, organ, piano and electric guitar weave dense, rolling textures under Sam Beam's still-soft voice. Pagan Angel and a Borrowed Car opens on a loping groove; Boy with a Coin rides hand claps and a looped rhythm; House by the Sea has a nearly African guitar feel; Flightless Bird, American Mouth closes the album slowly on a waltz that later became his most famous song.","Sub Pop released it in September 2007, again produced by Brian Deck. Beam had toured and recorded with the Tucson band Calexico on the 2005 EP In the Reins, which opened his ears to fuller arrangements, and he spoke of the album as shaped by the confusion he felt about American politics after the 2004 election. Flightless Bird, American Mouth reached a mass audience in 2008 through the soundtrack of the film Twilight.","The songs are full of people caught between faith and doubt, and between belonging and exile. Listen for how the dense percussion is often recorded close and dry while the voice floats far back in reverb, inverting the old balance. Many of the sound loops were built in the studio from small hand-played fragments.","Critics were largely enthusiastic, many calling it his most adventurous and best record, and it sold more than his earlier albums. Some early fans lamented the loss of the bare sound. The change of direction is now seen as decisive: it showed that the quiet indie folk of the early 2000s could absorb world-music rhythm and studio colour without losing its centre.","Start with Boy with a Coin to hear the new rhythmic engine, then Flightless Bird, American Mouth for the old tenderness. Listen to the percussion as its own story under the songs. Compare Paul Simon's rhythm-driven later records, then go back to The Creek Drank the Cradle to measure how far the sound travelled in five years. Bon Iver's second album took a similar step."],"laura-marling-i-speak-because-i-can":["Ten songs in which a twenty-year-old's voice has dropped into a grave, assured alto, over fingerpicked and strummed acoustic guitar driven hard by a small band of banjo, upright bass and drums, with strings and brass at the edges. Devil's Spoke opens it with a stomping, minor-key urgency; Rambling Man is a resolute anthem; Goodbye England (Covered in Snow) is tender and pastoral; Alpha Shallows and the title song close darkly. The sound is closer to English folk-rock than to London indie.","Marling grew up in Hampshire, left home young for London, and was at the centre of the West London nu-folk scene alongside Mumford & Sons and Noah and the Whale. Released by Virgin in March 2010 and produced by Ethan Johns, this second album was recorded largely live, with members of Mumford & Sons in the band. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and the following year she won the Brit Award for best British female solo artist.","Listen to how the songs are written about women's duties, guilt and the weight of being responsible for others, rather than simple romance. Many guitar parts are in open tunings that let low strings drone beneath the melody, a habit inherited from Bert Jansch and Nick Drake. Ethan Johns kept the room sound, including performers' breaths.","Critics greeted the album as a leap from her charming debut, praising the maturity of the writing and the deep new voice; it topped a few British year-end lists and the Mercury nomination confirmed it. A few heard the folk-revival trappings of her scene as fashionable. It now reads as the start of the run of records that made her one of the most respected British songwriters of her generation.","Start with Devil's Spoke and Rambling Man for drive, then Goodbye England for the gentle side. Listen to how the bass and drums push behind the beat. Compare Sandy Denny's solo records and Joni Mitchell's Blue for women writing seriously over acoustic guitar, and Bert Jansch for the tunings. Ethan Johns's natural production rewards a good stereo."],"laura-marling-once-i-was-an-eagle":["Sixteen songs over an hour, opening with a run of four songs that flow into each other without a break for some sixteen minutes, all in the same open guitar tuning and darkening as they go. Marling's voice and guitar dominate, shadowed by a cello and by hand drums that pulse rather than keep time. An instrumental Interlude splits the album in two; the second half, with songs like Master Hunter and Where Can I Go?, is lighter, more varied and freer in mood.","Released by Virgin in May 2013 and again produced by Ethan Johns, who also plays drums and percussion, the album was recorded quickly, largely live, with Marling playing guitar and singing simultaneously. She had recently ended a relationship and moved toward a period of travel and relocation to Los Angeles. It was her third Mercury Prize nomination and was widely hailed as her most ambitious work.","The long opening suite is held together by a single guitar tuning and a recurring melodic shape, so that the four songs work almost as movements. Listen for Indian and Middle Eastern inflections in the guitar and the hand drums, which recall the raga-touched guitar of the 1960s British folk baroque. The songs track a woman refusing to be cast as victim, ending far from where they begin.","Reviews were outstanding, with several British critics calling it the album of the year and comparing its scope to Joni Mitchell's Hejira. Some found its length and uniformity of mood demanding. It is now often regarded as Marling's central work and one of the best British folk albums of the century, though listeners split over whether it surpasses her later, sparer records.","Play the first four songs straight through without interruption; that is the heart of the album. Notice the cello acting as a second voice, then wait for Interlude and hear how the mood lifts. Compare Joni Mitchell's Hejira and Joanna Newsom's Have One on Me for long-form songwriting, and Bert Jansch's open-tuned guitar for the playing tradition behind it."],"laura-marling-song-for-our-daughter":["Ten songs addressed, broadly, to a daughter Marling did not yet have, about what a young woman should know about love, power and self-respect. The sound is warm and unhurried: fingerpicked guitar and light piano, softly layered harmony vocals, a string section arranged with gentle restraint, and a band that sounds like it is in the room. Alexandra opens brightly; Held Down is a quiet anthem; the title song and For You end the record in near-lullaby simplicity.","Marling brought the release forward to April 2020 because of the first wave of the pandemic, issuing it on Chrysalis and Partisan with little warning. She co-produced it with Ethan Johns and engineer Dom Monks. It was nominated for the Mercury Prize and for the Grammy Award for Best Folk Album, and led to a series of livestreamed concerts during lockdown; Marling later had a daughter of her own.","Alexandra is an answer song to Leonard Cohen's Alexandra Leaving, reimagining the woman in Cohen's song as someone with her own reasons rather than a figure of a man's loss. The string arrangements consciously echo the orchestrations of late-1960s and early-1970s singer-songwriter records. Listen to how the harmony vocals are multitracked Marling herself, building a small choir of one voice.","Critics welcomed it as her warmest and most direct album, and it featured in many 2020 year-end lists; its lockdown timing gave its calm an unexpected resonance. A few longtime admirers missed the darker edge of her earlier work. It is now generally ranked among her finest records and is often the album new listeners are pointed to first.","Start with Alexandra, then go to Leonard Cohen's Alexandra Leaving to hear the conversation. Then listen to Held Down and the title track and notice how little the strings do and how much it matters. Compare Joni Mitchell's Ladies of the Canyon and Nick Drake's Bryter Layter for the string-laced folk tradition the album consciously joins."],"rhiannon-giddens-tomorrow-is-my-turn":["Eleven songs, nearly all associated with women singers across a century of American and European music: Dolly Parton, Patsy Cline, Odetta, Nina Simone, Elizabeth Cotten, Sister Rosetta Tharpe, the blues singer Geeshie Wiley and others, plus one original. Giddens's operatically trained voice moves from country tears to gospel shouting to a cabaret whisper. The band, assembled by T Bone Burnett, is spacious and woody, with fiddle, banjo and an organ. Waterboy surges; Last Kind Words is haunted; the title song is French chanson filtered through Simone.","Giddens had trained in opera at Oberlin Conservatory before co-founding the Carolina Chocolate Drops, the Grammy-winning string band that restored Black musicians to the story of old-time music. A showstopping appearance at a 2013 New York concert celebrating the film Inside Llewyn Davis led T Bone Burnett to produce this, her first solo album, released by Nonesuch in February 2015.","Each choice is an argument: the album is a quiet history of women, Black and white, who carried American music, and of the way songs moved between gospel, country, blues and French cabaret. Last Kind Words comes from a 1930 recording by Geeshie Wiley, a singer about whom almost nothing is known. Angel City, the original, is her only song on the record and points toward the writer she would become.","Reviews were excellent, singling out the range and control of the voice, and the album launched her solo career. Some critics wished for more of her own writing and found the covers too reverent. It now reads as a manifesto for the career that followed, which brought her a MacArthur Fellowship in 2017 and later a Pulitzer Prize for the opera Omar, written with Michael Abels.","Start with Waterboy and then go back to Odetta's version for comparison; then hear Last Kind Words. Listen to how Giddens changes vocal colour completely from song to song. Pair it with Nina Simone's Pastel Blues and Elizabeth Cotten's Folksongs and Instrumentals with Guitar, both direct sources, and then the Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig."],"rhiannon-giddens-freedom-highway":["Twelve songs, mostly originals, that move through the history of Black Americans from slavery to police violence. Giddens plays minstrel-era banjo and fiddle and sings with rough-edged power; the band adds blues guitar, brass, gospel choir harmonies and, on one song, a rap verse. At the Purchaser's Option opens over a hypnotic banjo; Julie is a dialogue between an enslaved woman and her mistress; Birmingham Sunday is a sorrowful cover; the title song, from the Staple Singers, closes in jubilation.","Released by Nonesuch in February 2017 and co-produced by Giddens and the multi-instrumentalist Dirk Powell, largely in Powell's home studio in Breaux Bridge, Louisiana. Giddens had been reading slave narratives and collections of advertisements for enslaved people, and the record appeared during the national argument over police killings and the Black Lives Matter movement, which it addresses directly in one song.","At the Purchaser's Option was prompted by a real nineteenth-century advertisement offering an enslaved young woman for sale with her nine-month-old baby included at the buyer's choice. Birmingham Sunday, written by Richard Fariña, remembers the 1963 church bombing. Listen to the banjo itself: Giddens plays a gourd or fretless style that recalls the instrument's African ancestry, a history she has spent her career reasserting.","Critics welcomed it as her most ambitious and personal record, praising the songwriting and the courage of its subjects; some found the stylistic shifts, especially the hip-hop track, uneven. It sealed her reputation as a musician-historian, and it remains a key album in the conversation about Black roots music and who owns the banjo and the fiddle.","Start with At the Purchaser's Option and listen to the banjo pattern under the voice; then hear Julie as a short play for two characters. End with the title song and then play the Staple Singers' original. Pair with Nina Simone's protest songs and with Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig. Read the liner notes, which give each song's history."],"rhiannon-giddens-there-is-no-other":["A duo record with the Italian multi-instrumentalist Francesco Turrisi, who plays frame drums, accordion, piano and early-music instruments. Twelve songs drawn from many places: American spirituals and ballads, a southern Italian tarantella-style dance, a Gian Carlo Menotti aria, an Oscar Brown Jr. song and originals. The sound is bare, physical and drum-led. Ten Thousand Voices opens with a pulse of frame drum; Wayfaring Stranger is slowed to a trance; Pizzica di San Vito whirls; Black Swan is sung like an old ballad.","Giddens had moved to Ireland, and she and Turrisi recorded in Dublin in a few days with producer Joe Henry. Nonesuch released it in May 2019. The project grew from their shared conviction that the banjo and the frame drum trace routes from Africa through the Mediterranean and Europe to America, so that there is no pure folk music, only exchange.","The title is the thesis: that what we call one tradition is always another's in disguise. Listen for Turrisi, a musician with roots in early music as well as jazz, switching between frame drum, accordion and piano, quietly connecting older European practice to folk. Black Swan comes from Menotti's 1946 opera The Medium, sung here as if it were an Appalachian ballad, which is precisely the point. Many takes were captured live with both players in one room.","Critics praised the album's daring and intimacy, and it was widely admired as a model of cross-cultural collaboration. Some listeners found it more of a scholarly exercise than an emotional record. It began a lasting partnership; the pair's follow-up, They're Calling Me Home, came in 2021, and the duo has become a central part of Giddens's work.","Start with Ten Thousand Voices for the drum and voice, then Pizzica di San Vito for the Italian dance, then Wayfaring Stranger. Listen to how few instruments there are. Pair with Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances, another case of art music made from shared folk dance, and with Alan Lomax's Columbia World Library, which set out the same idea of connected traditions in the 1950s."],"anais-mitchell-young-man-in-america":["Eleven songs, many sung by Mitchell in the voices of men: sons, fathers, wanderers and a shepherd. Her high, slightly nasal voice is set against a full band of guitars, piano, strings and a deep, rolling rhythm section, with a darker, more cinematic sound than her earlier folk records. The title song is a slow, ominous portrait of a restless young man; Shepherd tells a rural tragedy over a long, patient build; He Did is a quiet elegy-like portrait of her father; Coming Down glows.","Released on Mitchell's own Wilderland label in February 2012 and produced by Todd Sickafoose, it was her first album after the concept record Hadestown of 2010, the folk opera that would years later become a Tony-winning Broadway musical. Mitchell grew up on a sheep farm in Vermont, and the album draws on that world and on her father's writing, using the stage-trained skill of singing through characters she had developed on Hadestown.","Shepherd is a retelling of a story from her father Don Mitchell's novel The Souls of Lambs, about a farmer whose wife dies in childbirth while he works in the fields. Listen for the way recurring images of land, inheritance and debt link the songs into a loose cycle about American manhood. The album closes on songs of forgiveness that soften its darker portraits.","Reviews were warm and often admiring, calling it her most fully realised album and noting the confident, filmic production. Some reviewers missed the intimacy of her earlier acoustic work, and the Hadestown project overshadowed it in public memory. Folk listeners still hold it as her finest solo record and the bridge between her songwriting and her theatre writing.","Start with the title song and then Shepherd; follow the story in Shepherd as you would a short film. Notice how often she sings as a man and how her voice changes to do it. Then hear the original Hadestown album for comparison, and Gillian Welch's The Harrow & the Harvest for another meditation on rural American life."],"anais-mitchell-anais-mitchell":["Nine songs of reflective, quietly autobiographical folk-pop, gently produced and warmly sung. Mitchell's voice is clearer and calmer than before, set against fingerpicked guitar, piano, soft drums and a pale wash of keyboards. Brooklyn Bridge opens with a song about nostalgia for an earlier New York life; Bright Star is a gentle love song; Little Big Girl looks at a young woman's ambition; Revenant and Real Life probe memory and ageing; Now You Know closes in a radiant, simple tune.","Released by BMG in January 2022, produced by her Bonny Light Horseman bandmate Josh Kaufman, and recorded with musicians from the Hudson Valley scene, the album was her first collection of new original songs in roughly a decade. In that time Hadestown had grown from a folk album into a Broadway musical that won eight Tony Awards in 2019; Mitchell then moved back toward Vermont during the pandemic and wrote these songs looking back on her years in New York.","Listen to the economy of the arrangements: after years writing for a full Broadway pit, Mitchell and Kaufman deliberately limited each song to a few textures, often a single guitar figure. Several songs, including Brooklyn Bridge and On Your Way (Felix Song), name real places and people from her past. The self-title signals a reset, an album of herself after years of writing in characters' voices.","Critics welcomed the album as graceful and generous, some finding it her most personal record and others thinking it understated after the scale of Hadestown. It placed on some year-end folk lists. It is generally heard as a satisfying coda to the Broadway years and a companion to the more traditional songs of Bonny Light Horseman.","Begin with Brooklyn Bridge, then Bright Star and Now You Know. Listen to the warmth of the production and the restraint of the band. Pair it with Joni Mitchell's Ladies of the Canyon for the comfortable, domestic folk-pop sound, and with Bonny Light Horseman's debut to hear the same players on older material."],"sam-amidon-all-is-well":["Traditional Appalachian ballads, hymns and children's songs, sung in a flat, unhurried, conversational voice over banjo or guitar, then wrapped in chamber arrangements of strings, brass and winds by the composer Nico Muhly. The old songs, including Saro and Wild Bill Jones, are slowed and pulled apart so the arrangements can drift around them like mist. The effect is half folk record and half contemporary chamber music, with long, still introductions and sudden swells.","Amidon grew up in Brattleboro, Vermont, the son of folk musicians who led community singing and taught traditional song, and he played fiddle from childhood. He recorded the album in Reykjavík for Valgeir Sigurðsson's label Bedroom Community, which also released music by Muhly and Ben Frost. It appeared in 2008 and placed him in an unlikely circle where American folk song met New York and Icelandic contemporary composition.","Muhly's arrangements often take a single phrase from the tune and turn it into a repeating pattern that keeps going after the voice stops, so minimalist technique emerges from the song itself. Amidon learned many of these songs from field recordings and from his parents rather than from records. Listen for the different speed of the singer and the ensemble: he often sings slightly behind the arranged pulse, keeping the old songs loose.","Reviews in indie and folk circles were warm, with critics admiring the strangeness of the pairing and the honesty of Amidon's singing. Some traditionalists found the arrangements too fussy; some art-music listeners found the voice too plain. The record is now valued as a pioneering meeting of folk song and contemporary composition, and as the start of Amidon's long reinvention of the American songbook.","Start with Saro and hear how long the ensemble takes to settle before the voice arrives. Listen to the arrangements as a separate piece of music. Compare Harry Smith's Anthology of American Folk Music for the kind of sources Amidon draws on, and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for a similar stillness. Then hear I See the Sign for the next step."],"sam-amidon-i-see-the-sign":["Old ballads, shape-note hymns, a spiritual and one pop cover, recast with livelier arrangements than on All Is Well. Amidon's flat, unfussy voice and banjo are joined by Nico Muhly's strings and brass, a rhythm section, and duets with the English singer Beth Orton. The title song is a jittery revival chant; Way Go Lily has a nursery-rhyme bounce; Relief, an R. Kelly song, is quietly turned into folk lament; Kedron is a shape-note hymn made eerie by its open harmonies.","Released by Bedroom Community in 2010 and recorded in Reykjavík with Valgeir Sigurðsson producing, it was the follow-up to All Is Well and widened the circle of collaborators. Muhly had meanwhile become one of the busiest young composers in New York. The record joined a growing current of folk musicians, from the Punch Brothers to Rhiannon Giddens, who treated American traditional music as a living repertoire rather than a museum.","Kedron comes from the Sacred Harp shape-note tradition, sung in rural Southern churches since the nineteenth century, and its bare fourths and fifths give it a medieval sound that Muhly's arrangement brings out. The R. Kelly cover is a deliberate test: by singing a contemporary R&B song with folk plainness, Amidon shows that the old tradition and pop belong to one continuum of song.","Critics liked the album's warmth and variety and many named it one of the most original folk records of 2010; some still found Amidon's deadpan delivery hard to love. It confirmed him as an influential, if cult, figure whose approach to traditional song inspired later experimental folk musicians, and is often the best introduction to his work.","Begin with the title track for energy, then Kedron for the shape-note eeriness, then Relief to hear an R&B song as an old ballad. Listen to Beth Orton's harmony on the duets. Compare Shirley Collins's Anthems in Eden, another bold dressing of old songs, and Sufjan Stevens's Illinois for related arrangements."],"the-weather-station-ignorance":["Ten songs in which Tamara Lindeman's soft, breathy, conversational voice floats over a band that pulses rather than strums: two drummers, a running bass, rippling piano and electric guitar, with flute, saxophone and strings drifting in and out. Robber opens with a nervous, propulsive groove; Atlantic is about watching the sea while thinking of catastrophe; Tried to Tell You is close to a pop song; Parking Lot and Heart are slower and more fragile. The sound recalls soft rock and jazz-inflected art pop more than acoustic folk.","Lindeman, a Toronto songwriter and former actor, had made four quieter folk albums as the Weather Station before this one, released by Fat Possum in February 2021 and co-produced with Marcus Paquin. She had become involved in climate activism, and the album was written out of grief and fear about the climate crisis and the struggle to feel and speak about it. It arrived during the pandemic's second winter and became her breakthrough.","The double drumming is the engine: two players lock into a light, steady grid, so the songs move like a nervous heartbeat beneath the calm vocal. Lindeman deliberately set personal, anxious lyrics against warm, almost seductive arrangements to mirror the way climate dread sits under ordinary life. Listen to the flute and saxophone as the record's weather, entering and receding without solos.","Critics greeted it as a major leap and among the best albums of 2021; it was shortlisted for the Polaris Music Prize in Canada. Some folk listeners missed the acoustic intimacy of her earlier work, and some found the climate theme stated too gently. It is now heard as one of the defining Canadian albums of its decade.","Begin with Robber and listen to the drums, then Atlantic. Notice how the voice never pushes, even when the band builds. Compare Joni Mitchell's The Hissing of Summer Lawns for jazzy, wide-eyed songwriting, and Radiohead's In Rainbows for anxious, beautiful grooves. Then go back to her earlier folk albums."],"lankum-the-livelong-day":["Traditional Irish and English songs and one or two originals, slowed to a crawl and stacked on enormous drones from uilleann pipes, harmonium, concertina and bowed strings, so that the music sounds closer to doom metal or drone music than to the pub session. Radie Peat's unadorned voice leads the slow ballads; the brothers Ian and Daragh Lynch take others. The Wild Rover, usually a drinking song, becomes a menacing dirge; Katie Cruel is stark; Hunting the Wren ends in a long, swirling instrumental.","Lankum, a Dublin quartet originally called Lynched, grew out of the city's squat and punk scenes as much as from traditional music. Produced by John Murphy and released on Rough Trade in October 2019, this third album was recorded in Dublin and drew wide acclaim in Ireland and Britain. It came as a young generation of Irish musicians, including Lisa O'Neill and Ye Vagabonds, were reclaiming traditional song in an Ireland transformed by boom and bust.","Listen to the drones themselves: the band layers pipes, harmonium and bowed instruments that are slightly out of tune with one another, so the overtones beat against each other and the sound seems to throb. Murphy recorded instruments in reverberant spaces and with close microphones together, so you hear breath and key clicks inside a vast sound. The Wild Rover, slowed and stripped of its chorus energy, reveals a song about poverty and regret.","Critics in Ireland and Britain hailed it as a radical reinvention of Irish folk, and it topped several lists. Some traditional players found the drones and darkness too heavy-handed. It prepared the ground for the wider breakthrough of False Lankum, and is now heard as a founding document of a darker, experimental Irish revival.","Start with The Wild Rover, ideally loud, and let the drone fill the room. Then listen to Katie Cruel. Compare Planxty's The Well Below the Valley for the classic Irish revival, then Swans' The Seer for the drone side. Notice how silence and decay are part of the music. Headphones help reveal the layers."],"lankum-false-lankum":["Over seventy minutes, traditional ballads and two originals stretched into slow, massive structures, separated by three short instrumental pieces called Fugue. Go Dig My Grave opens with Radie Peat's lone voice before drones, distortion and pounding percussion swallow it; The New York Trader is a ghostly sea ballad; Master Crowley's is a reel that disintegrates; The Turn is the band's own. The ending, The Turn, is huge, slow and cathartic. It is folk music played like experimental noise.","Released on Rough Trade in March 2023 and again produced by John Murphy in Dublin, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, bringing Lankum to international festival stages. The title plays on the Child ballad False Lankin, a song of murder and revenge, and on the band's own name. It appeared as Irish traditional music was attracting audiences who also followed doom, drone and ambient music.","The three Fugues are short studies in texture and processed sound rather than tunes, giving the ear somewhere to rest between the ballads. Listen to the long tails of reverb and decay, which Murphy treats as part of the arrangement rather than as ambience. Go Dig My Grave uses a ballad that also exists in American versions, linking Dublin to Appalachia across the Atlantic.","Reviews were rapturous, many calling it the year's best album in any genre; it reached audiences far beyond folk. A few listeners found the length and density daunting, and purists questioned how much tradition remained. It is now considered a landmark of twenty-first-century Irish music and of experimental folk anywhere.","Start with Go Dig My Grave and give it all of its length; the crescendo is the point. Then The New York Trader. Play loud or on good headphones. Compare Planxty's Cold Blow and the Rainy Night, a classic revival, with this, and Sunn O)))'s Monoliths and Dimensions for a cousin in drone. Then go back to The Livelong Day."],"sarah-jarosz-undercurrent":["Eleven songs, sparely arranged around Sarah Jarosz's clear alto and her guitar, octave mandolin or clawhammer banjo, with only bass and occasional percussion or a second voice behind her. The songs are written with folk economy and bluegrass precision, but the feel is intimate and reflective rather than virtuosic. House of Mercy rolls on a mandolin pattern; Green Lights is airy; Comin' Undone and Jacqueline are restless; Lost Dog closes in near silence. Nothing is busy; every note sounds chosen.","Jarosz grew up in Wimberley, Texas, a mandolin prodigy in the Austin bluegrass scene, signed to Sugar Hill while still in high school, and studied at the New England Conservatory. Released by Sugar Hill in June 2016 and produced with Gary Paczosa, this fourth album was her first after moving to New York and her first written largely alone. It won the Grammy Awards for Best Folk Album and, for House of Mercy, Best American Roots Performance.","Listen to the octave mandolin: it is tuned an octave below a standard mandolin, so it gives Jarosz a low, ringing voice somewhere between guitar and bouzouki. Several songs were co-written with friends from the Nashville and New York acoustic scenes but deliberately recorded with few players, the opposite of her more crowded early records. Her earlier album covered Joanna Newsom's Book of Right-On, a hint of her taste.","Critics praised the album's confident simplicity and her growth as a writer, and the Grammy wins brought her a larger audience. Some found it subdued compared with her bluegrass beginnings. It is now generally heard as the moment she became a songwriter first and an instrumentalist second, leading toward her trio I'm With Her and the Grammy-winning World on the Ground.","Start with House of Mercy and listen to the instrument pattern under the voice, then Green Lights and Lost Dog. Notice how much space there is. Compare Alison Krauss's So Long So Wrong for an earlier generation's precision, and Gillian Welch's Revival for duo spareness. Punch Brothers' records show where the instrumental side of her world goes."],"punch-brothers-punch":["The centrepiece is The Blind Leaving the Blind, a four-movement suite of about forty minutes for bluegrass quintet: mandolin, fiddle, banjo, guitar and bass, with Chris Thile singing lead. It moves through slow introductions, fast contrapuntal passages where each instrument has its own line, song sections and long instrumental developments, much closer to a string quartet than to a bluegrass band. A few shorter songs frame it. The sound is acoustic and dry, every instrument distinct, the playing ferociously exact.","Punch Brothers formed in 2006 around Chris Thile, the mandolin prodigy of Nickel Creek, with Gabe Witcher, Noam Pikelny, Chris Eldridge and Greg Garrison. Thile wrote the suite while coming through a divorce, and Nonesuch released the album in 2008. The band was openly aiming to bring chamber-music thinking into bluegrass, as Thile also recorded Bach on mandolin and later received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2012.","The suite uses recurring motifs: a phrase from the first movement returns, transformed, in later movements, the way themes work across a classical symphony. Listeners have long heard Bach's partitas and Bartók's string quartets behind it, and Thile has recorded Bach on mandolin. Listen to the banjo: Pikelny often plays sustained, melodic lines instead of rolling bluegrass patterns, so the instrument behaves more like a harp or a piano.","Critics and musicians were impressed, and the suite was widely discussed as a breakthrough for acoustic music, though some found it more admirable than lovable and wondered whether it was bluegrass at all. It is now seen as a founding document of progressive acoustic chamber music in America, and the launch pad for one of the most technically celebrated bands in any genre.","Hear the suite in one sitting, as you would a chamber piece. Follow one instrument, for example the fiddle, through a movement, then listen again following the banjo. Compare Bach's Violin Partita No. 2 and Bartók's String Quartet No. 4 for the models, and Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky to measure how far bluegrass travelled."],"punch-brothers-the-phosphorescent-blues":["An album about how people connect in the age of the smartphone, built on the band's all-acoustic quintet but sounding as layered as an art-rock record. Familiarity opens with a ten-minute, multi-part song that swings from hushed choral harmony to driving rock; Julep and My Oh My are tuneful and wistful; two pieces are arrangements of piano works by Debussy and Scriabin; I Blew It Off is jaunty. Vocal harmonies are lush, close and often wordless, and the playing remains dazzlingly exact.","Released by Nonesuch in January 2015 and produced by T Bone Burnett, who had worked with members of the band on film soundtracks. By now the line-up had settled with Paul Kowert on bass. The band were touring large venues and their ambitions had widened from bluegrass to art rock, with Radiohead's influence openly acknowledged. Burnett's production gave them a warm, enveloping sound.","Passepied is an arrangement of a movement from Debussy's Suite bergamasque, the same suite that includes Clair de lune, for mandolin, fiddle, banjo, guitar and bass, and it shows how naturally impressionist harmony lies on bluegrass instruments. Familiarity's wordless chorale was built from stacked vocal lines in the studio. Listen for the banjo and mandolin playing short repeated cells in the minimalist manner.","Critics praised its ambition and beauty; it reached high on folk and Americana charts and many year-end lists, though some reviewers found its preoccupation with technology dated or heavy-handed. It is now widely heard as the band's most complete album and the clearest statement of what progressive acoustic music could be in the twenty-first century.","Start with Familiarity and stay for its whole ten minutes, then hear Passepied and play Debussy's Clair de lune afterwards for the source. Listen to the vocal harmonies in Julep. Compare Radiohead's In Rainbows for the art-rock side, and Steve Reich's Music for Pieces of Wood for the minimalist patterns. Punch is their earlier, more austere suite."],"jake-blount-spider-tales":["Old-time fiddle tunes, ballads, blues and spirituals, all drawn from the music of Black and Indigenous Americans, played with deep, driving rhythm on fiddle and banjo by Jake Blount and a small circle of guests. His voice is rich and unhurried; the fiddle saws with the insistent drive of a dance hall. Some tracks are stripped to fiddle and voice, others build to full string-band momentum. The playing is traditional in style but the selection makes a pointed, coherent statement.","Blount, a Black and queer banjo player and fiddler who studied ethnomusicology, released this debut on Free Dirt Records in May 2020, weeks before the George Floyd protests. He had spent years researching the forgotten Black roots of old-time music, a scene long presented as mainly white and rural. The title refers to the trickster spider Anansi of West African and Caribbean stories, who survives by wit, a figure for how this music survived erasure.","Each song was chosen from a documented Black or Indigenous source, many from field recordings by players whose names appear only in archives, so the album is also a work of research. Listen to the rhythm of the fiddle bow, which emphasises off-beats in a style that traces back to African-American dance fiddling and through it to West African string traditions. The liner notes give every song's origin.","Reviews were very strong in folk and roots circles and the album appeared on many 2020 year-end lists; critics praised both the playing and the argument. A few noted that the austere sound might limit its reach. It is now considered an important record in the reclamation of Black string-band music, in a line with the Carolina Chocolate Drops and Rhiannon Giddens.","Listen first for the fiddle's rhythm, then the voice. Pick the songs that are only fiddle and voice and hear how little is needed. Pair with the Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig and with Lead Belly's Last Sessions for the older sources. Read the liner notes alongside; they turn the album into a lesson in history."],"jake-blount-the-new-faith":["A concept album of spirituals and old religious songs, imagined as the hymns of survivors in a future ravaged by climate change. Banjo, fiddle and Blount's voice sit alongside drum machines, distorted bass, synthesizers and choral overdubs, with rap verses and guest singers. Some songs stay close to traditional performance; others are rebuilt as stark electronic soundscapes. The effect is of folk music heard through a broken radio from the future, both ancient and brand new.","Released by Smithsonian Folkways in September 2022, the label that holds much of the archive of American traditional music, this was a striking choice: a folk album framed as Afrofuturist speculation. Blount described a story in which displaced Black people in a climate-ravaged future preserve their faith through old spirituals. It came as Black musicians across genres were turning to science fiction to imagine survival.","Many songs were taken from early twentieth-century recordings of Black congregational singing, and Blount kept their call-and-response structure even when the sound is electronic. Listen for field-recording textures and sampled crowd noise used as a choir from the past. The project quietly argues that the spirituals were themselves a kind of future-oriented music, written by people imagining freedom they could not yet see.","Critics praised the concept's daring and the album received strong reviews and folk awards attention; a few traditionalists found the electronic treatment jarring, and some reviewers felt the narrative was hard to follow from the music alone. It is now seen as one of the most adventurous American folk records of its decade.","Start with the most traditional-sounding tracks and then move to the most electronic, listening for the old call and response beneath. Read the story Blount supplies with the record. Compare Sam Amidon's I See the Sign for another experimental take on hymns, and Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome for spirituals as songs of struggle."],"bonny-light-horseman-bonny-light-horseman":["Ten traditional Anglo-Irish and American folk songs, rewritten and rearranged by a trio: Anaïs Mitchell and Eric D. Johnson share lead vocals and harmonies over Josh Kaufman's guitars and a soft, drum-led band. The title song, a ballad of a woman mourning a soldier lost in the Napoleonic wars, opens slowly and swells; Deep in Love is a lovely duet; The Roving and Magpie's Nest are lighter. The sound is warm, unhurried and close to 1970s folk rock, with hints of Fleet Foxes harmony.","The trio first performed together at the Eaux Claires festival run by Bon Iver's Justin Vernon, and the album was released on Vernon's associated label 37d03d in January 2020, partly recorded during a residency in Berlin. Mitchell had just seen Hadestown open on Broadway, and Johnson led the indie band Fruit Bats. The record was nominated for the Grammy Award for Best Folk Album.","Many of the songs were altered: the band added verses, changed melodies or combined fragments of different versions, so this is a new composition from old material, in the way folk singers have always worked. Listen to the title song's source, a ballad associated with Ireland's long tradition of songs about soldiers who did not return. Mitchell and Johnson's voices are balanced so neither dominates.","Critics welcomed it warmly as a graceful renewal of folk-rock and a fine showcase for Mitchell's voice outside theatre; a few found it too smooth or polite for songs about loss and violence. It has become a staple of the Americana-folk scene, and the band continued with further albums.","Start with the title song, then Deep in Love. Listen to how the two voices trade lines. Compare Fairport Convention's Liege & Lief and Planxty's The Well Below the Valley for the classic folk-rock treatment of old songs, and Fleet Foxes' first album for the harmonies. Then hear Anaïs Mitchell's 2022 solo record for the same players."],"adrianne-lenker-songs-and-instrumentals":["Two albums released together. songs is eleven tracks for voice and acoustic guitar alone, recorded in a wooden cabin so that rain, wind and birds can be heard around the music: Two Reverse, Anything, Zombie Girl, Ingydar and Forwards Beckon Rebound are full of lucid, tender detail. instrumentals is two long pieces, music for indigo and mostly chimes, the first a meandering guitar improvisation, the second built from wind chimes and the room. It is about as close and unadorned as recorded folk can be.","When the pandemic cancelled Big Thief's 2020 tour, Lenker retreated to a one-room cabin in western Massachusetts and recorded with the engineer Philip Weinrobe on analog tape, after the end of a relationship. 4AD released the double album in October 2020. It came after Big Thief's two 2019 albums and became her most celebrated solo work, deepening the mythology of her as a songwriter of rare openness.","Weinrobe recorded to tape with simple equipment and made no attempt to seal out the world beyond the walls. Listen to the outdoors: rain on the roof on some songs, wind chimes and birds on others, so the place is the album's other instrument. Some songs here were later reworked by Big Thief, showing how Lenker's solo writing feeds the band.","Reviews were rapturous, critics calling songs one of the year's best albums and praising its emotional honesty; some found the instrumentals self-indulgent. The album has come to symbolise the hushed, intimate recordings of the pandemic year, and many listeners regard it as the purest expression of Lenker's art.","Listen to songs late at night, quietly, in order. Start with Anything and Zombie Girl and listen for the room around them. Save instrumentals for a long walk or a still hour. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for solitude and guitar, and Big Thief's U.F.O.F. for how her songs grow in the band. Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago is another cabin record."],"adrianne-lenker-bright-future":["Twelve songs recorded live by Lenker with three friends, Nick Hakim, Mat Davidson and Josefin Runsteen, around a single room: guitars, an upright piano, fiddle and voices. Real House opens with a slow, bare piano and a memory of childhood; Sadness as a Gift is a gentle country song; Free Treasure and Vampire Empire are older songs reworked; Ruined and Donut Seam are tender; Evol plays with language. The playing sounds unrehearsed, with mistakes and laughter left in.","4AD released the album in March 2024. It was recorded at a studio deep in the woods, again with engineer Philip Weinrobe and onto analog tape, the four musicians playing together without headphones. Lenker made it as Big Thief's profile was growing, and the band's bassist Max Oleartchik left soon after; the solo record served as a quieter counterweight to the band's large, celebrated albums.","Vampire Empire had been played live by Big Thief for some time; here it is slowed and softened, so the same song shows two different characters. Listen for the background: the performers react to one another, a piano chair creaks, voices join late. Runsteen's fiddle often doubles the melody slightly ahead or behind, giving an old-time string-band roughness to otherwise intimate songs.","Critics praised its warmth and the communal feel; the album placed on many year-end lists, though some found it less striking than songs and instrumentals. It has been embraced as an affectionate, communal companion to her solitary earlier work, with Real House widely singled out as one of her finest songs.","Start with Real House and listen to how the piano and voice sit in the room. Then hear Sadness as a Gift and Vampire Empire. Notice the humanity of the performance, the breath and stray sounds. Compare songs and instrumentals for the solo version of her voice, and Gillian Welch's Time (The Revelator) for live, close-knit acoustic intimacy."],"nick-mulvey-first-mind":["Songs built on intricate fingerpicked guitar patterns that owe as much to West African and Cuban music as to English folk, sung in a soft, low voice. The guitar often plays repeating cross-rhythms in which melody and accompaniment interlock; light percussion and bass add pulse, and occasional synthesizer colours deepen the sound. Cucurucu is bright and hypnotic; Fever to the Form and Juramidam are warmer and more expansive. The record's calm, rippling surface hides complicated rhythmic design.","Mulvey, from Cambridge, studied ethnomusicology at SOAS in London, spent time in Havana, and was a founding member of the Mercury-nominated Portico Quartet, playing the hang drum. Leaving the group, he became a singer-songwriter, and this debut was released on Fiction Records in May 2014. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and arrived amid a British folk scene that was looking beyond Anglo-American roots.","Listen for polyrhythm: many of his guitar patterns set three against two or four, a technique he learned from West African kora and guitar traditions, so the songs seem to move at two speeds at once. His years playing the hang drum, a melodic steel percussion instrument, show in the way the guitar patterns sound like bells. Juramidam's title refers to a Brazilian spiritual tradition.","Critics welcomed it warmly, praising the guitar writing and the global influences, and the Mercury nomination widened his audience; some reviewers found the songs too gentle and the lyrics vague. It is now remembered as one of the more musically curious British folk debuts of its decade.","Start with Cucurucu and try to tap the two rhythms in the guitar at once. Then hear Fever to the Form. Compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for interlocking patterns, and Nick Drake's Bryter Layter for English fingerpicking. Then seek out West African guitar records to hear the deeper roots."],"julia-jacklin-crushing":["Ten songs about the aftermath of a breakup and the right to one's own body and space, sung in a clear, steady voice that rarely raises itself. The arrangements are sparse indie-folk: chiming electric guitar, brushed or tight drums, and much empty space. Body opens over a single guitar line; Pressure to Party is the one fast, defiant rocker; Don't Know How to Keep Loving You and Comfort are quietly devastating ballads. The restraint is the force; nothing is overplayed.","Jacklin grew up in the Blue Mountains near Sydney and released her debut in 2016. This second album, released in February 2019 by Polyvinyl and Transgressive and produced by Burke Reid in Sydney, was written after two years of touring and the end of a long relationship. It arrived amid wider conversations about consent and personal boundaries, and several songs speak to them directly but quietly.","Listen to the drums: on several songs they enter only halfway through, changing the meaning of what came before. The vocal is placed very close and dry, as if the singer were standing her ground in the same room as the listener. Comfort, the last song, is sung to a former partner and turns the album's focus from her own hurt to care for someone else.","Reviews were excellent and the album appeared on many year-end lists, especially in Australia and Britain; critics praised its emotional precision. A few found the mood too uniform. It established Jacklin as one of the leading singer-songwriters of her generation, often grouped with Phoebe Bridgers and Big Thief.","Start with Body and Don't Know How to Keep Loving You, then hear Pressure to Party for contrast. Listen to the silences between lines. Compare Phoebe Bridgers's Stranger in the Alps and Big Thief's U.F.O.F. for the shared sound, and Joni Mitchell's Blue for the tradition of plain, unflinching confession."],"wynton-marsalis-black-codes-from-the-underground":["A quintet record built on the acoustic language of the mid-1960s, played with frightening precision. Wynton Marsalis's trumpet leads his brother Branford on tenor and soprano saxophone, with Kenny Kirkland on piano, Charnett Moffett on bass and Jeff \"Tain\" Watts on drums. The tunes, among them the title track, Phryzzinian Man and Chambers of Tain, lean on shifting meters and tempos that speed up and slow down by agreement, so the band seems to stretch and compress time while the soloists keep their footing.","In 1985 jazz on major labels meant fusion, smooth radio sounds or reissues of older acoustic records. Marsalis, a New Orleans prodigy who had come through Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers and was also recording Haydn and Hummel trumpet concertos for Columbia, argued loudly for swing, the blues and acoustic instruments as the core of the music. The title points to the laws passed across the South after the Civil War to restrict the freedom of Black Americans, a sign that he heard the music as history as well as style.","Listen to Watts and Kirkland more than to the horns. The rhythm section is quoting the elastic playing of the Miles Davis quintet of 1965 to 1968, where tempo and pulse are bargained over bar by bar, but Watts adds a heavier, more muscular backbeat of his own. On several tracks the time feel changes under a soloist without warning, and the soloist has to follow it while carrying on with the line.","The album won two Grammy awards in 1986 and made Marsalis the public face of a young-lions revival. Critics split over it then and still do: admirers heard real command and a corrective to commercial fusion, while detractors heard a gifted player re-enacting the Davis quintet rather than moving past it. Many now hear the record as the strongest work of that argument, and Kirkland and Watts as its unsung engine.","Start with the title track and keep your ear on the drums: count the moments the pulse seems to slip and then re-lock. Then play the second Davis quintet's mid-sixties records beside it and listen for what Branford's saxophone does differently from Wayne Shorter's. Any remaster of the original Columbia LP will do; the sound is clean, close and dry."],"wynton-marsalis-blood-on-the-fields":["An oratorio for big band and three singers, nearly three hours long and issued on three discs. It follows two Africans, Jesse and Leona, through capture, the Middle Passage and enslavement toward a hard-won freedom, with Cassandra Wilson as Leona, Miles Griffith as Jesse and Jon Hendricks as Juba, an older man who guides them. The writing moves through work songs, blues, marches, ballads, shout choruses and dense ensemble passages, each section built from a different strand of the Black musical past.","Marsalis wrote it for Jazz at Lincoln Center, the institution he had co-founded in 1987 to give jazz the repertory standing of an orchestra or opera house. It was first performed in 1994 and recorded the following year. In 1997 it became the first jazz composition to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music, a prize that had famously been refused to Duke Ellington in 1965. That history gave the award a weight well beyond this one piece.","Marsalis maps each stage of the story onto a historical idiom: the scenes on the ship and in the field lean on field hollers and call and response, while the later scenes borrow from swing-era ensemble writing and Ellington's sound for reeds. The journey of the characters is therefore also a walk through the history of the music itself, ending in the language of the big band Marsalis was leading.","The Pulitzer brought praise and resentment in equal measure. Supporters heard a serious extended work in the line of Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige; skeptics found it long, uneven and more summary than discovery, with a libretto some thought heavy-handed. It has been revived by the Lincoln Center orchestra but is rarely heard complete, and its reputation now rests as much on its symbolic place as on the score.","Do not try it in one sitting. Take one disc at a time, follow the story in the booklet, and listen for how Cassandra Wilson's dark, unhurried voice changes the temperature whenever she enters. Then play Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige to hear the model Marsalis was answering."],"cassandra-wilson-blue-light-til-dawn":["A quiet, acoustic record that sounds as if it were made on a porch at night. Cassandra Wilson sings low and slow over spare acoustic guitars, hand percussion, violin and the occasional cornet, with no piano and no drum kit swinging in the usual jazz sense. The songs come from everywhere: Robert Johnson's Hellhound on My Trail and Come On in My Kitchen, Joni Mitchell's Black Crow, Van Morrison's Tupelo Honey, Ann Peebles's I Can't Stand the Rain, and a handful of her own pieces.","Wilson had spent the 1980s in Brooklyn among the M-Base circle of Steve Coleman, recording angular, funk-driven records for the German label JMT. Signing to Blue Note in 1993, she worked with the producer Craig Street, who stripped the sound back to wood and strings. The guitarist Brandon Ross and the cornetist Olu Dara were among the players. The result reached listeners far beyond jazz and helped set a template for the roots-minded jazz singing of the next decade.","Listen to how slow everything is. Wilson often sings well behind the beat and lets phrases trail off into silence, so that the songs from the Delta, the 1970s singer-songwriter shelf and Memphis soul all end up sharing one tempo and one mood. The blues numbers are not treated as relics; the guitars play them in open, ringing tunings that point to Mississippi, where Wilson grew up.","Reviewers welcomed it at once, and it sold strongly for a jazz vocal record. Some jazz purists asked whether an album with so little swing and so much pop and blues repertoire belonged in the category at all. That debate now looks dated: the record is widely treated as one of the defining jazz vocal albums of its decade, and as the moment Wilson found her own sound.","Start with Hellhound on My Trail and then play the Robert Johnson original: notice how she keeps the menace but slows it to a crawl. Then try Black Crow beside Joni Mitchell's Hejira. Play the album late and quietly; it rewards a room with the lights down."],"cassandra-wilson-new-moon-daughter":["The second Wilson record with the producer Craig Street, darker and more varied than the first. It opens with Strange Fruit, sung almost without inflection over a bed of low drones, and moves through Son House's Death Letter, Hank Williams's I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry, Neil Young's Harvest Moon, U2's Love Is Blindness and even the Monkees' Last Train to Clarksville, alongside originals such as Solomon Sang. Pedal steel, acoustic guitar and cornet weave around her slow contralto.","After the surprise success of Blue Light 'til Dawn, Blue Note backed Wilson in carrying the approach further, and she treated rock and country songs as material as open to reinterpretation as anything by Gershwin. The guitarist Brandon Ross returned, with the cornetist Graham Haynes among the guests. The album won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Performance and confirmed her as the most widely heard jazz singer of the mid-1990s.","The Monkees song is the giveaway. Wilson slows a bright pop single into a brooding, almost menacing blues, showing how much of a song's meaning lives in tempo and arrangement rather than in the tune. Her Strange Fruit, by contrast, refuses the dramatic build most singers bring to it; the horror is carried by restraint, and by the sour drone that never resolves.","It was praised on release and won its Grammy, though some critics felt the formula of the first record was being repeated and that a few covers were more clever than deep. Over time its Strange Fruit and Death Letter have been singled out as among her finest recorded performances, and the album sits beside its predecessor as a pair.","Begin with Death Letter and then play the Son House recording: one man shouting over a slide guitar against a singer who barely raises her voice. Then hear Strange Fruit beside Billie Holiday's 1939 version and ask which unsettles you more. Listen on good speakers; the low end matters."],"joe-lovano-from-the-soul":["A quartet record on which an unusually broad, breathy tenor saxophone sound meets three very different partners: the French pianist Michel Petrucciani, the bassist Dave Holland and the New Orleans drummer Ed Blackwell. Lovano's tone is the first thing you notice, a big, furry, slightly husky sound that bends pitches and slides between notes, and his lines move freely between straight-ahead swing, open-ended improvising and tender ballad playing.","Lovano, from Cleveland, had spent years as a sideman with Woody Herman, Mel Lewis's big band and the trio of the drummer Paul Motian with Bill Frisell. Signed by Blue Note around 1990 in his late thirties, he became one of the label's flagship players of the decade. Blackwell, a long-time partner of Ornette Coleman, was gravely ill with kidney disease at the time, and this was among his last recordings before his death in 1992.","Listen to Blackwell. His drumming comes out of New Orleans parade rhythms, and he tunes his kit so that each drum sings a distinct pitch; you can follow his solos almost as melodies. Against him Petrucciani plays crisp, bright, classically trained piano, and Holland holds the middle. Lovano's tenor seems to borrow a little from each, which is why the record feels like a conversation rather than a saxophonist with accompaniment.","Critics welcomed it as proof that Lovano was a major voice rather than a well-schooled journeyman, and it helped launch a run of acclaimed Blue Note albums. It is sometimes overshadowed by his later, more ambitious projects, but players often name it as the cleanest portrait of his sound.","Start with any ballad and listen to the edges of Lovano's notes, the air and grain around them. Then play a track with Blackwell at the front and follow his drums as if they were a second horn. Compare with Ornette Coleman's quartet recordings to hear Blackwell in his original home."],"joe-lovano-rush-hour":["Lovano's tenor saxophone set inside chamber-orchestral writing by Gunther Schuller, who arranged and conducted. Some tracks are small and spare, others full ensemble pieces in which strings, woodwinds and brass weave independent lines around the saxophone rather than simply padding beneath it. Judi Silvano adds wordless vocal lines on some pieces. The repertoire mixes jazz-composer tunes with Schuller's own scores, and the overall mood moves between urban bustle and nocturnal calm.","Schuller had coined the phrase third stream in 1957 for music that joined jazz improvisation to classical composition, and by the 1990s he was a senior figure: composer, conductor, horn player, historian and teacher. Pairing him with Lovano was Blue Note's way of reviving that idea for a new generation. Lovano, the son of a Cleveland tenor player, had a lifelong interest in orchestral colour and welcomed the challenge of improvising inside fully written scores.","Listen to how often Schuller writes a counter-melody for a single instrument, a bassoon or a solo violin, that shadows Lovano's line a beat behind. The orchestra is not a cushion; it is a second improviser in disguise, and in places it is hard to tell which notes were written and which were made up on the spot.","Reviews were warm, and many critics named it among the year's best jazz records. Some felt the orchestral settings softened Lovano's edge; others thought it the most convincing third-stream album in decades precisely because neither side gives way. It is now heard as a high point of the 1990s attempts to reconcile jazz and concert music.","Choose a track with full ensemble and follow one orchestral instrument the whole way through rather than the saxophone. Then go back and hear how Lovano reacts to it. Compare Schuller's own third-stream work, or Miles Davis and Gil Evans's Miles Ahead, for an older version of the same marriage."],"dave-holland-extensions":["A quartet record without piano, in which Steve Coleman's alto saxophone and Kevin Eubanks's electric guitar trade lines over Holland's bass and the drums of Marvin \"Smitty\" Smith. The pieces run in long, lopsided cycles of beats and lock into grooves that feel like funk but refuse to settle into four. Holland's bass is the centre of gravity: a fat, woody sound that carries both the harmony and the hook.","Holland, born in Wolverhampton, England, had played in Miles Davis's band around 1968 to 1970 and in the free-improvising group Circle before making his name as a leader on ECM in the 1970s. By the late 1980s he was working with younger New York musicians from the circle around Coleman's M-Base collective, whose interest in odd meters and dense rhythmic cycles fed into his writing. Extensions was recorded at the end of that decade.","Count along to any tune and you will usually lose your place; Holland writes riffs that run in unusual lengths and lets the drums cut across them, so that the groove seems to tilt. Eubanks, later famous as a television bandleader, plays some of his most adventurous guitar here, using a clean, almost acoustic tone that blends with the alto rather than overpowering it.","It was widely praised on release and is often named among the best ECM jazz records of its period. It has sometimes been eclipsed by Holland's 1972 debut, Conference of the Birds, but many listeners prefer this record's tighter rhythmic language, which points ahead to his celebrated quintet of the late 1990s.","Put on the opening track and follow only the bass for a full minute, then switch your attention to the drums. Notice how the two seem to argue and still agree. Then play Holland's Prime Directive to hear where this approach led ten years later."],"dave-holland-prime-directive":["Holland's quintet at full strength: Chris Potter on saxophones, Robin Eubanks on trombone, Steve Nelson on vibraphone and marimba, Billy Kilson on drums, and Holland on bass. Instead of piano, Nelson's shimmering vibraphone fills in the harmony, which gives the group a lighter, ringing sound. The tunes run in odd meters, but the band plays them with such ease that they sound like dance music, building toward long collective climaxes in which saxophone and trombone trade riffs.","Holland formed the quintet in the late 1990s, and over the next several years it became one of the most admired working bands in jazz, winning polls and touring constantly. ECM, a label associated with spacious, reflective music, gave it room to record a series of albums. Potter, then in his late twenties, emerged from this group as one of the leading tenor saxophonists of his generation.","Listen to the backgrounds behind each soloist. Holland writes interlocking riffs for the other horns and the vibraphone that come in partway through a solo, so the music thickens as it goes rather than staying a solo with rhythm section. The meters are often in fives, sevens or longer cycles, but Kilson plays them with a backbeat you can nod to.","Critics greeted it with enthusiasm and the quintet was voted among the best groups of the period in jazz polls. Some found it polished to a fault, a model of ensemble craft rather than of risk. Musicians, however, still study these records as textbooks of how to write odd-meter tunes that groove.","Start with the title track and tap your foot; when you lose count, listen to the bass, which always shows you where the cycle begins. Then follow the vibraphone behind a saxophone solo. Afterwards compare Extensions from 1990 to hear the earlier, guitar-led version of Holland's rhythmic ideas."],"john-zorn-naked-city":["A record that cuts between surf twang, film-noir cool, country picking, lounge jazz and short bursts of hardcore noise, sometimes within seconds. Zorn on alto saxophone leads Bill Frisell on guitar, Wayne Horvitz on keyboards, Fred Frith on bass and Joey Baron on drums, with the Japanese vocalist Yamatsuka Eye screaming on some tracks. Alongside originals, the band plays film and television themes by Ennio Morricone, Henry Mancini and John Barry, and Ornette Coleman's Lonely Woman.","Zorn was the central figure of the New York downtown scene, a loose network of improvisers, rock musicians and composers gathered around Lower Manhattan clubs such as the Knitting Factory. He had been developing game pieces that directed improvisers with cue cards, and he loved cartoon music, Japanese hardcore and spaghetti westerns. The band took its name and its cover image from the crime photographer Weegee's book Naked City.","Listen to the edits. Many tracks change style at a single drum hit, and the band does it live, not by splicing tape; Zorn cues the cuts with hand signals in the manner of his game pieces. The short hardcore numbers, some under a minute, are the record's real experiment: noise played with the discipline of chamber music.","It confused many jazz critics, who heard parody and racket, and delighted others, who heard a new kind of composition built from the clutter of mass media. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now regarded as a landmark of the downtown aesthetic and of genre-collapsing music in general.","Start with the Morricone and Mancini covers to get your bearings, then try a run of the short noise pieces at volume. Hear the band's Lonely Woman beside Ornette Coleman's 1959 original and notice what survives the assault. Afterwards, play anything by the Boredoms to hear where Eye came from."],"john-zorn-masada-alef":["The first album by Masada, a piano-less acoustic quartet: Zorn on alto saxophone, Dave Douglas on trumpet, Greg Cohen on bass and Joey Baron on drums. The tunes are short, tightly written melodies in the scales of Jewish liturgical and klezmer music, with their characteristic augmented intervals, launched into fast, free-swinging improvisation. The sound is lean and urgent: two horns in unison or close harmony, then breaking apart as Baron drives the band forward.","In 1993 Zorn began writing a book of short tunes he called Masada, setting himself the task of creating a body of explicitly Jewish music in the 1990s, partly in response to what he and others in downtown New York called Radical Jewish Culture. The book eventually ran to hundreds of pieces. The quartet's first record was released by the Japanese label DIW, which was releasing many of Zorn's projects at the time.","The instrumentation is a deliberate tribute to Ornette Coleman's quartet of 1959, with Douglas in Don Cherry's role and Baron playing with Billy Higgins's lightness. Listen to how the two horns often end a tune on a long, unresolved interval rather than a cadence. The Jewish modes and the Ornette model fit together surprisingly well because both put melody before chord changes.","Masada was quickly recognised as one of Zorn's most accessible and durable projects, and the quartet recorded prolifically through the decade. Some listeners questioned the identity politics around it; others simply heard great tunes and one of the most exciting small bands of the 1990s. The songbook has since been played by dozens of other groups.","Start with any short tune and listen for the opening melody in unison; then follow how quickly the band unravels it. Play Ornette Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come next to hear the model, and Naftule Brandwein's klezmer clarinet to hear the other root."],"bill-frisell-have-a-little-faith":["A tour of American music played by a quintet of guitar, clarinet, accordion, bass and drums. Frisell takes on Aaron Copland's Billy the Kid, music by Charles Ives and John Philip Sousa, Stephen Foster, Muddy Waters, Sonny Rollins, Bob Dylan's Just Like a Woman, John Hiatt and Madonna's Live to Tell, treating all of it with the same gentle seriousness. His guitar, swelled and echoed by pedals, can sound like a pedal steel or a church organ.","Frisell, raised in Denver and trained on clarinet before guitar, had become a fixture of the New York downtown scene and of John Zorn's projects. With Nonesuch, a label that also issued contemporary classical music, he began to explore Americana in a broad sense. The band here included Don Byron on clarinet, Guy Klucevsek on accordion, Kermit Driscoll on bass and Joey Baron on drums.","Listen to how he treats Copland and Madonna with exactly the same affection, lingering on a melody's turns and letting the band hold chords longer than expected. The accordion and clarinet give the record an old-time, small-town sound, which makes Copland's cowboy ballet feel as if it were being played on a bandstand in a park.","Reviewers greeted it with delight and some puzzlement about its pop selections. It is now seen as the turning point at which Frisell moved from downtown experimentalist to the patient interpreter of American song he became, and as a forerunner of the broader jazz-Americana movement.","Play the Copland sequence first, then the Madonna, and notice how the band treats them as equals. Compare Copland's own orchestral Billy the Kid to hear what Frisell keeps and what he strips away, and listen for how the accordion takes over the strings' role. The original Nonesuch CD sounds warm and natural."],"bill-frisell-nashville":["Frisell's electric guitar among Nashville acoustic players, including the dobro master Jerry Douglas and the bassist Viktor Krauss. Drums are mostly absent, so the music floats on plucked strings: banjo rolls, mandolin chop and the slide of the dobro around Frisell's gentle, reverberant lines. The tunes, mostly his own with a few covers, are short, plain-spoken melodies that sound like old country songs heard in a dream.","After years of working in New York with improvisers, Frisell, who had moved to Seattle, went to Nashville to record with bluegrass and country session musicians who had no interest in jazz conventions. Nonesuch backed the project. It was part of a wider 1990s turn among American jazz musicians toward roots music, and Frisell treated it as a meeting of equals rather than a jazz player borrowing a flavour.","Listen for the moments when no one solos. The players trade small phrases and fills in the manner of a bluegrass jam, but Frisell slows the tempos so far that the fast picking style becomes a shimmering texture. His guitar often enters with the attack removed, the note swelling up from nothing, which makes him sound like a ghost among the acoustic players.","It was named album of the year in at least one major jazz critics' poll, which provoked debate about whether a record with almost no swing or jazz harmony belonged in the category. That argument has faded; it is now seen as a gentle classic and a key text of jazz Americana.","Put it on in the morning and do not wait for solos; listen to how the instruments fit together like a quilt. Then play Bill Monroe or the Stanley Brothers to hear the tradition Frisell is visiting, and Have a Little Faith to hear where the journey began."],"vijay-iyer-historicity":["A piano trio record that sounds less like a cocktail lounge than a machine room. Iyer, with Stephan Crump on bass and Marcus Gilmore on drums, plays tight, repeating figures that lock into grooves of unusual length and gradually shift. The repertoire mixes his own pieces with Stevie Wonder's Big Brother, M.I.A.'s Galang, Leonard Bernstein's Somewhere, Andrew Hill's Smoke Stack and Julius Hemphill's Dogon A.D., all reworked into the trio's dense rhythmic language.","Iyer, born in upstate New York to Indian Tamil parents, studied physics and wrote a doctoral thesis on music cognition and rhythm in the body before committing to music. He had worked with Steve Coleman and in the cooperative trio Fieldwork. This was the first album with this trio, issued by the German label ACT, and it earned a Grammy nomination and topped many year-end lists.","Listen to the hip-hop and pop covers closely: Iyer keeps their bass lines and rhythmic hooks rather than their melodies, turning a Stevie Wonder song or an M.I.A. track into a loop that the trio bends and stretches. Gilmore, grandson of the drummer Roy Haynes, plays the drums like a sampler, repeating tiny patterns with small changes each time.","The album was among the most praised jazz records of 2009 and established Iyer as a leading figure in a new generation. Some traditionalists found the trio too cerebral and short on lyrical melody; admirers heard a fresh approach to the oldest format in modern jazz, drawn from hip-hop and minimalism as much as from bebop.","Start with Galang or Big Brother, and listen to the bass and drums before the piano; notice how the loop slowly mutates. Then play Andrew Hill and Julius Hemphill to hear Iyer's jazz ancestors, and Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear his minimalist cousin."],"vijay-iyer-accelerando":["The second record by Iyer's trio with Stephan Crump and Marcus Gilmore, built around the idea of music for dancing. The covers include Michael Jackson's Human Nature, Flying Lotus's MmmHmm, Henry Threadgill's Little Pocket Size Demons and Herbie Nichols's Wild Flower, alongside originals whose grooves gradually speed up, overlap or slip against each other. The sound is lighter and more springy than on Historicity, with more space between the three players.","Iyer described the album in terms of dance and the body, drawing on his research into how people feel rhythm physically. By 2012 he was a widely honoured figure, and the record swept major jazz critics' polls that year. His choice of material, from a Los Angeles beat producer to an AACM elder, mapped the wide range of what jazz musicians of his generation listened to.","The title is a classical tempo marking meaning getting faster, and several pieces use metric modulation, a technique in which a subdivision of the old beat becomes the new beat, so the music seems to accelerate without anyone rushing. Threadgill's tune is especially knotty; listen to how the trio keeps its odd, lopsided phrase lengths intact.","Critics praised it as warmer and more open than its predecessor, and it was widely named among the best jazz records of 2012. Some still found Iyer's music more interesting in concept than in feeling, a criticism he has long faced. The record is now seen as the peak of this trio's work.","Start with Human Nature and notice how close the trio stays to Michael Jackson's gentle groove. Then play Little Pocket Size Demons and compare Threadgill's own recordings, listening for the lopsided phrase lengths. Finish with the Flying Lotus original to hear how a beat track becomes trio music."],"jason-moran-modernistic":["A solo piano album that ranges across a century of music. Moran plays James P. Johnson's stride showpiece You've Got to Be Modernistic, a song from Robert Schumann's Liederkreis, Afrika Bambaataa's electro anthem Planet Rock and his own pieces, including entries in his ongoing Gangsterism series. He moves between thundering stride left hand, delicate classical voicings and percussive, hip-hop-style repetition, sometimes in a single piece.","Moran, from Houston, studied with the pianist Jaki Byard and later with Andrew Hill and Muhal Richard Abrams, and signed to Blue Note in his early twenties. By 2002 he had recorded with his trio Bandwagon and with the saxophonist Greg Osby. Making a solo record so early in a career was a statement; Moran wanted to show his whole lineage, from Harlem stride to German Romantic song to the South Bronx.","Listen to his Planet Rock. Moran keeps the robotic pulse of the Roland drum machine in his left hand while his right hand plays shards of melody, so the piece sounds like a stride pianist imitating a sampler. In the Schumann, he lets the song's original harmony sit almost untouched before nudging it into blues inflections.","Critics praised it highly, and some named it the best jazz record of its year. A few found it more a catalogue of influences than a coherent statement. In hindsight it reads as an early, clear map of the eclectic, history-conscious approach that defined Moran's career and that of many pianists after him.","Hear James P. Johnson's original You've Got to Be Modernistic first, then Moran's version, and notice how much faster and harder his left hand hits. Then put the Schumann beside a recording of the Liederkreis with a singer. Finish with Planet Rock and imagine a dance floor."],"jason-moran-ten":["A record celebrating ten years of The Bandwagon, Moran's trio with Tarus Mateen on bass and Nasheet Waits on drums. It ranges from Thelonious Monk's Crepuscule with Nellie to Conlon Nancarrow's Study No. 6, originally for player piano, and Leonard Bernstein's Big Stuff, alongside Moran's own pieces. The trio can sound like a single body: they speed up, drop out and re-enter together, and Waits's drumming is as melodic as the piano.","Moran had formed The Bandwagon around 2000, and by 2010 it was one of the most admired working piano trios in jazz. That year he also received a MacArthur Fellowship, unusual for a jazz musician. His interests had widened to collaborations with visual artists and art galleries, and Ten shows that range in its choice of material from player-piano studies to Broadway.","The Nancarrow piece is the giveaway. Nancarrow punched his studies into player-piano rolls precisely because no human could play their layered tempos; Moran's trio plays a version by hand, letting the bass and drums take parts of the texture. In Big Stuff, a song Bernstein wrote for his ballet Fancy Free and Billie Holiday recorded, listen to how slowly the trio lets it unfold.","Many critics named it among the best jazz albums of 2010, and it was voted top album in at least one major poll. A few found its range of sources restless. It is now widely considered the definitive Bandwagon record, the place where the trio's long-developed group language is at its clearest.","Start with Crepuscule with Nellie, then Study No. 6, and compare Nancarrow's own player-piano version to hear what human hands change. Listen for the moments when the three players speed up or stop together without any obvious cue; that shared instinct is the record's real subject."],"mary-halvorson-away-with-you":["A record for octet in which Halvorson's guitar, with its warped, pitch-bending lines, sits beside the pedal steel guitar of Susan Alcorn. Jonathan Finlayson on trumpet, Jon Irabagon and Ingrid Laubrock on saxophones, Jacob Garchik on trombone, John Hébert on bass and Ches Smith on drums complete the band. The pieces are intricate, long-form compositions that move between brass-band fanfares, grinding dissonance and lyrical passages, with Alcorn's steel adding a weeping, floating colour that no jazz horn section usually has.","Halvorson studied with Anthony Braxton at Wesleyan and became one of the leading guitarists of the New York avant-garde in the 2000s. Her groups grew step by step from trio to quintet to septet; for this record she added the pedal steel, an instrument tied to country music and almost never heard in jazz. The album was issued by Firehouse 12, a New Haven label and venue that documented much of the new improvised music of the period.","Listen to how often Halvorson's guitar dips in pitch at the end of a phrase, an effect she makes with a delay pedal pushed past its normal setting. It is her signature, and Alcorn answers it with the pedal steel, which slides between notes by its nature. In places their lines merge so closely that you cannot tell which guitar is playing.","Critics praised it widely, and it was a fixture on year-end jazz lists. Some listeners find her writing knotty and dry, with too much plotted detail; others find it exhilarating precisely for that density. It is often cited as the moment her ensemble writing caught up with her reputation as a guitarist, and as proof that Braxton's teaching had found a distinctive new voice.","Start with the title track and follow the pedal steel all the way through; then play it again and follow the guitar. Notice how the horns often enter as a block, like a small brass band. Afterwards hear Anthony Braxton's Creative Orchestra Music 1976 to meet her teacher's way with a large ensemble."],"mary-halvorson-code-girl":["A double album of songs, the first for which Halvorson wrote her own words. Amirtha Kidambi sings, with Ambrose Akinmusire on trumpet, Michael Formanek on bass and Tomas Fujiwara on drums. The songs move between delicate art-song passages, jagged rock riffs and long instrumental stretches; Kidambi's voice is clear and exact, sometimes doubled by Halvorson's guitar note for note. The texts are oblique, closer to poetry than storytelling, and the whole has the slow-burning shape of a song cycle.","Halvorson had been known mainly as a guitarist and instrumental composer. Writing words was a new direction, prompted partly by her love of singer-songwriters and art rock. Kidambi, a singer with roots in Carnatic music and experimental improvisation, and Akinmusire, one of the most admired trumpeters of his generation, gave the project its unusual palette. Formanek and Fujiwara had played with her in other groups, so the band already shared a language.","Listen to Akinmusire's solos. He plays long, slowly unfolding lines in which notes crack, smear and whisper, very different from Halvorson's angular guitar, and the contrast gives the songs their emotional range. Listen also for how often voice and guitar share a melody exactly, so that the song seems to come from a single strange instrument.","It was praised as a bold new direction, though some reviewers found the lyrics opaque and the singing cool to the point of distance. It led to a second Code Girl album with a changed lineup and to much wider recognition, crowned by Halvorson's MacArthur Fellowship in 2019. Debate continues over whether the project is jazz at all, which is partly the point.","Pick a song where Akinmusire solos and listen once for the voice alone, then once for the guitar alone. Then go to Akinmusire's own When the Heart Emerges Glistening to hear him lead. For a kindred cross of art song and rock, try Kate Bush's Hounds of Love afterward."],"ambrose-akinmusire-when-the-heart-emerges-glistening":["Akinmusire's Blue Note debut, a quintet record with Walter Smith III on tenor saxophone, Gerald Clayton on piano, Harish Raghavan on bass and Justin Brown on drums. The trumpet sound is the point: breathy, cracked, sometimes barely voiced, then suddenly bright and piercing. Pieces unfold slowly, with long passages of quiet, inward group playing and few conventional head-solo-head shapes; one track, My Name Is Oscar, is a spoken elegy delivered over drums alone.","Akinmusire, from Oakland, California, won the Thelonious Monk International Jazz Competition in 2007 and signed to Blue Note soon after. Jason Moran produced the album. My Name Is Oscar refers to Oscar Grant, a young man shot dead by a transit police officer at an Oakland station on New Year's Day 2009, an event that marked the Bay Area deeply and which Akinmusire chose to address directly on a major-label debut.","Listen to the gaps. Akinmusire often lets a phrase fade almost to silence before the band moves on, and Brown's drums fill the space with small, crisp figures rather than steady time. The cracked notes are deliberate: he uses unstable sounds as expression, the way a singer uses a break in the voice, and he often holds back the trumpet's bright upper register until late in a piece.","It was widely praised as one of the strongest jazz debuts in years and established Akinmusire as a leading trumpeter of his generation. Some listeners found it austere and slow to reward. In hindsight many hear it as a turn in jazz trumpet away from the polished brilliance of the 1980s toward something more fragile and searching.","Start with My Name Is Oscar, then move to one of the long quintet pieces and follow the trumpet's tone rather than its notes. Then put on Wynton Marsalis's Black Codes to hear two generations' ideas of what a jazz trumpet should sound like, and decide which moves you more."],"ambrose-akinmusire-the-imagined-savior-is-far-easier-to-paint":["A long, ambitious record in which Akinmusire's quintet is joined by a string quartet and guest singers, among them Becca Stevens, Theo Bleckmann and Cold Specks. The music moves from chamber-like string passages to art-song ballads to extended group improvisations. Rollcall for Those Absent, in which a child recites names of people killed by police and in other violence, is one of the most direct statements on any jazz record of the decade, all the more powerful for its plainness.","By 2014 Akinmusire had toured widely with his quintet, which now included guitar, and he wanted a broader canvas. The strings and singers brought him close to contemporary classical music and to indie rock. The album appeared as public argument over police violence in the United States was intensifying, and Akinmusire, like several jazz musicians of his generation, placed that argument inside the music rather than in a liner note.","Listen to how the string quartet often plays sustained harmonies that shift very slowly under the trumpet, a texture closer to Morton Feldman than to the lush jazz-with-strings albums of the 1950s. And listen to Akinmusire's own solos for how often he chooses a whisper over a shout, letting the singers carry the climaxes.","It was praised for ambition and moral courage, and appeared on many year-end lists. Some critics found it overlong and uneven, with the guest songs sitting awkwardly beside the instrumental pieces. It is now often cited as a key jazz album of the years when the Black Lives Matter movement took shape, and as a forerunner of the protest-minded jazz records that followed.","Start with Rollcall for Those Absent, then a song with Becca Stevens, and notice how differently the trumpet behaves around a voice. Then play Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, released the following year with many jazz players aboard, to hear the same moment from the other side."],"esperanza-spalding-radio-music-society":["A record of songs meant for the radio, built on Spalding's bass and voice with horns, keyboards, drums and guests including Joe Lovano, Jack DeJohnette, Lalah Hathaway and Gretchen Parlato. It covers Wayne Shorter's Endangered Species and Michael Jackson's I Can't Help It, a Stevie Wonder song, alongside originals such as Black Gold. The sound mixes soul, funk, pop and jazz, with brass arrangements, a big-band feel on some tracks, and Spalding's agile, high, bright voice on top.","Spalding, from Portland, Oregon, had won the 2011 Grammy for Best New Artist, a surprise victory over pop stars that made her famous outside jazz. This album was conceived as a companion to Chamber Music Society, its more intimate predecessor, and aimed squarely at a broader audience. Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest co-produced some tracks. It went on to win the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album.","Listen to the bass lines. Spalding sings and plays at once, often with lines that move against the vocal melody in rhythm and direction, a feat very few musicians attempt. The Shorter cover keeps his twisting harmony but adds words, turning an instrumental piece from the jazz canon into a song with a message.","It was warmly received and won its Grammy, though some critics found it too polished and radio-ready, and others missed the chamber intimacy of its predecessor. Over time it has come to be heard as a careful, generous attempt to reconnect jazz with soul and R&B listeners, a project Robert Glasper was pursuing from the other direction the same year.","Start with Black Gold, then Endangered Species, and compare Wayne Shorter's original instrumental. Notice how often the bass line is as catchy as the vocal. Then play Chamber Music Society to hear the paired record, and Robert Glasper's Black Radio for a neighbour from the same year."],"esperanza-spalding-emilys-d-evolution":["A loud, theatrical, guitar-heavy record built around a character named Emily, Spalding's middle name and childhood alter ego. Matthew Stevens's electric guitar and Karriem Riggins's drums push the music toward progressive rock and funk, with Spalding's voice and electric bass at the front. The songs are knotty and quick-changing, layered with stacked backing vocals, and a cover of Pure Imagination from the film Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory sits among the originals.","Spalding co-produced the album with Tony Visconti, best known for his work with David Bowie, and presented it on a staged tour with choreography, costumes and spoken interludes. The move away from acoustic jazz was deliberate, part of her wish to escape the image of a polite jazz prodigy she had acquired after her Grammy win. The record was released by Concord, which had absorbed her earlier label.","Listen to the bass. Spalding plays electric bass with the agility of her upright playing but in rock-style riffs, often in uneven meters, and the guitar locks into those patterns in a way that recalls 1970s progressive rock more than jazz. The vocal harmonies are tracked in many layers, a studio technique foreign to most jazz records.","Some longtime fans were bewildered by the volume and the rock trappings; others were thrilled. Critics generally praised her daring while differing on the songs themselves. The record now looks like the hinge of her career, the point at which she claimed the freedom that produced the experimental 12 Little Spells and her later work with Wayne Shorter on the opera Iphigenia.","Start with a fast track at volume and follow the bass rather than the voice. Then play Joni Mitchell's Hejira, which Spalding has often named as a touchstone, and Jaco Pastorius's debut for a fretless bass ancestor. Finally go back to Radio Music Society to hear how far she moved in four years."],"cecile-mclorin-salvant-dreams-and-daggers":["A double album recorded mostly live at the Village Vanguard in New York with Aaron Diehl on piano, Paul Sikivie on bass and Lawrence Leathers on drums, framed by studio tracks with the Catalyst Quartet. Salvant sings songs from the Bessie Smith repertoire, Broadway and Tin Pan Alley, including Noël Coward's Mad About the Boy, alongside her own pieces. Her voice ranges from a deep, growling low register to a light, almost operatic top, and her phrasing is theatrical, witty and exact.","Salvant, born in Miami to a Haitian father and a French mother, studied classical voice and Baroque singing in Aix-en-Provence before turning to jazz, and won the Thelonious Monk competition in 2010. By the time of this record she had a long-standing partnership with Diehl's trio, honed on the road. The Vanguard, the most storied jazz room in New York, gave the album its intimate, close-up sound.","Listen to how she treats old songs about women. Salvant often chooses material whose attitudes now sound uncomfortable and sings it straight-faced but with a slight edge, so the listener hears the song's assumptions rather than simply enjoying them. The string quartet interludes are her own arrangements and frame the live set like chapters in a book.","It was widely praised and won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album, her second win in the category. A few listeners find her mannered, every phrase a performance; most hear the most gifted jazz singer of her generation, regularly compared to Sarah Vaughan and Betty Carter for range and to Abbey Lincoln for seriousness. The album is often named the fullest portrait of her live art.","Start with Mad About the Boy, then pick one of the Bessie Smith songs and play Bessie's original 1920s recording beside it. Listen for the moments when Salvant drops suddenly from her light top voice to her low growl, and for how Diehl's trio answers."],"cecile-mclorin-salvant-the-window":["A duo record with the pianist Sullivan Fortner, partly recorded live at the Village Vanguard. The songs range from Broadway and the Great American Songbook to Stevie Wonder and French chanson. With no bass or drums, the music is intimate and loose; Fortner follows Salvant closely, stretching and compressing time, adding sudden harmonies and stride flourishes, and the two improvise together as equals. The mood moves between hushed ballads, comic numbers and moments of real darkness.","The album followed Dreams and Daggers and won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album, Salvant's third. Fortner, from New Orleans, had become one of the most sought-after pianists of his generation, partly through his work with the trumpeter Roy Hargrove. The voice-and-piano duo, rare in recent jazz singing, recalls Ella Fitzgerald's and Sarah Vaughan's recordings with a single accompanist and the cabaret tradition.","Listen to the silences. Without a rhythm section, Salvant and Fortner can pause, slow down or rush at will, and those tempo changes carry much of the drama; a phrase may hang in the air for a long moment before the piano catches it. Fortner often answers her lines with tiny, unexpected harmonies that change the meaning of the words she has just sung.","Critics praised it as her most intimate record and among her best, and it took the Grammy. Some missed the swing of a full band. It is now often cited as a model of the voice-piano duo, and as evidence that Salvant's art depends as much on her choice of partner as on her voice.","Start with a slow ballad and listen once only to the piano, then once only to the voice. Then compare Ella Fitzgerald's duo work, or Bill Evans's Peace Piece for the patience of a pianist alone. Play it late, quietly, as if in a small club."],"makaya-mccraven-in-the-moment":["An album edited from live improvisations by McCraven's bands during a long weekly residency at a small Chicago venue. McCraven recorded the shows, then cut, looped and layered the best moments into short tracks, much as a hip-hop producer builds beats from samples. The result sounds like a beat tape made from live jazz: tight drum grooves, looping bass figures, fragments of horn and guitar solos, with McCraven's crisp drumming at the centre of nearly every track.","McCraven, born in Paris and raised in western Massachusetts, settled in Chicago and became central to its new jazz scene. The album was released by International Anthem, a small Chicago label that grew into a hub of that scene. The approach, which McCraven called organic beat music, drew on the production of J Dilla and Madlib as much as on jazz, and on cheap digital editing that let one musician shape hours of tape at home.","Listen for the edits. Many tracks repeat a short passage of live playing, with small variations, as if a sampler were looping it, and you can hear the seams if you listen for them. The crowd and room noise are often audible, a reminder that the source was a bar on an ordinary weeknight rather than a studio.","It was praised as a genuinely new way to make jazz records and became a cult favourite, launching McCraven's wider career. Some purists questioned whether editing improvisation honours it or betrays it, since the finished tracks never happened in that order. Teo Macero's edits for Miles Davis are the usual counter-argument, and the debate has since become part of the record's story.","Start anywhere and listen as you would to a beat tape, letting the loops settle rather than waiting for solos. Then play J Dilla's Donuts to hear the model, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way, whose tape edits did something similar forty-six years earlier."],"makaya-mccraven-universal-beings":["A double album recorded with different bands in four cities: New York, Chicago, London and Los Angeles, each given its own side and mood. Players include the harpist Brandee Younger, the saxophonists Shabaka Hutchings and Nubya Garcia, the tuba player Theon Cross, the cellist Tomeka Reid, the vibraphonist Joel Ross and the guitarist Jeff Parker. As before, McCraven recorded live sessions and edited the results, but here the playing itself comes further forward and the tracks breathe longer.","By 2018 several young jazz scenes were in conversation: London's players around Hutchings and Garcia, Chicago's International Anthem circle and the beat-influenced jazz of Los Angeles. McCraven connected them in a single project, travelling to record with local musicians in each city. The album was among the most praised jazz releases of its year and was followed by a documentary film of the same name.","Listen to how each city's side has its own character: the London tracks drive hard toward the dance floor, with tuba carrying the bass, while the Los Angeles side is spacious and hazy. The edits often loop a single moment of collective improvisation into a groove, so that a few seconds of spontaneity become the foundation of a whole track.","It was hailed as a landmark of the new international jazz moment and appeared at or near the top of many year-end lists. Some critics wanted longer, uninterrupted performances and felt the editing flattened the players' arcs. Most heard it as proof that the producer's chair could be a jazz musician's instrument.","Play the London side first, then the Los Angeles side, and compare how the same drummer sounds in each. Listen for the harp on the New York side. Then hear Kamasi Washington's The Epic, its west-coast cousin, and Flying Lotus's Cosmogramma for the beat-music side of the family."],"tyshawn-sorey-verisimilitude":["A trio record with Cory Smythe on piano and Chris Tordini on bass. The pieces are long, slow and spacious, unfolding over many minutes through quiet sustained sounds, silences and gradual swells. Sorey's drums are often played softly, with mallets, bowed cymbals and hand percussion, creating textures rather than keeping time. At moments the trio erupts into dense, loud playing, but the prevailing atmosphere is closer to contemporary classical chamber music than to a conventional jazz piano trio.","Sorey, from Newark, New Jersey, is a drummer, trombonist, pianist and composer who studied with Anthony Braxton at Wesleyan and later at Columbia. He works equally in jazz and in concert music, and had recorded with this trio before. Pi Recordings, a New York label devoted to adventurous jazz, released the album in the year Sorey received a MacArthur Fellowship, and it appeared on many critics' year-end lists.","Listen to how long each sound is allowed to last. Sorey writes and improvises with extreme patience, so that a single chord or cymbal roll may hold for close to a minute, and the structure becomes clear only over time. Ideas return much later in altered form, which is why the record rewards listening straight through.","Critics praised it as a major statement from a composer-performer coming into his own, though some found its slowness and quiet demanding to the point of frustration. It helped establish Sorey's reputation beyond jazz circles, paving the way for commissions from classical ensembles and, eventually, a Pulitzer Prize.","Listen in a quiet room without distraction, and resist skipping ahead when nothing seems to happen; the stillness is the material. Then play Morton Feldman's Triadic Memories or Rothko Chapel to hear a kindred patience from the concert world, and Anthony Braxton for Sorey's teacher."],"tyshawn-sorey-pillars":["A three-disc work close to four hours long for a large ensemble of improvisers, with Sorey on drums, trombone and other instruments. The music unfolds as slow sustained drones, quiet scraping textures, sudden eruptions and long stretches near silence, with several double basses giving it a dark low floor. There are no tunes and almost no steady beat. It feels more like an installation or a ritual than a jazz record, a single span to be lived inside rather than followed.","Sorey conceived the work as one long composition for musicians he trusted, mixing written material, cues and open improvisation. It was released by Firehouse 12, the New Haven label that also issued Mary Halvorson's records. It came the year after his MacArthur Fellowship, when he was receiving growing attention as a composer, and it showed how far he was willing to take duration as a musical material.","Listen to how the ensemble moves from near-silence to loud, dense passages and back over spans of many minutes, often so gradually that you notice the change only after it has happened. The bowed basses and sustained brass blur together into a single shifting sound, so individual players are hard to pick out.","Critics praised it as a major, uncompromising statement and it appeared on year-end lists; others found its length forbidding and wondered how many listeners would ever hear it whole. It is now discussed alongside the long late works of Morton Feldman as a test of what duration can do to attention.","Take one disc at a time, in a quiet room, ideally at night, and do not multitask. Notice how your sense of time shifts after twenty minutes. Afterwards, Feldman's String Quartet No. 2 makes a natural companion, and Coltrane's Ascension shows a far louder idea of collective sound."],"henry-threadgill-in-for-a-penny-in-for-a-pound":["A double album by Threadgill's group Zooid: Threadgill on alto saxophone and flutes, Liberty Ellman on guitar, Jose Davila on tuba and trombone, Christopher Hoffman on cello and Elliot Humberto Kavee on drums. The music is intricate, with lines weaving around one another like counterpoint while the drums keep a light, springy pulse. Guitar, cello and tuba make an unusual middle and low register, and Threadgill's sour, keening alto cuts through on top.","Threadgill, a Chicago-born veteran of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, had led Zooid since the early 2000s and was over seventy when this record appeared. It won the 2016 Pulitzer Prize for Music, placing him beside Wynton Marsalis and Ornette Coleman among the jazz musicians so honoured. Pi Recordings had become his regular home and issued the set as a two-disc release.","The players are not improvising over chords. Threadgill developed a system in which each musician is given a set of intervals to work within, so the lines move independently yet always fit together. That is why the music sounds both free and strangely ordered, like several conversations that keep agreeing without meeting.","Critics praised it highly, and the Pulitzer brought Threadgill late recognition well beyond the jazz world. Some listeners find the music cerebral and hard to grasp on first hearing; others treat it as the culmination of fifty years of searching. It has since become one of the most discussed jazz composition records of its decade.","Start anywhere and follow one instrument, the tuba, say, for a full piece, then go back and follow the cello. Notice how each line is complete in itself. Then hear Muhal Richard Abrams and the Art Ensemble of Chicago to meet the AACM world Threadgill came from."],"henry-threadgill-old-locks-and-irregular-verbs":["A four-part suite for Threadgill's Ensemble Double Up, in which he composes and directs but does not play. The band pairs two pianos, played by Jason Moran and David Virelles, with two alto saxophones, cello, tuba and drums. The paired instruments shadow, echo and contradict each other, creating a doubled, mirrored texture. The suite moves through spacious, almost mournful passages and dense, knotted ensemble writing, with the pianos often taking the lead in turn.","The suite is dedicated to Lawrence \"Butch\" Morris, the cornetist and conductor whose system of conducted improvisation, which he called conduction, influenced many New York musicians; Morris died in 2013. Threadgill had known him since the 1970s. The album appeared on Pi Recordings shortly after Threadgill's Pulitzer Prize was announced, which brought it more attention than his records usually received.","The doubling of pianos and altos is the key. Listen for moments when the two pianists play nearly the same thing slightly apart, producing a blurred echo, and others when they split into opposing lines. The tuba and cello beneath them often carry the true melody while the doubled instruments decorate it.","Critics welcomed it as a moving tribute and a strong companion to the Zooid records, often praising Moran's and Virelles's interplay. Some found it austere and short on Threadgill's own alto. It has come to be heard as one of his most personal recent works, a memorial written in his most complex language.","Hear it straight through, about forty minutes, and try to keep the two pianos apart in your ear; on headphones they are usually set left and right. Then play Jason Moran's Ten to hear one of the pianists in his own trio, and In for a Penny, In for a Pound for the Zooid sound."],"garbarek-witchi-tai-to":["Five long performances by the quartet Jan Garbarek co-led with the Swedish pianist Bobo Stenson, with Palle Danielsson on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. Almost nothing here is by the band: there is a Carla Bley tune, a Cuban song in praise of Che Guevara, Jim Pepper's chant-like title piece and, to close, Don Cherry's \"Desireless\", stretched to around twenty minutes. Garbarek's soprano is keening and bright, the rhythm section loose and breathing, the mood ecstatic rather than cool.","Recorded in Oslo in late 1973 for Manfred Eicher's young Munich label ECM, this was the point where Garbarek moved past his early fiery, Coltrane-and-Ayler-inspired records toward a more open, song-led manner. The quartet was a pan-Scandinavian band of the kind ECM was quietly building, and the choice of material shows how widely these players listened: American free jazz, Latin American protest song and a Native American saxophonist's hit tune all sit together.","Jim Pepper, who wrote the title piece, was a Native American saxophonist who built it on a chant he had learned from his grandfather; Garbarek turns that repeated, almost ceremonial phrase into a slow-burning hymn. Listen to how Christensen keeps time mostly on cymbals and toms, never quite settling into swing, so the music floats. \"Desireless\" builds from near-silence into a long, hypnotic ostinato that anticipates much later Nordic jazz.","The album was well received on release and has grown steadily in reputation, often named as the best of Garbarek's early ECM records and a founding document of the Nordic sound. Some admirers of his later, sparer work find it surprisingly hot-blooded; others think that warmth is exactly what his later records lost. Its reissue in ECM's Touchstones series introduced it to a new generation of listeners.","Start with the title track and wait for the moment the melody returns after the solos, now carried with full force. Then give \"Desireless\" its full length on headphones and notice how little changes and how much it grows. Compare with Keith Jarrett's Belonging, which uses almost the same band, and with Don Cherry's own records."],"garbarek-dis":["A duo record for Jan Garbarek's saxophones and wooden flute and Ralph Towner's twelve-string and classical guitars, joined on some tracks by a wind harp, an instrument played only by the air moving across its strings. The pieces are slow, modal and spacious: long held saxophone notes over ringing guitar patterns, or over the harp's eerie, unpredictable drone. There is almost no swing and almost no harmony moving in the usual jazz way. It sounds like weather and distance more than a club.","By the mid-1970s ECM had become a home for a new kind of chamber jazz, and Manfred Eicher liked to pair musicians who had not played together as a regular unit. Towner, an American from the group Oregon, brought folk and classical guitar technique; Garbarek was turning more and more toward Norwegian folk song. The title is an old Norwegian word for mist or haze, and the cover photograph of grey sea set the visual style many listeners still associate with the label.","The wind harp was recorded outdoors on the Norwegian coast, so on those tracks the composer of the drone is literally the wind: the overtones that bloom and fade under Garbarek's line were not played by anyone. Listen for the way he shapes his notes to match them rather than the other way round. Towner's twelve-string patterns are often in odd groupings that make the pulse feel slightly unsteady.","Dis became one of Garbarek's best-known albums and, for many, the record that fixed the cliché of the ECM sound as fjords and fog. Jazz traditionalists asked whether it was jazz at all, a question that would follow Garbarek for decades. Others heard in it a serious attempt to make improvised music from northern landscape and folk melody, and it has long been admired by ambient and new-age musicians.","Listen in a quiet room, not as background. Start with the first track and follow the single saxophone line as if it were a singer. Notice how Towner never fills space, then wait for the wind harp and listen to its tones drifting in and out. Afterwards try Brian Eno's ambient records, then Garbarek's later Officium."],"garbarek-officium":["Jan Garbarek improvises on soprano and tenor saxophone around the Hilliard Ensemble, four male voices singing Gregorian chant and polyphony from the medieval and Renaissance church. The voices sing the old music as written; the saxophone weaves above and between them, sometimes doubling a line, sometimes floating free, sometimes answering a phrase like a fifth singer. The acoustic is huge and resonant. The whole album moves at the pace of a slow procession, with silence ringing after every cadence.","The project was Manfred Eicher's idea, made for ECM's New Series, which he had founded to record composers such as Arvo Pärt alongside the label's jazz. The sessions took place in 1993 in the Austrian monastery of St Gerold, chosen for its echo. The early 1990s were the years of chant on the pop charts and of Górecki's Third Symphony as a bestseller, and Officium arrived in the same climate of hunger for slow, sacred-sounding music.","Most of the repertoire is real early music: Cristóbal de Morales's \"Parce mihi Domine\", from his Office of the Dead, returns more than once, and Pérotin's \"Beata viscera\", a song from Notre-Dame around 1200, also appears. Listen for how Garbarek usually enters after the voices have established the mode, then stays inside it. Where the saxophone is silent, the singers are performing straight; the improvisation is added, never written into the parts.","Officium became one of the biggest sellers in ECM's history and charted in several countries in both classical and pop listings. Early-music purists objected that the saxophone turned liturgy into mood music; some jazz critics found the improvising too polite. Yet many listeners came to chant and Renaissance polyphony through it, and the team made two sequels, Mnemosyne and Officium Novum. Its long success has made it a touchstone in arguments about crossover.","Start with \"Parce mihi Domine\" and listen first only to the voices, then play it again and follow the saxophone. Notice the long reverberation and how the players let each phrase die away before moving. Then hear Pérotin's music sung plainly by a choir, and Arvo Pärt's Summa, to judge what the saxophone adds and what it changes."],"stanko-balladyna":["A quartet without piano: Tomasz Stańko's trumpet and Tomasz Szukalski's saxophones over the American bassist Dave Holland and the Finnish drummer Edward Vesala. The tunes are dark, slow-moving melodies that open into free, smeared, often wailing improvisation, then return. Stańko's tone is the recognisable thing: breathy, cracked at the edges, bending notes downward as if sighing. The absence of a chord instrument gives the music a raw, open feel, closer to Ornette Coleman's quartet than to cool jazz.","Stańko was the leading trumpeter of the Polish avant-garde and had played in Krzysztof Komeda's groups in the 1960s. In communist Poland jazz had been tolerated and sometimes celebrated, but recording in the West remained a privilege. This 1975 Oslo session, made for ECM, was his first for the label and his first wide exposure outside Eastern Europe. The title comes from a nineteenth-century Polish verse drama by Juliusz Słowacki about ambition and murder.","Listen to how often Holland plays a melody against the horns rather than a walking bass line; with no piano he becomes a second voice. Vesala's drumming is full of rattles and washes, closer to free jazz than to swing, but he keeps an undertow of pulse. Stańko frequently plays two or three notes and then lets them decay, using silence as part of the phrase in a way that would become his signature.","Balladyna was respected but not widely noticed at first. Its standing rose after Stańko's return to ECM in the 1990s made him a major international name, when critics went back and heard in it the seeds of his late style. Some prefer its harder, freer edges to the lyrical later records. It remains a key document of how Eastern European jazz musicians absorbed American free jazz and then made something darker of it.","Start with the title track and listen to Stańko's very first phrases: notice how he bends and frays the notes. Then follow Holland's bass as an equal voice. Compare with Ornette Coleman's piano-less quartet of 1959, and then jump forward to Stańko's Soul of Things to hear how the same tone sounds in a gentler setting."],"stanko-litania":["Tomasz Stańko leads a septet through the music of Krzysztof Komeda, the Polish pianist and film composer who had been his mentor. The band is Scandinavian and Swedish-heavy: Bobo Stenson on piano, Palle Danielsson on bass, Jon Christensen on drums, the tenor saxophonists Bernt Rosengren and Joakim Milder, and Terje Rypdal's guitar on some tracks. The music is slow, melancholy and lyrical, with brooding melodies, hushed ensemble passages and Stańko's breathy, grieving trumpet always at the centre.","Komeda had died in 1969, aged thirty-seven, after an accident in Los Angeles, at the height of a career that included the scores for Roman Polański's films, among them Knife in the Water and Rosemary's Baby. Stańko had played in Komeda's quintet as a young man, and Rosengren too had played with him. Recorded in Oslo in 1997, the album was both a tribute and a statement that Komeda's music belonged in the international jazz canon.","Listen for the lullaby from Rosemary's Baby, \"Sleep Safe and Warm\", which appears in more than one version: what was a sinister nursery tune in the film becomes, slowed down, a tender ballad. Komeda's themes often move in modal patterns that recall Polish folk music, and Stenson's piano voicings hint at Chopin. Rosengren's presence links the recording to Komeda's own 1960s band.","Litania was greeted as one of the finest ECM records of the 1990s and helped spark a revival of interest in Komeda, whose own records had been hard to find outside Poland. Critics praised Stańko for honouring the composer without nostalgia. Some felt the ECM production smoothed away the urgency of the originals. It is now regarded as a central work of Polish jazz and of Stańko's late career.","Start with \"Sleep Safe and Warm\", then watch the opening of Rosemary's Baby to hear the original. Notice how slowly the band lets the melody unfold, and how the two tenors blend almost into one voice. Then seek out Komeda's own album Astigmatic, with the young Stańko on trumpet, to hear where this all began."],"stanko-soul-of-things":["Thirteen pieces, all called simply \"Soul of Things\" and numbered I to XIII, played by Tomasz Stańko with a trio of young Polish musicians: Marcin Wasilewski on piano, Sławomir Kurkiewicz on bass and Michał Miśkiewicz on drums. The music is mostly slow and lyrical, with ballads and gently swinging pieces in which the trumpet sings in long, smoky phrases. The trio plays with real delicacy and fluidity, and the whole record has the shape of one long, continuous meditation.","Stańko, by now in his late fifties, chose to work with a trio of musicians in their twenties who had grown up admiring him. He had heard them as a group from Koszalin and adopted them as his band. Recorded in Oslo in 2001 for ECM, this was their first album together; they went on to make Suspended Night and Lontano with him before becoming famous in their own right as the Marcin Wasilewski Trio.","The thirteen pieces are separate compositions, but several share melodic cells and moods, so the numbering is more than a gimmick: it invites you to hear one suite. Listen to how Wasilewski often lays out entirely during Stańko's solos, leaving bass and drums alone with the trumpet, then re-enters softly. Miśkiewicz plays mostly with brushes and cymbals, keeping a pulse that never insists.","Soul of Things was widely praised as one of Stańko's finest records and among the most beautiful jazz albums of its decade. Critics noted a new serenity in his playing after years of rawer, freer work. A few missed the edge of his earlier music. The album also launched the young trio's international career, and their later records on ECM have kept its reputation alive.","Play it from the beginning and let it run; the album works best whole. Notice how Stańko rarely plays fast, and how much feeling he gets into single sustained notes. Then compare with Miles Davis's ballad playing on Kind of Blue, and with the Bill Evans trio's interplay, to hear the tradition this record carries forward."],"kenny-wheeler-gnu-high":["Three long pieces, \"Heyoke\", \"Smatter\" and \"Gnu Suite\", for Kenny Wheeler's flugelhorn with Keith Jarrett on piano, Dave Holland on bass and Jack DeJohnette on drums. Wheeler writes melodies that leap in wide intervals and then fall back with a wistful, almost Romantic ache; his tone is warm and dark, his solos full of sudden flights into the high register. The rhythm section is a dream team, and Jarrett, unusually, plays strictly as a sideman, supporting another composer's music.","Wheeler, born in Canada, had lived in London since the early 1950s and was a central figure in the British free-improvisation and big-band scenes, yet he was barely known in America. This 1975 New York session for ECM was his first album for the label and gave him an international profile. It was also one of the very few times Jarrett, by then a major star, recorded as a sideman after the early 1970s.","\"Heyoke\" lasts more than twenty minutes and is built in sections that return like a rondo; listen for the opening melody coming back in altered form near the end. Wheeler's characteristic leaps of an octave or more are hard to play on the flugelhorn, and he lands them almost casually. Listen also to how Jarrett holds back his trademark long solos and comps with real restraint.","Gnu High was welcomed as a fine record at the time and has since become a cult favourite, often named as one of the best ECM albums of the 1970s and as Wheeler's finest small-group statement. Musicians prize Wheeler's compositions, which are now studied in jazz schools. The record is also cherished for showing a side of Jarrett rarely heard, as a sympathetic accompanist.","Start with \"Heyoke\" and simply follow Wheeler's melody, noticing its leaps and sighs. Then listen again to Jarrett behind him. Compare with Jarrett's own Facing You to hear the pianist in charge, and with Wheeler's Angel Song to hear his writing in a very different, drumless setting."],"kenny-wheeler-angel-song":["A quartet with no drums and no piano: Kenny Wheeler on flugelhorn, Lee Konitz on alto saxophone, Bill Frisell on guitar and Dave Holland on bass. The music is quiet chamber jazz, almost all slow or medium tempo, with Wheeler's melodies passed between horn and saxophone while Frisell's guitar adds soft clouds of harmony and Holland holds everything together. Without a drummer the pulse is implied rather than stated, and the record has an intimate, conversational, searching feel throughout.","Recorded in New York in 1996 and released by ECM the following year, Angel Song brought together four musicians from different generations and traditions: Konitz from the cool jazz of the late 1940s, Holland from Miles Davis's electric bands, Frisell from the downtown New York scene and Americana. Wheeler, by now in his late sixties, wrote all the music, and the group's line-up had never played together as a band before the session.","Konitz is a famously unpredictable improviser who rarely plays a phrase the same way twice, and his lines often run against Wheeler's long arcs in a quiet counterpoint. Listen to how Frisell uses volume swells so that his chords seem to appear from nowhere, with no attack. The absence of drums means every player must keep time inside their own phrasing, and you can hear them listening intently.","Critics praised it as one of the most beautiful records of the decade and a model of chamber jazz; the British press in particular embraced it as a crowning statement of Wheeler's writing. Some found it too subdued. It is now widely considered among his finest albums and is often recommended to listeners who come to jazz from classical chamber music.","Listen at low volume, ideally late at night. Start with the title track and follow the interplay between flugelhorn and alto: which one leads, and when? Then notice Frisell's guitar entering underneath. Compare with Lee Konitz's early records, and with Gnu High to hear Wheeler's tunes with a full rhythm section."],"surman-the-amazing-adventures-of-simon-simon":["A duo between John Surman, playing soprano and baritone saxophones, bass clarinet and synthesisers, and Jack DeJohnette on drums, congas and keyboards. The album is laid out as a loose story in short episodes, each with an evocative title. Surman often builds a repeating synthesiser pattern first, then plays long, folk-like melodies over it on baritone or soprano, while DeJohnette moves between crisp jazz drumming and rolling, tribal percussion. The sound is bright, rhythmic and unusually lyrical.","Surman, from Devon, had been a leading figure in British jazz since the late 1960s, famous for making the baritone saxophone fly. During the 1970s he became fascinated by synthesisers and overdubbing and began working regularly with ECM. DeJohnette had played with Miles Davis and Keith Jarrett and led his own ECM groups. The two had played together before, and this 1981 Oslo session was a chance to combine Surman's electronic experiments with a great drummer.","Many of the synthesiser patterns are simple repeating figures, close to the early minimalism of Terry Riley and Steve Reich, and Surman plays against them in English modal melodies that sound like folk song or church music. Listen to how DeJohnette shifts the pulse under a pattern so that the same loop suddenly feels like a different metre. The story behind the titles is never spelled out: it is left to your imagination.","The album was warmly received and has remained one of Surman's most popular records; it was reissued in ECM's Touchstones series. Some purists disliked the synthesisers, which date the sound to the early 1980s. Others hear it as a forerunner of later fusions of jazz, minimalism and electronics. It is often cited as the best introduction to Surman's work.","Start at the beginning and listen for the moment the baritone first enters over the synthesiser loop: it is deep yet light on its feet. Follow DeJohnette as he changes the feel beneath a repeating pattern. Then try Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Brian Eno's Another Green World for kindred ideas."],"surman-road-to-saint-ives":["A solo album on which John Surman plays everything himself, layering soprano and baritone saxophones, bass clarinet, keyboards and percussion through overdubbing. The tracks are named after places in Cornwall, from Bodmin Moor to Marazion, and each is a short sound-picture: a drone or keyboard pattern sets the scene, then a melody arrives on reed or saxophone, sometimes doubled, sometimes answered by another of Surman's instruments. The music is pastoral, misty and melodic, with an English folk accent.","Surman had been making overdubbed solo records since the early 1970s, and he returned to the format for ECM at the end of the 1980s. He was born in Tavistock, close to the Cornish border, and the album is a portrait of the landscape of his youth. By this time he was living largely in Norway, and the record has the distance of a memory: Cornwall imagined from far away.","Because Surman plays every part, listen for how the instruments are voiced like a small church choir, with the bass clarinet often taking the bass line and soprano saxophones singing the top. Several melodies use old English modes, the scales of folk song and Tudor church music, and some phrases sound like hymn tunes. The keyboard patterns repeat with tiny changes, a technique closer to minimalism than to jazz.","The record was praised for its atmosphere and has a devoted following, especially in Britain. Some critics thought it closer to ambient music than to jazz, with too little improvisation in the obvious sense. Others value it precisely as a portrait in sound, comparing it to English pastoral composers such as Vaughan Williams. It remains one of the most personal records in Surman's large catalogue.","Read the track titles as a map and imagine the road west. Start with a single piece and listen for the layering: how many Surmans can you count? Notice the folk-like turns in the melodies. Then try Shirley Collins's English folk singing, and Steve Reich's Electric Counterpoint, another work built from one player multiplied."],"eberhard-weber-the-colours-of-chloe":["Four long pieces led by Eberhard Weber's bass, with Rainer Brüninghaus on piano and synthesiser, Ack van Rooyen on flugelhorn, Peter Giger on drums and a section of orchestral cellos. The music moves in slow, shimmering layers: repeated piano figures, a singing bass melody, swelling strings and soft percussion. It sounds closer to film music or impressionist chamber music than to bebop, and Weber's bass tone, high, sustained and glowing, is unlike any jazz bass heard before.","Weber, from Stuttgart, had played in the German free-jazz and rock scenes and with the pianist Wolfgang Dauner. In the early 1970s he designed a modified electric upright bass with a slim body and an extra string, which gave him a sustained, cello-like sound. This, his first album as leader, recorded near Stuttgart in late 1973, was one of the early ECM records that defined the label's polished, spacious production. The cellists came from the local radio symphony orchestra.","Listen to how often the piano and synthesiser play short repeating patterns, very much like the minimalism Steve Reich was then developing in America; Weber was one of the first jazz musicians to build pieces this way. The cellos sometimes play long chords that sound like a second, deeper bass. Notice too how Weber uses his bass to play melodies in a high register usually reserved for horns.","The album was a surprise success, establishing Weber as an important new voice. Some critics heard it as soft, more like soundtrack than jazz. Over time it has been recognised as a model of European jazz composition, and many bassists cite Weber's tone as an inspiration. It also helped define ECM's identity in its first years.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the bass melody emerges above the shimmering piano. Notice the cellos entering beneath. Then compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, with Pat Metheny's Bright Size Life, and with Weber's next album Yellow Fields, which puts the same ideas into a tighter quartet."],"eberhard-weber-yellow-fields":["Four pieces by a quartet: Eberhard Weber on his custom electric bass, Charlie Mariano on soprano saxophone and the South Indian nagaswaram and shehnai, Rainer Brüninghaus on piano and synthesiser, and Jon Christensen on drums. The music is driving and bright, with repeating keyboard patterns, long rising melodies and a pulse that builds steadily. Mariano's Indian reeds give some passages a piercing, nasal colour, and Weber's bass alternates between deep grooves and high, singing lines.","After the success of The Colours of Chloë, Weber formed a working band, later called Colours, to play his music live. Mariano, an American saxophonist who had played with Charles Mingus, had studied Indian music and brought instruments from that tradition. Recorded in 1975 and released in 1976 by ECM, the album came out at a time when jazz-rock fusion was at its height, but Weber's music took a cooler, more architectural direction.","Listen to the title track for the way the keyboards repeat a pattern while the harmony underneath shifts slowly, so the same figure changes colour; it is a technique taken from minimalism. Christensen plays with a light touch, often around the beat rather than on it. Mariano's nagaswaram, a long double-reed instrument from South Indian temple music, has a sound you will not hear on many other jazz records.","Yellow Fields was well received and is often considered the best record of Weber's Colours band. Some critics thought the music too polished, but many have praised its compositional strength and the unique blend of jazz, Indian and minimalist influences. It remains a favourite among fans of ECM's 1970s catalogue.","Start with the title track and follow how the energy builds over its length. Listen for Mariano's switch to the Indian reed, and notice the difference in colour. Then compare with Shakti's fusion of jazz and Indian music, and with Weather Report's Heavy Weather for a contemporary American take on electric jazz."],"stenson-war-orphans":["A piano trio record by Bobo Stenson with Anders Jormin on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. The material ranges widely: Ornette Coleman's title piece, a song by the Cuban singer-songwriter Silvio Rodríguez, Jormin's own compositions and free, open pieces. The trio plays with great patience, letting melodies unfold slowly, then dissolving them into conversational improvisation where piano, bass and drums are equal voices. The sound is clear and resonant, the mood often melancholy and searching.","Stenson had been a central figure in Scandinavian jazz since the late 1960s, playing with Jan Garbarek, Charles Lloyd and Tomasz Stańko. His trio with Jormin and Christensen began in the late 1980s and became one of the most respected in Europe. This was their second ECM album, following Reflections, and it came after a long period in which Stenson was better known as a sideman than as a leader.","Ornette Coleman's \"War Orphans\" is one of his darkest pieces, a lament; listen to how the trio slows it down further and leaves silences in place of the expected harmony. Christensen rarely plays a steady beat, instead using cymbal colours and short bursts; the pulse lives in Jormin's bass. The Silvio Rodríguez song connects the record to Latin American protest music, a recurring interest in Nordic jazz going back to the Garbarek–Stenson quartet's Witchi-Tai-To.","War Orphans was widely praised and helped establish Stenson as one of the great European piano trio leaders. Critics compared the trio's freedom and interplay to that of Bill Evans and Paul Bley. Some found the free sections hard going. The trio's later records, Serenity and Goodbye, have built on its reputation.","Start with the title track and listen to the space between notes. Follow the bass as the anchor while the drums float. Then hear Ornette Coleman's own records to compare, and Keith Jarrett's Belonging to hear Stenson's Nordic colleagues in a quartet setting from twenty years before."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-from-gagarins-point-of-view":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio, Svensson on piano, Dan Berglund on double bass and Magnus Öström on drums, plays original tunes that sound as much like pop songs as jazz: simple, singable melodies, clear verse-and-chorus shapes and grooves that borrow from rock. The title track opens with a hushed, floating theme that slowly builds; elsewhere there are bluesy, driving pieces and tender ballads. The piano trio format is traditional, but the sound is clean, modern and emotional.","The trio had formed in Stockholm in the early 1990s and already had several albums out in Sweden. This was the record that first reached audiences across Europe through the German label ACT, which had begun to promote a new, rock-influenced European jazz. The title nods to the Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin, the first man in space, and the record's mood of wonder and distance matches it.","Listen to how often Berglund doubles the piano melody on bass, a pop arranging technique rarely used in jazz trios. Öström's drumming often follows rock patterns, with a backbeat, but he plays them with jazz brushes and touch. Svensson sometimes plays the same simple phrase over and over, building intensity through repetition rather than through fast runs, a sign of his love of pop and minimalism.","The album marked the start of the trio's international breakthrough. Critics praised its freshness and accessibility, and younger listeners who had never bought jazz took to it. Some purists complained that it was too close to pop. Over time it has been seen as the beginning of a European piano-trio movement that includes bands like GoGo Penguin.","Start with the title track and notice how simply the melody is stated, then how much the trio builds from it. Listen to the bass doubling the piano. Then compare with Bill Evans's trio to hear the tradition, and with Radiohead's OK Computer for the rock mood the trio admired."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-seven-days-of-falling":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio at full strength: piano, double bass and drums, now coloured by electronic effects, distorted bowed bass and studio processing. Some tracks, like \"Ballad for the Unborn\", are slow, aching songs; others, such as the title track and \"Mingle in the Mincing-Machine\", build to rock-like climaxes with crashing drums and repeated piano riffs. The album moves between delicacy and noise, and its emotional arc is clear even to listeners who know little about jazz.","By 2003 e.s.t. had become one of the most popular jazz groups in Europe, playing rock venues and festivals as well as jazz clubs. Released by ACT, Seven Days of Falling was a major commercial success, especially in Sweden and Germany. The early 2000s were the era of Radiohead's Kid A, and many young jazz musicians were drawn to electronics, atmosphere and song form; e.s.t. led the way among European piano trios.","Listen to Berglund's bowed bass on some tracks: he runs it through distortion and effects pedals so that it sounds like an electric guitar. Svensson sometimes plays inside the piano, damping the strings. The trio recorded largely live in the studio, then added layers and processing afterwards, so the album is a blend of real-time playing and studio construction, more like a rock record than a traditional jazz date.","Seven Days of Falling was a critical and commercial triumph and is often named as e.s.t.'s finest album. It drew a young audience to jazz and filled concert halls across Europe. Traditionalists criticised the use of effects and the pop-like structures; many younger critics saw it as the future of the piano trio. It is now regarded as a classic of 2000s European jazz.","Start with \"Ballad for the Unborn\" for the melody, then play the title track loud. Listen for the distorted bass and how the trio builds a climax. Then compare with Radiohead's Kid A, and with GoGo Penguin's v2.0, which carries this approach further into electronica."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-leucocyte":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio's last studio album, and its most experimental. Alongside a few melodic, song-like pieces are long passages of free improvisation, electronic noise, processed piano and distorted bass. The four-part title suite at the end stretches into near-ambient soundscapes, with drones and textures that barely resemble a piano trio. The mood is darker and more restless than on earlier albums, as if the band were deliberately breaking apart its own style.","The music was recorded in Sydney in January 2007 during a tour of Australia, in a few days of largely improvised sessions. Svensson died in June 2008 in a diving accident near Stockholm, at the age of forty-four, and Leucocyte was released a few months later. The album therefore became a farewell, though the band had intended it as a new beginning. Further music from the same sessions was released as the album 301 in 2012.","Listen to how much of the album was improvised in the studio rather than composed in advance: the trio would start with a simple idea or sound and let it grow. Some passages use the piano as a source for electronic processing, so that the notes are smeared and stretched. The title, the name of a white blood cell, reflects the band's interest in organic, cell-like growth in music.","Leucocyte's release so soon after Svensson's death made calm judgment difficult. Many critics praised it as a bold step forward; some found the electronic passages self-indulgent. With time it has come to be heard as evidence of where e.s.t. might have gone, and it is often discussed alongside 301. Svensson's death left a gap in European jazz that many musicians have spoken about.","Start with one of the melodic pieces, then listen to the title suite in full, preferably on headphones. Notice how the piano sound is changed by electronics. Then compare with Seven Days of Falling to hear how far the band had travelled, and with Aphex Twin's ambient work for the textures they were drawn to."],"molvaer-khmer":["Nils Petter Molvær's trumpet floats over electronic beats, samples, ambient textures and guitars. Some tracks are slow and spacious, with long, echoing trumpet notes over drones; others lock into drum-and-bass and trip-hop rhythms, with layered guitar noise and pulsing programmed drums. Molvær's tone is breathy and melancholy, often processed with effects. The album sounds like a cross between Miles Davis's electric period, the ECM aesthetic and the club music of the 1990s.","Molvær had played in the Norwegian group Masqualero and as a sideman for ECM before this, his debut as a leader. It was a surprising release for the label, which was known for acoustic chamber jazz. The late 1990s saw drum-and-bass, trip-hop and electronic music dominate European club culture, and a new generation of Norwegian musicians, sometimes called the Nu jazz scene, set out to combine improvisation with electronics.","Listen to how many sounds come from samples and loops rather than live instruments; Molvær treats the studio as a band member. The guitar of Eivind Aarset adds textures that sound more like synthesisers than a guitar. Notice too that the trumpet often plays very little, a few notes repeated or sustained, leaving room for the electronic backdrop. The title refers to the Khmer people of Cambodia, though the music makes no direct reference to their tradition.","Khmer was a major success and is considered one of the founding albums of Nu jazz. It was widely praised for its fresh approach and sold far beyond the usual audience for an ECM jazz record. Some jazz purists objected to the electronics and simple grooves. It influenced a wave of Nordic musicians who combined jazz and electronica, and it is often named as one of the most important European jazz albums of the 1990s.","Start with the title track and listen to the trumpet floating over the electronic pulse. Then try a faster, beat-driven track. Notice the guitar textures. Then compare with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew and In a Silent Way, the electric records it grows from, and with Massive Attack's Mezzanine for its trip-hop cousins."],"molvaer-solid-ether":["Nils Petter Molvær's second album for ECM pushes further into electronic music. The beats are harder and faster, often drawing on drum-and-bass, while the trumpet weaves above in long, cool, echoing lines. Distorted guitar, samples and turntables add layers of noise and texture. The Norwegian singer Sidsel Endresen appears on some tracks, adding a wordless, strange vocal colour. The album moves between dense, driving pieces and quieter, ambient passages.","After Khmer's success, Molvær toured widely with his band and played jazz festivals and club venues alike. The turn of the millennium was a fertile time for Norwegian music, with labels and musicians exploring the boundaries between jazz, electronica and ambient. Solid Ether built on the first album's ideas, with a band that had grown together on stage, and was again released by ECM, which by now accepted that its catalogue could include electronics.","Listen to how the drum programming and live drumming are mixed, so that you often cannot tell which is which. Molvær processes his trumpet heavily on some tracks, adding delay and filtering until it sounds almost like a synthesiser. The vocal pieces use Endresen's improvised, wordless singing, an approach she developed in her own work. The title is a paradox: something that should be weightless made solid.","Solid Ether was well received, though some critics felt it repeated Khmer's formula. Others praised its harder edge and stronger beats. It confirmed Molvær as the leading figure of Nordic Nu jazz and was popular in clubs as well as jazz venues. Today it is often seen as the second panel of a pair with Khmer.","Start with one of the fast tracks and listen for the trumpet riding over the drum-and-bass rhythm. Then try a slower, ambient piece. Notice how the electronics and live instruments blend. Compare with Roni Size's New Forms and Squarepusher's Hard Normal Daddy for the electronic side, and with Khmer for the starting point."],"rava-the-pilgrim-and-the-stars":["Enrico Rava's trumpet leads a quartet with John Abercrombie on guitar, Palle Danielsson on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. The pieces are Rava's own: lyrical, often melancholy melodies that open into free, spacious improvisation. Rava's tone is warm and singing, with a hint of Italian opera in his phrasing, and Abercrombie's guitar adds a cloud of chords and fast, fluid lines. The music moves between gentle ballads and passages of loose, open energy.","Rava, born in Trieste and raised in Turin, had played with Steve Lacy and Gato Barbieri and spent years in New York and Buenos Aires before this, his first record for ECM. It was recorded in 1975 in Germany. ECM was then building a catalogue of European and American musicians playing a new, open style of jazz, and Rava brought an Italian lyricism to it that would become his trademark.","Listen to how Rava often plays short, simple melodic fragments, then repeats them with small variations, a technique that recalls Miles Davis but has a different, more operatic warmth. The Nordic rhythm section is the same pair, Danielsson and Christensen, who played with Keith Jarrett's European Quartet, giving the record a family resemblance to Belonging. Abercrombie's guitar often fills the role of a piano, but with a lighter, more transparent sound.","The album was well received and established Rava as a European voice to watch. It has remained a favourite among fans of 1970s ECM, though it has been somewhat overshadowed by his later, more famous records. Critics have praised its blend of Italian lyricism and Nordic space.","Start with the title track and listen to Rava's tone, warm and singing. Notice Abercrombie's guitar behind him. Then compare with Miles Davis's Milestones to hear Rava's model, and with Stańko's Balladyna to hear another European trumpeter on ECM in the same year."],"petrucciani-pianism":["Michel Petrucciani leads a piano trio with Palle Danielsson on bass and Eliot Zigmund on drums. The album mixes his own compositions with standards, among them Cole Porter's \"Night and Day\". Petrucciani's playing is bright, powerful and lyrical, with crystal-clear runs, strong left-hand chords and a sense of joy in every phrase. The trio swings with ease, and the ballads have a tender, singing quality that recalls Bill Evans, the pianist he most admired.","Petrucciani, born in France with a rare bone disease that limited his height to about one metre, moved to the United States in the early 1980s, where he joined Charles Lloyd's band. In 1985 he signed with the newly revived Blue Note Records, often described as its first European artist, and Pianism was his first album for the label. He was then in his early twenties and already considered one of the most gifted pianists of his generation.","Listen to his left hand: despite his small frame, Petrucciani produced a full, powerful sound and played wide chords, using a mechanical extension device to reach the pedals. Danielsson, a Swede, had played with Keith Jarrett's European Quartet, so the trio has a hint of the ECM sound. Notice how Petrucciani often restates a melody after his solo with fresh harmonies, a habit learned from Bill Evans.","Pianism was warmly received and helped establish Petrucciani as an international star. Critics praised his technique and lyricism, though some felt he leaned too heavily on Evans. Over time his originality has been more widely recognised, and the album remains a favourite among his Blue Note records.","Start with \"Night and Day\" and listen for how Petrucciani reshapes the familiar melody. Then try one of his own ballads. Notice the strength of his left hand. Then compare with Bill Evans's Sunday at the Village Vanguard to hear his model, and with Keith Jarrett's Standards, Vol. 1."],"petrucciani-power-of-three":["A live trio of piano, guitar and saxophone with no bass or drums: Michel Petrucciani with the guitarist Jim Hall and, on some pieces, the saxophonist Wayne Shorter. The music is intimate and conversational, with standards like \"In a Sentimental Mood\" alongside originals by the players, including Shorter's \"Limbo\". Petrucciani supplies bass lines and rhythm with his left hand, Hall adds soft, glowing chords and melodic lines, and Shorter's saxophone floats above, enigmatic and lyrical.","The concert took place at the Montreux Jazz Festival in July 1986, and the album was released by Blue Note the following year. Hall was a veteran guitarist who had played with Sonny Rollins and Bill Evans; Shorter was a founder of Weather Report and a former member of Miles Davis's quintet. Petrucciani, then in his mid-twenties, was the youngest player on stage, and the concert showed how highly his elders regarded him.","Without a bassist, listen to how Petrucciani and Hall share the role: sometimes the piano plays the bass line, sometimes the guitar. Shorter rarely played acoustic jazz in this period, as Weather Report was electric, so his presence here is a rare treat. Notice how the three musicians leave space for each other, with almost no moments when all three play at full volume.","Power of Three was praised for its intimacy and the rare meeting of these three musicians. Some critics found the lack of a rhythm section limiting; others thought it gave the music unusual clarity. It remains a favourite among fans of Petrucciani and Shorter and is often recommended as an example of chamber jazz in a live setting.","Start with \"In a Sentimental Mood\" and listen to how the three instruments share the melody. Then try \"Limbo\" for Shorter's writing. Notice the absence of drums and how the pulse is kept. Then compare with Jim Hall's playing on Sonny Rollins's The Bridge, and with Weather Report's Heavy Weather to hear Shorter electric."],"grappelli-jalousie":["Stéphane Grappelli and the classical violinist Yehudi Menuhin play popular songs of the 1930s, by composers such as George Gershwin, Cole Porter and Jerome Kern, along with Jacob Gade's tango \"Jalousie\". Grappelli improvises freely with his light, swinging tone; Menuhin, less at home in jazz, mostly plays written parts with his rich classical sound. The contrast between the two violinists is the point: one dances, the other sings, and both enjoy the meeting.","Menuhin, one of the most famous classical violinists of the century, had first played with Grappelli on British television and was charmed by his improvising. Their collaboration led to a series of records for EMI through the 1970s and early 1980s, and this was the first. Grappelli, then in his mid-sixties, was enjoying a late revival of his career in Britain, where he had spent the war years, and these records brought him a wider audience.","Listen to how the two violinists divide the work: Menuhin often takes the melody straight while Grappelli weaves around it, and when Menuhin attempts something like an improvised line, you can hear how different his phrasing is. The arrangements were written for Menuhin, who could not improvise, so his parts are carefully notated jazz phrases. Grappelli's violin has a lighter, faster vibrato, an immediate clue to which is which.","The album was a popular success and introduced Grappelli to many classical listeners. Jazz critics were sometimes dismissive of Menuhin's stiff swing, while classical critics found the music lightweight. Yet the warmth of the meeting has kept these records popular, and they are often cited as early examples of classical–jazz crossover.","Start with \"Jalousie\" and listen for the two different violin tones. Notice how Grappelli plays around the beat while Menuhin stays on it. Then compare with Grappelli and Django Reinhardt's swing version of Bach's double concerto, and with the original Bach for two violins."],"grappelli-young-django":["Stéphane Grappelli leads a string quartet of jazz: his violin, the guitars of Philip Catherine and Larry Coryell, and the double bass of Niels-Henning Ørsted Pedersen. The material includes tunes associated with Django Reinhardt and new pieces in his spirit. The two guitarists play with fire and speed, sometimes trading phrases, while Grappelli's violin glides on top with elegant, effortless swing. The sound is the Quintette du Hot Club updated for the 1970s.","Grappelli had co-founded the Quintette du Hot Club de France with Django Reinhardt in Paris in 1934, and after Reinhardt's death in 1953 he carried their music for decades. By the late 1970s he was touring the world in his seventies. This album, made for the German label MPS, paired him with younger guitarists who had grown up with jazz-rock: Coryell from America and Catherine, a Belgian, both deeply admirers of Reinhardt.","Listen to how Catherine and Coryell divide the role Reinhardt once played alone: one keeps the rhythm, the other solos, then they swap, and sometimes both solo at once. Ørsted Pedersen, a Danish bassist, was a virtuoso who could play melodic lines as fast as a guitarist. Notice how Grappelli's phrasing, after more than forty years, still carries the same light swing as on his records with Reinhardt.","The album was well received and is often cited as one of the best of Grappelli's late career. Critics praised the energy of the guitarists and Grappelli's undimmed playing. Some found the more modern guitar styles out of place, but most heard it as a lively tribute that kept Reinhardt's music alive.","Start with one of the up-tempo tunes and listen to the guitars trading phrases. Then notice Grappelli's violin. Compare with the original Quintette du Hot Club records, Minor Swing and Djangology, to hear how the music has changed and stayed the same."],"joachim-kuhn-colors-live-from-leipzig":["A duo concert by Joachim Kühn on piano and Ornette Coleman on alto saxophone. The music is free and energetic, with Coleman's original tunes as a starting point for wide-ranging improvisation. Kühn's piano moves between dense clusters, flowing runs and passages of classical clarity, while Coleman plays his distinctive bluesy, crying alto lines. The two players listen intently, building and releasing tension together, and the concert has the feel of a long, intense conversation.","Coleman had rarely worked with pianists since the late 1950s, when he abandoned the instrument to free his music from fixed harmony. Kühn, born in Leipzig in 1944, had left communist East Germany in the 1960s and become a leading figure in European free jazz. The concert took place in Leipzig in 1996, Kühn's home city, a few years after German reunification, and was released by Coleman's own Harmolodic label through Verve.","Listen to how Kühn avoids playing standard chords under Coleman: he responds with lines and clusters instead, matching Coleman's harmolodic theory, in which melody, harmony and rhythm are all equal. Kühn had studied classical piano in Leipzig, and some of his runs recall Bach. Notice too the symbolism of the concert itself: an exile returning home with one of the founders of free jazz.","The album was praised as a rare and successful meeting of Coleman with a pianist, and it is often considered one of Kühn's finest recordings. Some critics felt the music lacked the earthiness of Coleman's own bands. Others found it a fascinating glimpse of how Coleman's music could work with a European, classically trained partner.","Start at the beginning and follow how the two players react to each other. Notice Kühn's clusters and Coleman's crying alto. Then compare with Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come and his Dancing in Your Head, and with Cecil Taylor's Unit Structures for another approach to free piano."],"shabaka-hutchings-wisdom-of-elders":["Shabaka Hutchings's tenor saxophone with a band of South African musicians, billed as Shabaka and the Ancestors. The music is spiritual jazz: long, chant-like melodies over rolling, polyrhythmic drums and percussion, with the voice of Siyabonga Mthembu adding sung prayers and invocations. The tracks build slowly, the saxophone and alto interweaving like two singers, and the whole album has the feeling of a ceremony, both joyful and solemn.","Hutchings, born in London and raised partly in Barbados, had been visiting South Africa and playing with musicians in Johannesburg's jazz scene. The album was recorded there in a single day in 2015 and released by Gilles Peterson's Brownswood label. It came at the start of a boom in London jazz, but it looked beyond London, connecting British jazz with South Africa's long tradition, from Abdullah Ibrahim to the exiled Blue Notes.","Listen to how the melodies often move in short, repeating cycles, similar to South African church and protest music. Tumi Mogorosi's drumming draws on both jazz and traditional South African rhythms. Notice too that the vocals are not lyrics in the pop sense but chants and invocations, giving the album a ritual feel. The title points to the idea of learning from ancestors, a theme in both African spiritual tradition and jazz.","The album was widely praised and helped make Hutchings one of the most talked-about figures in jazz. Critics compared it to the spiritual jazz of Pharoah Sanders and Alice Coltrane and to South African jazz of the 1960s and 70s. It was seen as a bridge between the London scene and the African diaspora, and its success led to a follow-up, We Are Sent Here by History.","Start at the beginning and let the album build. Listen for the voice and the saxophone weaving together. Notice the drums. Then compare with Pharoah Sanders's Karma and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda, and with Abdullah Ibrahim's Mannenberg for the South African roots."],"shabaka-hutchings-perceive-its-beauty-acknowledge-its-grace":["Shabaka Hutchings's first album under his own name, and a striking change of direction: he plays mostly flutes, including the Japanese bamboo shakuhachi, instead of saxophone. The music is quiet, meditative and spacious, with soft harps, piano, gentle percussion and occasional voices. Guests include the harpist Brandee Younger, the bassist and singer Esperanza Spalding, the electronic producer Floating Points and André 3000 on flute. The mood is calm and contemplative, closer to ambient music than to his earlier, high-energy bands.","Released by Impulse! in 2024, the album followed Hutchings's announcement that he would stop playing saxophone in public at the end of 2023, after more than a decade as the leading saxophonist of the London jazz scene. He had been studying flutes for some years, drawn to their breath and stillness. The album was recorded partly in Los Angeles with musicians from the city's spiritual and ambient scene, including the percussionist and producer Carlos Niño.","Listen to the breath in the flute playing: Hutchings lets you hear air and noise, part of the shakuhachi's tradition, where the breath is as important as the note. The album's slow pace and spare textures are a deliberate contrast to his work with Sons of Kemet and The Comet Is Coming. Notice too how André 3000's appearance links the record to his own flute album, New Blue Sun.","The album was widely praised as a brave and beautiful reinvention, though some fans missed his saxophone and the energy of his earlier bands. Critics placed it in a lineage of spiritual jazz and ambient music, from Alice Coltrane to Harold Budd. It confirmed that Hutchings's search was as much spiritual as musical.","Listen in a quiet space, from beginning to end. Notice the breath in the flute and the spaces between notes. Then compare with Alice Coltrane's Universal Consciousness and Floating Points's Promises with Pharoah Sanders, and with Sons of Kemet's Your Queen Is a Reptile to hear how far he has travelled."],"sons-of-kemet-burn":["Sons of Kemet's first album, with an unusual line-up: Shabaka Hutchings on tenor saxophone and clarinet, Oren Marshall on tuba, and two drummers, Tom Skinner and Seb Rochford. There is no piano or guitar. The tuba plays bass lines and riffs, the two drummers create dense, interlocking rhythms drawn from Caribbean, African and New Orleans traditions, and the saxophone or clarinet soars above. The music is loud, danceable and full of energy, closer to a carnival band than to a jazz club.","Hutchings formed Sons of Kemet in London in 2011, drawing on his Caribbean roots, his experience in London's club and jazz scenes, and his interest in African music. Kemet is an ancient name for Egypt. Released by Naim in 2013, the album was an early landmark in what became a London jazz revival, a scene of young musicians mixing jazz with grime, Afrobeat, calypso and dub. The band won a MOBO award for best jazz act that year.","Listen to how the two drummers play complementary parts rather than doubling each other: one may play a straight pulse while the other plays across it. The tuba's role recalls New Orleans brass bands, where the sousaphone plays bass. Hutchings's clarinet playing reflects his classical training. Notice too the Caribbean rhythms underlying many tracks, from calypso and other Barbadian traditions he grew up with.","Burn was praised for its energy and originality and helped put the new London jazz scene on the map. Critics compared it to Fela Kuti, to New Orleans brass bands and to the Art Ensemble of Chicago. Some found the music repetitive, but many saw it as a bold new direction for British jazz.","Play it loud, and listen for the two drummers. Notice the tuba playing bass lines and riffs. Then compare with Fela Kuti's Zombie for the Afrobeat energy, with the Art Ensemble of Chicago's Fanfare for the Warriors, and with Sons of Kemet's later Your Queen Is a Reptile."],"sons-of-kemet-your-queen-is-a-reptile":["Sons of Kemet's third album: Shabaka Hutchings on tenor saxophone, Theon Cross on tuba, and two drummers, Tom Skinner and Eddie Hick. Each track is named after a woman of African heritage the band honours, among them Harriet Tubman, Angela Davis and Doreen Lawrence, offered as alternatives to the British monarchy. The music is rhythmic, urgent and danceable, with tuba riffs, interlocking drums and soaring saxophone. The poet Joshua Idehen adds spoken words on some tracks.","Released by Impulse!, the historic American jazz label, the album came at the height of the London jazz revival and of debates about race, Britishness and empire after the Brexit vote and the Windrush scandal. Hutchings framed the album as a challenge to the monarchy and a celebration of Black women. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2018, bringing the band to a wide audience.","Listen to the track titles as a list of heroes: each was chosen for a reason, from the Jamaican Maroon leader Nanny of the Maroons to the South African activist Albertina Sisulu. The rhythms draw on Caribbean, African and British club music, with grime and dub echoes. Notice how the tuba often plays the role of a bass guitar in a dancehall band. Hutchings has said the title refers to the unelected power of the monarchy.","The album was widely praised as one of the best jazz records of the decade and was a Mercury Prize nominee. Critics hailed its political force and musical energy. Some found the political framing heavy-handed; others thought it was exactly the point. It confirmed Sons of Kemet as one of the most important bands in British jazz.","Start with the first track and listen for the tuba and drums locking together. Read the track titles and look up the women named. Then compare with Fela Kuti's Coffin for Head of State for another political jazz-adjacent record, and with Little Simz's GREY Area for London's hip-hop of the same moment."],"nubya-garcia-source":["Nubya Garcia's debut full-length album, led by her tenor saxophone with Joe Armon-Jones on keyboards, Daniel Casimir on bass and Sam Jones on drums. The music mixes jazz with dub, cumbia and Caribbean rhythms. The title track is long and dub-influenced, with echoing bass and drums; elsewhere there are ballads and driving, spiritual pieces. Garcia's tenor tone is warm and full, her phrases long and flowing. The Colombian group La Perla appears on one track, adding cumbia rhythms and voices.","Garcia, born in London to a Guyanese mother and a Trinidadian father, grew up in the city's youth jazz programmes, including Tomorrow's Warriors, which nurtured many of the London scene's stars. She had played with Maisha, Nérija and Ezra Collective, and released an EP before this, her debut album, released by Concord Jazz in 2020. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and its release during the pandemic meant it reached audiences mostly through streaming.","Listen to the title track's dub influences: the echoing drums and deep bass recall Jamaican sound systems, which were central to Garcia's London upbringing. The cumbia track with La Perla reflects her interest in Latin American music and connections across the Caribbean. Notice too how Garcia uses space in her solos, letting phrases breathe before building to climaxes.","Source was widely praised and shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, confirming Garcia as one of the leading voices of the London jazz scene. Critics praised her tone and her ability to blend jazz with Caribbean and Latin influences. Some found the album long, but most heard it as a confident and personal debut.","Start with the title track and listen for the dub echoes. Then try the cumbia track with La Perla. Notice Garcia's tone. Then compare with Sonny Rollins's Saxophone Colossus for the tenor tradition, and with Sons of Kemet's Your Queen Is a Reptile and Massive Attack's Blue Lines for London and Bristol's sound-system culture."],"nubya-garcia-odyssey":["Nubya Garcia's second album pairs her tenor saxophone and band with orchestral strings she arranged herself. The music ranges from lush, cinematic ballads to driving grooves rooted in jazz, Caribbean rhythms and R&B. Guest vocalists include the bassist and singer Esperanza Spalding on the title track. Garcia's tenor sound is full and warm as ever, and the strings add depth and drama. The album moves like a journey, from contemplative opening to energetic peaks and reflective close.","Released by Concord Jazz in 2024, four years after Source, Odyssey reflected Garcia's growth as a composer and arranger. She had spent the intervening years touring internationally and widening her ambitions as a writer. The album's string writing places her in a lineage of jazz musicians who have embraced orchestral colour, from Charlie Parker with Strings to Alice Coltrane, and it confirmed the London scene's ambitions beyond the club.","Listen to how the strings are integrated rather than simply added: they often play counter-melodies against Garcia's saxophone, or take over the groove themselves. The arrangements are Garcia's own, and they show a composer thinking in orchestral colours. Notice the Caribbean rhythms underlying some tracks, a continuation of Source's themes. Spalding's vocal on the title track blends with the saxophone in a way that recalls her own chamber-jazz albums.","Odyssey was praised for its ambition and Garcia's growth as a composer and arranger. Critics welcomed the strings and the album's cinematic sweep, though some missed the rawer energy of Source. It was widely seen as a major statement from one of the leading jazz musicians of her generation.","Start with the title track and listen for the strings and Spalding's voice. Notice how the saxophone and strings interact. Then compare with Alice Coltrane's Universal Consciousness for jazz with strings, with Esperanza Spalding's Chamber Music Society, and with Garcia's own Source to hear how her sound has widened."],"jimmy-smith-the-sermon":["The whole first side is one blues, the title track, lasting about twenty minutes at a slow, lazy strut. Jimmy Smith lays down a walking bass line with his feet and left hand while a parade of soloists take long turns: Kenny Burrell on guitar, Tina Brooks on tenor, Lee Morgan on trumpet, Lou Donaldson on alto, and Smith himself, who builds from murmured chords to a full-drawbar roar. Art Blakey keeps a shuffle underneath. The second side holds two shorter, faster tracks with smaller groups.","Smith had arrived in New York only in 1956 and was recording for Blue Note at an astonishing pace, sometimes cutting enough material in one session for several albums. These tracks come from sessions in 1957 and 1958 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Hackensack, and the label released them as albums when it saw fit. Blue Note's founder Alfred Lion encouraged long jam-session takes; the long-playing record had only recently made a twenty-minute blues commercially possible.","The title is not decoration. The tempo is that of a slow church march, and the soloists behave like successive preachers taking the pulpit, each beginning quietly and working towards a shout. Listen to how Smith comps behind the horns with short, stabbing organ chords like a congregation answering. The organ's Leslie speaker, a rotating horn inside a wooden cabinet, is audible as a slow wobble on sustained chords; Smith speeds it up at climaxes.","The album was received as a solid blowing session rather than an event, and some critics found the length self-indulgent. Its reputation grew steadily, partly because its lineup now reads like a roll call of Blue Note's golden age, partly because it codified the slow organ blues as a form. It is now often named as the best entry point to Smith's huge Blue Note catalogue, and to the organ-jazz tradition generally.","Give the title track your full twenty minutes, without skipping. Notice that nothing changes harmonically; the drama is all in tone, rhythm and volume. Follow each soloist's arc, then listen for the moment Smith pulls out all the stops. Afterwards play Horace Silver's The Preacher to hear the same churchgoing impulse in a quintet."],"jimmy-smith-back-at-the-chicken-shack":["A quartet record of medium-tempo grooves: Jimmy Smith on organ, Stanley Turrentine on tenor saxophone, Kenny Burrell on guitar and Donald Bailey on drums. The title track is a loping blues with a riff anyone could hum, stated by organ and tenor together. Elsewhere there is a ballad, a standard and a slow-burning minor blues. With no bass player, Smith supplies the bass on the pedals, and the sound is compact, warm and conversational rather than fiery.","The album was recorded in 1960 but held back by Blue Note until 1963, by which time Smith had left the label for Verve and big-band records. The music therefore belongs to the moment when organ combos were the soundtrack of Black neighbourhood bars across Newark, Philadelphia and Harlem, where a Hammond was often a fixture of the room. Turrentine, newly married to the organist Shirley Scott, was the ideal partner for this kind of bar-room soul.","The title names a kind of place, the roadside chicken joint with an organ in the corner, and the music is tailored to it: tempos that encourage dancing and drinking rather than concentration. Listen for how Smith and Turrentine trade short phrases near the end of the title track, finishing each other's sentences. Burrell rarely solos at length; his chunky rhythm chords hold the groove where a bass player would normally sit.","Released after Smith's departure, it might have been a contractual leftover, but it became one of his most loved records and one of Blue Note's steadiest sellers. Critics now generally rank it alongside The Sermon! as his best work for the label. Turrentine's playing here is often cited as the definitive soul jazz tenor sound, and the album is a common recommendation for listeners new to the organ combo.","Start with the title track and hum the riff after one chorus; that is the point. Then notice the absence of a bass player and listen to Smith's feet doing the work. Compare Turrentine here with his much more lavish CTI album Sugar a decade later to hear how the same voice was dressed up for the 1970s."],"jimmy-smith-the-cat":["Hammond organ against a blaring big band, arranged and conducted by Lalo Schifrin. The title track is a prowling minor-key strut where trumpets and trombones stab out riffs and Smith's organ answers with snarling runs. Elsewhere the album draws on film music, including the theme from the French thriller Joy House, which Schifrin had scored. Tracks are short, tightly arranged and loud, more like soundtrack cues than jam sessions. The organ is always in front, the brass a wall behind.","After moving to Verve, Smith was produced by Creed Taylor, who would later found CTI, and paired with orchestral arrangers to reach a wider market. Schifrin, an Argentine pianist who had worked with Dizzy Gillespie, was beginning a Hollywood career and would soon write the Mission: Impossible theme. The album was a commercial success, and the cinematic, spy-movie atmosphere matched the James Bond boom of 1964.","Listen to how Schifrin voices the brass: rather than swinging section riffs in the Count Basie manner, he uses tight, dissonant chords and sudden silences, the language of film suspense. Smith in turn plays like a lead horn, bending notes with the organ's percussion setting and swells. The album is a direct ancestor of Schifrin's later television and film themes, and a sketch for Taylor's CTI production style.","Jazz critics were cool on Smith's Verve big-band albums, finding them commercial and overarranged compared with his Blue Note sessions. Listeners disagreed, and The Cat sold strongly. Opinion has since softened considerably; the record is admired as a piece of orchestration, and its title track became a standard for organists and a favourite with DJs who prize its punch and brevity.","Play the title track loud, and listen for the call and response between organ and brass. Then put on Schifrin's Mission: Impossible theme and hear the same composer's taste for tension. For contrast, return to The Sermon! and notice how much freedom Smith gave up, and what he gained, by working inside an arrangement."],"jimmy-smith-root-down":["A live set from a Los Angeles club in which Jimmy Smith fronts a young funk rhythm section with electric bass, guitar, drums and congas. The grooves are long and hard: the title track rides a churning bass riff for minutes at a time while Smith digs in with clipped, percussive organ phrases. Covers of soul hits of the day, including Al Green's Let's Stay Together, sit beside blues jams. The sound is raw and close, with audience noise and a room you can feel.","By 1972 the organ-trio circuit that had made Smith a star was shrinking, and soul and funk dominated Black radio. Verve recorded him at the Bombay Bicycle Club in Los Angeles with a band of local session players, among them the bassist Wilton Felder of the Crusaders, aiming to put the organ back into the funk conversation. It was one of Smith's last important records for the label.","The Beastie Boys sampled the title track, Root Down (And Get It), for their own 1994 song Root Down, and the album was a staple of the crate-digging DJs who built early hip-hop beats. Listen to how the electric bass frees Smith's feet: for once he is not playing bass, so his left hand comps chords like a funk guitarist and the organ sounds lighter and more stabbing than on his Blue Note records.","It received little critical attention on release, seen as one more live date from a veteran past his prime. Its afterlife came through sampling and the rare-groove scene of the 1980s and 1990s, when it was reappraised as one of the hardest funk records any jazz organist made. It is now often ranked among Smith's essential recordings, though some jazz writers still prefer his earlier work.","Begin with the title track, and listen for the bass riff and the space between Smith's phrases. Then hear Let's Stay Together and compare it with Al Green's original. If you know the Beastie Boys' Check Your Head and its organ-funk instrumentals, you will hear exactly where they learned the sound."],"grant-green-idle-moments":["Four long tracks played by a sextet: Grant Green on guitar, Joe Henderson on tenor saxophone, Bobby Hutcherson on vibraphone, Duke Pearson on piano, Bob Cranshaw on bass and Al Harewood on drums. The title track, Pearson's slow, hushed blues ballad, lasts about fifteen minutes and sets the mood of the whole record: late-night, unhurried, almost whispered. There is also a version of John Lewis's Django and a gentle Latin-tinged piece. Nothing is loud; everything breathes.","Recorded in November 1963 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1965, the album caught Green at the height of his Blue Note period, when he was playing on dozens of other leaders' sessions. Pearson, a pianist and arranger, was becoming central to the label's sound. The record sits between soul jazz and the more reflective post-bop of Henderson and Hutcherson, both then rising stars at Blue Note.","The title track ran so long because the players misread its structure and took double-length choruses; producer Alfred Lion liked the result, so the other tunes were re-recorded at greater length to fit the LP's sides. Later CD issues include the original shorter takes, which let you hear the change. Listen to Green's solo on the title track: he repeats tiny phrases almost obsessively, a habit that becomes hypnotic at this slow tempo.","The album was not a hit, but it became one of the most cherished records in the Blue Note catalogue, often called Green's finest and one of the great late-night jazz albums. The vibraphone and guitar blend has been widely admired. Debate, where it exists, concerns whether the short or long takes of the shorter tunes are better; the long versions remain the canonical album.","Play the title track at night, quietly, and resist doing anything else. Listen for how the vibraphone and guitar share the same soft register without crowding each other. Then compare Henderson's dry, searching tenor with Green's round tone. For the next step, try Hutcherson's own records or Green's livelier Alive!"],"grant-green-alive":["A live club recording of hard, extended funk. Grant Green leads a band with organ, saxophone, conga and Idris Muhammad's drums through long workouts on soul and pop material of the day, including Kool & the Gang's Let the Music Take Your Mind and Don Covay's Sookie Sookie. Green's guitar is the centre: a dry, biting tone repeating short riffs dozens of times, locked to the drums. The crowd is audible and the band responds to it.","Recorded in 1970 at the Cliché Lounge in Newark, New Jersey, a working club on the organ-bar circuit, during Green's return to Blue Note after a period away from recording. By then soul jazz had turned towards funk, and Green chose current hits by James Brown-era bands rather than standards. Blue Note itself had been sold and was chasing a younger Black audience with more danceable records.","A Tribe Called Quest built Vibes and Stuff on The Low End Theory partly from Green's Down Here on the Ground, the record's slow soul ballad. Listen closely to how Green solos: rather than running scales, he picks one short phrase and repeats it with tiny rhythmic shifts until the band locks to him, a technique closer to a funk guitarist's than a bebop player's. Idris Muhammad's drumming is a lesson in tight, swinging funk.","Jazz critics of the time largely dismissed Green's funk period as commercial. Collectors and DJs in the 1980s and 1990s reversed the judgment, and the album is now valued as one of the most physically exciting live jazz-funk records. Some still argue that Green's earlier Blue Note work is deeper, but few dispute that his rhythmic drive was never stronger than here.","Start with the opening Kool & the Gang cover and let it run; the length is the pleasure. Listen for the repeated guitar figure and the moment the crowd starts shouting. Then hear Down Here on the Ground and put on A Tribe Called Quest's Low End Theory afterwards to catch where it went."],"lou-donaldson-alligator-bogaloo":["A party record of the boogaloo era. The title track is a strutting, almost comic groove built on a riff any listener can remember, with Donaldson's alto bright and tart over Lonnie Smith's organ and a crisp backbeat from the drums. George Benson, then a young organ-band guitarist, plays chunky rhythm and quick, clean solos. The rest of the album mixes more grooves with a ballad and a bebop flourish, all compact and good-humoured.","Recorded in 1967, at the height of the boogaloo dance craze, the album was Donaldson's biggest seller and one of Blue Note's. The label had learned from Lee Morgan's The Sidewinder that a catchy groove at the start of an album could reach jukeboxes and soul radio. Donaldson, a bebop veteran, embraced the move wholeheartedly, and organ combos on the East Coast bar circuit were his natural setting.","The drummer is credited as Leo Morris, the name Idris Muhammad used before his conversion to Islam; his New Orleans training is why the backbeat feels so loose and springy. Listen for Benson's guitar comping on the title track, already precise and rhythmically sure, years before he became a star. Donaldson quotes snatches of pop tunes in his solos, a habit that marks his whole style.","Purists treated the record as evidence of jazz selling out to dance music, and it was rarely discussed seriously at the time. It sold, however, and its reputation has grown among DJs and collectors who value groove records of the period. It is now regarded as a classic of boogaloo jazz, and a document of the early careers of Benson and Lonnie Smith.","Play the title track and move to it; that is its whole purpose. Then listen again for the drums, which are doing far more than they seem. Pair it with Lee Morgan's The Sidewinder and Cannonball Adderley's Mercy, Mercy, Mercy to hear how jazz chased the jukebox in the mid-1960s."],"ramsey-lewis-the-in-crowd":["A piano trio recorded live in a club, with an audience that claps, shouts and laughs throughout. The title track turns a current pop hit into a gospel-blues stomp: Ramsey Lewis plays the tune in big block chords, Eldee Young's bass walks hard and Redd Holt hits a backbeat. The rest of the set mixes standards, blues and pop tunes, each treated the same way, with churchy chords and a light, bouncing swing. It sounds like a very good Friday night.","Recorded in May 1965 at the Bohemian Caverns, a basement club in Washington, D.C., and released on Chess Records' jazz imprint Argo. The trio had been together for a decade and was popular in Chicago but not nationally known. The title song had been a recent hit for the soul singer Dobie Gray, and the trio's version, issued as a single, became a crossover hit and won a Grammy.","The audience is central: its clapping on two and four is effectively a fourth instrument, and Lewis plays to it, leaving gaps for shouts. Listen for the moments in the title track where Lewis thins the texture to a few repeated chords and lets the room fill the gap. The pop song is barely changed; the transformation is entirely rhythmic and harmonic.","It was a commercial phenomenon, a jazz trio album selling to a mass audience, and that made some critics suspicious. Over time it has been understood as part of the broader soul jazz moment alongside Mercy, Mercy, Mercy, and as a model for the live, audience-driven jazz record. Debate continues over whether it counts as jazz at all; Lewis himself shrugged at the question.","Play the title track first and listen for the audience's clapping, then the block chords. Notice how little Lewis decorates the melody. Then hear Ahmad Jamal's Poinciana, an earlier live trio hit, to compare two very different ideas of how a club audience can shape a record."],"ramsey-lewis-sun-goddess":["Ramsey Lewis plugs in. The title track is a long, sunny groove of Fender Rhodes electric piano, chattering percussion, wah-wah guitar and wordless voices chanting in the background, built over a light funk beat. Other tracks move between soft-focus ballads, Latin rhythms and harder funk. Lewis plays acoustic piano too, but the record's identity is the electric keyboard and the warm, communal sound of a band in which everyone is a percussionist as well as a melody player.","By 1974 the trio format that had made Lewis famous was out of step with the times. Maurice White of Earth, Wind & Fire, who had played drums in Lewis's trio in the 1960s, produced the title track and brought members of his band to the session. The record was released on Columbia and became one of the best-selling jazz albums of the year, reaching soul radio and a young audience.","White's presence changes everything: the title track sounds like an Earth, Wind & Fire instrumental, with the same kalimba-like percussion textures and chanted vocals, and Lewis becomes a soloist inside a band sound rather than its centre. Listen to how the electric piano's tremolo blurs into the voices. The record is a hinge between the 1960s soul jazz trio and the late-1970s jazz-funk that would dominate radio.","Jazz critics were mostly dismissive, seeing a fine pianist diluted into background funk. Commercially it was a triumph, and it has since been reassessed by listeners who value the sunny, unforced mood of 1970s jazz-funk. It remains contested ground, a record loved by its audience and still sometimes used as a shorthand for the dilution of jazz in the 1970s.","Begin with the title track and let it unfold; it is about atmosphere, not development. Listen for the voices behind the electric piano. Then play Earth, Wind & Fire's That's the Way of the World to hear the shared sound, and go back to The In Crowd to hear how far Lewis travelled in nine years."],"les-mccann-swiss-movement":["A live festival set by a quintet co-led by pianist and singer Les McCann and tenor saxophonist Eddie Harris, with Benny Bailey on trumpet. The opening track, Compared to What, is a churning protest song over a relentless minor-key vamp, with McCann's raw voice and stabbing piano driving it forward. The remaining tracks are long instrumentals, gospel-tinged blues and grooves where Harris's light tenor and Bailey's trumpet take extended solos over a band that plays as if lives depended on it.","Recorded at the Montreux Jazz Festival in Switzerland in June 1969. McCann and Harris had not played together before; they were thrown together for the festival and barely rehearsed, with Bailey, an American expatriate in Europe, joining at short notice. Compared to What was written by Gene McDaniels and attacks the Vietnam War and the hypocrisy of American life. The album was released by Atlantic and sold extremely well.","The excitement is partly nerves: the band was improvising arrangements on the spot, and you can hear moments where players work out who will solo next. Listen to Bailey, a musician little known in America, whose solos are among the highlights. The title is a pun on the precision of Swiss watches, ironic for a set held together by instinct and goodwill.","It was one of the best-selling jazz albums of its time. Compared to What became a soul jazz standard and a protest anthem covered by many artists. Critics have since praised it as a rare example of a live recording that captures the danger and joy of improvisation at full stretch, while some regard its long instrumental tracks as uneven.","Start with Compared to What and listen to how the band keeps the vamp going while McCann pushes against it. Then listen for Bailey's trumpet. Pair it with Nina Simone's protest songs of the same period, and with Eddie Harris's own The Electrifying Eddie Harris, for the saxophonist's studio sound."],"eddie-harris-the-electrifying-eddie-harris":["Eddie Harris plays tenor saxophone through the Varitone, an electronic attachment that could add an octave below the note, echo and tremolo, giving his horn a thick, doubled, slightly eerie sound. The opener, Listen Here, is a funky riff over a loping groove that stretches for several minutes and became his biggest hit. The rest of the album mixes more riff tunes with ballads and fast pieces, often with a brass section punching behind him.","Harris was signed to Atlantic, which was eager to reach soul radio, and the label's producer Joel Dorn encouraged him to experiment. The Varitone, made by the Selmer company and introduced in the mid-1960s, was one of the first electronic devices for wind instruments. Harris was among its earliest and most committed users. Listen Here was released as a single and crossed over to the rhythm and blues charts.","The octave effect makes a single saxophone sound like two playing in unison, and Harris exploits this for riffs that seem to come from a section rather than a soloist. Listen for the moments where the electronic doubling slips slightly, producing a wobbling, phasing tone. Harris was also a technically gifted player in the high register, and his fast, light runs on the ballads show he did not need electronics to dazzle.","Listen Here was a genuine hit and the album sold strongly. Critics were divided: some heard novelty, others real innovation. The Varitone has largely disappeared, but Harris's experiments are now credited as an important step towards the electric jazz of the following years, alongside Miles Davis's turn to electric instruments. The album remains a favourite of DJs and soul jazz collectors.","Start with Listen Here and focus on the saxophone's doubled sound. Notice how the groove barely changes; the interest is in the timbre and the riff. Then hear Swiss Movement to compare Harris's acoustic tenor on stage, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way for where the electric idea went next."],"donald-byrd-a-new-perspective":["A jazz septet joined by a wordless gospel choir. Donald Byrd's trumpet, Hank Mobley's tenor saxophone, Herbie Hancock's piano and Kenny Burrell's guitar play blues and spirituals while the voices hum, chant and swell in arrangements by Duke Pearson. The best-known piece, Pearson's Cristo Redentor, is slow and solemn, the choir carrying a mournful melody while the trumpet floats above. Elsewhere the music is joyful and swinging, a jazz band in church.","Recorded in January 1963 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, the album reflected Byrd's interest in the roots of Black American music; he was studying composition and would later teach at Howard University. The use of a choir in a small-group jazz record was unusual. The civil rights movement was at its height, and gospel and spirituals carried political as well as spiritual weight.","Cristo Redentor, named after the statue above Rio de Janeiro, is by Pearson, and its slow, swaying melody owes as much to a hymn as to bossa nova. Listen to Hancock, then a young sideman before his own fame, who plays spare, churchy chords. The choir never sings words, which keeps the record from sounding like a concert spiritual and makes the voices another section of the band.","The album was well received and Cristo Redentor became a minor hit, covered by Harvey Mandel and others. Some critics found the choir sentimental. Its standing has grown with time, and it is now seen as an important precursor of the spiritual and gospel-inflected jazz of later decades, and of Byrd's own move towards voices in his 1970s records.","Start with Cristo Redentor and listen to how the choir and trumpet interact. Then hear the faster tracks for Hancock and Mobley. Compare it with Mahalia Jackson's recordings to hear the gospel tradition it draws on, and with Black Byrd to hear Byrd's later use of voices."],"donald-byrd-black-byrd":["Smooth, insistent jazz-funk produced by Larry Mizell. Donald Byrd's trumpet, often treated with echo, floats over electric bass lines, clavinet, synthesiser and soft group vocals that chant short phrases rather than sing songs. The tracks are compact, built on repeating grooves rather than chord changes, and the mood ranges from the brooding title track to the airy Flight Time and the laid-back Mr. Thomas. It sounds more like a soul record than anything Blue Note had made.","Byrd was teaching at Howard University and looking for a new direction when he began working with Larry and Fonce Mizell, brothers with Motown experience who ran a production company. Their combination of jazz soloing, soul production and catchy vocal hooks was entirely new for Blue Note. Black Byrd became the best-selling album in the label's history up to that point, reaching soul and pop audiences.","DJ Premier sampled Flight Time for Nas's N.Y. State of Mind, and the album was a deep well for hip-hop producers generally. Listen to how little Byrd actually plays: the trumpet is one colour among many, entering briefly, and much of the record's identity comes from the bass and the vocal hooks. The Mizells' Motown background shows in the tight, layered rhythm arrangements.","Jazz critics were scathing, and Byrd was accused of abandoning jazz for commerce. The public disagreed, and the album sold in huge numbers. Its reputation was transformed by hip-hop sampling and the rare-groove scene, and it is now seen as a founding record of jazz-funk. The argument over whether it is jazz at all has never fully gone away.","Start with Flight Time and listen to the bass and vocal hooks, then put on Nas's N.Y. State of Mind. Then hear the title track. Compare it with A New Perspective to hear Byrd's career in two records, and with Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters, from the same year."],"donald-byrd-places-and-spaces":["The Mizell production style at its most lush. Strings swirl over soft disco-era beats, voices sing short, airy hooks, and Donald Byrd's trumpet floats in and out like a guest at his own party. The opener, Change (Makes You Want to Hustle), is a gliding dance track; the title piece is slower and more dreamlike; Wind Parade is light and buoyant. Everything is polished, warm and designed for movement rather than close listening.","By 1975 the Mizell brothers were among the most successful producers in jazz and soul, working with Byrd, Bobbi Humphrey, Johnny Hammond and others. Disco was rising, and the hustle was the dance of the moment. Byrd was also guiding his Howard University students in the Blackbyrds, a group whose records sold heavily. Places and Spaces was his most sophisticated collaboration with the Mizells.","Listen for how the strings are used: not as a romantic wash but as rhythmic punctuation, short and crisp, borrowed from Philadelphia soul production. The record's sense of space comes from echo applied to voices and trumpet, a studio technique that makes the sound seem to float. Byrd plays fewer notes than on any earlier record, and his solos are almost melodic decorations.","Jazz critics largely ignored or dismissed it. It was a solid seller and has become one of the most loved records of the Mizell sound, reappraised through DJ culture and sampling. Many now regard it as the high point of Byrd's 1970s work. Its sleekness divides listeners: some hear a perfect mood record, others the beginning of jazz dissolving into background music.","Play Change (Makes You Want to Hustle) first and notice the crisp strings and the light beat. Then hear the title track for its dreamlike atmosphere. Compare it with Black Byrd to hear how the Mizell sound smoothed out in two years, and with Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine for kindred warmth."],"bobbi-humphrey-blacks-and-blues":["Bobbi Humphrey's flute floats over grooves produced by Larry Mizell: electric bass lines, chiming electric keyboards, light percussion and soft group vocals singing short hooks. The album is compact, six tracks, and the mood is warm, airy and unhurried. Harlem River Drive is the best known, a gliding groove with a vocal refrain, but every track works the same way, with the flute as the singing voice and the rhythm section never letting the groove drop.","Humphrey had been signed to Blue Note in 1971 and her first records were conventional soul jazz. Producer Larry Mizell, fresh from Donald Byrd's Black Byrd, gave her the same treatment, and the result sold well. Female instrumentalists were rare at Blue Note and in jazz generally, and Humphrey's success opened doors. The album came out in the same year as Byrd's record and shares much of its sound.","Listen to how the flute is recorded: close, breathy and slightly echoed, so that it sounds almost like a voice. The Mizells write the melodies to suit the flute's range rather than adapting horn lines. Harlem River Drive names an actual Manhattan highway, and the music evokes cruising rather than any specific story. The vocal hooks are sung by a small group, not soloists.","It was a solid seller and helped establish the Mizell sound. Critics paid it little attention. In the 1980s and 1990s it became a favourite of British jazz-funk and rare-groove DJs, and its reputation has grown. It is now seen as one of the defining records of 1970s Blue Note, though still rarely discussed in mainstream jazz histories.","Begin with Harlem River Drive and listen for the flute's breathy, singing tone. Then play the title track. Compare it with Donald Byrd's Black Byrd to hear the shared production, and with Lonnie Liston Smith's Expansions for another take on gentle, danceable jazz."],"george-benson-breezin":["Six tracks of polished, relaxed guitar music with string arrangements by Claus Ogerman. The title track, by Bobby Womack, is a gliding instrumental where George Benson's clean, round guitar plays the melody over a light groove. The one vocal, This Masquerade by Leon Russell, has Benson singing in a warm, supple voice and scatting in unison with his own guitar solo. Everything is smooth, tasteful and carefully produced, with space for long, fluent guitar improvisations.","Benson had moved from CTI to Warner Bros., where producer Tommy LiPuma aimed to make a record that could reach pop radio without losing the jazz soloing. The sessions took place in Los Angeles. The album became the first jazz record to reach the top of the Billboard album chart and went multi-platinum. This Masquerade won the Grammy for Record of the Year.","The unison singing and guitar is the trick: Benson sings exactly the notes he plays, a technique he had practised for years, and the combination creates a timbre that sounds like a new instrument. Listen to the solos, which are genuinely complex jazz improvisations, fast and harmonically sophisticated, even when the setting is soft. The string arrangements are subtle, placed far back in the mix.","It was a massive commercial success and earned several Grammys. Jazz critics were divided; some saw a great guitarist selling out, others noted the quality of the playing. It is often cited as a founding record of smooth jazz, which colours its reputation. Many guitarists regard the soloing as among Benson's finest, and the album remains a touchstone of 1970s production.","Start with This Masquerade and listen for the moment the voice and guitar become one. Then hear the title track for Benson's tone. Compare with Wes Montgomery, Benson's model, and with Benson's earlier organ-band work on Lou Donaldson's Alligator Bogaloo to hear how far he came."],"grover-washington-jr-mister-magic":["Four long tracks of warm, sensual jazz-funk. The title track is a slow, sultry groove that stretches over nine minutes, with Grover Washington Jr.'s soprano saxophone singing a simple, insistent melody over electric piano, bass and percussion. Bob James's arrangements add strings and brass in soft layers. The other tracks are similar: relaxed tempos, long solos, and a mood that is intimate rather than energetic, built for late-night listening.","The album was released on Kudu, Creed Taylor's label for soul-oriented records, an offshoot of CTI. Bob James, Taylor's chief arranger, was at the height of his influence. The title track, written by Ralph MacDonald and William Salter, became a crossover hit and the album crossed from jazz to soul and pop audiences. It was Washington's commercial breakthrough. The CTI family of labels gave its records matching gatefold sleeves and glossy photography, so the look sold the mood before a note was heard.","Listen to the percussion on the title track: the congas and shakers give the groove its gentle propulsion, more important than the drums. Washington's soprano tone is round and vocal, without the nasal edge many soprano players have. The title track's length is deliberate; the melody returns again and again, each time slightly varied, and the effect is hypnotic.","It was a major hit and established Washington as a star. Jazz critics were mixed, though many admired his tone. It is often cited as a key record in the development of smooth jazz, and the title track has been widely covered. Its reputation has held up better than much of the genre it helped create.","Start with the title track and let it run; the length is part of the effect. Listen for the percussion and the soprano's singing tone. Then hear Winelight, from five years later, and Bob James's One, to hear the arranger's hand."],"grover-washington-jr-winelight":["Six tracks of polished, intimate music. The title track is a slow, glowing groove for soprano saxophone. The best known, Just the Two of Us, is a love song sung by Bill Withers, with Grover Washington Jr.'s saxophone weaving around the voice and taking a long solo. Marcus Miller plays bass, Steve Gadd drums, and the sound is soft, warm and impeccably recorded. Nothing is rushed; every note sits in place.","Washington had moved to Elektra, and the album was produced with Ralph MacDonald and William Salter, who co-wrote Just the Two of Us with Withers. The song became a huge crossover hit and won the Grammy for Best R&B Song; the album won for Best Jazz Fusion Performance. It defined the sound of what would soon be called quiet storm and smooth jazz radio.","Listen for Marcus Miller's bass, which is unusually melodic and rhythmically playful, foreshadowing his later work with Miles Davis. Withers's vocal is relaxed and conversational, recorded close, and Washington's saxophone answers him like a second singer; the light, Latin-tinged percussion keeps the song moving so it never sags into a slow ballad.","It was a huge commercial success and won Grammys. Jazz critics were mostly dismissive, seeing it as background music. Over time it has become a touchstone of smooth jazz and quiet storm radio, loved by its audience and sometimes mocked by jazz purists. Just the Two of Us has been covered and sampled widely, and remains one of the most recognisable songs of its era.","Start with Just the Two of Us and listen for how the saxophone and voice converse. Then hear the title track for Washington's soprano tone. Compare with Mister Magic to hear how his sound smoothed further, and with George Benson's Breezin' for a parallel crossover."],"roy-ayers-everybody-loves-the-sunshine":["Four minutes of slow, shimmering heat. Roy Ayers's vibraphone and synthesisers lay down a hazy, swaying groove; voices, his own among them, sing a gentle, repeated refrain about the pleasures of a summer day. The tempo is languid, the bass round and slow, and the sound feels like warm air moving. There is no solo to speak of; the song is all mood, a single texture held for its whole length.","Ayers recorded the song with Roy Ayers Ubiquity for an album of the same name on Polydor. He had moved from hard bop vibraphone to soul, funk and early disco, and was working in New York with a rotating band. The song was not a major hit on release but became a staple of DJs and of the community block parties that would shape hip-hop.","Mary J. Blige's 1994 song My Life is built on it, and producers from many genres have sampled or replayed its chords and refrain. Listen closely to the vibraphone: Ayers uses it for sustained, shimmering chords rather than fast runs, blending it with synthesiser so the two instruments become one hazy sound. The song's simplicity is its strength; it barely changes, and that is the point.","It was a modest success on release. Its reputation has grown enormously through sampling and DJ culture, and it is now regarded as one of the defining soul-jazz songs of the 1970s, a symbol of summer for several generations. Some jazz critics still see Ayers's 1970s work as a loss, but few dispute the song's influence.","Play it on a warm day and do nothing else. Listen for the blend of vibraphone and synthesiser, then for the slow bass. Then hear Mary J. Blige's My Life to hear how it was reused, and Donald Byrd's Places and Spaces for kindred warmth."],"lonnie-liston-smith-expansions":["Lonnie Liston Smith and the Cosmic Echoes play gentle, spacious jazz-funk with a spiritual undertow. The title track rides a buoyant, endlessly circling bass line under washes of electric piano and synthesiser, with a soft vocal from Donald Smith, the leader's brother, delivering a message of consciousness and unity. Other tracks lean towards Latin percussion or slower, meditative drift. The textures are soft, the grooves insistent, the mood hopeful and calm.","Smith had played with Pharoah Sanders, whose spiritual jazz albums used drones and devotional moods, and with Miles Davis's electric band of 1972 and 1973. On Bob Thiele's Flying Dutchman label he combined both influences with funk rhythms. Expansions sold well and found an audience well beyond jazz, especially in British clubs where it became an anthem of the jazz-funk scene.","Listen to the bass line on the title track: it never stops, a single circular figure that makes the track as much a trance record as a funk one. The electric piano chords are held and allowed to decay, creating a floating sense of space. The vocal is spoken-sung rather than belted, which keeps the record meditative. It is the Pharoah Sanders influence carried onto the dance floor.","It was a commercial success and became a dance-floor classic, particularly in Britain. Jazz critics were mostly uninterested at the time, but its reputation has grown through DJ culture, and it is now seen as a crucial bridge between spiritual jazz and later dance music. It is often cited by house and broken-beat producers as an influence.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass line, then the electric piano washes. Then hear Pharoah Sanders's Karma, to which Smith contributed, to hear where the spiritual mood comes from. Compare with Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine for another warm, communal groove."],"freddie-hubbard-red-clay":["A quintet record that blends hard bop with funk and electric sound. The title track rides a loping, insistent bass line and a funky drum groove, with Freddie Hubbard's brassy trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor stating a punchy theme before long, fiery solos. Herbie Hancock plays electric piano, Ron Carter bass and Lenny White drums. The album has four long tracks, all adventurous but grounded in groove, with room for real improvisation.","Recorded in January 1970 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, it was one of the first releases on Creed Taylor's new CTI label. Taylor wanted jazz with high production values and wider appeal, and he gathered the best players of the day. Hubbard had been a Blue Note star; here he was moving towards a more electric, funk-influenced sound, in step with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew of the same period.","Listen to Hancock's electric piano, which he was just beginning to explore; its slightly distorted, bell-like tone shapes the record's sound. The title track's bass line is a simple repeated figure, but Carter varies it subtly throughout. The record balances CTI's polish with the raw energy of a blowing session; there are no strings or overdubs, just a quintet in a room.","It was well received and became one of CTI's most admired records, often cited as Hubbard's finest album. Critics generally praised its balance of groove and improvisation. It has been less controversial than later CTI albums with strings, and is regarded as a model of early-1970s jazz that embraced electric sound without losing its edge.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass line, then follow Hubbard's solo, which builds to dazzling high notes. Compare with Herbie Hancock's Maiden Voyage, on which Hubbard and Carter also play, and with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for a more radical take on electric jazz."],"freddie-hubbard-first-light":["Freddie Hubbard's trumpet set against lush orchestral arrangements by Don Sebesky. The title track, Hubbard's own, is a long, slowly building piece where strings and woodwinds lay down a soft, shimmering backdrop and the trumpet rises above in long, lyrical lines. The album also includes a version of a Paul McCartney song and a film theme by Henry Mancini. The mood is warm, cinematic and polished, with the trumpet always in front.","Creed Taylor had built CTI on lush production, and Sebesky was his signature arranger. The album was a commercial success and won a Grammy. Hubbard, a fiery hard bop player, was here presented as a romantic soloist, part of Taylor's strategy to reach a wider audience. The sessions used top New York studio players of the CTI circle. The album was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, as nearly all CTI records were.","Listen to how Sebesky voices the strings: not as a continuous wash but in short, shifting chords that respond to Hubbard's phrases. Hubbard's tone on the title track is unusually soft and rounded, closer to a flugelhorn than his usual brassy sound. The album shows a different side of a player usually associated with speed and power, and that restraint was a deliberate choice.","It won a Grammy and sold well. Critics were divided: some praised Hubbard's lyricism, others found the strings sentimental. It has come to represent the polished CTI sound, admired and criticised in equal measure. Many listeners regard the title track as one of Hubbard's most beautiful recordings, while purists prefer Red Clay.","Start with the title track and listen to how the trumpet floats above the strings. Then compare with Red Clay to hear Hubbard without an orchestra. For context, hear Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain, an earlier and more austere model of trumpet with orchestra."],"bob-james-one":["Bob James's first CTI album mixes funk grooves, orchestral arrangements and classical pieces. It includes a funk version of Mussorgsky's Night on Bald Mountain, with strings and brass rising over a hard backbeat, and In the Garden, built on Pachelbel's Canon. There is also a version of Roberta Flack's Feel Like Making Love and the brooding closing track Nautilus, a slow groove with a chiming Fender Rhodes and a stark drumbeat.","James was Creed Taylor's chief arranger, and this album was his debut as a leader for the label. CTI's records were known for their lush production and crossover ambitions, and James combined jazz, funk, classical and pop in a single package. The album sold well and established him as a leading figure in the emerging smooth jazz and jazz-funk scenes.","Nautilus became one of the most sampled tracks in hip-hop; Ghostface Killah's Daytona 500 is one of many records built on it. Listen to the track's bare opening, just Rhodes and drums, which is what producers wanted. In the Mussorgsky, notice how the famous swirling string figures are given to synthesiser and electric piano, while the brass keeps the original's menace over the backbeat.","Jazz critics were sceptical of the classical adaptations, seeing them as gimmicks. The album sold well and James's career flourished. Its reputation was transformed by hip-hop, which made Nautilus a foundational sample. It is now valued both as a CTI artefact and as a source text for a later genre, though debate over the classical adaptations continues.","Start with Nautilus and listen to the opening; then imagine it looped under a rapper's voice. Then hear Night on Bald Mountain and compare it with Mussorgsky's original. Follow with Two, his next album, which has the Bizet adaptation and Take Me to the Mardi Gras."],"bob-james-two":["Bob James's second CTI album continues the mix of funk, orchestral arrangements and classical adaptation. It opens with Take Me to the Mardi Gras, Paul Simon's song, which starts with a few bars of solo bells and drums before a gentle groove takes over. Farandole is a funk version of Bizet's L'Arlésienne, with the march tune carried by brass over a hard backbeat. The rest of the album is smooth, tuneful and polished, with James's Fender Rhodes at the centre.","The album followed the success of One and used the same team of top New York studio players. CTI was at the height of its influence, though Creed Taylor's empire would soon run into financial trouble. James was arranging for many CTI artists, and his own records served as showcases for his production style, blending jazz soloing with pop melody and classical grandeur.","The opening bells and drums of Take Me to the Mardi Gras became one of the most famous breaks in hip-hop: Run-DMC built Peter Piper, on Raising Hell, on them. Listen to how the bells ring alone before the band enters; that brief isolation is what made the passage usable by DJs. In Farandole, listen for how the stately march tune keeps its dignity even over a drum kit.","It sold well, and like One received mixed reviews from jazz critics. Its afterlife in hip-hop has given it a new reputation, and the Mardi Gras break is among the most widely used samples in rap history. The Bizet adaptation remains divisive: some hear an imaginative crossover, others a cheapening of the classical source.","Play the opening of Take Me to the Mardi Gras and listen for the bells alone, then put on Run-DMC's Peter Piper. Then hear Farandole beside Bizet's original. Compare with One for the Mussorgsky adaptation and with Deodato's Also sprach Zarathustra for another CTI take on a classical source."],"bob-james-angela":["A wistful, gently swinging melody played on Fender Rhodes electric piano, with soft synthesiser and a light rhythm section. The tune moves in a lilting rhythm with a hint of melancholy, rising and falling over a patient bass line. In its full-length version on James's album Touchdown it stretches out with room for improvisation; on television the opening was trimmed to under a minute. It is instantly recognisable from its first phrase.","James wrote it for the American sitcom Taxi, which premiered in 1978 and ran until 1983. The show followed a group of New York cab drivers, and the music, played over the opening shots of a cab crossing the Queensboro Bridge, set a tone of quiet loneliness that the comedy played against. James had started his own label, Tappan Zee, distributed by Columbia, after leaving CTI.","The theme does not hurry: it lets a single phrase hang, then answers it a step lower, a shape that suits the show's bittersweet character better than any upbeat sitcom jingle would. Listen to the Rhodes tone: James uses the instrument's natural bell-like attack and slow decay to give the melody a slightly blurred, nostalgic quality. The rhythm section stays very light, almost brushed, so the melody can float.","It became one of the most famous television themes ever written and introduced James's music to millions who never heard his albums. Critics have praised its economy of mood, and it is often cited as a model of how a short theme can define a show. Its success also tied James firmly to the smooth jazz label, for better or worse.","Listen to the full version on Touchdown and follow the melody through its gentle variations. Notice the Rhodes tone. Compare with Henry Mancini's Peter Gunn, another jazz-inflected television theme, and with Bernard Herrmann's Taxi Driver, a darker vision of the same city in the same decade."],"deodato-also-sprach-zarathustra":["A funk version of the opening of Richard Strauss's tone poem. The famous rising trumpet motif is stated over a hard, Latin-tinged groove of electric bass, conga and drums, then the piece opens out into long, jazzy improvisations on electric piano and guitar. The original's grand fanfare becomes a vamp, repeated and varied. Brass and strings punctuate the groove. The track runs about nine minutes on the album, much shorter as a single.","Deodato, a Brazilian arranger working in New York, recorded it for his CTI album Prelude. Stanley Kubrick's film 2001: A Space Odyssey, released in 1968, had made Strauss's opening world famous, and the track's parenthetical title acknowledged the film. Released as a single, it became a major pop hit and won a Grammy. It was one of the biggest hits CTI ever had.","Listen to how Deodato handles Strauss's famous opening: he keeps the rising three-note motif but replaces the original's organ pedal and timpani with a funk groove, so the grandeur becomes a dance. The Brazilian rhythmic feel, a light, syncopated lilt, is subtle but distinctive. After the opening statement, the track becomes essentially a jazz jam, with Strauss abandoned.","It was a huge commercial success. Classical listeners were mostly bemused, jazz critics mostly dismissive. Over time it has been seen as a defining example of 1970s crossover, alongside other jazz-funk adaptations of classical music. Its kitsch value has grown, but so has appreciation of its groove and arrangement. It also opened the door for many disco-era classical remakes.","Listen first to Strauss's original opening, then to Deodato's version, noticing what is kept and what is changed. Then follow the improvisations after the motif. Compare with Bob James's adaptations of Mussorgsky and Bizet for other CTI takes on classical themes."],"stanley-turrentine-sugar":["Three long tracks led by Stanley Turrentine's big, bluesy tenor. The title track is a slow, sultry minor-key groove where Turrentine's smoky tone stretches out over electric piano, organ and a patient bass line, followed by solos from Freddie Hubbard on trumpet and George Benson on guitar. The album also includes a version of John Coltrane's Impressions, played fast and hard. The sound is CTI-polished but rooted in soul jazz and the blues.","Turrentine had moved from Blue Note to Creed Taylor's CTI, and this was his first album for the label. Taylor surrounded him with stars, including Ron Carter on bass, and the result sold strongly. The title track became Turrentine's signature tune. It was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, whose clean, warm sound defined both Blue Note and CTI. The label's photographic sleeves made it look like a luxury product.","Listen to how Turrentine sits just behind the beat on the title track, stretching each phrase so it seems to lag and then catch up. The organ and electric piano are both present, giving the groove a doubled keyboard texture. The Coltrane cover shows that Turrentine, often thought of as a soul player, could handle modal jazz at speed.","It was well received and sold well, becoming one of CTI's best-loved records. Critics praised Turrentine's tone and the quality of the supporting players. It has been less criticised than later, more heavily produced CTI albums, and is seen as a model of soul jazz updated for the 1970s. The title tune has since become a jam-session standard.","Start with the title track and listen to Turrentine's tone, then the solos by Hubbard and Benson. Then hear the Coltrane cover. Compare with Back at the Chicken Shack, where Turrentine plays with Jimmy Smith a decade earlier. The voice is the same; only the setting changed."],"hubert-laws-the-rite-of-spring":["Hubert Laws's flute plays classical pieces in jazz arrangements by Don Sebesky. The title track condenses parts of Stravinsky's ballet into a groove, with Laws's flute carrying melodies over electric bass and percussion. There are also versions of Fauré's Pavane, Debussy's Syrinx, the solo flute piece, and a movement from Bach's Third Brandenburg Concerto. The arrangements are lush, the flute tone pure, and the mood moves between meditative and propulsive.","Laws was a classically trained flautist who also played in symphony orchestras. Creed Taylor's CTI label was known for polished crossover records, and this album was one of the most ambitious, applying jazz techniques to a classical repertoire. The late 1960s and early 1970s saw a wave of such crossover projects, among them Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach of 1968, which had turned Bach into a synthesiser best-seller.","Listen to the Syrinx, originally a piece for one unaccompanied flute, and notice how Laws's pure, vibrato-light classical tone survives the jazz setting. The Stravinsky arrangement keeps fragments of the original's rhythms but turns its violence into a groove. The Bach is played almost straight, with a jazz rhythm section adding a light swing. The album's interest lies in these contrasts.","It received mixed reviews: some praised Laws's skill and Sebesky's arrangements, others found the classical adaptations superficial. It has become a cult item, valued by flautists and collectors of CTI. Debate over its classical adaptations continues; some see it as a bridge between traditions, others as a gimmick. It remains an unusual listening test of how far a groove can carry a score.","Start with Syrinx and compare it with a classical recording. Then hear the title track beside Stravinsky's original, noticing which rhythms survive. Compare with Bob James's adaptations of Mussorgsky and Bizet, and with Deodato's Strauss, for other CTI takes on the classics."],"larry-young-unity":["An organ quartet that sounds nothing like the soul jazz organ combos. Larry Young plays Hammond organ with long, flowing lines and sustained chords, not churchy blues, while Woody Shaw on trumpet, Joe Henderson on tenor saxophone and Elvin Jones on drums push through complex, modal compositions. The opener, Shaw's Zoltan, begins with a march-like theme before exploding into fast swing. There is also a version of Monk's Dream and a ballad. The energy is relentless.","Recorded in November 1965 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1966. Young had begun as a soul jazz organist but was moving towards the modal jazz of John Coltrane, whose drummer Elvin Jones plays here. Shaw, then twenty, contributed three compositions. Blue Note was recording a wide range of styles, from boogaloo to the avant-garde. Van Gelder's close miking puts the organ and drums right in your face.","Zoltan, named for the composer Zoltán Kodály, borrows a march theme from his Háry János before turning into modal jazz. Listen to Young's left hand: rather than walking a bass line in the usual organ way, he often plays sustained chords, giving the music a floating, open feel. Jones's drumming is dense and polyrhythmic, as with Coltrane, and Young matches him.","It was not a commercial success but has become one of the most admired organ jazz records ever made, often cited as the moment the organ entered modern jazz. Critics praise its originality and the quality of Shaw's compositions. It is regularly included in lists of essential Blue Note records and is a touchstone for organists who want to move beyond soul jazz.","Start with Zoltan and listen for the march theme, then the explosion into swing. Notice how Young's organ sounds unlike Jimmy Smith's. Then hear Coltrane's A Love Supreme for the modal influence, and The Sermon! for the organ tradition Young was leaving."],"richard-groove-holmes-misty":["Erroll Garner's ballad played by an organ trio at a slow, swinging medium tempo. Richard Holmes states the melody with big, swelling organ chords, then walks a deep bass line under his own improvisations, building gradually without ever losing the tune. Gene Edwards's guitar comps softly, and the drums keep a light, patient swing. The result is warm, relaxed and gently grooving, a ballad turned into something you could dance slowly to.","Holmes recorded it for the album Soul Message on Prestige, which specialised in organ combos for the Black jukebox and bar market. The track was released as a single and became a surprise crossover hit, reaching pop and rhythm and blues audiences. Garner had written the tune in 1954 and it had become a standard through vocal versions, especially Johnny Mathis's.","Listen to Holmes's bass line: he plays it with his left hand and feet together, giving it a weight and punch that few organists matched, and that is the reason for his nickname. The tempo is faster than most versions of the ballad, which keeps it from becoming sentimental. The organ's swell pedal is used for dramatic crescendos at the ends of phrases.","It was a hit and made Holmes a star of the organ-jazz circuit. Critics generally praised it as a fine example of the organ trio's ability to transform a standard. It remains his best-known recording and a staple of organ-jazz collections, though some prefer his harder-swinging work. It showed that an organ trio could sell a ballad, not only a blues.","Listen first to the bass line, then the chords. Compare with Erroll Garner's own piano version to hear how the organ changes the tune. Then hear Jimmy Smith's The Sermon! for the organ trio's other side, the long, slow blues."],"the-kinks-face-to-face":["Fourteen short songs, every one by Ray Davies, and the first Kinks album that sounds like a single sensibility rather than a run of singles with filler. The guitars that made 'You Really Got Me' are still there, but the record is dominated by music-hall bounce, harpsichord, and small character sketches: a session musician, a dandy, a family selling up its grand house, a holidaymaker in a fake Hawaii. It closes its first run with the lazy, descending 'Sunny Afternoon'.","Made at Pye in London across 1966, a year in which Ray Davies had suffered a collapse from exhaustion and the band had been banned from touring America after a union dispute. Cut off from the US market that every British group was chasing, Davies turned inward and toward England: its class system, its suburbs, its taxman. The album came out in the autumn, a few months after 'Sunny Afternoon' had topped the British chart, and in the same season as Revolver.","Davies wanted sound effects linking every track, a kind of radio play, and Pye cut most of them; the survivors, a ringing telephone at the start, thunder in 'Rainy Day in June' and surf in 'Holiday in Waikiki', are the fossil of that plan. Listen to 'Fancy', where the guitar holds a single droning note under the voice in the manner of an Indian drone, a quieter cousin of the Byrds' raga experiments the same year.","Reviews were warm but sales were modest, and the album was overshadowed by the louder events of 1966. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often named as one of the first rock albums organised around a point of view rather than a sequence of hits. Arguments continue over whether Pye's removal of the linking effects spoiled a concept album or saved a set of songs from a gimmick.","Begin with 'Session Man', where the harpsichord and the joke about hired-hand musicians are the same gesture, then 'Sunny Afternoon' for Davies's voice at its most deadpan. Listen to how often the tunes come from the music hall rather than the blues. Follow it with Village Green and Waterloo Sunset to hear the idea mature, and with Revolver to hear what else was possible that year."],"the-kinks-arthur":["Twelve songs following one ordinary Englishman, a carpet-layer named Arthur, through the twentieth century: Victorian childhood, two world wars, a suburban semi named Shangri-La, and a son who emigrates to Australia. The music is fuller and louder than Village Green, with brass and strings over a tough rhythm section, and two long pieces, 'Shangri-La' and 'Australia', that stretch out past five minutes. 'Victoria' opens it with a fanfare-like riff that is half celebration, half sneer.","Ray Davies wrote the songs as the score for a Granada television play he devised with the novelist Julian Mitchell; the play was never made, and the album went out alone in October 1969. Arthur was modelled on Davies's brother-in-law, who had emigrated to Australia with Davies's elder sister. It was the first album with John Dalton on bass after Pete Quaife left, and it arrived a few months after the Who's Tommy, which took the attention and the label 'rock opera'.","Listen to how 'Shangri-La' changes speed and mood in sections, from a hushed, almost hymn-like opening to a jaunty music-hall middle and a massive orchestral climax; the structure mirrors a man's pride in his little house curdling into the realisation that it is a trap. 'Some Mother's Son', about a soldier killed in the First World War, sets that scene to the gentlest tune on the record, and the gentleness is the point.","American critics in particular championed it at the time, some preferring it to Tommy, but it sold modestly. Its standing has risen since, largely on the strength of 'Shangri-La' and 'Victoria', and it is now seen as the end of a run of four Kinks albums about Englishness that few British bands matched. The debate is whether Davies is mocking Arthur or mourning him; most listeners now hear both at once.","Start with 'Shangri-La' and follow its sections like movements of a short symphony, then hear 'Victoria' and 'Some Mother's Son' back to back for the record's range. Read about the unmade television play first; it makes the running order make sense. Compare it with Tommy, released the same year, to hear two very different British ideas of a story told in songs."],"the-who-i-cant-explain":["Two minutes of chopped power chords, a lead vocal from Roger Daltrey that sounds tense rather than tough, and a song about a young man who cannot find the words for what he feels about a girl. Pete Townshend's guitar riff, a few ringing chords hit hard and then cut off, does most of the work; Keith Moon's drumming is already busier than anyone else's in British pop, filling every gap with rolls; John Entwistle's bass holds the floor still underneath.","The Who's first single under that name, recorded in London with the American producer Shel Talmy, who had just made the Kinks' early hits. It was released early in 1965 and slowly climbed into the British top ten, helped by a television appearance on Ready Steady Go!. The band were the house group of the London mod scene, playing for crowds of sharp-dressed teenagers on scooters, and their managers sold them as the sound of that tribe.","Townshend has said openly that he wrote it as an imitation of the Kinks' 'You Really Got Me', and once you know, the family likeness in the riff is obvious. Listen to the backing vocals: they are not the band but a hired vocal group, the Ivy League, brought in because Talmy wanted a smoother pop sheen. The tension between that polish and Moon's drums is the sound of the Who being tidied up for radio and refusing to stay tidy.","It was received as a promising mod single rather than an event, and the band's bigger statement came with 'My Generation' later that year. Over time it has come to be seen as the template for power pop and for a whole line of short, frustrated guitar songs, from the Jam to Britpop. It remained in the Who's live set for decades, usually as an opener, which says something about how well the riff wears.","Play it loud and listen only to Moon on the first pass, then only to the guitar. Notice how short it is and how little happens, and how that brevity feels like the song's subject: a boy who cannot get the words out. Then play 'You Really Got Me' and 'My Generation' around it to hear the line from one to the other."],"the-who-a-quick-one":["The Who's second album, a deliberately mixed bag: songs by every member, a cover of Martha and the Vandellas' 'Heat Wave', and a closing nine-minute piece, 'A Quick One, While He's Away', that strings six short sections into a story about a woman whose lover is away for a year, a seduction, a confession and a forgiveness. John Entwistle's 'Boris the Spider', with its growling bass voice, sits alongside Townshend's tender 'So Sad About Us'. The horns heard here are Entwistle's own.","Released at the end of 1966 on the small Reaction label; in America it appeared as Happy Jack, named after the single. The band's publishing deal required each member to supply songs, which explains Keith Moon's instrumental and Roger Daltrey's single contribution. When they ran short of material, their manager Kit Lambert, son of the composer Constant Lambert, pushed Townshend to write something longer and joined together, and the mini-opera was the result.","The mini-opera's final section, the forgiveness, builds a chorus of overlapping voices over a simple repeated chord pattern until it becomes almost hymn-like, and that build is the direct ancestor of the climax of Tommy three years later. Listen also to the cello-like sound in the third section: the band could not afford strings, so they sang the word for them instead, in imitation of bowing.","The album was reviewed as uneven, which it is by design, and the band themselves treated it as a stopgap. Its reputation rests on the title piece, which the Who performed with ferocious energy at the Rolling Stones' Rock and Roll Circus in December 1968, a performance often said to have outplayed the hosts. Historians of the concept album now point to it as the hinge between the pop single and Tommy.","Go straight to 'A Quick One, While He's Away' and treat it as six tiny songs; notice how each section changes tempo and voice. Then hear 'So Sad About Us' and 'Boris the Spider' for the band's other faces. If you can find the Rock and Roll Circus footage, watch it: the live version shows what the studio take was reaching for."],"the-who-live-at-leeds":["Six tracks on the original LP: three rock-and-roll and rhythm-and-blues covers (Mose Allison's 'Young Man Blues', Eddie Cochran's 'Summertime Blues', Johnny Kidd's 'Shakin' All Over'), the band's own 'Substitute' and 'Magic Bus', and a fifteen-minute 'My Generation' that turns into a medley and a long improvisation. It is four players at maximum volume, with no overdubbed rhythm guitar, so Townshend's chords and Entwistle's lead-like bass carry all the harmony while Moon plays continuously around them.","Recorded at the University of Leeds refectory in February 1970, when the Who had been touring Tommy for nearly a year and were as tight as they would ever be. The band wanted a live record that sounded like the real thing rather than a cleaned-up studio product. The original sleeve imitated a bootleg: plain brown card with the title stamped on it, and inside, a folder of facsimile contracts, posters and receipts from the band's early years.","The original label carried an instruction to engineers not to remove the crackles, because the tapes had audible surface noise and the band preferred it to a fix. Listen in 'Young Man Blues' to the moments where everyone stops dead and starts again on a single beat; those breaks are rehearsed but sound reckless. Expanded reissues restored the whole show, including the complete Tommy, and revealed how much the LP left out.","It was received at once as one of the best live rock albums ever issued, and that verdict has barely moved. It is often named as a blueprint for hard rock and heavy metal performance, and a model for how a live record can stand as a work in itself. The only real argument concerns which version to hear: the tight six-track LP or the sprawling complete concert.","Start with 'Young Man Blues' and focus on Entwistle, whose bass is doing what a lead guitar would do elsewhere. Then 'Summertime Blues' alongside Eddie Cochran's original, to hear what volume and Moon did to a 1958 song. Hear the original six-track LP first; come to the expanded editions later."],"the-zombies-shes-not-there":["A two-and-a-half-minute minor-key single carried by Rod Argent's electric piano rather than guitar, Colin Blunstone's breathy, almost whispered lead vocal, and a chorus where the band's voices crowd in close. The song is about a man who was warned about a girl too late. The middle features a jazz-tinged electric piano solo, and the drums play with a light, swinging touch that sets it apart from the guitar bands of 1964.","The Zombies were schoolboys from St Albans, near London, who won a local beat-group competition and with it a contract with Decca. Argent wrote the song for their first session, and it was released in the summer of 1964, when the British Invasion was at its height. It was a modest hit at home and a much bigger one in America, which led the band to tour the United States while barely known in Britain.","Listen to the harmony: instead of the blues-derived chords of most British beat records, Argent uses the kind of shifting minor chords found in modal jazz and in the church music he had sung as a chorister. Blunstone's quiet delivery was unusual for a 1964 beat record, and it makes the song feel confessional rather than performed.","It was admired from the start as an unusually sophisticated pop single, and its standing has never really dipped. Musicians cite it as an early example of jazz harmony inside British pop, and it has been covered many times, notably by Santana in 1977. Critics sometimes wonder whether the band's later failure was a matter of being too subtle for their moment; this single suggests why.","Listen first to the electric piano, which plays the role a lead guitar would in most bands of the time, then to Blunstone's breath-heavy phrasing. Compare it with 'You Really Got Me' from the same year, to hear two opposite ideas of British pop. Then go to Odessey and Oracle to hear where the same ears ended up."],"the-yardbirds-shapes-of-things":["A tightly marching two and a half minutes, with Keith Relf's wary vocal over a pounding bass line, a song musing on war, progress and the future. It builds toward a guitar solo by Jeff Beck that does not sound like a guitar solo at all: a sustained, bending, feedback-laced line with an Indian or Middle Eastern cast, held in place with the controlled howl of an amplifier pushed past its limits.","Released early in 1966, written by Relf, the drummer Jim McCarty and the bassist Paul Samwell-Smith. Beck had replaced Eric Clapton in 1965, after Clapton left in protest at the band's turn toward pop. The Yardbirds were then the most adventurous of the London rhythm-and-blues groups, and they recorded parts of their work in America, including at Chess Studios in Chicago, the home of the blues records they had started out copying.","Beck's solo is built from feedback that he is controlling with his body, moving toward and away from the amplifier to bring the howl in and out. That technique, here only a few seconds long, would become a basic vocabulary of rock guitar within two years, through Hendrix and others. Beck liked the song enough to re-record it in 1968 on his album Truth, with Rod Stewart singing.","It was a British top-ten hit and was soon described as one of the first psychedelic singles. Critics now treat it as a key moment in the history of the electric guitar, the point where feedback moved from accident to instrument. Its status is complicated only by the fact that the Yardbirds have often been remembered for whose guitarists they launched rather than for their own records.","Wait for the solo, then play it again and listen only to the sound of the guitar: the sustain, the bending, the moment the amplifier seems to sing back. Notice how the verses tighten the tension before it. Then hear 'Eight Miles High' and Hendrix's Are You Experienced to follow where that sound went."],"the-yardbirds-roger-the-engineer":["Twelve original songs, mostly short, with Jeff Beck as the sole lead guitarist: blues shuffles sit beside stranger things: 'Hot House of Omagararshid' and 'Over Under Sideways Down' bring in drones, chanting and modal guitar lines. 'Jeff's Boogie' is a jaunty instrumental showcase, and 'Lost Woman' opens the album on a hypnotic bass riff. The sound is raw, live in the room, with Beck's guitar switching between fuzz, feedback and clean blues picking.","Recorded in London in 1966 and produced by Paul Samwell-Smith and the band's manager Simon Napier-Bell; in America it came out on Epic as Over Under Sideways Down. It was the only Yardbirds studio album made up entirely of their own songs. Samwell-Smith left soon after, and Jimmy Page joined first on bass and then as a second lead guitarist beside Beck, a line-up that lasted only months and made few records.","The title and the cover drawing come from the rhythm guitarist Chris Dreja's caricature of the studio's engineer, Roger Cameron, an affectionate joke that became the name the album is now known by. Listen to 'Over Under Sideways Down': its guitar line imitates a sitar's twang and slides, and the chanting, humming backing vocals owe more to Eastern devotional music than to Chicago blues.","It sold respectably in Britain but was soon overshadowed by bigger statements later that year. Its reputation has grown among guitarists, who treat it as the best document of Beck within a band, and among historians of psychedelia, who hear it as a bridge from British blues to the studio experiments of 1967. Some still find it too uneven to rank with the albums it anticipates.","Start with 'Over Under Sideways Down' and 'Hot House of Omagararshid' for the band's strangest side, then 'Lost Woman' for its blues roots. Follow Beck's tone from song to song; he rarely plays the same way twice. Then hear Cream's Fresh Cream and Hendrix's debut, both made within the year, to hear what the Yardbirds set in motion."],"small-faces-itchycoo-park":["A gentle, sing-along song about skipping school or work to spend a sunny day in a park, sung by Steve Marriott and Ronnie Lane in turn. The verses are light and acoustic, almost nursery-rhyme simple; the chorus swells with harmonies and Ian McLagan's organ. Then, in the instrumental break, the whole sound seems to tilt and whoosh, the drums sweeping like a jet engine passing overhead. That whooshing passage is what everyone remembers.","Released in the summer of 1967 on Immediate, the independent label run by Andrew Loog Oldham, after the band left Decca. The Small Faces were an East London mod band who had built their name on hard soul-influenced singles; 'Itchycoo Park' was their move into the psychedelic summer, with a gentler, pastoral sound. It became a top-ten hit in Britain and their only big American success during the band's lifetime.","The whooshing effect is phasing, made in the studio by running two copies of the same recording slightly out of step so that the sound sweeps against itself; the Olympic Studios engineer George Chkiantz is usually credited with the technique. Few British singles had used it so prominently, and its presence on a top-ten record helped spread the technique. The song's innocent tone carries a coy double meaning, which the band enjoyed and broadcasters mostly missed.","It was a hit and has stayed one of the most played songs of 1967 on British radio, but the band later felt it misrepresented their harder, soul-based sound. Critics sometimes dismiss it as lightweight, but the phasing alone gives it a place in studio history. Its cheerful mood is now often heard as the essence of a particular English moment.","Wait for the instrumental break and listen on headphones as the phasing sweeps across the drums. Then compare Marriott's lighter singing here with his full-throated soul voice elsewhere. Follow it with Ogdens' Nut Gone Flake to hear the same band at full stretch, and with 'Waterloo Sunset' for another English pastoral from 1967."],"small-faces-ogdens-nut-gone-flake":["Two sides that could hardly be more different. Side one is six separate songs: a heavy organ-led title instrumental, the soul ballad 'Afterglow', and the cockney music-hall singalong 'Lazy Sunday'. Side two is a fairy-tale suite about Happiness Stan, a young man searching for the missing half of the moon, with songs linked by the comedian Stanley Unwin narrating in his invented, tumbling gobbledygook language. Hard rock, soul, music hall and nonsense sit side by side.","Released by Immediate in 1968 and the Small Faces' most ambitious record, made at Olympic Studios in London with Glyn Johns engineering. The title parodied a well-known brand of pipe tobacco, and the original sleeve was round, folding out like the lid of a tobacco tin. It went to the top of the British album chart, but the band found it impossible to perform live, and within a year Steve Marriott had left to form Humble Pie.","Stanley Unwin was a British radio comedian famous for 'Unwinese', a speech in which real words bend and break into new ones; the band wrote his narration as a framing device for the whole side. Listen to 'Afterglow', where Marriott's voice moves from a hush to a full soul shout in a single verse, a reminder that these were mods who learned to sing from American soul records.","It was a commercial peak and a critical success, but its very richness ended the band; the Small Faces only ever performed it once in public, on a BBC television programme. It is now regarded as one of the key British psychedelic albums and an early concept record, admired for its mix of humour and ambition. Some critics still prefer the plainer first side.","Hear 'Lazy Sunday' and 'Afterglow' back to back to grasp the band's range, then put on side two straight through, letting Unwin's narration carry you between songs. Notice the music-hall accent and piano rhythms. Compare it with the Kinks' Arthur and the Who's Tommy, both of which tried in different ways to make an album tell a story."],"the-byrds-younger-than-yesterday":["Eleven songs in under half an hour, opening with 'So You Want to Be a Rock 'n' Roll Star', a sardonic piece about the pop machine, set against a screaming audience and a trumpet. Roger McGuinn's jangling twelve-string guitar is there, but the album spreads wider: David Crosby's jazz-tinged 'Everybody's Been Burned', Chris Hillman's country-leaning songs, the space-themed 'C.T.A.-102', and a cover of Bob Dylan's 'My Back Pages' that became one of their best known recordings.","Recorded in Hollywood late in 1966 and released by Columbia early in 1967, produced by Gary Usher. The Byrds had lost their main songwriter, Gene Clark, the year before, and the space he left was filled by Hillman, who suddenly emerged as a writer, and by Crosby. The band was moving between Los Angeles folk rock and the new San Francisco sounds, and between pop success and a more experimental studio approach.","The trumpet on the opening track is played by the South African musician Hugh Masekela, then living in America. On 'Time Between' and elsewhere, the guitar of the bluegrass and country player Clarence White appears, a quiet hint of the country-rock turn the Byrds would take on Sweetheart of the Rodeo the next year. Listen to the backwards guitar on 'Mind Gardens', a sign that the band were listening hard to the Beatles' Revolver.","It sold less than earlier Byrds records and was received as a curiosity at first, but its reputation rose steadily, and many critics now rate it as the band's most consistent album. It is valued as a bridge between 1965's folk rock and the country rock of 1968, and as the record on which Hillman and Crosby became writers. Crosby's contributions remain the most debated.","Start with 'So You Want to Be a Rock 'n' Roll Star' and 'My Back Pages', then try Hillman's 'Have You Seen Her Face' and 'Time Between' to hear country edging in. Listen for how short and economical everything is. Follow it with Sweetheart of the Rodeo and Gram Parsons's records to hear where the country thread led."],"the-byrds-notorious-byrd-brothers":["Eleven songs that flow into one another with crossfades, making the album feel like a single drifting suite. There are two songs by Gerry Goffin and Carole King, 'Goin' Back' and 'Wasn't Born to Follow'; there is a hushed anti-war song, 'Draft Morning'; there is a cheerful country song, 'Old John Robertson', with a string-quartet interlude. The record ends with 'Space Odyssey', in which the electronic tones of a Moog synthesizer replace the familiar twelve-string jangle.","Made in Hollywood across the second half of 1967 with producer Gary Usher, during a period of turmoil: David Crosby was sacked from the band in the middle of the sessions, and the drummer Michael Clarke also departed. The remaining Byrds, Roger McGuinn and Chris Hillman, finished the album with session players. It was released early in 1968 by Columbia and sold poorly compared to the band's earlier work.","On the cover four windows in an old stable frame the band's faces, but only three Byrds appear; a horse looks out of the fourth. Fans long took this as a joke at Crosby's expense; band members have given differing accounts. Crosby's song 'Triad', which the others refused to release, ended up with Jefferson Airplane. Listen for the Moog throughout; it was among the first rock records to use the instrument.","Sales were weak and reviews were warm but confused, but critical opinion has turned firmly in its favour, and it is now often rated the Byrds' finest album, admired for its seamless flow and studio adventurousness. It is cited as a forerunner of the album as a continuous listening experience. Arguments continue over how much Crosby contributed before his departure.","Play it straight through without stopping, as a single half-hour piece, and notice how the songs fade into each other. Pay attention to 'Goin' Back' and 'Draft Morning' for their quiet beauty and the moment the Moog enters. Compare it with the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds and the Zombies' Odessey and Oracle for other studio albums of this moment."],"buffalo-springfield-for-what-its-worth":["A slow, uneasy song built on a cool, restrained groove, about young people and police facing off in the street. Stephen Stills sings in a calm, almost detached voice, and the chorus asks everyone to stop and listen. The defining sound is a single guitar figure from Neil Young: two high harmonic notes, chiming like a bell or a warning signal, repeated throughout. The understatement is the point; it sounds like tension rather than rage.","Stills wrote it in November 1966 after the Sunset Strip curfew riots in Los Angeles, when young people protested against a crackdown on clubs and loitering on the Strip. The band recorded it in December and Atco released it as a single within weeks; it became a top-ten hit early in 1967 and was added to later pressings of the band's debut album. Its message about the street soon attached itself to Vietnam protests.","The song is not about Vietnam, though it has become the soundtrack to countless documentaries about that war; it is about a local dispute over a nightclub strip. Listen to how little the drums and bass do, keeping a lazy, steady beat, and how Young's harmonics sit on top like an alarm. The record was made fast, and its looseness is part of its watchful mood.","It was the band's only big hit, and it has become one of the most used protest songs of the sixties, sometimes to the irritation of those who know its origins. Critics praise it for its restraint and its feeling of watchfulness. Its success arguably distorted the band's reputation, since their albums show a much wider and stranger range.","Listen first to Young's two-note figure and count how often it returns; then focus on the calm of Stills's voice. Notice that the band never builds to a climax. Follow it with Buffalo Springfield Again for their fuller sound, and with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young's 'Ohio' for a later protest song from the same musicians, written in anger rather than unease."],"buffalo-springfield-again":["Ten songs by three writers that rarely sound like the same band. Neil Young opens with 'Mr. Soul', a hard, riff-driven song, and contributes 'Expecting to Fly', a lush, orchestral dream, and 'Broken Arrow', a collage that splices in crowd noise and a jazz piano trio. Stephen Stills offers 'Bluebird', which moves from electric guitars to banjo, and 'Rock & Roll Woman'. Richie Furay brings brighter, country-tinged songs like 'A Child's Claim to Fame'.","Recorded in Los Angeles across 1967 and released by Atco late that year, while the band was coming apart: Young left and rejoined more than once, and the members often recorded separately with session players. The result is less a group statement than three solo projects sharing a sleeve. The band had become central to the Laurel Canyon scene, and its members would go on to Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young, Poco and Young's solo career.","'Expecting to Fly' is essentially a Neil Young solo track made with the arranger Jack Nitzsche, who had worked with Phil Spector, and its echo-laden strings come straight from that school. 'Broken Arrow' ends with the sound of a heartbeat, an early sign of Young's interest in sound collage. Listen to the banjo and fiddle at the end of 'Bluebird': country music is already inside this rock band.","It was well reviewed and is often called the band's best album, though its fragmentation is plain. Critics have argued that its very lack of unity is what makes it interesting: a portrait of three major songwriters before they went their own ways. Its influence on country rock and on the singer-songwriters of the early seventies is now widely recognised.","Start with 'Mr. Soul' and 'Bluebird' for the band's electric side, then 'Expecting to Fly' on headphones for Nitzsche's orchestration. Finish with 'Broken Arrow' and listen to how its sections are cut together. Then hear Crosby, Stills and Nash's first album and Neil Young's Everybody Knows This Is Nowhere to follow the band's members outward."],"jefferson-airplane-surrealistic-pillow":["Eleven songs that set the template for the San Francisco sound: folk-rock harmonies, Jorma Kaukonen's piercing lead guitar and Jack Casady's melodic, rumbling bass. Grace Slick's voice, sharp and declamatory, dominates 'Somebody to Love' and 'White Rabbit', a slow crescendo that recasts Alice in Wonderland as an allegory for drug experience. Marty Balin's tender 'Today' and 'Comin' Back to Me' provide the gentle side, and 'Embryonic Journey' is a short acoustic guitar instrumental.","Recorded in Los Angeles late in 1966 with producer Rick Jarrard and released by RCA Victor early in 1967. Slick had just joined from the Great Society, bringing two songs from that band; 'Somebody to Love' was written by her brother-in-law, Darby Slick. The album and its two hit singles helped make San Francisco the focus of national attention in the run-up to the Summer of Love.","'White Rabbit' is built on a steady bolero rhythm and a slow crescendo; Slick has said she was thinking of Ravel's Boléro and of Miles Davis and Gil Evans's Sketches of Spain, which she had listened to obsessively. Jerry Garcia of the Grateful Dead plays on several tracks and is credited on the sleeve as 'musical and spiritual adviser'. 'My Best Friend' was written by the band's former drummer, Skip Spence, who had left to join Moby Grape.","It was a commercial breakthrough and the definitive record of San Francisco psychedelia. Some critics have noted that it is more polished and folk-rooted than the band's live shows, which were looser and louder. Its two hit singles are now among the most familiar songs of the sixties, and 'White Rabbit' in particular is endlessly used in film and television to signal the era.","Listen to 'White Rabbit' alongside Ravel's Boléro to hear how the crescendo works in each, then try 'Comin' Back to Me' for the album's gentler side. Notice Casady's bass, which often carries a melody of its own. Follow it with the Grateful Dead's Live/Dead to hear the San Francisco scene stretched out on stage."],"jefferson-airplane-volunteers":["The Airplane's most openly political album, opening with 'We Can Be Together', a call for youth revolution sung in shouted unison, and closing with the title song, a rallying chant. Between them come 'Good Shepherd', an old hymn rearranged by Jorma Kaukonen, the country-flavoured 'The Farm', the post-apocalyptic 'Wooden Ships', and 'Meadowlands', a brief organ rendering of a Soviet song. Nicky Hopkins's piano runs through much of the album.","Recorded in San Francisco during 1969 and released by RCA late that year, a few months after the band played Woodstock. It was the last album with the original drummer Spencer Dryden and the last before the band splintered into side projects. The political mood reflected the Vietnam War, the urban riots and student uprisings of 1968, and the counterculture's turn from flower power toward confrontation.","'Wooden Ships' was written jointly by Paul Kantner, David Crosby and Stephen Stills, and Crosby, Stills and Nash released their own version the same year; hearing the two side by side shows the difference between the bands. Listen for Jerry Garcia's pedal steel guitar on 'The Farm', a sign of the Grateful Dead's simultaneous turn to country. 'Meadowlands' is a Red Army song, chosen as a provocation.","It was a commercial success and is often regarded as the Airplane's last great album, though some critics found its revolutionary slogans naive even at the time. RCA objected to some of its language, and the original title was shortened after a charity with a similar name complained. It is now valued as a document of the counterculture's most militant moment and as a showcase for some of the band's best playing.","Start with 'Wooden Ships' and compare it with the version on Crosby, Stills and Nash's debut. Then hear 'Good Shepherd' for Kaukonen's guitar and the band's harmonies at their best. Notice how Hopkins's piano fills the spaces. Pair it with Fortunate Son and 'Ohio' for other protest songs from the end of the decade."],"grateful-dead-live-dead":["A double album of seven performances in which songs dissolve into long collective improvisations. It opens with a twenty-three-minute 'Dark Star', where Jerry Garcia's guitar spins slow, searching lines over a floating, unhurried groove. 'St. Stephen' and 'The Eleven', the latter in a rolling eleven-beat cycle, follow without a break. Then comes Bobby Bland's 'Turn On Your Lovelight', sung by the organist Pigpen, the Reverend Gary Davis's 'Death Don't Have No Mercy', a passage of pure feedback, and a closing a cappella hymn.","Recorded at the Fillmore West and the Avalon Ballroom in San Francisco early in 1969 and released by Warner Bros. late that year. The band had spent heavily on studio albums that failed to capture their live energy, and they now recorded with a sixteen-track machine, then a new technology for live work. The line-up was at its largest, with two drummers, Bill Kreutzmann and Mickey Hart, and the keyboard player Tom Constanten.","Both Phil Lesh, the bassist, and Constanten had studied composition with Luciano Berio at Mills College, and Lesh's bass playing, which moves melodically rather than holding down the root, owes a debt to classical counterpoint and to free jazz. 'The Eleven' takes its name from its unusual eleven-beat metre, a feat few rock bands of the time would have attempted, sustained here for minutes at a time.","It was widely praised as the first record to capture the Grateful Dead's real nature, and it remains a reference point for the band's fans and for improvising rock musicians. Critics debate whether its long improvisations are profound or self-indulgent; the band's admirers point out that 'Dark Star' sounds different every time, and that this recording is only one of hundreds of versions.","Set aside time for 'Dark Star' and listen to it as you might a long jazz improvisation, following Garcia's line as it wanders and returns. Notice how Lesh's bass talks back. Then compare it with Coltrane's Live at the Village Vanguard Again or Miles Davis's Bitches Brew to hear what rock was borrowing from jazz."],"grateful-dead-workingmans-dead":["Eight compact songs, mostly acoustic and country-flavoured, a sharp turn from the long improvisations of Live/Dead. 'Uncle John's Band' opens with layered harmonies over acoustic guitars and a shifting rhythm. 'Dire Wolf' is a mock ballad, 'Cumberland Blues' a bluegrass-tinged song about a mining town, 'Black Peter' a slow lament from a dying man. 'Casey Jones' rides a train-like beat to tell the story of a reckless engineer. Robert Hunter's lyrics draw on American folk tales and work songs.","Recorded quickly in San Francisco early in 1970 and released by Warner Bros. in the spring. The band was deep in debt and reacting both to the expense of their earlier experiments and to the violence at the Altamont festival the previous December, which haunts 'New Speedway Boogie'. They had been spending time with Crosby, Stills and Nash, whose close harmony singing they set out to imitate.","Listen to the harmonies in 'Uncle John's Band': the band were not natural singers, and they worked hard to learn harmony from Crosby, Stills and Nash, which is why the singing is careful and slightly fragile. The song changes metre in its instrumental passage, a trace of the band's improvising past inside a folk song. 'Casey Jones' carries a passing drug reference in its chorus, set to the steady rhythm of a train.","It was the band's first commercial success and was immediately recognised as a turning point, opening up a large new audience. It is now regarded as one of the defining country-rock albums of its era. Some longtime fans regretted the loss of the long jams, but most critics see this and American Beauty as the band's finest studio work.","Start with 'Uncle John's Band' and listen to the harmonies and the shifting rhythm, then hear 'Cumberland Blues' for the bluegrass influence. Compare the album's compact songs with the sprawl of Live/Dead. Pair it with the Band's Music from Big Pink and the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo for other records of rock's return to American roots."],"grateful-dead-american-beauty":["Ten songs that complete the turn begun on Workingman's Dead, warmer and more varied. Phil Lesh sings 'Box of Rain', written for his dying father, over a gentle country rhythm. 'Friend of the Devil' is a fast, fingerpicked song about an outlaw on the run; 'Ripple' a near-hymn about the passage of life; 'Brokedown Palace' a farewell; 'Sugar Magnolia' a sunny love song; and 'Truckin'', the closer, a rambling account of life on the road.","Recorded in San Francisco in the late summer of 1970 and released by Warner Bros. in November, only months after Workingman's Dead. The sessions came during a hard year for the band, including the death of family members, and the album's gentleness carries a shadow of grief. The title is a pun on 'American Reality', and the sleeve's lettering can be read both ways.","The mandolin player David Grisman, later a leading figure in new acoustic music, plays on 'Friend of the Devil' and 'Ripple'. Listen to the harmonies of 'Attics of My Life', where the band's three voices move in close chords almost like a church choir, a skill they had developed from Crosby, Stills and Nash. 'Truckin'' became the band's anthem, and the drama of its lyrics was drawn from their own lives.","It was received as one of the band's best records and has been regarded as a classic ever since; many critics rate it as their finest studio album. It is often paired with Workingman's Dead as a single statement. Some fans who preferred the improvising band missed the long jams, but these songs became the core of their live sets for decades.","Start with 'Box of Rain' and 'Ripple', listening to the voices and acoustic guitars, then hear 'Truckin'' for the band's rocking side. Notice the slow tempos and the space between instruments. Pair it with Workingman's Dead and with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young's Déjà Vu, made at the same time and in the same atmosphere."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-green-river":["Nine songs in under half an hour, played by a four-piece band with almost no studio decoration. John Fogerty's rasping voice and twangy, echo-drenched guitar dominate the title track, a homesick song about a river from childhood. 'Bad Moon Rising' sets a warning of disaster to a bright, almost cheerful country shuffle. 'Lodi' is a weary song about a musician stranded in a small town, and 'Commotion' a fast, rattling rockabilly about modern noise.","Recorded at Wally Heider's studio in San Francisco and released by Fantasy in 1969, the second of three albums the band released that year. Creedence came from El Cerrito, across the bay from San Francisco, but ignored the psychedelic scene around them, playing short, tight songs drawn from rockabilly, Memphis soul and Louisiana swamp music. John Fogerty wrote, sang, arranged and produced nearly everything.","Fogerty has said the title came from the label of a soft-drink syrup, but the song's imagined river comes from childhood summers spent at a creek in Northern California. The 'swamp' sound of Creedence, which sounds as if it came from Louisiana, was entirely invented by a band who had never lived there; they built it from records by Sun artists, Bo Diddley and Louisiana swamp-pop singers.","It was a major success and has remained one of the band's most admired records. Critics praised its economy, a sharp contrast to the long jams of the San Francisco bands. Creedence was sometimes dismissed by the underground press as a singles band, but their reputation has grown steadily, and they are now regarded as one of the great American rock bands of the era.","Start with 'Green River' and listen to Fogerty's guitar echo and the push of the rhythm section, then 'Bad Moon Rising' for the gap between its bouncy tune and its dark warning. Notice how short and lean the songs are. Compare it with the Band's records from the same years for a different take on American roots music."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-fortunate-son":["Two and a quarter minutes of furious, driving rock, with John Fogerty's voice at its most raw. The song is an attack on the privileged families whose sons avoided military service in Vietnam while working-class boys went to fight. It starts with a sudden crash of drums and a jangling guitar riff, then rides a relentless beat with no slow-down, no solo of any length and no softening chorus. The anger is in the speed.","Released by Fantasy in the autumn of 1969 as a double-sided single with 'Down on the Corner', and included on the album Willy and the Poor Boys. Fogerty had served in the Army Reserve and was disgusted by the way the children of the powerful escaped the draft. The song appeared at the height of the anti-war movement, in the same season as the large Moratorium protests across America.","The song is often misheard or misused as patriotic, and has been played at political events in support of the very things it attacks; it is a song against privilege, not against soldiers. Listen to the band's restraint: the rhythm section plays straight, with no fills, so that the force comes from Fogerty's voice and the hard-strummed guitar. It is among the shortest songs in the band's catalogue.","It was a hit and became one of the defining protest songs of the Vietnam era, used in countless films about the war. Critics praise its directness and its class consciousness, which set it apart from more abstract anti-war songs. Its frequent use in contexts that ignore its meaning has become a subject of discussion in itself, and Fogerty has objected to its misuse.","Listen to the first second, the drum crash, and notice how the song never lets up. Focus on Fogerty's voice and the way his phrasing seems to spit the words out. Pair it with 'Ohio' and 'For What It's Worth' for three very different protest songs, and with Pete Seeger's 'Waist Deep in the Big Muddy' for an earlier generation's approach."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-cosmos-factory":["Eleven tracks that mix new John Fogerty songs with covers of the records he grew up on. The originals include 'Travelin' Band', a Little Richard-style rocker; 'Lookin' Out My Back Door', a cheerful, almost childlike song; 'Run Through the Jungle', dark and swampy, with backward guitar; 'Up Around the Bend'; and 'Who'll Stop the Rain'. The covers include Bo Diddley, Roy Orbison, Arthur Crudup and an eleven-minute version of 'I Heard It Through the Grapevine'.","Released by Fantasy in the summer of 1970, the band's fifth album in two years, at the peak of their commercial success. The title comes from the band's rehearsal space, a warehouse in Berkeley that the drummer Doug Clifford, nicknamed Cosmo, called 'the factory'. Within months, the band's guitarist Tom Fogerty, John's brother, would leave, tensions over John's control of the group having grown impossible.","'Run Through the Jungle' is often taken to be about Vietnam, but Fogerty has said it was about gun violence in America. Listen to the long 'Grapevine', where the band stretches out into a slow, groovy jam; it is a rare example of Creedence playing at length, and the result is closer to a Memphis soul band than to the San Francisco jam bands. The saxophone on some tracks is played by Fogerty himself.","It was the band's biggest selling album and is often regarded as their finest. Critics praise its range, from simple pop to swamp-blues menace, and its tribute to the band's influences. Some have found the long 'Grapevine' unnecessary, but others see it as a clue to the band's roots. It is now one of the most played rock albums of its time.","Start with 'Run Through the Jungle' and 'Who'll Stop the Rain' for the darker side, then 'Lookin' Out My Back Door' for the lighter. Then hear the 'Grapevine' and compare it with Marvin Gaye's version. Notice how tight the band is even when stretching out. Pair it with Green River to hear how much the band developed in a year."],"big-brother-and-the-holding-company-cheap-thrills":["Seven tracks, built around Janis Joplin's voice, which moves from a whisper to a scream within a phrase, over a ragged, overdriven band. 'Piece of My Heart' became a hit; 'Summertime' turns George Gershwin's lullaby into a slow, twisting guitar duet and a cry of pain; 'Ball and Chain', from the repertoire of Big Mama Thornton, runs nearly ten minutes and ends in a howl. 'Turtle Blues', a Joplin original, is a quiet, piano-backed blues.","Released by Columbia in 1968 after the band's explosive appearance at the Monterey Pop Festival the year before made Joplin a star. The producer John Simon struggled to capture the band in the studio, and the record was assembled mostly from studio takes with crowd noise added, to give it a live feeling; 'Ball and Chain' alone was recorded at a real concert. The cover was drawn by the underground cartoonist Robert Crumb.","Listen to 'Summertime': the two guitarists, Sam Andrew and James Gurley, weave an interlocking, almost Baroque counterpoint, a strange and touching meeting of classical habits and acid rock. The album's 'live' atmosphere is mostly an illusion, created in the studio. Joplin's voice owed its rawness to her deep study of blues singers like Bessie Smith, whose grave she helped to mark with a headstone.","It was a huge success, topping the American album chart, and made Joplin the leading female voice in rock. Some critics found the band sloppy and believed Joplin was held back by them; others argued their rawness was the right setting for her. Joplin left the band soon after, and the album is now valued as the best record of her with the group that made her famous.","Start with 'Summertime' and compare it with the original in Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, then hear 'Piece of My Heart' and 'Ball and Chain' for Joplin at full power. Notice how she uses breaks and cracks in her voice as expression. Pair it with Pearl to hear her with a tighter band."],"janis-joplin-pearl":["Ten tracks with the Full Tilt Boogie Band, a tighter and more soulful group than Big Brother. Kris Kristofferson's 'Me and Bobby McGee', a song about a drifter remembering a lost companion, starts soft and acoustic and builds to a joyful, rough-voiced climax. 'Cry Baby' and 'Get It While You Can' are soul ballads at full volume. 'Mercedes Benz' is sung unaccompanied, a wry prayer for luxury goods. 'Buried Alive in the Blues' is an instrumental.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1970 with the producer Paul Rothchild, best known for his work with the Doors. Joplin had left Big Brother, struggled with a first solo band, and found a sympathetic group in Full Tilt Boogie. She died in October 1970, before the sessions were finished, and the album was completed and released by Columbia in 1971. Pearl was her nickname.","'Buried Alive in the Blues' is an instrumental because Joplin had been scheduled to record her vocal the day after she died; the band left the track as it stood. 'Mercedes Benz' was recorded in a single take, and you can hear her laugh at the end. Listen to 'Me and Bobby McGee' for how she changes the song's mood from a folk ballad to a soul celebration.","It was a posthumous success, topping the American album chart, and 'Me and Bobby McGee' became a number one single. Critics have praised it as her most accomplished record and lamented the unfinished career. The debate is whether the polished band brought out her best or tamed her; most listeners now regard it as the best showcase for her voice.","Start with 'Me and Bobby McGee' and listen to how the arrangement builds with her voice, then hear 'Cry Baby' for her soul style. Notice the control as well as the power. Compare it with Cheap Thrills to hear how the band changes the singer, and with Aretha Franklin's records of the same years."],"moby-grape-moby-grape":["Thirteen short songs, written and sung by all five members, played with three interlocking guitars. 'Omaha' is a speedy, shouted song that crashes in and out; 'Hey Grandma' a bright, jangling rocker; '8:05' a gentle acoustic country ballad; 'Sitting by the Window' a wistful folk song. The sound is tight and concise, more like a British beat group or a country band than the long-jamming San Francisco bands around them.","Recorded in Los Angeles with producer David Rubinson and released by Columbia in 1967, at the height of the San Francisco boom. The label, trying to promote the band, released five singles at once, which confused radio and became a notorious marketing failure. Legal troubles followed soon after, and the band never recovered its momentum. Skip Spence, one of the guitarists, had been Jefferson Airplane's first drummer.","Listen to the three guitars of Spence, Jerry Miller and Peter Lewis, which play distinct parts that fit together like pieces of a puzzle, a texture very different from most San Francisco bands. On the original cover, the drummer Don Stevenson is making a rude gesture with his finger, which was airbrushed out of later pressings. Spence went on to make a cult solo album, Oar, in 1969.","It received excellent reviews but sold poorly, due in part to the label's mishandling. It has since been recognised as one of the finest debut albums of the sixties, and many musicians cite it as an influence. The band's story is often told as a cautionary tale about the music industry, but the record itself is now praised for its energy and songwriting.","Start with 'Omaha' and 'Hey Grandma' for the guitar interplay, then '8:05' for the country side. Listen to how quickly each song gets to the point. Pair it with Jefferson Airplane's Surrealistic Pillow and the Byrds' Younger Than Yesterday for other West Coast records of the same year."],"van-dyke-parks-song-cycle":["Ten songs that sound like a musical collage of American history, played by an orchestra and studio musicians and sung by Van Dyke Parks in a slightly nasal, playful voice. The arrangements shift constantly: ragtime piano, banjos, string swells, marching bands, accordions, music-box sounds. 'Vine Street', by Randy Newman, opens it; 'Palm Desert' evokes the Californian landscape; 'The All Golden' is a portrait of a friend. The production uses tape speed changes, echo and sudden cuts.","Released by Warner Bros. in 1968 and produced by Lenny Waronker, with engineer Bruce Botnick. Parks had been Brian Wilson's lyricist on the unfinished Smile project, and he made this album with lavish studio resources. It sold very poorly, and Warner Bros. responded with self-mocking advertisements making a joke of how much money it had lost. Parks went on to work as an arranger and producer for others.","Listen to the way Parks quotes and alludes to older American music: a fragment of a hymn, a parlour song, a march. The influence of Charles Ives, who also layered American tunes on top of one another, is everywhere. Parks's lyrics, as on Smile, are dense with puns and wordplay, and his studio tricks owe as much to radio drama as to pop records.","It was greeted with confusion and some admiration, and it sold poorly. Its reputation has grown slowly, as a cult record and a forerunner of later orchestral pop. Critics are still divided: some hear a visionary work about America, others a self-indulgent and impenetrable exercise. Musicians have often championed it, while general listeners find it hard work.","Treat it as a sound collage rather than a set of songs. Start with 'Vine Street' and 'Palm Desert', and listen for the constant shifts in orchestration. Pair it with the Beach Boys' Smile sessions and with Ives's Three Places in New England to hear its roots."],"crosby-stills-and-nash-crosby-stills-and-nash":["Ten songs built on close three-part harmony, a sound in which no single voice leads. 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' opens the album with a seven-minute piece in several sections, played on tuned-down acoustic guitars. 'Marrakesh Express' is Graham Nash's bright travelogue; 'Guinnevere' a slow, modal ballad by David Crosby; 'Wooden Ships' imagines survivors after a nuclear war; 'Helplessly Hoping' a delicate song with tight harmonies; and 'Long Time Gone' a slow, angry response to the assassination of Robert Kennedy.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1969 and released by Atlantic in May, a few months before the trio played Woodstock. Each member came from a successful band: Crosby from the Byrds, Stephen Stills from Buffalo Springfield, Graham Nash from the British group the Hollies. They found their sound singing together at a party, and the album helped establish the singer-songwriter era in rock. Stills played most of the instruments, with Dallas Taylor on drums.","The harmonies are recorded with each voice close to the microphone and carefully blended, so that you hear a single sound rather than three people. Listen to 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' and notice how the guitar tuning lets Stills play rich, ringing chords, and how the song moves through sections with different tempos. The closing section is sung in Spanish.","It was a huge success and became one of the defining records of the late sixties and early seventies. Critics praised the harmonies but some found the songs too sweet or self-satisfied. Its influence on soft rock and on the Laurel Canyon singer-songwriters is enormous, and its harmonies became a model for many bands, including the Grateful Dead.","Start with 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' and follow its sections, then 'Helplessly Hoping' for the harmonies. Notice how the voices move together. Pair it with Buffalo Springfield Again and the Byrds' Younger Than Yesterday to hear where the members came from, and with Déjà Vu for what came next."],"crosby-stills-nash-and-young-deja-vu":["Ten songs by four writers, with Neil Young joining the trio and bringing a rougher edge. 'Carry On' opens with a burst of acoustic guitars and harmonies; 'Teach Your Children' is a country song with pedal steel; 'Almost Cut My Hair' a ragged, angry Crosby blues; 'Helpless' Young's slow, aching song about a northern Ontario town; 'Woodstock' Joni Mitchell's song, turned into a hard rocker; 'Our House' Nash's cosy domestic portrait; and 'Déjà Vu' a jazzy, shifting meditation on reincarnation.","Recorded in San Francisco and Los Angeles in late 1969 and released by Atlantic in March 1970. Young, Stills's old colleague from Buffalo Springfield, had joined for live work in 1969, including Woodstock. The sessions were long and difficult, with each member often recording separately. Crosby was grieving the death of his partner, Christine Hinton, in a car accident, and the album's mood carries that loss.","Jerry Garcia of the Grateful Dead plays the pedal steel on 'Teach Your Children', a sign of the close ties between the two bands. Listen to the title track, where Crosby's song shifts metre and key, a nod to his interest in jazz. Joni Mitchell wrote 'Woodstock' without attending the festival, and this version, loud and electric, is very different from her own gentle recording.","It was a massive success and has remained one of the best-selling albums of its era. Critics praised its range but noted the divisions within the band. Young's songs are often singled out as the strongest, and the record is seen as the high point of the four together. Its harmonies and its picture of the counterculture made it one of the defining records of 1970.","Start with 'Helpless' and 'Teach Your Children', then hear 'Woodstock' and compare it with Joni Mitchell's version on Ladies of the Canyon. Notice the different voices of the four writers. Pair it with American Beauty and Neil Young's After the Gold Rush, made at the same time."],"crosby-stills-nash-and-young-ohio":["A three-minute song of grief and fury about the shooting of students by soldiers at a university campus in Ohio. Neil Young's song is built on a dark, menacing guitar riff and a slow, heavy beat. The band's harmonies, usually sweet, are here strained and anguished. Young's guitar and Stephen Stills's trade brief, stinging lines. At the end, the voices repeat a single phrase, building to a cry.","Written by Young in May 1970 after National Guard troops shot four students dead at Kent State University during a protest against the expansion of the Vietnam War into Cambodia. The band recorded it within weeks, live in the studio in Los Angeles, and Atlantic released it in June, with Stills's 'Find the Cost of Freedom' on the other side. It was rushed out while 'Teach Your Children' was still on the charts.","The recording was made in just a few takes, with the band playing together in one room; you can hear the rawness in the slightly rough edges. Listen to the end, where David Crosby's voice breaks away from the harmony into anguished shouts. The song names the president of the day, which made some radio stations refuse to play it. It was the band's most direct political statement.","It was a hit despite radio bans and has become one of the most powerful protest songs of its era. Critics praise its directness and urgency. It is often paired with Fortunate Son and For What It's Worth as a defining anti-war song, and it is now widely regarded as one of the greatest examples of rock responding to current events.","Listen to the opening riff and notice its darkness, then focus on the voices, especially in the final minute. Notice how the song never resolves into comfort. Pair it with 'Fortunate Son' and 'For What It's Worth' for three different approaches to protest, and with Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' for an earlier generation's."],"the-mamas-and-the-papas-california-dreamin":["A song about longing for California's warmth during a cold East Coast winter, sung by four voices in tight harmony over a folk-rock backing. Denny Doherty sings lead, with the others answering in close echoes; Cass Elliot's voice gives the harmonies their warmth. The guitar intro, a descending figure, is instantly recognisable. In the middle, a solo on alto flute adds a soft, jazzy colour.","Written by John and Michelle Phillips in New York during the winter of 1963, before they moved to California. It was recorded in Los Angeles in 1965 with producer Lou Adler and released by Dunhill late that year, becoming a hit early in 1966. The group came from the New York folk scene and the record helped bring the harmony-rich folk rock of Los Angeles to the charts.","The backing track was originally recorded for the singer Barry McGuire, with the group on backing vocals; Doherty's lead vocal later replaced McGuire's. The alto flute solo was played by the jazz musician Bud Shank. Listen to how the harmonies are arranged as call and response, with the backing voices echoing the lead line, a trick borrowed from gospel.","It was a huge success and has remained one of the defining songs of the sixties. Critics have praised its harmonies and its evocation of longing. It is often seen as the start of the Laurel Canyon era and has been widely covered. Its use in films and advertising has made it one of the most familiar songs of its time.","Listen to the guitar intro and then the harmonies, noticing how the voices answer each other. Wait for the flute solo and notice its jazzy colour. Pair it with the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man' and the Beach Boys' 'California Girls' for other visions of California in the mid-sixties."],"steely-dan-cant-buy-a-thrill":["Ten songs that sound like a working band and a pair of sly songwriters at once. 'Do It Again' opens on a patient Latin groove of hand percussion and bass, then gives its middle to two strange solos, one on electric sitar and one on a thin, buzzing combo organ. 'Reelin' in the Years' rides a sprinting guitar break; 'Dirty Work' is a soft soul ballad sung by a different voice. Polished, jazz-inflected chords sit under deceptively breezy pop.","Walter Becker and Donald Fagen met at Bard College in upstate New York, scraped by in the New York song trade and were hired as staff writers at ABC Records in Los Angeles through the producer Gary Katz. Their songs were too odd for other singers, so Katz helped them assemble a band of their own, named after an object in William S. Burroughs's novel Naked Lunch. This debut, made quickly in Los Angeles, was the result.","The lead vocals are split: Fagen sings most songs, but David Palmer, brought in because Fagen was nervous about singing, takes 'Dirty Work' and 'Brooklyn'. Listen to the guitar solo on 'Reelin' in the Years': it is played not by a band member but by the session guitarist Elliott Randall, an early sign of the hired-hand method that would later define Steely Dan entirely.","Both main singles became radio staples and the album sold well, an unusual start for a group whose lyrics were cryptic and sardonic. Early reviewers heard a clever pop band; later critics heard the first draft of a sensibility, ironic, literate and harmonically rich, that the duo would refine through the decade. Some fans still prefer this looser, band-like record to the studio perfectionism that followed.","Start with 'Do It Again' and wait for the two solos in the middle, each with its own odd timbre. Then play 'Reelin' in the Years' for the guitar. Compare 'Dirty Work' with Fagen's own singing elsewhere to hear why he eventually took over. Afterwards jump to Aja to hear how far the method travelled in five years."],"steely-dan-pretzel-logic":["Eleven songs in barely more than half an hour, most of them under three minutes and packed tight with chords. 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number' opens with a gentle wooden marimba and a loping bass figure before a sunny chorus; 'Night by Night' and the title track lean on horns and blues; 'Parker's Band' is a breathless tribute to Charlie Parker; and a cover of Duke Ellington's 'East St. Louis Toodle-Oo' turns a 1920s jungle-band number into a guitar showcase.","By their third album Becker and Fagen were already drifting from the idea of a fixed band, calling on Los Angeles session players alongside the original members. It was the last record Steely Dan made as a touring group: after the 1974 shows they withdrew from the road and became a studio partnership. ABC gave them unusual freedom because the earlier singles had sold, and they used it to pursue their love of jazz.","The bass line under the opening of 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number' is borrowed from the piano vamp of Horace Silver's 'Song for My Father', a deliberate tip of the hat to a Blue Note record. In the Ellington cover, Walter Becker's guitar, bent and filtered, is imitating the growling plunger-muted trumpet that Bubber Miley played on the original. Both are clues to the duo's real record collection.","'Rikki' became their biggest hit single and the album was well reviewed, though some critics found the duo's irony chilly and their brevity frustrating. Over time the record has been reappraised as their most compact statement, a bridge between the rock band of the first two albums and the jazz-session laboratories of Aja and Gaucho. Jazz writers noticed the Ellington and Parker homages with some amusement and some respect.","Play 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number', then put on Horace Silver's 'Song for My Father' and hear the borrowing. Next, play 'East St. Louis Toodle-Oo' beside an early Ellington recording and listen for Miley's trumpet in Becker's guitar. Notice how short every song is: the whole record moves like a book of sharp short stories."],"steely-dan-aja":["Seven long, glossy songs played by a rotating cast of the best session musicians in Los Angeles and New York. 'Black Cow' rides a slinky groove; 'Deacon Blues' unfolds a loser's dream of becoming a saxophonist over rich horn chords; 'Peg' is bright and bouncy; and the eight-minute title track moves through several sections like a small jazz suite, climaxing in a tenor saxophone solo over furious drumming. Every surface is clean, warm and exactly placed.","Becker and Fagen had stopped touring and now worked purely in the studio with the producer Gary Katz and engineers including Roger Nichols, auditioning player after player for single parts. The method was expensive and slow, made possible by ABC's faith and the late-seventies boom in album sales. Aja arrived at the height of the punk year, as a deliberate opposite: the most carefully made record imaginable.","On the title track, the tenor solo is by Wayne Shorter of Weather Report and the drums by Steve Gadd, who reportedly played his famous fills in very few takes. On 'Peg', several guitarists were tried before Jay Graydon's solo was kept; Michael McDonald's high harmony stacks the chorus. A decade later De La Soul looped 'Peg' for 'Eye Know', carrying the record into hip-hop.","It became the duo's best-selling album and won a Grammy for its engineering, and hi-fi shops used it to demonstrate speakers. Critics argued about whether such perfection was soulless; jazz musicians generally admired its harmonic sophistication. Its reputation has only grown, revived by later generations of producers who study its drum sounds, and it is now a reference point for so-called yacht rock and for studio craft generally.","Play the title track on good speakers and wait for Shorter's entrance, then follow Gadd's drums under him to the end. 'Deacon Blues' rewards attention to its horn voicings. Then hear De La Soul's 'Eye Know' to catch 'Peg' in a new life. Any decent remaster will do; this is a record made for careful listening."],"steely-dan-gaucho":["Seven songs with a cooler, more nocturnal sheen than Aja. 'Babylon Sisters' opens on a slow, heavy groove with women's voices answering Fagen; 'Hey Nineteen' is a wry song about an older man discovering how little he shares with a younger generation; 'Time Out of Mind' has a guest guitar line from Mark Knopfler; the title track drifts over a long saxophone-led groove. Grooves are slower and the horn writing is denser than ever.","The album took around two years and an enormous studio budget, during a period when the label situation changed and MCA absorbed ABC. Becker was injured when struck by a car in New York, the duo were exhausted, and a finished track, 'The Second Arrangement', was accidentally erased by an assistant engineer. After its release Becker and Fagen went their separate ways for more than a decade.","The title track's main theme closely resembles Keith Jarrett's 'Long As You Know You're Living Yours' from his 1974 quartet album Belonging; after Jarrett objected, he was given a writing credit. Listen for how often the songs fade on a groove rather than ending, and for the drum machine-like precision that Roger Nichols pursued with an early sampling device of his own design.","It sold well and 'Hey Nineteen' was a hit, but reviewers were divided: some heard the duo's richest harmonies, others a sterile, airless record made by men who had lost interest in rock. The punishing sessions became legend. Later listeners, and the producers who sample it, rank it close to Aja, and its quiet, adult melancholy now sounds more personal than it seemed in 1980.","Begin with 'Babylon Sisters' at night and turn it up enough to feel the bass. Then play the title track beside Jarrett's 'Long As You Know You're Living Yours' to hear the borrowed melody. 'Time Out of Mind' is worth hearing for the Knopfler line, which sounds like a guest from Dire Straits walking into the room."],"eagles-take-it-easy":["A three-and-a-half-minute road song about a restless young man telling himself not to let life's troubles weigh him down. It opens with a bright strum of acoustic guitar, settles into a rolling mid-tempo beat, and lifts into a chorus of close, high four-part harmony. A twanging electric guitar and a banjo threaded through the final choruses tie it to country music, while the drums and volume keep it firmly rock.","The Eagles formed in Los Angeles in 1971 from musicians who had backed Linda Ronstadt: Glenn Frey, Don Henley, Bernie Leadon and Randy Meisner. Signed to David Geffen's new Asylum label, they recorded their debut in London with the producer Glyn Johns. 'Take It Easy' was the first single. Jackson Browne had begun the song and could not finish it; Frey, his neighbour, completed it.","The song's best-known verse, set on a street corner in Winslow, Arizona, was Frey's contribution to Browne's unfinished draft, and the town later built a park around it. Listen for Leadon's banjo, played in a fast bluegrass roll that sits low in the mix: he was the band's bridge to bluegrass and the Flying Burrito Brothers, and his departure later changed their sound.","The single was a hit and launched the band, and Browne released his own version the next year. Critics in the early seventies sometimes found the Eagles too smooth beside the country-rock pioneers they followed, Gram Parsons above all. The song has outlasted the argument, becoming a standard of American radio and the blueprint for the Southern Californian sound of the decade.","Listen first to the chorus harmonies, four voices stacked so close they almost blur. Then find the banjo in the closing minute. For background, play the Flying Burrito Brothers' Gilded Palace of Sin and the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo, then Browne's Late for the Sky to hear where the writing went next."],"eagles-hotel-california":["Nine songs that together form a portrait of Los Angeles excess and disillusion. The title track opens with a twelve-string figure over a lilting, almost reggae rhythm, tells a dreamlike story of a traveller trapped in a luxurious hotel, and ends with two electric guitars trading lines and then playing in harmony for two minutes. 'New Kid in Town' is soft and wistful, 'Life in the Fast Lane' hard and riffing, and 'The Last Resort' a long elegy.","By 1976 the Eagles were among the biggest bands in America, and success had changed both them and their city. Bernie Leadon had left and been replaced by Joe Walsh, a hard-rock guitarist, pulling the band away from country. The producer Bill Szymczyk recorded them in Miami and Los Angeles over many months. Henley and Frey intended the album as a commentary on the American dream and the music business.","The music of the title track began as a demo by guitarist Don Felder; Henley and Frey wrote the words. The famous outro is a dialogue between Felder and Walsh, each taking alternate phrases before they lock into harmony. 'Try and Love Again' is sung and written by Randy Meisner, his last major contribution before leaving: listen for his gentler, higher voice amid Henley's and Frey's.","It sold enormously and the title track won the Grammy for Record of the Year. Rock critics of the punk years treated the Eagles as everything wrong with corporate rock, while listeners kept buying the record. The title song attracted fanciful rumours about hidden meanings, which the band dismissed. Today the album is widely read as the band's most ambitious statement, and the guitar outro remains one of rock's most studied solos.","Play the title track and follow the two guitars in the last two minutes, one in each speaker. Then listen to 'The Last Resort' as the album's real conclusion, a long piano-led lament. For contrast, put on the Ramones' first album from the same year: two utterly different answers to what rock should be in 1976."],"allman-brothers-band-at-fillmore-east":["A double live album of only seven performances. It opens with Duane Allman's sliding bottleneck guitar tearing into Blind Willie McTell's 'Statesboro Blues', moves through Elmore James and T-Bone Walker, then stretches out: Dickey Betts's instrumental 'In Memory of Elizabeth Reed' builds through modal, jazz-style improvising, and Gregg Allman's 'Whipping Post' runs past twenty minutes. Two drummers lock together underneath while two guitars weave melodies in and out of harmony.","The band formed in Jacksonville in 1969 and based itself in Macon, Georgia, on Phil Walden's Capricorn label, as an unusually integrated Southern group with a Black drummer, Jaimoe, alongside Butch Trucks. Their studio albums had not captured them, so the producer Tom Dowd recorded several shows at Bill Graham's Fillmore East in New York in March 1971. Months after release, Duane Allman died in a motorcycle accident in Macon.","Duane said he had absorbed Miles Davis's Kind of Blue and John Coltrane's modal playing, and you can hear it in 'Elizabeth Reed', which sits on long stretches of one harmony while soloists build tension the way a jazz quartet would. Listen too for Berry Oakley's bass, which roams like a third melodic voice rather than holding the root, and for the two drummers splitting the beat between them.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and is routinely named among the greatest live rock albums. Critics praised it at once as proof that long improvisation could be disciplined rather than self-indulgent. Later expanded editions, presenting the full shows, have allowed comparison between nights; most listeners still consider Dowd's original selection the definitive shape, and the record is the founding document of Southern rock.","Start with 'Statesboro Blues' to hear Duane's slide, then give 'In Memory of Elizabeth Reed' your full attention and follow how the solos rise and fall. Save 'Whipping Post' for when you can sit through all of it. Afterwards, play Kind of Blue and listen for the shared sense of space."],"allman-brothers-band-eat-a-peach":["A double album that is half studio and half stage. The studio side includes 'Blue Sky', a sunny country-tinged song by Dickey Betts in which two guitars trade warm, singing solos, and 'Little Martha', a short acoustic duet. Other studio songs, such as 'Melissa' and 'Ain't Wastin' Time No More', were cut by the band after Duane's death. The live half is dominated by 'Mountain Jam', more than half an hour of improvisation from the same Fillmore concerts.","Duane Allman died in October 1971 while the band was midway through recording. The surviving five completed the studio songs, and Capricorn filled out the release with live material left over from the March Fillmore East shows. Released early in 1972, it became a memorial and a statement that the band would carry on; Berry Oakley died the following year in a similar accident a few streets away in Macon.","'Little Martha' is the only song Duane Allman ever wrote, a delicate fingerpicked duet with Betts. 'Mountain Jam' grows out of a melody from Donovan's 'There Is a Mountain', which the band turned into a launching pad for long solos; listen for the tune surfacing at the start and returning near the end. 'Blue Sky' holds some of Duane's last studio solos, alternating with Betts.","It sold strongly and was received with affection and grief. Critics singled out 'Blue Sky' and 'Melissa' as the band's warmest songs, while opinions on the long 'Mountain Jam' divided along familiar lines about improvisation. It now stands as a companion to At Fillmore East, the second half of Duane's legacy, and 'Melissa' has become one of Gregg Allman's most enduring ballads.","Play 'Blue Sky' first and follow the two guitars passing the solo back and forth. Then 'Little Martha' for its intimacy. If you have half an hour, 'Mountain Jam' rewards listening for how the band shifts tempo and mood. Compare the guitar duet with the twin-lead style that Thin Lizzy later made into a rock signature."],"lynyrd-skynyrd-free-bird":["A nine-minute song in two halves about a man telling a lover he must leave because he cannot change. The first half is a slow ballad, with piano, organ and a weeping slide guitar under Ronnie Van Zant's plain, unhurried voice. Then the tempo doubles and the last four minutes are an extended guitar finale, the band's lead players overlapping and racing through fast, repeated phrases until the song finally collapses.","Lynyrd Skynyrd came from Jacksonville, Florida, and named themselves, with a twist of spelling, after a gym teacher who disliked long hair. Al Kooper, who had played organ for Bob Dylan, signed them to his Sounds of the South label, distributed by MCA, and produced their 1973 debut album. 'Free Bird' closed that record and became the band's signature, particularly in concert, where it often ran far longer.","The piano introduction was added by Billy Powell, who had worked as a roadie before the band heard him play. In concert the band dedicated the song to Duane Allman, and the slide guitar of the first half is a clear homage to his style. Listen in the coda for how the three guitars are layered, one holding a rhythm while two others trade and double the lead.","Released as an edited single in 1974, it became a radio standard and a staple of album-oriented rock stations. Its length and its finale made it a byword for the guitar epic, and shouted requests for it became a running joke at concerts of every kind. Beneath the joke, guitarists continue to study the coda, and it remains Southern rock's best-known song.","Hear the full album version, not the single edit. In the first half, notice how slowly the song is allowed to move. Then, when the tempo jumps, follow one guitar and try to keep track of it as the others swirl around. Afterwards play the Allman Brothers' At Fillmore East to hear the model it honours."],"lynyrd-skynyrd-sweet-home-alabama":["A mid-tempo anthem of regional pride, written partly as a reply to Neil Young's criticisms of the South. It opens with a clean, chiming guitar riff of three chords that cycle through the whole song, a relaxed drum groove and a spoken instruction to turn up the volume. Ronnie Van Zant half-sings, half-talks the verses; women's voices answer him in a gospel-style chorus, and Billy Powell's piano rolls under the final instrumental section.","It opened the band's second album, Second Helping, again produced by Al Kooper. Neil Young had written 'Southern Man' and 'Alabama', songs condemning racism in the South, and Van Zant, an admirer of Young, answered on behalf of Southerners who felt caricatured. The song also refers to Alabama's governor and to Watergate, at a moment when American politics was in crisis, and to the Muscle Shoals session musicians.","The riff and solo come from Ed King, then one of the band's three guitarists, who said the music came to him in a dream. The friendly feud with Neil Young was mostly public theatre: Van Zant often wore Young's T-shirts on stage and Young later admired the song. Listen for the backing singers, whose call-and-response gives a gospel colour to a song about the white South.","It became the band's biggest hit. Arguments about its politics began at once and have not ended: some hear a defence of Governor George Wallace, others note that the lyrics seem to boo him and that the song is more ambiguous than its reputation. Musicians admire the riff's economy, and it has become a fixture of sports grounds and films, where its meaning is often flattened.","Listen to the riff first: it never changes, yet the song does not tire. Then play Neil Young's 'Southern Man' on After the Gold Rush and 'Alabama' on Harvest to hear both sides of the argument. Notice how relaxed the drumming is; the song swings rather than pushes."]});